What the hell is this?

284
00:25:00,860 --> 00:25:02,700
The school day is as ordinary as ever.

285
00:25:03,100 --> 00:25:04,780
And it's over before I know it.

286
00:25:06,680 --> 00:25:11,840
After I pack up my things, I stare blankly at the wall, looking for an ounce of motivation.

287
00:25:13,960 --> 00:25:14,480
Clubs.

288
00:25:18,230 --> 00:25:20,190
Sayori wants me to check out some clubs.

289
00:25:21,150 --> 00:25:22,150
Nice classroom.

290
00:25:22,610 --> 00:25:23,410
Very neat.

291
00:25:25,110 --> 00:25:28,230
So many, uh, these, uh, boards where you can pin things on.

292
00:25:29,470 --> 00:25:31,730
I guess I have no choice but to start with the anime club.

293
00:25:36,650 --> 00:25:37,050
Sayori.

294
00:25:39,130 --> 00:25:42,010
Sayori must have come into the classroom while I was spacing out.

295
00:25:43,090 --> 00:25:46,630
I look around and realize that I'm the only one left in the classroom.

296
00:25:47,870 --> 00:25:50,550
I thought I'd catch you coming out of the classroom.

297
00:25:50,890 --> 00:25:53,430
But I saw you just sitting here and spacing out.

298
00:25:53,650 --> 00:25:54,770
So I came in.

299
00:25:55,690 --> 00:25:57,790
Honestly, you're even worse than me sometimes.

300
00:25:58,250 --> 00:25:59,150
I'm impressed.

301
00:26:00,970 --> 00:26:04,390
You don't need to wait up for me if it's going to make you late to your own club.

302
00:26:05,690 --> 00:26:07,650
Well, I thought I might need some encouragement.

303
00:26:08,090 --> 00:26:09,430
So I thought, you know...

304
00:26:10,910 --> 00:26:11,630
Know what?

305
00:26:12,970 --> 00:26:15,050
Well, that you could come to my club.

306
00:26:17,150 --> 00:26:17,590
Sayori.

307
00:26:18,690 --> 00:26:19,130
Yeah?

308
00:26:21,350 --> 00:26:22,990
There's no way I'm going to your club.

309
00:26:28,420 --> 00:26:30,960
Sayori is vice president of the literature club.

310
00:26:32,060 --> 00:26:34,840
Not that I was ever aware that she had any interest in literature.

311
00:26:35,760 --> 00:26:41,140
In fact, I'm 99% sure she only did it because she thought it would be fun to help start a new club.

312
00:26:42,860 --> 00:26:48,460
Since she was the first one to show interest after the one who proposed the club, she inherited the title vice president.

313
00:26:49,540 --> 00:26:52,680
That said, my interest in literature is guaranteed to be even less.

314
00:26:54,020 --> 00:26:54,520
Yeah.

315
00:26:55,220 --> 00:26:56,640
I'm going to the anime club.

316
00:26:57,820 --> 00:26:59,120
Come on, please.

317
00:27:01,200 --> 00:27:02,920
Why do you care so much anyway?

318
00:27:04,980 --> 00:27:05,480
Well...

319
00:27:06,500 --> 00:27:10,800
I kind of told the club yesterday I would bring in a new member.

320
00:27:11,960 --> 00:27:14,600
And Natsuki made cupcakes and everything.

321
00:27:15,520 --> 00:27:16,180
Real shit?

322
00:27:18,780 --> 00:27:20,680
Don't make promises you can't keep.

323
00:27:21,640 --> 00:27:24,780
I can't tell if Sayori is really that much of an airhead.

324
00:27:24,940 --> 00:27:28,260
Or if she's so cunning as to have planned all of this out.

325
00:27:29,320 --> 00:27:31,020
I let out a long sigh.

326
00:27:35,500 --> 00:27:36,100
Fine.

327
00:27:36,720 --> 00:27:38,740
I'll stop by for a cupcake.

328
00:27:39,060 --> 00:27:39,600
Okay?

329
00:27:40,800 --> 00:27:41,400
Yes!

330
00:27:41,760 --> 00:27:42,720
Let's go!

331
00:27:46,180 --> 00:27:47,680
And thus...

332
00:27:47,680 --> 00:27:50,800
Today marks the day I sold my soul for a cupcake.

333
00:27:52,460 --> 00:27:53,240
What?

334
00:27:53,920 --> 00:27:57,440
I dejectedly follow Sayori across the school and upstairs.

335
00:27:58,250 --> 00:28:00,500
A section of the school I rarely visit.

336
00:28:01,620 --> 00:28:02,400
Sayori.

337
00:28:02,760 --> 00:28:03,700
Full of energy.

338
00:28:04,080 --> 00:28:05,920
Swings open the classroom door.

339
00:28:08,200 --> 00:28:08,980
Everyone!

340
00:28:09,480 --> 00:28:11,380
The new member is here!

341
00:28:12,200 --> 00:28:13,360
I told you!

342
00:28:13,660 --> 00:28:15,180
Don't call me a new member!

343
00:28:15,600 --> 00:28:16,320
Oh wait, that's me.

344
00:28:16,680 --> 00:28:17,260
I told you!

345
00:28:17,560 --> 00:28:18,900
Don't call me a new member!

346
00:28:20,060 --> 00:28:20,580
Eh?

347
00:28:20,960 --> 00:28:22,240
I glance around the room.

348
00:28:23,880 --> 00:28:24,920
Girl 1.

349
00:28:25,840 --> 00:28:28,100
Welcome to the literature club!

350
00:28:28,420 --> 00:28:30,400
It's a pleasure meeting you!

351
00:28:31,900 --> 00:28:35,060
Sayori always says nice things about you!

352
00:28:36,260 --> 00:28:37,080
Seriously?

353
00:28:37,080 --> 00:28:38,900
You brought a boy?

354
00:28:40,160 --> 00:28:41,680
Way to kill the atmosphere?

355
00:28:43,440 --> 00:28:44,280
Ah!

356
00:28:44,500 --> 00:28:45,080
Oliver!

357
00:28:45,420 --> 00:28:46,680
What a nice surprise!

358
00:28:46,680 --> 00:28:48,660
Oh fuck in hell!

359
00:28:48,960 --> 00:28:50,040
What is this shit?

360
00:28:51,360 --> 00:28:52,660
Welcome to the club!

361
00:28:55,860 --> 00:28:58,080
All words escape me in this situation.

362
00:28:58,720 --> 00:29:00,540
This club...

363
00:29:00,540 --> 00:29:03,260
Is full of incredibly cute girls!

364
00:29:05,300 --> 00:29:07,000
What are you looking at?

365
00:29:07,580 --> 00:29:09,560
If you want to say something, say it!

366
00:29:11,120 --> 00:29:11,780
Sorry.

367
00:29:13,780 --> 00:29:14,260
Natsuki.

368
00:29:16,960 --> 00:29:17,440
Natsuki.

369
00:29:19,340 --> 00:29:25,080
The girl with the sour attitude, whose name is apparently Natsuki, is one I don't recognize.

370
00:29:26,180 --> 00:29:28,940
Her small figure makes her look like a first year student.

371
00:29:30,240 --> 00:29:34,000
She's also the one who made cupcakes, according to Sayori.

372
00:29:35,820 --> 00:29:37,760
You can just ignore her when she gets moody.

373
00:29:39,020 --> 00:29:43,000
Sayori says that quietly into my ear, then turns back towards the other girls.

374
00:29:44,260 --> 00:29:44,860
Anyway!

375
00:29:45,340 --> 00:29:46,560
This is Natsuki.

376
00:29:46,860 --> 00:29:48,280
Always full of energy.

377
00:29:49,140 --> 00:29:50,320
And this is Yuri!

378
00:29:50,720 --> 00:29:52,220
The smartest in the club.

379
00:29:53,400 --> 00:29:55,040
Don't say things like that.

380
00:29:56,260 --> 00:30:03,460
Yuri, who appears comparably more mature and timid, seems to have a hard time keeping up with people like Sayori and Natsuki.

381
00:30:04,740 --> 00:30:05,280
Oh!

382
00:30:05,580 --> 00:30:07,900
This is taking every ounce of my...

383
00:30:07,900 --> 00:30:09,520
fiber in my being.

384
00:30:09,520 --> 00:30:11,260
I butchered that expression, too.

385
00:30:14,080 --> 00:30:14,300
Ah!

386
00:30:16,280 --> 00:30:16,760
Ah!

387
00:30:16,980 --> 00:30:18,680
Well, it's nice to meet both of you.

388
00:30:20,240 --> 00:30:22,440
And it sounds like you already know Monica!

389
00:30:22,780 --> 00:30:23,440
Is that right?

390
00:30:25,040 --> 00:30:25,760
That's right.

391
00:30:26,460 --> 00:30:28,380
It's great to see you again, Oliver.

392
00:30:29,280 --> 00:30:30,500
Monica smiles sweetly.

393
00:30:33,940 --> 00:30:35,160
We do know each other.

394
00:30:35,400 --> 00:30:36,900
Well, we rarely talked.

395
00:30:37,220 --> 00:30:39,360
But we were in the same class last year.

396
00:30:41,100 --> 00:30:43,780
Monica was probably the most popular girl in class.

397
00:30:44,240 --> 00:30:45,740
Smart, beautiful, athletic.

398
00:30:46,860 --> 00:30:48,780
Basically, completely out of my league.

399
00:30:49,620 --> 00:30:52,900
So, having her smile at me so genuinely feels a little...

400
00:30:54,780 --> 00:30:55,780
You too, Monica.

401
00:30:57,440 --> 00:30:58,540
Come sit down, Oliver!

402
00:30:58,820 --> 00:31:01,680
We made room for you at the table, so you can sit next to me or Monica.

403
00:31:02,060 --> 00:31:03,740
I'm gonna forget all these voices I made.

404
00:31:04,420 --> 00:31:05,440
I'll get the cupcakes!

405
00:31:06,680 --> 00:31:07,800
Hey, I made them!

406
00:31:08,060 --> 00:31:08,880
I'll get them!

407
00:31:10,020 --> 00:31:11,660
Sorry, I got a little too excited.

408
00:31:11,800 --> 00:31:14,040
I hate the little thing they do with their hands!

409
00:31:15,900 --> 00:31:17,680
Then how about I make some tea as well?

410
00:31:19,640 --> 00:31:21,060
What is this game about?

411
00:31:22,780 --> 00:31:25,500
The girls have a few desks arranged to form a table.

412
00:31:26,960 --> 00:31:32,560
As Sayori mentioned, it's been widened so that there is one space next to Monica and one space next to Sayori.

413
00:31:33,700 --> 00:31:35,040
When does the game start, though?

414
00:31:35,620 --> 00:31:36,500
Like gameplay.

415
00:31:37,920 --> 00:31:43,820
Natsuki and Yuri walk over to the corner of the room, where Natsuki grabs a wrapped tray and Yuri opens the closet.

416
00:31:44,880 --> 00:31:47,700
Still feeling awkward, I take a seat next to Sayori.

417
00:31:48,700 --> 00:31:50,320
Do I not have a choice in this?

418
00:31:50,780 --> 00:31:52,120
What if I just want to leave?

419
00:31:53,720 --> 00:31:57,000
Natsuki proudly marches back to the table, tray in hand.

420
00:31:57,960 --> 00:31:58,520
Okay!

421
00:31:59,300 --> 00:32:00,200
Are you ready?

422
00:32:05,450 --> 00:32:12,670
Natsuki lifts the foil off the tray to reveal a dozen white fluffy cupcakes decorated to look like little cats.

423
00:32:13,510 --> 00:32:14,490
Okay, that's cute.

424
00:32:15,210 --> 00:32:19,630
The whiskers are drawn with icing, and little pieces of chocolate were used to make ears.

425
00:32:21,010 --> 00:32:22,250
So cute!

426
00:32:23,250 --> 00:32:26,070
I had no idea you were so good at baking, Natsuki.

427
00:32:27,170 --> 00:32:28,530
Eh, well...

428
00:32:28,530 --> 00:32:30,750
You know...

429
00:32:30,750 --> 00:32:32,070
Just hurry and take one.

430
00:32:33,070 --> 00:32:35,050
Sayori grabs one first, then Monica.

431
00:32:35,370 --> 00:32:36,290
I follow.

432
00:32:37,790 --> 00:32:38,990
It's delicious.

433
00:32:39,910 --> 00:32:44,110
Sayori talks with her mouth full, and has already managed to get icing on her face.

434
00:32:45,110 --> 00:32:50,010
I turn the cupcake around in my fingers, looking for the best angle to take a bite.

435
00:32:52,230 --> 00:32:52,790
Phew.

436
00:32:54,430 --> 00:32:55,410
Natsuki is quiet.

437
00:32:56,750 --> 00:32:59,810
I can't help but notice her sneaking glances in my direction.

438
00:33:02,900 --> 00:33:04,420
This is making me uncomfortable.

439
00:33:04,980 --> 00:33:06,440
How old is she even?

440
00:33:06,560 --> 00:33:08,040
Like, what is this fucking game?

441
00:33:09,240 --> 00:33:11,200
Is she waiting for me to take a bite?

442
00:33:13,140 --> 00:33:14,660
I finally bite down.

443
00:33:15,160 --> 00:33:16,700
I hope I get to just leave.

444
00:33:17,020 --> 00:33:18,840
The icing is sweet and full of flavor.

445
00:33:19,240 --> 00:33:20,920
I wonder if she made it herself.

446
00:33:22,880 --> 00:33:24,400
This is really good.

447
00:33:25,440 --> 00:33:27,120
Thank you, Natsuki.

448
00:33:28,360 --> 00:33:28,900
But...

449
00:33:28,900 --> 00:33:29,940
Why are you thanking me?

450
00:33:30,220 --> 00:33:31,480
It's not like I...

451
00:33:32,100 --> 00:33:33,880
Haven't I heard this somewhere before?

452
00:33:34,860 --> 00:33:36,600
Made them for your...

453
00:33:36,600 --> 00:33:36,840
Anything.

454
00:33:37,920 --> 00:33:38,500
Huh?

455
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:39,840
I...

456
00:33:39,840 --> 00:33:40,900
Thought you technically did.

457
00:33:41,200 --> 00:33:42,020
Sayori said...

458
00:33:42,480 --> 00:33:43,320
Well...

459
00:33:43,320 --> 00:33:43,800
Maybe.

460
00:33:44,560 --> 00:33:45,960
But not for...

461
00:33:45,960 --> 00:33:46,880
You know...

462
00:33:46,880 --> 00:33:47,660
You...

463
00:33:47,660 --> 00:33:48,320
Dummy.

464
00:33:49,460 --> 00:33:50,020
Alright.

465
00:33:50,480 --> 00:33:51,000
Alright.

466
00:33:51,900 --> 00:33:55,580
I give up on Natsuki's weird logic and dismiss the conversation.

467
00:33:56,420 --> 00:33:57,340
Thank god.

468
00:33:58,140 --> 00:34:00,580
Yuri returns to the table, carrying a tea set.

469
00:34:01,280 --> 00:34:07,360
She carefully places a teacup in front of each of us, before setting down the teapot next to the cupcake tray.

470
00:34:07,980 --> 00:34:09,340
Wow, what a sentence.

471
00:34:09,600 --> 00:34:10,440
Beautifully written.

472
00:34:12,380 --> 00:34:15,000
You keep a whole tea set in this classroom?

473
00:34:16,620 --> 00:34:17,380
Don't worry.

474
00:34:17,660 --> 00:34:18,880
The teachers gave us permission.

475
00:34:20,280 --> 00:34:23,580
After all, doesn't a hot cup of tea help you enjoy a good book?

476
00:34:24,360 --> 00:34:25,480
That's so random.

477
00:34:27,120 --> 00:34:27,660
I...

478
00:34:27,660 --> 00:34:28,400
Guess?

479
00:34:29,620 --> 00:34:31,780
Haha, don't let yourself get intimidated.

480
00:34:32,420 --> 00:34:33,660
Yuri's just trying to impress you.

481
00:34:34,720 --> 00:34:35,020
Ah!

482
00:34:36,140 --> 00:34:37,260
That's not...

483
00:34:37,260 --> 00:34:39,380
Insulted, Yuri looks away.

484
00:34:40,920 --> 00:34:42,160
I meant that, you know.

485
00:34:43,600 --> 00:34:44,660
I believe you.

486
00:34:45,500 --> 00:34:48,720
Well, tea and reading might not be a pastime for me.

487
00:34:49,360 --> 00:34:50,820
But I at least enjoy tea.

488
00:34:51,800 --> 00:34:52,640
I'm glad.

489
00:34:53,420 --> 00:34:55,840
Yuri faintly smiles to herself in relief.

490
00:34:56,880 --> 00:34:59,300
Monika raises an eyebrow, then smiles at me.

491
00:35:02,700 --> 00:35:04,820
So what made you consider the Literature Club?

492
00:35:07,660 --> 00:35:08,160
Uh...

493
00:35:08,160 --> 00:35:10,440
Honestly, I'm not even sure of myself, man.

494
00:35:10,820 --> 00:35:12,430
I'm questioning this to this...

495
00:35:14,020 --> 00:35:14,520
minute.

496
00:35:15,760 --> 00:35:17,260
I was afraid of this question.

497
00:35:18,940 --> 00:35:22,800
Something tells me I shouldn't tell Monika that I was practically dragged here by Sayori.

498
00:35:23,480 --> 00:35:25,400
Bro, I can't keep track of who is who.

499
00:35:25,600 --> 00:35:26,800
They all fucking look alike.

500
00:35:27,640 --> 00:35:32,040
Well, I haven't joined any clubs yet, and Sayori seemed really happy here, so...

501
00:35:32,040 --> 00:35:32,240
You know.

502
00:35:33,280 --> 00:35:33,920
That's okay.

503
00:35:34,280 --> 00:35:35,260
Don't be embarrassed.

504
00:35:35,960 --> 00:35:37,780
We'll make sure you feel right at home.

505
00:35:38,000 --> 00:35:38,400
Okay?

506
00:35:39,240 --> 00:35:45,860
As president of the Literature Club, it's my duty to make the club fun and exciting for everyone.

507
00:35:48,260 --> 00:35:49,980
Monika, I'm surprised.

508
00:35:51,020 --> 00:35:53,500
How come you decided to start your own club?

509
00:35:56,400 --> 00:35:59,600
You could probably be a board member for any of the major clubs.

510
00:36:00,980 --> 00:36:03,280
Weren't you a leader of the debate club last year?

511
00:36:04,340 --> 00:36:05,880
Ah, well, you know.

512
00:36:06,840 --> 00:36:10,260
To be honest, I can't stand all of the politics around the major clubs.

513
00:36:11,000 --> 00:36:15,280
It feels like nothing but arguing about the budget and publicity and how to prepare for events.

514
00:36:16,080 --> 00:36:19,780
I'd much rather take something I personally enjoy and make something special out of it.

515
00:36:20,640 --> 00:36:24,500
And if it encourages others to get into literature, then I'm fulfilling that dream.

516
00:36:25,440 --> 00:36:26,940
Monika really is a great leader.

517
00:36:27,920 --> 00:36:29,400
Yuri also nods in agreement.

518
00:36:30,780 --> 00:36:33,900
Then I'm surprised there aren't more people in the club yet.

519
00:36:35,380 --> 00:36:37,680
It must be hard to start a new club.

520
00:36:38,780 --> 00:36:39,960
You could put it that way.

521
00:36:40,540 --> 00:36:44,400
Not many people are very interested in putting out all the effort to start something brand new.

522
00:36:45,240 --> 00:36:47,440
Especially when it's something that doesn't grab your attention.

523
00:36:47,840 --> 00:36:48,680
Like literature.

524
00:36:49,780 --> 00:36:52,580
You have to work hard to convince people that you're both fun and worthwhile.

525
00:36:54,240 --> 00:36:55,780
What a weird sentence.

526
00:36:58,000 --> 00:36:58,900
Is that...

527
00:36:59,420 --> 00:37:00,980
Is that how I'm supposed to think?

528
00:37:01,180 --> 00:37:03,280
How can I make this person think I'm fun and worthwhile?

529
00:37:05,860 --> 00:37:07,760
It's like an alien came to Earth.

530
00:37:08,260 --> 00:37:09,680
And tried to act human.

531
00:37:10,820 --> 00:37:13,580
But it makes school events like the festival that much more important.

532
00:37:14,360 --> 00:37:17,860
I'm confident that we can all really grow this club before we graduate.

533
00:37:18,520 --> 00:37:19,400
Right everyone?

534
00:37:20,040 --> 00:37:20,560
Yeah!

535
00:37:21,260 --> 00:37:22,380
We'll do our best.

536
00:37:22,820 --> 00:37:23,780
You know it!

537
00:37:25,180 --> 00:37:26,220
Everyone enthusiast...

538
00:37:26,800 --> 00:37:27,320
Enthusiast...

539
00:37:27,320 --> 00:37:28,700
Statistically agrees.

540
00:37:29,560 --> 00:37:30,700
Such different girls.

541
00:37:30,900 --> 00:37:32,140
All interested in the same goal.

542
00:37:33,240 --> 00:37:35,940
Monica must have worked really hard just to find these three.

543
00:37:36,820 --> 00:37:40,580
Maybe that's why they were all so delighted by the idea of a new member joining.

544
00:37:41,820 --> 00:37:45,800
Though I still don't really know if I can keep up with their level of enthusiasm about literature.

545
00:37:47,680 --> 00:37:50,880
So Oliver, what kinds of things do you like to read?

546
00:37:51,700 --> 00:37:53,900
Do I not get any choices in what I'm saying?

547
00:37:55,640 --> 00:37:57,300
Like, what kind of a game is this?

548
00:37:58,700 --> 00:37:59,580
Maybe it's the intro.

549
00:37:59,700 --> 00:38:00,020
I don't know.

550
00:38:00,140 --> 00:38:00,760
But, like...

551
00:38:00,760 --> 00:38:01,080
Hello?

552
00:38:03,740 --> 00:38:04,540
Well, uh...

553
00:38:05,240 --> 00:38:09,220
Considering how little I've read these past few years, I don't really have a good way of answering that.

554
00:38:09,760 --> 00:38:10,600
Yeah, that's true at least.

555
00:38:11,880 --> 00:38:12,280
Manga.

556
00:38:12,540 --> 00:38:13,780
That is so untrue.

557
00:38:13,780 --> 00:38:15,640
I have never read a manga in my life.

558
00:38:17,520 --> 00:38:19,940
I muttered quietly to myself, half-joking.

559
00:38:20,120 --> 00:38:21,500
I kind of did just now.

560
00:38:21,760 --> 00:38:23,800
Well, not joking, but, like, fully not joking.

561
00:38:25,020 --> 00:38:26,480
Natsuki's head suddenly perks up.

562
00:38:27,020 --> 00:38:29,660
It looks like she wants to say something, but she keeps quiet.

563
00:38:30,780 --> 00:38:32,960
Not much of a reader, I guess.

564
00:38:33,600 --> 00:38:34,080
Oh yeah?

565
00:38:34,280 --> 00:38:35,040
Are you disappointed?

566
00:38:35,440 --> 00:38:36,700
I hate literature!

567
00:38:37,240 --> 00:38:38,140
What do you say to that?

568
00:38:40,040 --> 00:38:40,680
Well...

569
00:38:40,680 --> 00:38:42,000
That can change.

570
00:38:43,260 --> 00:38:44,440
What am I saying?

571
00:38:44,920 --> 00:38:47,780
I spoke without thinking after seeing Yuri's sad smile.

572
00:38:49,300 --> 00:38:50,500
Anyway, uh...

573
00:38:50,500 --> 00:38:51,180
What about you, Yuri?

574
00:38:52,060 --> 00:38:54,080
Well, let's see...

575
00:38:54,600 --> 00:38:57,080
Yuri traces the rim of her teacup with her finger.

576
00:38:57,920 --> 00:39:03,180
My favorites are usually novels that build deep and complex fantasy worlds.

577
00:39:03,940 --> 00:39:07,920
The level of creativity and craftsmanship behind them is amazing to me.

578
00:39:08,600 --> 00:39:12,380
And telling a good story in such a foreign world is equally impressive.

579
00:39:13,460 --> 00:39:15,960
Yuri goes on, clearly passionate about her reading.

580
00:39:16,620 --> 00:39:19,580
She seems so reserved and timid since the moment I walked in.

581
00:39:19,840 --> 00:39:24,700
But it's obvious by the way her eyes light up that she finds her comfort in the world of books, not people.

582
00:39:25,780 --> 00:39:27,720
But you know, I like a lot of things.

583
00:39:28,740 --> 00:39:31,760
Stories with deep psychological elements usually immerse me as well.

584
00:39:32,920 --> 00:39:38,880
Isn't it amazing how a writer can so deliberately take advantage of your own lack of imagination to completely throw you for a loop?

585
00:39:40,360 --> 00:39:42,480
Anyway, I've been reading a lot of horror lately.

586
00:39:43,840 --> 00:39:45,840
Ah, I read a horror book once.

587
00:39:46,700 --> 00:39:50,180
I desperately grasp something I can relate to at the minimal level.

588
00:39:50,840 --> 00:39:54,140
At this rate, Yuri might as well be having a conversation with a rock.

589
00:39:55,720 --> 00:39:56,160
Really?

590
00:39:56,640 --> 00:39:58,320
I wouldn't have expected that, Yuri.

591
00:39:59,040 --> 00:40:00,600
For someone as gentle as you.

592
00:40:01,380 --> 00:40:02,600
I guess you could say that.

593
00:40:03,260 --> 00:40:08,280
But if a story makes me think, or takes me to another world, then I really can't put it down.

594
00:40:09,140 --> 00:40:12,560
Surreal horror is often very successful at changing the way you look at the world.

595
00:40:12,840 --> 00:40:14,340
If only for a brief moment.

596
00:40:15,400 --> 00:40:16,960
Ugh, I hate horror.

597
00:40:17,960 --> 00:40:18,440
Oh?

598
00:40:18,660 --> 00:40:19,280
Why's that?

599
00:40:20,000 --> 00:40:21,280
Well, I just...

600
00:40:22,280 --> 00:40:24,820
Natsuki's eyes dart over to me for a split second.

601
00:40:26,020 --> 00:40:26,740
Never mind.

602
00:40:28,200 --> 00:40:28,880
That's right.

603
00:40:29,100 --> 00:40:31,540
You usually like to write about cute things, don't you, Natsuki?

604
00:40:32,240 --> 00:40:33,420
B-b-what?

605
00:40:34,300 --> 00:40:35,660
What gives you that idea?

606
00:40:36,640 --> 00:40:39,460
You left a piece of scrap paper behind last club meeting.

607
00:40:40,160 --> 00:40:43,880
It looked like you were working on a poem called...

608
00:40:43,880 --> 00:40:44,640
Don't say it out loud!

609
00:40:45,520 --> 00:40:46,580
And give that back!

610
00:40:47,580 --> 00:40:48,620
Fine, fine.

611
00:40:49,640 --> 00:40:50,660
Ahaha, your cupcakes!

612
00:40:50,940 --> 00:40:51,680
Your poems!

613
00:40:52,360 --> 00:40:55,100
Everything you do is just as cute as you are!

614
00:40:56,060 --> 00:40:59,380
Sayori sidles up behind Natsuki and puts her hands on her shoulders.

615
00:40:59,380 --> 00:41:01,180
I'M NOT CUTE!

616
00:41:01,840 --> 00:41:04,640
Natsuki, you write your own poems?

617
00:41:05,740 --> 00:41:06,340
Eh?

618
00:41:06,680 --> 00:41:08,560
Well, I guess sometimes.

619
00:41:09,260 --> 00:41:10,440
Why do you care?

620
00:41:11,140 --> 00:41:12,860
I think that's impressive.

621
00:41:13,600 --> 00:41:15,280
Why don't you share them sometime?

622
00:41:16,500 --> 00:41:17,060
N-no!

623
00:41:17,820 --> 00:41:19,660
Natsuki averts her eyes.

624
00:41:20,300 --> 00:41:21,640
You wouldn't...

625
00:41:21,640 --> 00:41:22,340
like them.

626
00:41:23,460 --> 00:41:23,880
Ah!

627
00:41:24,200 --> 00:41:25,980
Not a very confident writer yet!

628
00:41:26,740 --> 00:41:28,980
I understand how Natsuki feels.

629
00:41:30,180 --> 00:41:32,520
Sharing that level of writing takes more than just confidence.

630
00:41:33,240 --> 00:41:35,440
The truest form of writing is writing to oneself.

631
00:41:36,620 --> 00:41:42,760
You must be willing to open up to your readers, exposing your vulnerabilities, and showing even the deepest reaches of your heart.

632
00:41:45,600 --> 00:41:47,400
Don't you have writing experience too, Yuri?

633
00:41:48,260 --> 00:41:49,560
Who the fuck is Monika?

634
00:41:50,880 --> 00:41:55,580
Maybe if you share some of your work, you can set an example and help Natsuki feel comfortable enough to share hers.

635
00:41:56,900 --> 00:41:58,340
God, I can't keep track of these names.

636
00:41:59,640 --> 00:42:01,160
I guess it's the same for Yuri.

637
00:42:02,440 --> 00:42:02,840
Aww!

638
00:42:03,340 --> 00:42:05,180
I wanted to read everyone's poems.

639
00:42:05,320 --> 00:42:06,360
Dude, this conversation...

640
00:42:07,660 --> 00:42:08,540
is insane!

641
00:42:10,760 --> 00:42:13,000
This is like the seventh circle of hell.

642
00:42:15,140 --> 00:42:16,220
Oh, God!

643
00:42:17,680 --> 00:42:19,000
How do I get out of this?

644
00:42:19,880 --> 00:42:21,920
We all sit in silence for a moment.

645
00:42:22,120 --> 00:42:22,320
What?

646
00:42:23,220 --> 00:42:26,300
What an asinine and boring conversation, man.

647
00:42:27,500 --> 00:42:27,980
Okay!

648
00:42:28,940 --> 00:42:30,400
I have an idea, everyone!

649
00:42:32,960 --> 00:42:35,240
Natsuki and Yuri look quizzically at Monika.

650
00:42:36,460 --> 00:42:39,800
Let's all go home and write a poem of our own!

651
00:42:40,860 --> 00:42:44,520
Then, next time we meet, we'll all share them with each other!

652
00:42:45,240 --> 00:42:46,980
That way, everyone is even!

653
00:42:49,800 --> 00:42:50,280
Um...

654
00:42:50,280 --> 00:42:50,760
Ah!

655
00:42:53,300 --> 00:42:54,120
Let's do it!

656
00:42:55,360 --> 00:43:02,080
Plus, now that we have a new member, I think it will help us all get a little more comfortable with each other, and strengthen the bond of the club!

657
00:43:02,960 --> 00:43:04,180
Isn't that right, Oliver?

658
00:43:05,360 --> 00:43:07,640
Monika smiles warmly at me once again.

659
00:43:08,760 --> 00:43:09,560
Hold on!

660
00:43:10,400 --> 00:43:11,680
There's still one problem.

661
00:43:12,620 --> 00:43:13,140
Huh?

662
00:43:13,420 --> 00:43:14,140
What's that?

663
00:43:15,120 --> 00:43:21,460
Now that we're back to the original topic of me joining the club, I bluntly come forth with what's been on my mind the entire time.

664
00:43:23,040 --> 00:43:25,180
I never said I would join this club!

665
00:43:26,900 --> 00:43:29,940
Sayori may have convinced me to stop by, but I never made any decision!

666
00:43:31,060 --> 00:43:34,080
I still have other clubs to look at, and...

667
00:43:34,080 --> 00:43:35,060
Um...

668
00:43:36,080 --> 00:43:37,480
I lose my train of thought.

669
00:43:38,620 --> 00:43:41,780
All four girls stared back at me with dejected eyes.

670
00:43:43,120 --> 00:43:44,380
But...

671
00:43:45,080 --> 00:43:47,060
I'm sorry, I thought...

672
00:43:51,100 --> 00:43:51,660
Oliver...

673
00:43:52,340 --> 00:43:53,460
You all...

674
00:43:53,460 --> 00:43:53,860
That's me!

675
00:43:54,160 --> 00:43:55,060
You all...

676
00:43:56,460 --> 00:43:58,500
I'm defenseless against these girls!

677
00:43:58,640 --> 00:43:59,240
No, I'm not!

678
00:43:59,560 --> 00:44:01,800
My defenses are 100%...

679
00:44:01,800 --> 00:44:02,280
Fuck this!

680
00:44:03,640 --> 00:44:06,720
How am I supposed to make a clear-headed decision when it's like this?

681
00:44:07,740 --> 00:44:12,480
That is, if writing poems is the price I need to pay in order to spend every day with these beautiful girls.

682
00:44:12,760 --> 00:44:16,200
How the fuck is this game overwhelmingly positive on Steam?

683
00:44:18,280 --> 00:44:19,780
Is it like a joke game?

684
00:44:21,400 --> 00:44:21,900
Right.

685
00:44:23,660 --> 00:44:24,920
Okay, I've decided then.

686
00:44:26,480 --> 00:44:28,400
I'll join the literature club!

687
00:44:29,980 --> 00:44:30,480
God!

688
00:44:32,000 --> 00:44:34,600
One by one, the girl's eyes light up.

689
00:44:35,340 --> 00:44:35,880
Yes!

690
00:44:36,180 --> 00:44:37,500
I'm so happy!

691
00:44:39,940 --> 00:44:43,040
Sayori wraps her arms around me, jumping up and down.

692
00:44:44,060 --> 00:44:44,820
Fucking hell.

693
00:44:46,240 --> 00:44:46,560
Hey!

694
00:44:47,640 --> 00:44:49,920
You really did scare me for a moment.

695
00:44:51,180 --> 00:44:55,100
If you really just came for the cupcakes, I would be super pissed.

696
00:44:57,120 --> 00:44:58,620
And that makes it official!

697
00:44:59,800 --> 00:45:01,500
Welcome to the literature club!

698
00:45:03,240 --> 00:45:03,800
Uh...

699
00:45:03,800 --> 00:45:04,740
Thanks, I guess.

700
00:45:05,860 --> 00:45:06,800
Okay, everyone!

701
00:45:07,420 --> 00:45:11,020
I think with that, we can officially end today's meeting on a good note.

702
00:45:12,000 --> 00:45:13,600
Everyone remember tonight's assignment!

703
00:45:14,380 --> 00:45:17,340
Write a poem to bring to the next meeting so we can all share!

704
00:45:18,440 --> 00:45:20,460
Monica looks over at me once more.

705
00:45:21,620 --> 00:45:25,120
Oliver, I look forward to seeing how you express yourself.

706
00:45:26,260 --> 00:45:26,740
Hehehe!

707
00:45:28,440 --> 00:45:28,820
Yeah.

708
00:45:30,420 --> 00:45:34,580
Can I really impress the class star, Monica, with my mediocre writing skills?

709
00:45:36,180 --> 00:45:39,080
I already feel the anxiety welling up inside me.

710
00:45:39,980 --> 00:45:44,220
Meanwhile, the girls continue to chit-chat as Yuri and Natsuki clean up their food.

711
00:45:45,160 --> 00:45:48,800
Hey, Oliver, since we're already here, do you want to walk home together?

712
00:45:50,320 --> 00:45:51,140
That's right.

713
00:45:51,680 --> 00:45:56,000
Sayori and I never walk home together anymore, because she always stayed after school for clubs.

714
00:45:57,680 --> 00:45:58,180
Sure.

715
00:45:58,720 --> 00:45:59,500
Might as well.

716
00:46:00,640 --> 00:46:01,020
Yay!

717
00:46:08,200 --> 00:46:09,100
What the fuck?

718
00:46:10,760 --> 00:46:14,400
With that, the two of us depart the club room and make our way home.

719
00:46:15,340 --> 00:46:19,060
The whole way, my mind wanders back and forth between the four girls.

720
00:46:21,320 --> 00:46:26,700
Sayori, Natsuki, Yuri, and of course, Monica.

721
00:46:27,980 --> 00:46:28,480
Okay, hold on.

722
00:46:28,540 --> 00:46:29,060
Let's see if I...

723
00:46:29,060 --> 00:46:30,640
I cover the screen with the names.

724
00:46:30,760 --> 00:46:31,620
I don't know what the names are.

725
00:46:32,360 --> 00:46:33,460
The left one...

726
00:46:34,100 --> 00:46:36,180
Fuck, they look exactly alike, dude.

727
00:46:36,660 --> 00:46:41,320
The left one is Sayori, Natsuki, Monica, and Yuri.

728
00:46:41,740 --> 00:46:42,680
Boom, nailed it.

729
00:46:43,700 --> 00:46:47,160
Will I really be happy spending every day after school in a literature club?

730
00:46:48,400 --> 00:46:51,040
Perhaps I'll have the chance to grow closer to one of these girls.

731
00:46:53,060 --> 00:46:53,500
Alright.

732
00:46:54,520 --> 00:46:56,600
I'll just need to make the most of my circumstances.

733
00:46:56,960 --> 00:46:58,800
And I'm sure a good fortune will find me.

734
00:46:59,680 --> 00:47:01,920
And I guess that starts with writing a poem tonight.

735
00:47:03,100 --> 00:47:04,420
Can I actually write it, though?

736
00:47:06,440 --> 00:47:06,880
Oh!

737
00:47:08,020 --> 00:47:08,460
Gameplay!

738
00:47:08,920 --> 00:47:10,360
It's time to write a poem.

739
00:47:10,360 --> 00:47:13,940
Pick words you think your favorite club member will like.

740
00:47:14,160 --> 00:47:16,800
Something good might happen with whoever likes your poem the most.

741
00:47:17,260 --> 00:47:18,740
Bitch, let's get to writing.

742
00:47:19,040 --> 00:47:19,660
Let's do it.

743
00:47:21,220 --> 00:47:22,340
Wait, uh...

744
00:47:22,340 --> 00:47:24,120
Oh, I get to choose?

745
00:47:24,380 --> 00:47:25,600
Wait, what is happening here?

746
00:47:28,950 --> 00:47:30,050
What is this?

747
00:47:39,860 --> 00:47:40,420
Huh?

748
00:47:43,200 --> 00:47:44,320
Wait, for...

749
00:47:45,940 --> 00:47:47,740
Oh, wait, am I writing now?

750
00:47:50,640 --> 00:47:52,280
A treasure disaster.

751
00:47:56,200 --> 00:47:58,300
I don't understand what's going on.

752
00:47:58,420 --> 00:47:59,300
Excitement dark?

753
00:48:03,020 --> 00:48:03,740
Analysis.

754
00:48:09,750 --> 00:48:10,470
Anime.

755
00:48:13,730 --> 00:48:14,450
I...

756
00:48:14,450 --> 00:48:15,770
I don't...

757
00:48:15,770 --> 00:48:16,550
Wait, history?

758
00:48:20,960 --> 00:48:22,220
Can I go back?

759
00:48:22,680 --> 00:48:23,040
Like...

760
00:48:25,900 --> 00:48:26,620
Hopeless?

761
00:48:26,960 --> 00:48:28,040
That's what I'm feeling.

762
00:48:32,240 --> 00:48:34,360
A bit of wrath, too, actually.

763
00:48:34,480 --> 00:48:35,080
Agonizing.

764
00:48:35,080 --> 00:48:38,830
I don't know.

765
00:48:39,410 --> 00:48:40,330
Secretive, maybe?

766
00:48:41,290 --> 00:48:42,910
I'm feeling a little secretive.

767
00:48:43,330 --> 00:48:46,390
I'm just gonna pick the ones that vibe with me now.

768
00:48:47,830 --> 00:48:48,750
Whirlwind.

769
00:48:49,990 --> 00:48:50,570
Infinite.

770
00:49:00,700 --> 00:49:01,280
Electricity.

771
00:49:05,200 --> 00:49:05,780
Milk.

772
00:49:07,920 --> 00:49:08,500
Tragedy.

773
00:49:09,000 --> 00:49:09,920
I guess.

774
00:49:16,530 --> 00:49:17,110
Marshmallow?

775
00:49:19,450 --> 00:49:20,030
Kawaii?

776
00:49:20,030 --> 00:49:20,890
I don't even know what that is.

777
00:49:21,850 --> 00:49:22,150
Email.

778
00:49:24,290 --> 00:49:24,770
Empty.

779
00:49:26,690 --> 00:49:27,170
Sadness.

780
00:49:29,550 --> 00:49:30,030
Dazzle.

781
00:49:31,210 --> 00:49:33,330
I have no idea what the fuck is going on.

782
00:49:36,420 --> 00:49:37,280
Hi again, Oliver!

783
00:49:38,040 --> 00:49:38,760
Wait, what?

784
00:49:40,460 --> 00:49:41,620
What the hell?

785
00:49:43,240 --> 00:49:45,080
Glad to see you didn't run away on us.

786
00:49:45,260 --> 00:49:45,780
Ha ha ha.

787
00:49:47,000 --> 00:49:48,320
Nah, don't worry.

788
00:49:49,460 --> 00:49:52,980
This might be a little strange for me, but I at least keep my word.

789
00:49:53,300 --> 00:49:54,760
Dude, this is the worst poem of all time.

790
00:49:55,360 --> 00:50:00,200
It's just gonna be like, dog excitement, tragedy, house bed.

791
00:50:01,100 --> 00:50:01,560
Lives.

792
00:50:03,200 --> 00:50:03,660
Infinite.

793
00:50:04,680 --> 00:50:07,520
And then they're like, that's great, Oliver.

794
00:50:07,520 --> 00:50:08,220
Oliver, you...

795
00:50:08,680 --> 00:50:09,420
You...

796
00:50:09,420 --> 00:50:09,540
Wow.

797
00:50:09,940 --> 00:50:11,580
You got some skills, you know?

798
00:50:12,060 --> 00:50:13,960
Ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha.

799
00:50:16,490 --> 00:50:18,350
Well, I'm back at the literature club.

800
00:50:18,490 --> 00:50:19,630
I'm just gonna call it the LC.

801
00:50:20,990 --> 00:50:24,010
I was the last to come in, so everyone else is already hanging out.

802
00:50:24,310 --> 00:50:27,030
The reason I call it LC, I have trouble saying literature.

803
00:50:28,210 --> 00:50:30,010
Thanks for keeping your promise, Oliver.

804
00:50:30,370 --> 00:50:33,130
I hope this isn't too overwhelming of a commitment for you.

805
00:50:33,830 --> 00:50:37,550
Making you dive headfirst into literature when you're not accustomed to it?

806
00:50:38,430 --> 00:50:39,390
Oh, come on!

807
00:50:39,730 --> 00:50:41,010
Like he deserves any slack.

808
00:50:42,030 --> 00:50:44,790
Sayori told me you didn't even want to join any clubs this year.

809
00:50:45,450 --> 00:50:46,750
And last year, too!

810
00:50:47,570 --> 00:50:50,210
I don't know if you plan to just come here and hang out or what.

811
00:50:51,610 --> 00:50:54,670
But if you don't take us seriously, then you won't see the end of it.

812
00:50:55,970 --> 00:51:01,310
Natsuki, you certainly have a big mouth for someone who keeps her manga collection in the club room.

813
00:51:02,690 --> 00:51:05,050
How does that sentence even make any sense?

814
00:51:09,130 --> 00:51:12,370
Natsuki finds herself stuck between saying Monika and manga.

815
00:51:13,690 --> 00:51:15,310
Manga is literature!

816
00:51:16,950 --> 00:51:19,970
Swiftly defeated, Natsuki plops back into her seat.

817
00:51:20,910 --> 00:51:22,050
Don't worry, guys.

818
00:51:22,050 --> 00:51:25,670
Oliver always gives it his best, as long as he's having fun.

819
00:51:26,850 --> 00:51:29,450
He helps me with busy work without me even asking.

820
00:51:30,110 --> 00:51:30,870
Like cooking!

821
00:51:31,270 --> 00:51:32,030
Cleaning my room.

822
00:51:32,550 --> 00:51:35,690
That sounds to me like I'm in the friendzone, and I'm clueless.

823
00:51:36,130 --> 00:51:37,250
I don't like that.

824
00:51:37,850 --> 00:51:38,990
How dependable.

825
00:51:40,410 --> 00:51:43,190
Sayori, that's because your room is so messy, it's distracting.

826
00:51:44,390 --> 00:51:46,810
And you almost set your house on fire once.

827
00:51:48,190 --> 00:51:49,010
Is that so?

828
00:51:50,590 --> 00:51:52,630
You two are really good friends, aren't you?

829
00:51:53,250 --> 00:51:54,910
I might be a little jealous.

830
00:51:56,070 --> 00:51:56,690
How come?

831
00:51:56,970 --> 00:51:58,990
You and Oliver can become good friends too.

832
00:52:00,830 --> 00:52:02,570
Okay, real talk?

833
00:52:03,270 --> 00:52:04,810
What is this game even about?

834
00:52:08,170 --> 00:52:09,290
It's so weird.

835
00:52:11,570 --> 00:52:12,250
Sayori.

836
00:52:13,070 --> 00:52:13,550
Hmm?

837
00:52:16,780 --> 00:52:20,900
As usual, Sayori seems oblivious to the weird situations he just put me into.

838
00:52:21,880 --> 00:52:22,160
Oh!

839
00:52:22,600 --> 00:52:22,740
Oh!

840
00:52:23,140 --> 00:52:25,060
Yuri even brought you something today, you know?

841
00:52:25,980 --> 00:52:26,660
Wait!

842
00:52:26,960 --> 00:52:27,620
Sayori.

843
00:52:28,620 --> 00:52:29,140
Eh?

844
00:52:29,600 --> 00:52:30,280
Me?

845
00:52:31,700 --> 00:52:32,380
Um...

846
00:52:32,380 --> 00:52:33,340
Not really.

847
00:52:34,120 --> 00:52:34,980
Don't be shy!

848
00:52:35,740 --> 00:52:36,680
It's really nothing.

849
00:52:37,640 --> 00:52:38,440
What is it?

850
00:52:39,440 --> 00:52:40,180
Never mind.

851
00:52:41,660 --> 00:52:42,060
Ah.

852
00:52:43,120 --> 00:52:45,340
Sayori made it sound like a big deal when it's really not.

853
00:52:46,260 --> 00:52:47,700
Uh, what do I do?

854
00:52:49,480 --> 00:52:49,940
Eh?

855
00:52:50,120 --> 00:52:50,940
I'm sorry, Yuri.

856
00:52:51,020 --> 00:52:51,560
I wasn't thinking.

857
00:52:52,620 --> 00:52:55,840
I guess that means it's up to me to rescue this situation.

858
00:52:57,140 --> 00:52:58,400
Hey, don't worry about it.

859
00:52:59,020 --> 00:53:02,140
First of all, I wasn't expecting anything in the first place.

860
00:53:02,900 --> 00:53:05,480
So any nice gesture from you is a pleasant surprise.

861
00:53:06,460 --> 00:53:07,920
It'll make me happy no matter what.

862
00:53:08,880 --> 00:53:09,180
Eh?

863
00:53:09,400 --> 00:53:10,040
Is that so?

864
00:53:11,060 --> 00:53:11,460
Yeah.

865
00:53:12,020 --> 00:53:14,200
I won't make it a big deal if you don't want it to be.

866
00:53:15,480 --> 00:53:15,880
Alright.

867
00:53:16,900 --> 00:53:17,300
Well.

868
00:53:18,200 --> 00:53:18,600
Here.

869
00:53:19,640 --> 00:53:22,240
Yuri reaches into her bag and pulls out a book.

870
00:53:23,580 --> 00:53:25,800
I didn't want you to feel... left out.

871
00:53:26,740 --> 00:53:28,700
So I picked out a book that I thought you might enjoy.

872
00:53:29,540 --> 00:53:33,140
It's a short read, so it should keep your attention, even if you don't usually read.

873
00:53:33,300 --> 00:53:34,040
What are you saying about me?

874
00:53:34,960 --> 00:53:36,000
And we could...

875
00:53:36,000 --> 00:53:36,780
You know...

876
00:53:37,740 --> 00:53:38,540
Discuss it.

877
00:53:38,740 --> 00:53:39,360
If you...

878
00:53:39,360 --> 00:53:39,780
Want it.

879
00:53:41,120 --> 00:53:42,240
This is...

880
00:53:42,240 --> 00:53:44,940
How is this girl accidentally being so cute?

881
00:53:45,040 --> 00:53:45,620
Oh my god.

882
00:53:46,940 --> 00:53:49,360
She even picked out a book she thinks I'll like.

883
00:53:49,620 --> 00:53:51,220
Despite me not reading much.

884
00:53:52,920 --> 00:53:53,300
Yuri.

885
00:53:53,640 --> 00:53:54,160
Thank you.

886
00:53:54,480 --> 00:53:56,160
I'll definitely read this.

887
00:53:57,420 --> 00:53:59,040
I enthusiastically take the book.

888
00:54:00,000 --> 00:54:00,440
Phew.

889
00:54:01,320 --> 00:54:03,280
Well, you can read it at your own pace.

890
00:54:03,400 --> 00:54:04,120
What book is it?

891
00:54:05,080 --> 00:54:06,760
I look forward to hearing what you think.

892
00:54:07,720 --> 00:54:12,440
Now that everyone's settled in, I expected Monica to kick off some scheduled activities for the club.

893
00:54:13,320 --> 00:54:14,960
But that doesn't seem to be the case.

894
00:54:16,040 --> 00:54:18,980
Sayori and Monica are having a cheery conversation in the corner.

895
00:54:19,980 --> 00:54:21,860
Yuri's face is already buried in a book.

896
00:54:22,740 --> 00:54:26,780
I can't help but notice her intense expression, like she was waiting for this chance.

897
00:54:27,720 --> 00:54:30,600
Meanwhile, Natsuki is rummaging around in the closet.

898
00:54:32,980 --> 00:54:34,940
Did we just transition to the same screen?

899
00:54:37,000 --> 00:54:39,580
I'm really curious to talk to Yuri a little bit more.

900
00:54:41,040 --> 00:54:44,140
But at the same time, I would feel bad for distracting her from reading.

901
00:54:44,960 --> 00:54:47,080
I catch a glimpse of the cover of her book.

902
00:54:47,860 --> 00:54:49,940
It looks like the same book that she lent to me.

903
00:54:50,520 --> 00:54:53,400
More than that, she seems to be on the first few pages.

904
00:54:53,840 --> 00:54:54,340
Interesting.

905
00:54:56,660 --> 00:54:57,120
Ah.

906
00:54:59,140 --> 00:54:59,660
Crap.

907
00:55:01,560 --> 00:55:03,600
I think she noticed me looking at her.

908
00:55:05,320 --> 00:55:09,420
She sneaks another glance at me, and our eyes meet for a split second.

909
00:55:12,870 --> 00:55:15,550
But that only makes her hide her face deeper in her book.

910
00:55:17,210 --> 00:55:17,730
Sorry.

911
00:55:18,970 --> 00:55:20,290
I was just spacing out.

912
00:55:21,170 --> 00:55:23,770
I muttered this, sensing I made her uncomfortable.

913
00:55:25,390 --> 00:55:25,790
Oh.

914
00:55:26,990 --> 00:55:27,670
It's fine.

915
00:55:29,190 --> 00:55:32,710
If I was focused, then I probably wouldn't have noticed in the first place.

916
00:55:33,750 --> 00:55:36,670
But I'm just re-reading a bit of this, so...

917
00:55:36,670 --> 00:55:38,470
That's the book that you gave me, right?

918
00:55:39,490 --> 00:55:40,010
Mm-hmm.

919
00:55:40,870 --> 00:55:42,810
I wanted to re-read some of it.

920
00:55:43,810 --> 00:55:45,710
Not for any particular reason.

921
00:55:47,110 --> 00:55:47,910
Just curious.

922
00:55:48,410 --> 00:55:50,610
How come you have two copies of the same book?

923
00:55:53,820 --> 00:55:56,220
Well, when I stopped at the bookstore yesterday...

924
00:55:57,720 --> 00:55:59,420
Uh, that's not what I meant.

925
00:56:00,320 --> 00:56:01,200
I mean...

926
00:56:01,200 --> 00:56:04,200
I just happened to buy two of them.

927
00:56:05,140 --> 00:56:05,660
What?

928
00:56:07,460 --> 00:56:08,300
I see.

929
00:56:10,000 --> 00:56:13,100
There's something fairly obvious here that Yuri isn't telling me.

930
00:56:13,620 --> 00:56:15,260
But I decide to let it go.

931
00:56:17,300 --> 00:56:19,340
I'll definitely start reading it soon.

932
00:56:20,220 --> 00:56:21,820
He said, lying.

933
00:56:22,500 --> 00:56:23,480
I'm glad to hear.

934
00:56:24,440 --> 00:56:27,480
Once it starts to pick up, you might have a hard time putting it down.

935
00:56:28,160 --> 00:56:30,500
It's a very engaging and relatable story.

936
00:56:31,400 --> 00:56:32,420
Is that so?

937
00:56:33,380 --> 00:56:34,480
What's it about, anyway?

938
00:56:36,260 --> 00:56:36,760
Well...

939
00:56:36,760 --> 00:56:37,500
Spit it out!

940
00:56:39,800 --> 00:56:40,300
Mm...

941
00:56:40,300 --> 00:56:43,120
Yuri closes the book and scans her eyes over the back.

942
00:56:44,080 --> 00:56:45,860
The book is titled...

943
00:56:45,860 --> 00:56:47,100
Portrait of Markov.

944
00:56:47,800 --> 00:56:48,760
Oh, you like statistics?

945
00:56:49,460 --> 00:56:50,620
Hell yeah, let's go!

946
00:56:51,320 --> 00:56:53,900
There's an ominous-looking eye symbol on the front cover.

947
00:56:55,340 --> 00:56:55,840
Alright.

948
00:56:56,440 --> 00:56:59,620
I just wanted to make sure I don't accidentally give anything away.

949
00:57:01,220 --> 00:57:05,860
Basically, it's about this girl in high school who moves in with her long-lost younger sister.

950
00:57:08,820 --> 00:57:11,440
But as soon as she does so, her life gets really strange.

951
00:57:12,700 --> 00:57:16,640
She gets targeted by these people who escape from a human experiment prison.

952
00:57:17,440 --> 00:57:21,000
And while her life is in danger, she needs to desperately choose who to trust.

953
00:57:21,980 --> 00:57:26,900
No matter what she does, she ends up destroying most of her relationships and her life starts to fall apart.

954
00:57:27,400 --> 00:57:28,960
Bro, are you not spoiling it?

955
00:57:31,200 --> 00:57:32,080
That's kinda...

956
00:57:33,060 --> 00:57:34,460
That's kinda dark, isn't it?

957
00:57:36,180 --> 00:57:38,360
Yuri made it sound like it was gonna be a nice story.

958
00:57:38,780 --> 00:57:40,680
So that dark turn came from nowhere.

959
00:57:41,640 --> 00:57:42,100
Ha ha ha!

960
00:57:43,200 --> 00:57:45,280
Yuri gently giggles, all of a sudden.

961
00:57:46,800 --> 00:57:49,160
Are you not a fan of that sort of thing, Oliver?

962
00:57:50,620 --> 00:57:51,520
No, it's...

963
00:57:51,520 --> 00:57:52,120
It's not that.

964
00:57:52,820 --> 00:57:56,040
I mean, I can definitely enjoy those kinds of stories, so...

965
00:57:56,040 --> 00:57:56,600
Don't worry.

966
00:57:57,640 --> 00:57:58,480
I hope so.

967
00:57:59,260 --> 00:58:00,220
Yeah, I...

968
00:58:00,220 --> 00:58:02,080
Totally forgot that Yuri is into those things.

969
00:58:03,620 --> 00:58:07,700
She's so shy and reclusive on the outside, but her mind seems to be completely different.

970
00:58:09,060 --> 00:58:11,500
It's just that those kinds of stories...

971
00:58:11,500 --> 00:58:15,560
They challenge you to look at life from a strange new perspective?

972
00:58:16,460 --> 00:58:25,140
When horrible things happen not just because someone wants to be evil, but because they have their own goals, or their own philosophy that they believe in.

973
00:58:25,840 --> 00:58:36,020
Then suddenly, when you thought you related to the protagonist, they're made to be naive, for letting their one-sided morals interfere with the villain's plans.

974
00:58:37,000 --> 00:58:37,560
I'm...

975
00:58:37,560 --> 00:58:39,020
I'm rambling, aren't I?

976
00:58:39,980 --> 00:58:40,880
Not again.

977
00:58:41,980 --> 00:58:42,800
I'm sorry.

978
00:58:43,960 --> 00:58:44,420
Hey!

979
00:58:45,360 --> 00:58:46,240
Don't apologize.

980
00:58:47,320 --> 00:58:49,200
I haven't lost interest or anything.

981
00:58:52,780 --> 00:58:53,240
Well...

982
00:58:54,080 --> 00:58:55,380
I guess it's alright then.

983
00:58:56,120 --> 00:58:58,740
But I feel like I should let you know that I have this problem.

984
00:58:59,840 --> 00:59:06,100
When I let things like books and writing fill my thoughts, I kind of forget to pay attention to other people.

985
00:59:07,500 --> 00:59:09,660
I'm sorry if I end up saying something strange.

986
00:59:12,520 --> 00:59:14,860
Please stop me if I start talking too much.

987
00:59:14,980 --> 00:59:15,220
Stop!

988
00:59:18,640 --> 00:59:19,120
That's...

989
00:59:19,620 --> 00:59:21,420
I really don't think you need to worry.

990
00:59:22,860 --> 00:59:25,180
That just means you're passionate about reading.

991
00:59:26,160 --> 00:59:27,760
The least I can do is listen.

992
00:59:28,880 --> 00:59:31,020
It is a literature club after all.

993
00:59:32,760 --> 00:59:33,320
Ah...

994
00:59:34,000 --> 00:59:34,560
That's...

995
00:59:34,560 --> 00:59:35,460
Well, that's true.

996
00:59:36,440 --> 00:59:37,560
In fact...

997
00:59:37,560 --> 00:59:40,280
I might as well get started reading it, right?

998
00:59:41,260 --> 00:59:41,820
You...

999
00:59:41,820 --> 00:59:43,080
You don't have to...

1000
00:59:43,900 --> 00:59:44,280
Aha!

1001
00:59:44,540 --> 00:59:45,400
What are you saying?

1002
00:59:46,500 --> 00:59:49,080
Just a moment ago, you said you were looking forward to it!

1003
00:59:51,220 --> 00:59:52,280
Let me just get the book.

1004
00:59:53,200 --> 00:59:55,800
I quickly retrieved the book that I had put into my bag.

1005
00:59:57,360 --> 00:59:57,780
Alright!

1006
00:59:58,280 --> 00:59:59,400
It's fine if I sit here, right?

1007
01:00:00,340 --> 01:00:02,400
I slip into the seat next to Yuri's.

1008
01:00:02,600 --> 01:00:03,900
Where was I before then?

1009
01:00:05,320 --> 01:00:07,180
Was I just standing in a corner or something?

1010
01:00:08,700 --> 01:00:09,120
Ah!

1011
01:00:09,940 --> 01:00:10,360
Yeah.

1012
01:00:11,600 --> 01:00:12,440
Are you sure?

1013
01:00:13,740 --> 01:00:15,320
You seem a little apprehensive.

1014
01:00:18,440 --> 01:00:18,860
That's...

1015
01:00:18,860 --> 01:00:19,940
I'm sorry.

1016
01:00:20,160 --> 01:00:21,700
Stop apologizing!

1017
01:00:22,940 --> 01:00:24,440
It's not that I don't want you to.

1018
01:00:25,540 --> 01:00:27,380
It's just something I'm not very used to.

1019
01:00:28,320 --> 01:00:28,740
Wait.

1020
01:00:28,880 --> 01:00:29,320
Hold on.

1021
01:00:30,640 --> 01:00:31,900
We gotta get to the bottom of this.

1022
01:00:31,980 --> 01:00:33,060
What is it you're not used to?

1023
01:00:33,200 --> 01:00:34,060
I need to understand.

1024
01:00:36,700 --> 01:00:37,340
Wait a minute.

1025
01:00:40,460 --> 01:00:42,320
What the hell are you talking about?

1026
01:00:43,780 --> 01:00:45,980
That is reading in company with someone.

1027
01:00:45,980 --> 01:00:47,160
Are you for real?

1028
01:00:49,620 --> 01:00:50,820
How are you...

1029
01:00:50,820 --> 01:00:52,660
How did you survive until now?

1030
01:00:53,600 --> 01:00:56,360
Do you sit next to people at the cafeteria like...

1031
01:00:56,360 --> 01:00:57,020
I'm sorry.

1032
01:00:57,280 --> 01:00:57,940
Can I...

1033
01:00:57,940 --> 01:00:58,880
Can I sit here?

1034
01:00:59,000 --> 01:01:02,060
You know, I saw the seat was empty and I just...

1035
01:01:02,060 --> 01:01:04,100
I'm also eating food, it seems.

1036
01:01:04,320 --> 01:01:05,480
And you're eating food.

1037
01:01:05,480 --> 01:01:08,280
And I thought since we're both eating food, we could...

1038
01:01:08,280 --> 01:01:10,140
You know...

1039
01:01:10,140 --> 01:01:11,480
Eat food together?

1040
01:01:15,460 --> 01:01:16,180
I see.

1041
01:01:17,520 --> 01:01:20,080
Well, just tell me if I end up distracting you or anything.

1042
01:01:21,060 --> 01:01:21,540
All right.

1043
01:01:22,680 --> 01:01:24,620
I open the book and start the prologue.

1044
01:01:25,820 --> 01:01:27,040
Please let me read the book.

1045
01:01:28,240 --> 01:01:31,060
I soon understand what Yuri means about reading in company.

1046
01:01:32,380 --> 01:01:35,460
It's as if I can feel her presence over my shoulder as I read.

1047
01:01:35,940 --> 01:01:38,380
It's almost as if she's sitting right next to me.

1048
01:01:39,500 --> 01:01:41,360
It's not a particularly bad thing.

1049
01:01:42,160 --> 01:01:45,220
Maybe a little distracting, but the feeling is somewhat comforting.

1050
01:01:45,720 --> 01:01:47,860
Dude, I know exactly what they mean, man.

1051
01:01:48,120 --> 01:01:51,120
I was at the supermarket the other day, you know, and I was buying groceries.

1052
01:01:51,120 --> 01:01:56,620
I had them on the counter and everything, and I just had this feeling that someone was standing behind me, you know?

1053
01:01:56,920 --> 01:02:00,840
This feeling of buying groceries with someone next to you was just otherworldly, man.

1054
01:02:01,440 --> 01:02:01,960
Wild.

1055
01:02:03,020 --> 01:02:04,660
Yuri is in the corner of my eye.

1056
01:02:05,420 --> 01:02:08,180
I realize that she's not actually looking at her own book.

1057
01:02:08,700 --> 01:02:09,900
I glance over.

1058
01:02:10,660 --> 01:02:13,120
It looks like she's reading from my book.

1059
01:02:13,340 --> 01:02:13,660
It's...

1060
01:02:18,110 --> 01:02:18,630
Sorry.

1061
01:02:22,000 --> 01:02:23,020
I was just...

1062
01:02:24,120 --> 01:02:24,640
Yuri.

1063
01:02:24,640 --> 01:02:27,280
Yuri, you really apologize a lot, don't you?

1064
01:02:27,420 --> 01:02:27,940
Thank you!

1065
01:02:29,100 --> 01:02:29,540
I...

1066
01:02:29,540 --> 01:02:30,260
I do?

1067
01:02:31,000 --> 01:02:32,400
I don't really mean to.

1068
01:02:33,380 --> 01:02:33,940
Sorry.

1069
01:02:34,400 --> 01:02:35,760
I mean...

1070
01:02:35,760 --> 01:02:36,160
Aha!

1071
01:02:36,800 --> 01:02:37,600
Oh, that was me.

1072
01:02:38,020 --> 01:02:38,660
Ha ha ha!

1073
01:02:39,420 --> 01:02:39,980
Here!

1074
01:02:40,220 --> 01:02:41,040
This should work, right?

1075
01:02:41,720 --> 01:02:46,580
I slide my desk until it's up against Yuri's, then hold my book more between the two of them.

1076
01:02:47,460 --> 01:02:47,940
Ah.

1077
01:02:48,880 --> 01:02:50,060
I suppose so.

1078
01:02:51,200 --> 01:02:53,500
Yuri timidly closes her own copy.

1079
01:02:53,500 --> 01:02:54,380
What?

1080
01:02:54,460 --> 01:02:55,960
She wants to read from my book now?

1081
01:02:58,080 --> 01:02:58,520
Pfft.

1082
01:03:00,920 --> 01:03:02,360
This is a fucking comedy.

1083
01:03:03,940 --> 01:03:05,720
I feel like...

1084
01:03:05,720 --> 01:03:06,740
They're taking the piss.

1085
01:03:08,580 --> 01:03:12,400
Once we each lean in a little bit, our shoulders are almost dodgy.

1086
01:03:13,880 --> 01:03:18,740
It feels like my left arm is in the way, so instead I use my right hand to hold the book open.

1087
01:03:20,200 --> 01:03:22,820
Ah, I guess that makes it kind of difficult to turn the page.

1088
01:03:23,640 --> 01:03:24,080
Here.

1089
01:03:26,180 --> 01:03:30,960
Yuri takes her left arm and holds the left side of the book between her thumb and forefinger.

1090
01:03:31,740 --> 01:03:33,360
Ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha!

1091
01:03:34,700 --> 01:03:36,060
We're now holding...

1092
01:03:36,060 --> 01:03:40,240
Wait, she's holding the left side of the book, and I'm holding the right side of the book?

1093
01:03:41,600 --> 01:03:42,620
That's so pretty.

1094
01:03:43,000 --> 01:03:44,560
Oh my god, that's so romantic.

1095
01:03:45,820 --> 01:03:46,100
Ah.

1096
01:03:47,400 --> 01:03:50,060
I do the same with my right arm on the right side of the book.

1097
01:03:50,840 --> 01:03:55,040
That way I turn a page, and Yuri slides it under her thumb after it flips to her side.

1098
01:03:56,200 --> 01:03:59,040
Man, that's just how I make food when someone is over, you know?

1099
01:04:00,280 --> 01:04:05,600
I hold the pan, and the one to the left of me flips the burger, you know?

1100
01:04:05,600 --> 01:04:08,460
Like, while I'm holding the pan, and we sort of do a little dance.

1101
01:04:09,480 --> 01:04:14,920
But in holding it like this, we're huddled even closer together than before.

1102
01:04:15,420 --> 01:04:16,580
Oh shit!

1103
01:04:18,740 --> 01:04:20,920
It's actually kind of distracting me!

1104
01:04:21,780 --> 01:04:27,180
It's as if I can feel the warmth of Yuri's face, as she's in the corner of my vision.

1105
01:04:28,400 --> 01:04:29,580
Are you ready?

1106
01:04:31,100 --> 01:04:31,700
Eh?

1107
01:04:33,480 --> 01:04:35,020
To turn the page.

1108
01:04:35,920 --> 01:04:36,360
Ah!

1109
01:04:37,160 --> 01:04:37,600
Sorry.

1110
01:04:38,640 --> 01:04:40,740
I think I got a bit distracted for a second.

1111
01:04:42,040 --> 01:04:45,000
I glance over at Yuri's face again, and our eyes meet.

1112
01:04:45,940 --> 01:04:48,220
I don't know how I'll be able to keep up with her.

1113
01:04:50,600 --> 01:04:51,760
Ah, that's okay.

1114
01:04:52,380 --> 01:04:53,880
You're not as used to reading, right?

1115
01:04:54,840 --> 01:04:57,340
I don't mind being patient if it takes you a bit longer.

1116
01:04:59,040 --> 01:04:59,480
Christ.

1117
01:05:00,680 --> 01:05:02,180
It's probably the least I can do.

1118
01:05:03,300 --> 01:05:05,320
Since you've been so patient with me.

1119
01:05:06,500 --> 01:05:07,020
Yeah.

1120
01:05:08,160 --> 01:05:08,680
Thanks.

1121
01:05:09,600 --> 01:05:10,840
We continue reading.

1122
01:05:11,840 --> 01:05:14,480
Yuri no longer asks me if I'm ready to turn the page.

1123
01:05:15,560 --> 01:05:18,480
Instead, I just assume that she finishes the page before me.

1124
01:05:18,780 --> 01:05:20,840
So I turn it by my own volition.

1125
01:05:21,880 --> 01:05:22,560
Wow.

1126
01:05:23,600 --> 01:05:25,880
We continue the first chapter in silence.

1127
01:05:26,500 --> 01:05:31,040
Even so, turning each page almost feels like an intimate exchange.

1128
01:05:31,960 --> 01:05:34,440
My thumb gently letting go of the page.

1129
01:05:34,720 --> 01:05:39,220
Letting it flutter over to her side as she catches it under her own thumb.

1130
01:05:40,520 --> 01:05:41,240
Hey, Yuri.

1131
01:05:42,020 --> 01:05:44,500
This might be a silly thought, but...

1132
01:05:44,500 --> 01:05:47,220
The main character kind of reminds me of you a little bit.

1133
01:05:48,220 --> 01:05:49,400
You think so?

1134
01:05:50,580 --> 01:05:51,420
How does she?

1135
01:05:52,540 --> 01:05:55,420
Well, I guess she's more blunt in a lot of ways.

1136
01:05:56,240 --> 01:05:59,120
But she also second guesses all of the things she says and does.

1137
01:05:59,940 --> 01:06:01,760
Like she's afraid she'll do something wrong.

1138
01:06:02,660 --> 01:06:04,760
It's not like I can see into your head or anything.

1139
01:06:05,720 --> 01:06:08,400
But they're kind of reminiscent of some of your mannerisms.

1140
01:06:09,440 --> 01:06:10,000
I...

1141
01:06:10,000 --> 01:06:10,700
I see.

1142
01:06:12,980 --> 01:06:15,000
Yuri remains silent for a moment.

1143
01:06:17,460 --> 01:06:18,580
But Oliver...

1144
01:06:18,580 --> 01:06:19,580
That's...

1145
01:06:20,220 --> 01:06:20,780
Probably...

1146
01:06:20,780 --> 01:06:23,260
A terrible thing to have in common with her.

1147
01:06:23,880 --> 01:06:27,060
Did she stand up and do that pose and then say that?

1148
01:06:28,760 --> 01:06:31,440
Oh, that's so embarrassing that you think that.

1149
01:06:33,240 --> 01:06:33,800
Wait!

1150
01:06:34,540 --> 01:06:36,820
I didn't mean it in a bad way or anything!

1151
01:06:37,560 --> 01:06:38,120
Sorry!

1152
01:06:38,400 --> 01:06:41,160
I really didn't know you were self-conscious about that sort of thing!

1153
01:06:43,380 --> 01:06:45,120
I guess I more meant that it's...

1154
01:06:45,120 --> 01:06:45,960
Kind of cute!

1155
01:06:47,240 --> 01:06:47,680
Ah!

1156
01:06:48,160 --> 01:06:48,600
Ah!

1157
01:06:50,040 --> 01:06:51,500
What are you saying all of a sudden?

1158
01:06:54,040 --> 01:06:55,100
Okay, everyone!

1159
01:06:56,900 --> 01:06:59,720
I think it's about time we share today's poems with each other!

1160
01:07:00,520 --> 01:07:02,820
We might not have enough time if we wait too long!

1161
01:07:04,860 --> 01:07:05,300
Uh...

1162
01:07:05,300 --> 01:07:06,420
Yuri exhales.

1163
01:07:06,940 --> 01:07:08,600
Spared from finishing her thought.

1164
01:07:09,920 --> 01:07:10,500
Is that alright, Yuri?

1165
01:07:11,260 --> 01:07:12,380
You look kinda down.

1166
01:07:13,140 --> 01:07:15,140
I'm sorry if you haven't been looking forward to this.

1167
01:07:16,060 --> 01:07:17,260
Ah, it's not...

1168
01:07:17,260 --> 01:07:18,020
It's fine.

1169
01:07:19,040 --> 01:07:22,900
Yuri releases her hand from the book, causing it to close on top of my thumb.

1170
01:07:23,420 --> 01:07:23,900
Ow!

1171
01:07:23,900 --> 01:07:24,060
Oh!

1172
01:07:24,520 --> 01:07:24,920
Fuck!

1173
01:07:25,640 --> 01:07:26,020
Get!

1174
01:07:27,440 --> 01:07:27,840
Alright.

1175
01:07:29,220 --> 01:07:30,620
I guess I'll do some more reading tonight.

1176
01:07:32,100 --> 01:07:33,820
Oh, would you prefer I only read it with you?

1177
01:07:36,040 --> 01:07:36,440
Um...

1178
01:07:37,720 --> 01:07:38,120
I...

1179
01:07:38,560 --> 01:07:40,900
I guess I don't have too much of a preference either way.

1180
01:07:41,960 --> 01:07:42,360
Hmm.

1181
01:07:43,240 --> 01:07:45,380
In that case, I'll read a little more tonight.

1182
01:07:46,480 --> 01:07:49,620
It'll be more fun to read with you after it picks up a bit, you know?

1183
01:07:51,020 --> 01:07:52,040
That's good reasoning!

1184
01:07:52,680 --> 01:07:56,400
In that case, feel free to finish the first two chapters in your own time.

1185
01:07:57,560 --> 01:07:57,960
Alright.

1186
01:07:59,040 --> 01:08:00,100
I stand up.

1187
01:08:00,200 --> 01:08:01,580
What a great sentence!

1188
01:08:02,860 --> 01:08:06,940
I make a mental note of where I left off in the book, and then slip it back into my bag.

1189
01:08:08,240 --> 01:08:11,100
By the way, did you remember to write a poem last night?

1190
01:08:12,820 --> 01:08:13,300
Yeah?

1191
01:08:14,940 --> 01:08:16,600
My relaxation ends.

1192
01:08:17,660 --> 01:08:20,680
I can't believe I agreed to do something so embarrassing.

1193
01:08:21,820 --> 01:08:25,380
I couldn't really find much inspiration, since I've never really done this before.

1194
01:08:26,640 --> 01:08:29,560
Well, now that everyone's ready, why don't you find someone to share with?

1195
01:08:30,480 --> 01:08:32,060
I can't wait!

1196
01:08:34,020 --> 01:08:36,860
Sayori and Monica enthusiastically pull out their poem.

1197
01:08:38,220 --> 01:08:39,460
That's one way to put it.

1198
01:08:40,720 --> 01:08:44,440
Sayori's is on a wrinkled sheet of loose leaf torn from a spiral notebook.

1199
01:08:45,380 --> 01:08:48,780
On the other hand, Monica wrote hers in a composition notebook.

1200
01:08:49,660 --> 01:08:52,900
I can already see Monica's pristine handwriting from where I sit.

1201
01:08:53,740 --> 01:08:58,060
Natsuki and Yuri reluctantly comply as well, reaching into their bags.

1202
01:09:01,520 --> 01:09:02,740
You know what this is like?

1203
01:09:02,820 --> 01:09:03,500
This is like...

1204
01:09:05,180 --> 01:09:12,680
It's like being trapped in one of those crazy rooms, where there's like padded walls and they have a little children's cartoon playing on repeat.

1205
01:09:12,680 --> 01:09:15,700
What is the name for this song?

1206
01:09:16,220 --> 01:09:16,960
What is the name for this song?

1207
01:09:16,960 --> 01:09:18,000
This is a dog!

1208
01:09:19,100 --> 01:09:20,960
Do you like dog?

1209
01:09:20,960 --> 01:09:22,320
You know?

1210
01:09:23,320 --> 01:09:24,960
I do the same myself.

1211
01:09:28,810 --> 01:09:30,810
Who should I show my poem to first?

1212
01:09:31,650 --> 01:09:32,810
Fuck if I know.

1213
01:09:34,790 --> 01:09:36,930
Who was I even talking to just now?

1214
01:09:38,150 --> 01:09:40,050
Let's give it to...

1215
01:09:40,050 --> 01:09:41,910
I guess Sayori.

1216
01:09:42,810 --> 01:09:46,270
I'm definitely most comfortable sharing it with Sayori first.

1217
01:09:47,610 --> 01:09:48,990
She's my good friend after all.

1218
01:09:49,130 --> 01:09:49,450
Right!

1219
01:09:49,610 --> 01:09:49,870
Exactly!

1220
01:09:50,110 --> 01:09:50,430
That's why.

1221
01:09:53,400 --> 01:09:54,720
Oh wait, that was Sayori?

1222
01:09:55,060 --> 01:09:56,300
I thought it was...

1223
01:09:56,840 --> 01:09:58,100
I can't even tell them apart.

1224
01:09:59,000 --> 01:10:00,220
This is a good poem, Oliver!

1225
01:10:01,720 --> 01:10:03,280
Are you sure it's your first time?

1226
01:10:03,660 --> 01:10:04,640
Did you read it?

1227
01:10:06,000 --> 01:10:06,760
Of course.

1228
01:10:08,080 --> 01:10:09,020
It's not that good.

1229
01:10:10,500 --> 01:10:13,260
Am I the kind of guy who would be writing poems in his spare time?

1230
01:10:14,240 --> 01:10:15,860
I guess you're right.

1231
01:10:16,880 --> 01:10:18,300
But that's why it impressed me.

1232
01:10:19,160 --> 01:10:21,160
Well, to be honest...

1233
01:10:21,160 --> 01:10:23,760
I was afraid that you wouldn't do it seriously.

1234
01:10:24,760 --> 01:10:26,380
Or that you wouldn't write one at all.

1235
01:10:27,300 --> 01:10:29,460
I'm really happy just that you wrote one.

1236
01:10:30,060 --> 01:10:32,780
It just reminds me of how you're really a part of the club now.

1237
01:10:33,980 --> 01:10:37,040
Not to mention the fact that I'm standing in front of you in the club room.

1238
01:10:38,800 --> 01:10:39,760
Ah, well...

1239
01:10:39,760 --> 01:10:40,480
Of course.

1240
01:10:41,380 --> 01:10:44,320
I'm not really into it yet, but that doesn't mean I'll break my promise.

1241
01:10:45,300 --> 01:10:45,640
See?

1242
01:10:46,900 --> 01:10:48,800
It's like I said before, Oliver.

1243
01:10:49,600 --> 01:10:51,960
Deep down, you're not selfish at all, you know?

1244
01:10:52,960 --> 01:10:53,220
Huh?

1245
01:10:54,720 --> 01:10:56,840
Trying new things like this for other people?

1246
01:10:57,980 --> 01:11:00,580
That's something that only really good people do.

1247
01:11:01,740 --> 01:11:03,360
Thanks, Sayori.

1248
01:11:04,500 --> 01:11:07,680
I'm not sure if Sayori sees the full picture of my motive here.

1249
01:11:08,320 --> 01:11:09,900
Wait, what is the full picture of my motive?

1250
01:11:12,300 --> 01:11:12,920
Then again.

1251
01:11:14,100 --> 01:11:15,960
I can't deny that she's part of the reason I joined.

1252
01:11:17,380 --> 01:11:19,120
Knowing how much this means to her and all.

1253
01:11:20,240 --> 01:11:20,640
Yeah.

1254
01:11:22,020 --> 01:11:24,160
And I'm gonna make sure you have lots of fun here.

1255
01:11:24,460 --> 01:11:24,860
Okay?

1256
01:11:25,840 --> 01:11:27,360
That would be my way of thanking you.

1257
01:11:28,320 --> 01:11:28,720
Alright.

1258
01:11:29,100 --> 01:11:30,500
I'm gonna hold you to that then.

1259
01:11:31,660 --> 01:11:32,060
Yay!

1260
01:11:33,620 --> 01:11:35,480
Now you'll read my poem too, right?

1261
01:11:36,520 --> 01:11:37,160
Don't worry.

1262
01:11:37,460 --> 01:11:38,740
I'm really bad at this.

1263
01:11:40,900 --> 01:11:42,240
We'll see about that.

1264
01:11:43,320 --> 01:11:44,300
Oh, I get to read it?

1265
01:11:44,420 --> 01:11:44,820
Holy shit!

1266
01:11:45,700 --> 01:11:46,380
Dear Sunshine.

1267
01:11:47,020 --> 01:11:49,360
The way you glow through my blinds in the morning.

1268
01:11:49,580 --> 01:11:51,300
It makes me feel like you missed me.

1269
01:11:51,860 --> 01:11:53,880
Kissing my forehead to help me out of bed.

1270
01:11:54,280 --> 01:11:56,380
Making me rub the sleepy from my eyes.

1271
01:11:56,760 --> 01:11:58,560
Are you asking me to come out and play?

1272
01:11:58,560 --> 01:12:01,860
Are you trusting me to wish away a rainy day?

1273
01:12:02,840 --> 01:12:03,880
I look above.

1274
01:12:04,240 --> 01:12:05,440
The sky is blue.

1275
01:12:05,740 --> 01:12:06,580
It's a secret.

1276
01:12:06,880 --> 01:12:08,100
But I trust you too.

1277
01:12:08,460 --> 01:12:11,000
If it wasn't for you, I would sleep forever.

1278
01:12:11,460 --> 01:12:12,600
But I'm not mad.

1279
01:12:13,780 --> 01:12:14,300
Okay.

1280
01:12:16,580 --> 01:12:17,620
I mean...

1281
01:12:17,620 --> 01:12:18,160
Sayori?

1282
01:12:19,220 --> 01:12:21,580
This is just a guess, but...

1283
01:12:22,480 --> 01:12:24,600
Did you wait until this morning to write this?

1284
01:12:24,960 --> 01:12:26,040
Who am I to judge?

1285
01:12:28,560 --> 01:12:28,780
No!

1286
01:12:30,500 --> 01:12:31,700
Just a little bit?

1287
01:12:32,820 --> 01:12:35,680
You can't answer just a little bit to a yes or no question.

1288
01:12:38,200 --> 01:12:39,700
I forgot to do it last night.

1289
01:12:40,840 --> 01:12:43,120
Well, at least that makes me feel a little better about myself.

1290
01:12:44,420 --> 01:12:45,160
Don't be mean.

1291
01:12:46,040 --> 01:12:47,520
I still tried my best.

1292
01:12:48,760 --> 01:12:49,160
Ah.

1293
01:12:49,600 --> 01:12:50,000
Yeah.

1294
01:12:51,220 --> 01:12:53,340
I didn't mean to say that it was a bad poem.

1295
01:12:54,440 --> 01:12:56,540
It came out nice, or how should I put it?

1296
01:12:56,540 --> 01:12:58,500
Just, um...

1297
01:12:58,500 --> 01:13:00,040
It sounds just like you.

1298
01:13:01,460 --> 01:13:02,180
Really?

1299
01:13:03,760 --> 01:13:04,480
Yeah.

1300
01:13:05,580 --> 01:13:07,360
Especially that last line.

1301
01:13:08,620 --> 01:13:09,760
I made eggs and toast?

1302
01:13:10,120 --> 01:13:10,840
What?

1303
01:13:12,000 --> 01:13:14,220
Even though you were late to school?

1304
01:13:15,120 --> 01:13:17,100
It's bad to skip breakfast.

1305
01:13:17,680 --> 01:13:19,780
I get all cranky.

1306
01:13:20,680 --> 01:13:23,040
Well, I guess there's no point in arguing.

1307
01:13:24,360 --> 01:13:26,400
Anyway, thanks for showing me.

1308
01:13:28,380 --> 01:13:29,820
This was so much fun!

1309
01:13:30,740 --> 01:13:31,760
Monika's the best!

1310
01:13:32,720 --> 01:13:33,440
Uh, yeah.

1311
01:13:34,180 --> 01:13:35,820
But next time, I won't forget.

1312
01:13:36,380 --> 01:13:38,580
And I'm gonna write the best poem ever!

1313
01:13:39,720 --> 01:13:41,900
Well, I guess I look forward to it.

1314
01:13:43,060 --> 01:13:43,820
I don't.

1315
01:13:45,000 --> 01:13:46,720
Who should I show my po- What?

1316
01:13:46,960 --> 01:13:47,980
Are we gonna go through this?

1317
01:13:52,650 --> 01:13:54,290
Uh, alright, fine.

1318
01:13:54,630 --> 01:13:55,130
Natsuki.

1319
01:14:00,600 --> 01:14:04,240
Well, it's about what I expected from someone like you.

1320
01:14:05,220 --> 01:14:06,620
That's a little blunt.

1321
01:14:07,760 --> 01:14:09,220
Well, excuse me!

1322
01:14:10,020 --> 01:14:11,760
It's not like I said it was bad.

1323
01:14:12,940 --> 01:14:15,760
It just didn't evolve or evoke any emotions.

1324
01:14:17,100 --> 01:14:19,540
So basically, it's not cute enough for your taste.

1325
01:14:20,780 --> 01:14:21,880
Do you want to get naked?

1326
01:14:22,060 --> 01:14:22,640
I mean, smacked?

1327
01:14:22,860 --> 01:14:24,400
I really thought she said that.

1328
01:14:25,380 --> 01:14:26,240
I'll pass.

1329
01:14:27,420 --> 01:14:27,860
Ugh.

1330
01:14:29,120 --> 01:14:31,020
Well, anyway, I guess I need to show you mine.

1331
01:14:31,240 --> 01:14:31,480
What?

1332
01:14:33,380 --> 01:14:34,600
Not that you'll like it.

1333
01:14:36,940 --> 01:14:38,740
Eagles can fly.

1334
01:14:38,980 --> 01:14:40,340
Monkeys can climb.

1335
01:14:41,020 --> 01:14:42,200
Crickets can leap.

1336
01:14:42,880 --> 01:14:44,400
Horses can race.

1337
01:14:45,260 --> 01:14:46,540
Owls can seek.

1338
01:14:47,340 --> 01:14:48,660
Cheetahs can run.

1339
01:14:49,480 --> 01:14:50,860
Eagles can fly.

1340
01:14:51,480 --> 01:14:53,060
People can try.

1341
01:14:53,780 --> 01:14:55,820
But that's about it.

1342
01:14:56,360 --> 01:14:57,400
Fucking terrible.

1343
01:14:58,200 --> 01:14:58,840
Yeah.

1344
01:14:59,740 --> 01:15:02,000
I told you that you weren't gonna like it!

1345
01:15:04,020 --> 01:15:05,100
I like it.

1346
01:15:05,740 --> 01:15:06,300
What?

1347
01:15:07,440 --> 01:15:08,480
Just be honest.

1348
01:15:09,680 --> 01:15:10,700
I am?

1349
01:15:12,040 --> 01:15:14,740
Why are you so convinced that I wouldn't like it?

1350
01:15:17,120 --> 01:15:17,720
Well...

1351
01:15:17,720 --> 01:15:18,740
Because!

1352
01:15:19,960 --> 01:15:23,860
Everyone in high school thinks that writing has to be all sophisticated and stuff.

1353
01:15:24,600 --> 01:15:26,820
So people don't even take my writing seriously!

1354
01:15:28,040 --> 01:15:31,480
But isn't the point of poems for people to express themselves?

1355
01:15:32,760 --> 01:15:35,700
Your writing style wouldn't make your message any less valid.

1356
01:15:37,000 --> 01:15:37,520
Yes!

1357
01:15:38,140 --> 01:15:38,660
Exactly!

1358
01:15:39,520 --> 01:15:41,240
I like it when it's easy to read.

1359
01:15:41,480 --> 01:15:42,620
But it hits you hard.

1360
01:15:43,340 --> 01:15:44,460
Like in this poem.

1361
01:15:45,480 --> 01:15:48,480
Seeing everyone around you do great things can be really disheartening.

1362
01:15:49,240 --> 01:15:50,940
So I decided to write about it.

1363
01:15:51,780 --> 01:15:52,260
Yeah.

1364
01:15:53,000 --> 01:15:53,880
I understand.

1365
01:15:55,200 --> 01:15:58,760
But the other nice thing about simple writing is that it puts more weight on the wordplay.

1366
01:15:59,840 --> 01:16:02,720
Like I set up for a rhyme at the end but then made it fall flat on purpose.

1367
01:16:03,800 --> 01:16:05,920
It helps bring out the feeling in the last line.

1368
01:16:07,580 --> 01:16:08,460
So you did.

1369
01:16:09,400 --> 01:16:11,340
I guess more went into it than I realized.

1370
01:16:12,460 --> 01:16:13,960
That's what it means to be a pro.

1371
01:16:14,800 --> 01:16:16,220
I'm glad you learned something.

1372
01:16:17,080 --> 01:16:19,280
Didn't expect that from the youngest one here, did you?

1373
01:16:20,280 --> 01:16:21,280
Yeah, I guess not.

1374
01:16:22,100 --> 01:16:24,420
I decided to humor her with that last comment.

1375
01:16:25,440 --> 01:16:27,540
I don't really care how old everyone is.

1376
01:16:27,760 --> 01:16:30,760
But if Natsuki is feeling proud then I won't take that away from her.

1377
01:16:32,020 --> 01:16:32,860
Christ almighty.

1378
01:16:34,000 --> 01:16:35,760
Who should I show my poem to next?

1379
01:16:36,780 --> 01:16:37,240
Yuri.

1380
01:16:41,340 --> 01:16:44,320
As Yuri reads the poem I notice her eyes lighten.

1381
01:16:46,100 --> 01:16:46,640
Exceptional.

1382
01:16:47,540 --> 01:16:47,880
Eh?

1383
01:16:48,180 --> 01:16:49,020
What was that?

1384
01:16:51,300 --> 01:16:51,840
Did...

1385
01:16:51,840 --> 01:16:53,220
Can I say that out loud?

1386
01:16:54,420 --> 01:16:58,120
Yuri first covers her mouth but then ends up covering her whole face.

1387
01:16:59,720 --> 01:17:00,320
I...

1388
01:17:01,560 --> 01:17:02,160
Ooh...

1389
01:17:02,160 --> 01:17:03,780
He's gonna hate me.

1390
01:17:05,920 --> 01:17:06,520
Um...

1391
01:17:07,120 --> 01:17:09,200
You really didn't do anything wrong, Yuri.

1392
01:17:10,600 --> 01:17:11,200
Eh?

1393
01:17:12,880 --> 01:17:13,480
That's...

1394
01:17:13,480 --> 01:17:14,460
I...

1395
01:17:14,460 --> 01:17:15,600
Guess you're right.

1396
01:17:16,480 --> 01:17:18,240
What am I getting so nervous for?

1397
01:17:19,060 --> 01:17:19,660
Ahahaha.

1398
01:17:20,800 --> 01:17:22,120
Yuri takes a breath.

1399
01:17:23,400 --> 01:17:23,940
So...

1400
01:17:24,620 --> 01:17:26,640
What kind of writing experience do you have?

1401
01:17:27,680 --> 01:17:31,920
Your use of imagery and metaphors indicates you've written a lot of poetry before.

1402
01:17:33,140 --> 01:17:33,580
Really?

1403
01:17:34,620 --> 01:17:36,960
Wow, that's a huge compliment coming from you.

1404
01:17:37,660 --> 01:17:39,460
This is actually my first time, really.

1405
01:17:40,600 --> 01:17:40,940
Huh?

1406
01:17:41,860 --> 01:17:44,580
Yuri stares at me blankly, then looks at my poem again.

1407
01:17:47,080 --> 01:17:48,220
Well, I know that.

1408
01:17:48,820 --> 01:17:50,400
I just meant, uh, um...

1409
01:17:52,000 --> 01:17:54,000
Yuri trails off, unable to find an excuse.

1410
01:17:54,940 --> 01:17:59,120
She traces her finger along the words in the poem as if breaking it down more thoroughly.

1411
01:18:00,320 --> 01:18:00,800
Yeah.

1412
01:18:01,980 --> 01:18:02,460
Okay.

1413
01:18:03,880 --> 01:18:05,600
This is the reason I was able to tell.

1414
01:18:06,460 --> 01:18:10,660
It's just that there are specific writing habits that are usually typical of new writers.

1415
01:18:11,640 --> 01:18:16,400
And having been through that myself, I kind of learned to pick up on them.

1416
01:18:17,240 --> 01:18:22,180
I think the most noticeable thing I recognize in new writers is that they try to make their style very deliberate.

1417
01:18:23,460 --> 01:18:29,560
In other words, they tend to pick a writing style separate from the topic matter, and they form-fit the two together.

1418
01:18:30,440 --> 01:18:33,980
The end result is that both the style and the expressiveness are weakened.

1419
01:18:35,160 --> 01:18:39,100
Once Yuri finds the train of thought, it's as if her demeanor totally changes.

1420
01:18:40,080 --> 01:18:43,660
Her stammering is completely gone, and she sounds like an expert.

1421
01:18:44,680 --> 01:18:46,720
Of course, that's not something you can be blamed for.

1422
01:18:46,720 --> 01:18:51,680
However, there are so many different skills and techniques that go into writing even a single poem.

1423
01:18:52,500 --> 01:18:57,260
Not just finding them and building them, but getting them to work together is probably the most challenging part.

1424
01:18:57,920 --> 01:19:04,140
It might take you some time, but it all comes with practice, and learning by example, and trying new things.

1425
01:19:04,900 --> 01:19:08,160
I also hope that everyone else in the club gives you valuable feedback.

1426
01:19:09,280 --> 01:19:11,120
Natsuki can be a little bit biased, though.

1427
01:19:12,140 --> 01:19:12,620
Biased?

1428
01:19:13,220 --> 01:19:13,700
How?

1429
01:19:16,300 --> 01:19:16,760
Um...

1430
01:19:17,920 --> 01:19:18,380
Well...

1431
01:19:18,380 --> 01:19:19,300
Never mind.

1432
01:19:19,480 --> 01:19:21,180
Just spit it out already!

1433
01:19:21,420 --> 01:19:21,760
Fuck!

1434
01:19:26,140 --> 01:19:28,160
I shouldn't be talking about people like that.

1435
01:19:28,880 --> 01:19:29,340
Sorry.

1436
01:19:30,420 --> 01:19:31,320
It's fine!

1437
01:19:32,420 --> 01:19:35,900
I'm not sure if Yuri is apologizing to herself, to me, or to Natsuki.

1438
01:19:36,100 --> 01:19:40,960
Honestly, at this point, I don't think they even realize themselves who they're apologizing to.

1439
01:19:41,980 --> 01:19:43,660
Do you mind if I read your poem now?

1440
01:19:45,040 --> 01:19:45,700
Please do.

1441
01:19:46,540 --> 01:19:48,940
I'd love to share my thought process behind it.

1442
01:19:49,860 --> 01:19:53,560
Yuri smiles dreamily, as if that's a rare opportunity for her.

1443
01:19:54,480 --> 01:19:56,020
Which itself is kind of funny.

1444
01:19:57,260 --> 01:19:59,460
After all, isn't this supposed to be a literature club?

1445
01:20:02,180 --> 01:20:03,480
Fuckin' hell, she's a doctor.

1446
01:20:04,820 --> 01:20:06,500
Ghosts under the light.

1447
01:20:06,840 --> 01:20:10,040
The tentrils of my hair illuminate beneath the amber glow.

1448
01:20:10,840 --> 01:20:11,400
Battling.

1449
01:20:11,780 --> 01:20:13,140
It must be this one.

1450
01:20:13,460 --> 01:20:16,980
The last remaining streetlight to have withstood the test of time.

1451
01:20:17,320 --> 01:20:21,380
The last yet to be replaced by the sickening blue-green hue of the future.

1452
01:20:21,380 --> 01:20:22,740
I bathe.

1453
01:20:23,020 --> 01:20:26,140
Calm breathing air of the present, but living in the past.

1454
01:20:26,580 --> 01:20:27,660
The light flickers.

1455
01:20:28,120 --> 01:20:29,560
I flicker back.

1456
01:20:30,540 --> 01:20:30,820
Oh!

1457
01:20:34,150 --> 01:20:36,430
I'm sorry to have such terrible handwriting.

1458
01:20:37,290 --> 01:20:37,890
What?

1459
01:20:39,270 --> 01:20:41,010
I wasn't thinking that at all!

1460
01:20:41,770 --> 01:20:42,010
Huh.

1461
01:20:43,330 --> 01:20:44,690
But it took you a long time to read.

1462
01:20:44,870 --> 01:20:45,530
No it didn't.

1463
01:20:46,410 --> 01:20:46,750
Uh.

1464
01:20:47,610 --> 01:20:49,750
Well, I just don't read script very often.

1465
01:20:51,350 --> 01:20:52,250
That's very meta.

1466
01:20:52,730 --> 01:20:54,070
Since I'm reading one right now!

1467
01:20:55,590 --> 01:20:57,870
I actually think your handwriting is pretty.

1468
01:20:58,590 --> 01:20:59,170
Eh?

1469
01:21:00,590 --> 01:21:01,390
That's...

1470
01:21:01,390 --> 01:21:02,130
a relief.

1471
01:21:03,170 --> 01:21:03,970
Also...

1472
01:21:03,970 --> 01:21:05,090
I like the poem!

1473
01:21:05,910 --> 01:21:08,470
Even though it's short, it was really descriptive.

1474
01:21:08,950 --> 01:21:09,850
Lots of big words.

1475
01:21:11,130 --> 01:21:12,170
It was too short?

1476
01:21:12,690 --> 01:21:14,830
I usually write longer poems.

1477
01:21:15,950 --> 01:21:17,530
Not at all.

1478
01:21:18,510 --> 01:21:19,310
I'm...

1479
01:21:19,310 --> 01:21:20,690
really glad you like it.

1480
01:21:21,570 --> 01:21:22,490
I'll be honest.

1481
01:21:23,570 --> 01:21:28,390
Since it's our first time sharing, I wanted to write something a little more mild.

1482
01:21:29,650 --> 01:21:31,410
Something easy to digest, I suppose.

1483
01:21:33,010 --> 01:21:34,410
Are you into ghosts, Yuri?

1484
01:21:35,650 --> 01:21:36,190
Hoo-hoo!

1485
01:21:38,110 --> 01:21:40,590
Actually, the story isn't about a ghost at all, Oliver!

1486
01:21:41,870 --> 01:21:42,350
Really?

1487
01:21:43,310 --> 01:21:44,890
I must have totally missed the point.

1488
01:21:46,370 --> 01:21:49,530
Well, I suppose you did only glance over it after all.

1489
01:21:50,790 --> 01:21:55,390
But remember that poets often express their own thoughts, feelings, and experiences in their work.

1490
01:21:56,190 --> 01:21:59,770
They usually do more than tell a simple story, or paint a picture.

1491
01:22:00,570 --> 01:22:05,270
In this case, perhaps the subject of the poem is only being symbolically compared to a ghost.

1492
01:22:06,350 --> 01:22:08,830
Lingering in her last remaining place of comfort?

1493
01:22:09,330 --> 01:22:10,910
Unable to let go of the past?

1494
01:22:11,990 --> 01:22:13,510
And soon to be left with nothing?

1495
01:22:14,850 --> 01:22:17,130
That's a lot more solemn, putting it that way.

1496
01:22:17,830 --> 01:22:19,130
I hadn't even thought of that.

1497
01:22:19,990 --> 01:22:20,850
That's impressive.

1498
01:22:22,050 --> 01:22:22,470
Eh?

1499
01:22:24,070 --> 01:22:25,150
It's nothing, really.

1500
01:22:26,170 --> 01:22:27,890
Yours was impressive, too, so...

1501
01:22:28,930 --> 01:22:29,350
Nah.

1502
01:22:30,450 --> 01:22:32,950
If anything, I could probably learn a thing or two from you.

1503
01:22:33,590 --> 01:22:34,670
You think so?

1504
01:22:35,430 --> 01:22:36,710
Yeah, of course.

1505
01:22:37,770 --> 01:22:38,090
Ah.

1506
01:22:39,130 --> 01:22:40,370
You know...

1507
01:22:40,370 --> 01:22:43,110
I was really nervous about doing all this.

1508
01:22:43,890 --> 01:22:45,790
But in the end, I enjoyed it.

1509
01:22:45,850 --> 01:22:47,730
Why are all of these conversations...

1510
01:22:48,500 --> 01:22:50,730
so weirdly sexual-sounding when they're not?

1511
01:22:52,630 --> 01:22:53,250
Like...

1512
01:22:53,250 --> 01:22:54,490
Are you reading this?

1513
01:22:57,170 --> 01:22:58,470
Like, all of this.

1514
01:22:58,610 --> 01:22:59,630
It's weird, man.

1515
01:23:02,500 --> 01:23:04,880
I'm gonna keep doing my best for you all.

1516
01:23:08,230 --> 01:23:09,450
Yeah, you better.

1517
01:23:10,070 --> 01:23:11,970
Because so far, I ain't impressed.

1518
01:23:16,800 --> 01:23:17,420
Ugh.

1519
01:23:18,800 --> 01:23:19,700
Me, too.

1520
01:23:22,100 --> 01:23:22,720
Weird.

1521
01:23:24,660 --> 01:23:25,740
Alright, Monica.

1522
01:23:26,380 --> 01:23:27,560
Get it over with.

1523
01:23:28,400 --> 01:23:32,210
Good time so far.

1524
01:23:33,810 --> 01:23:34,290
Ah.

1525
01:23:35,530 --> 01:23:36,010
Yeah.

1526
01:23:37,490 --> 01:23:37,970
Good.

1527
01:23:38,330 --> 01:23:39,270
Glad to hear it.

1528
01:23:39,970 --> 01:23:42,650
By the way, since you're new and everything...

1529
01:23:43,130 --> 01:23:48,870
If you ever have any suggestions for the club, like new activities or things we can do better...

1530
01:23:49,450 --> 01:23:51,050
I'm always listening.

1531
01:23:51,910 --> 01:23:53,590
Don't be afraid to bring things up, okay?

1532
01:23:54,890 --> 01:23:55,370
Alright.

1533
01:23:55,890 --> 01:23:56,890
I'll keep that in mind.

1534
01:23:57,890 --> 01:24:00,110
Of course I'll be afraid to bring things up.

1535
01:24:00,690 --> 01:24:03,950
I'm much better off just going with the flow until I'm more settled in.

1536
01:24:06,070 --> 01:24:06,590
Anyway...

1537
01:24:06,590 --> 01:24:08,610
Wanna share your... poem?

1538
01:24:11,050 --> 01:24:13,090
It's kind of embarrassing, but...

1539
01:24:13,090 --> 01:24:14,350
I guess I have to.

1540
01:24:19,330 --> 01:24:21,410
We're all a little embarrassed today, you know?

1541
01:24:22,650 --> 01:24:25,870
But it's that sort of barrier that we all learn to get past soon.

1542
01:24:26,810 --> 01:24:27,990
Yeah, that's true.

1543
01:24:28,590 --> 01:24:30,090
I had Monica in my poem.

1544
01:24:32,650 --> 01:24:34,430
Great job, Oliver.

1545
01:24:34,970 --> 01:24:38,110
I was going mmm in my head while reading it.

1546
01:24:38,650 --> 01:24:40,650
It's really metaphorical.

1547
01:24:41,310 --> 01:24:46,110
I'm not sure why, but I didn't expect you to go for something so... deep.

1548
01:24:46,990 --> 01:24:48,950
I guess I underestimated you.

1549
01:24:49,390 --> 01:24:54,010
It's easiest for me to keep everyone's expectations low.

1550
01:24:55,150 --> 01:24:59,150
That way, it always counts when I put in some effort.

1551
01:24:59,790 --> 01:25:01,730
Ha ha ha, that's not very fair.

1552
01:25:02,710 --> 01:25:04,370
Well, I guess it worked anyway.

1553
01:25:05,930 --> 01:25:08,250
You know that Yuri likes this kind of writing, right?

1554
01:25:09,150 --> 01:25:11,810
Writing that's full of imagery and symbolism?

1555
01:25:13,150 --> 01:25:17,990
Unlike Sayori, who likes using simple and direct words to describe happiness and sadness?

1556
01:25:19,270 --> 01:25:22,210
Yuri likes it when readers are left to derive their own meaning out of it.

1557
01:25:22,850 --> 01:25:25,510
It's very challenging to write like that effectively.

1558
01:25:26,550 --> 01:25:29,910
Both allowing people to get something out of it just by feel.

1559
01:25:30,690 --> 01:25:34,410
Or letting them deeply analyze all of the nuances.

1560
01:25:35,470 --> 01:25:39,070
It can take years of practice, which I'm assuming Yuri has at this point.

1561
01:25:39,710 --> 01:25:41,190
I never really asked, though.

1562
01:25:42,010 --> 01:25:44,410
I'm sure I'm nowhere near her level yet.

1563
01:25:45,190 --> 01:25:46,670
Don't worry so much about that.

1564
01:25:47,270 --> 01:25:48,530
You do your own thing.

1565
01:25:49,370 --> 01:25:53,730
Just keep exploring and learn by trying new things.

1566
01:25:55,010 --> 01:25:57,990
I'm sure I'll end up trying different things a lot.

1567
01:25:58,770 --> 01:26:01,870
It could take a while before I feel comfortable doing this.

1568
01:26:02,530 --> 01:26:03,270
That's okay.

1569
01:26:03,990 --> 01:26:05,990
I'd love to see you try new things.

1570
01:26:07,150 --> 01:26:09,410
At this point, I'm just fucking going with it.

1571
01:26:09,490 --> 01:26:10,950
Because this is weird!

1572
01:26:12,190 --> 01:26:13,070
A shit!

1573
01:26:14,650 --> 01:26:17,410
That's the best way to find the kind of style that suits you.

1574
01:26:18,230 --> 01:26:21,330
Everyone else might be a little bit biased toward their own kinds of styles.

1575
01:26:22,190 --> 01:26:25,330
But I'll always help you find what suits you the most.

1576
01:26:26,010 --> 01:26:29,270
So don't force yourself to write the way everyone else wants you to write.

1577
01:26:29,990 --> 01:26:33,030
It's not like you have to worry about impressing them or anything.

1578
01:26:38,430 --> 01:26:40,910
Anyway, do you want to read my poem now?

1579
01:26:42,310 --> 01:26:44,110
Don't worry, I'm not very good.

1580
01:26:45,090 --> 01:26:48,170
You sound pretty confident for someone who claims to be not very good.

1581
01:26:49,550 --> 01:26:52,310
Well, that's because I have to sound confident.

1582
01:26:53,230 --> 01:26:55,670
That doesn't mean I always feel that way, you know?

1583
01:26:56,410 --> 01:26:57,330
I see.

1584
01:26:58,470 --> 01:26:59,770
Well, let's have it then.

1585
01:27:01,770 --> 01:27:03,490
Hole in wall.

1586
01:27:03,930 --> 01:27:05,410
It couldn't have been me.

1587
01:27:06,070 --> 01:27:08,310
See the direction the spackle protrudes?

1588
01:27:08,610 --> 01:27:09,890
A noisy neighbor?

1589
01:27:10,290 --> 01:27:11,590
An angry boyfriend?

1590
01:27:11,990 --> 01:27:12,810
I'll never know.

1591
01:27:13,030 --> 01:27:13,970
I wasn't home.

1592
01:27:14,730 --> 01:27:16,370
I peer inside for a clue.

1593
01:27:17,070 --> 01:27:17,330
No!

1594
01:27:17,670 --> 01:27:18,850
I can't see.

1595
01:27:19,050 --> 01:27:20,090
I reel blind.

1596
01:27:20,410 --> 01:27:22,510
Like a film that left out in the sun.

1597
01:27:22,970 --> 01:27:24,170
But it's too late.

1598
01:27:24,170 --> 01:27:25,590
My retinas.

1599
01:27:26,070 --> 01:27:30,390
Already scorched with a permanent copy of the meaningless image.

1600
01:27:30,910 --> 01:27:32,190
It's just a little hole.

1601
01:27:32,610 --> 01:27:33,690
It wasn't too bright.

1602
01:27:34,090 --> 01:27:35,250
It was too deep.

1603
01:27:35,630 --> 01:27:37,710
Stretching forever into everything.

1604
01:27:38,170 --> 01:27:40,330
A hole of infinite choices.

1605
01:27:40,790 --> 01:27:42,890
I realize now that I wasn't looking in.

1606
01:27:43,570 --> 01:27:44,670
I was looking out.

1607
01:27:44,970 --> 01:27:48,750
And he, on the other side, was looking in.

1608
01:27:50,410 --> 01:27:51,170
Beautiful.

1609
01:27:52,490 --> 01:27:53,630
That's magnificent.

1610
01:27:54,990 --> 01:27:56,030
So, uh...

1611
01:27:56,030 --> 01:27:57,010
What do you think?

1612
01:27:58,370 --> 01:28:00,150
Uh, it's very...

1613
01:28:01,190 --> 01:28:01,710
Freeform?

1614
01:28:02,010 --> 01:28:03,270
If that's what you call it.

1615
01:28:04,230 --> 01:28:07,230
Sorry, I'm not really the right person to ask for feedback.

1616
01:28:08,890 --> 01:28:09,710
It's... okay.

1617
01:28:10,610 --> 01:28:13,450
Yeah, that kind of style has gotten pretty popular nowadays.

1618
01:28:14,570 --> 01:28:15,530
That is...

1619
01:28:15,530 --> 01:28:19,650
A lot of poems have been putting emphasis on the timing between words and lines.

1620
01:28:21,030 --> 01:28:23,950
When performed out loud, it can be really powerful.

1621
01:28:24,930 --> 01:28:26,910
What was the inspiration behind this one?

1622
01:28:28,310 --> 01:28:28,790
Uh...

1623
01:28:28,790 --> 01:28:31,190
Well, I'm not sure if I know how to put it.

1624
01:28:32,290 --> 01:28:34,970
I guess you could say that I had some kind of...

1625
01:28:36,130 --> 01:28:36,970
Epiphany recently?

1626
01:28:38,230 --> 01:28:40,050
It's been influencing my poems a bit.

1627
01:28:41,270 --> 01:28:42,350
An epiphany?

1628
01:28:43,290 --> 01:28:43,850
Yeah!

1629
01:28:44,550 --> 01:28:45,530
Something like that.

1630
01:28:46,630 --> 01:28:49,970
I'm kind of nervous to talk about deep stuff like that because it's...

1631
01:28:49,970 --> 01:28:51,630
It's kind of coming on strongly.

1632
01:28:52,670 --> 01:28:55,150
Maybe after everyone is better friends with each other.

1633
01:28:57,470 --> 01:28:59,670
Anyway, here's Monica's writing tip of the day.

1634
01:29:00,870 --> 01:29:07,670
Sometimes when you're writing a poem or a story, your brain gets too fixated on a specific point.

1635
01:29:08,410 --> 01:29:12,550
If you try so hard to make it perfect, then you'll never make any progress.

1636
01:29:13,510 --> 01:29:17,710
Just force yourself to get something down on the paper and tidy it up later.

1637
01:29:18,810 --> 01:29:20,370
Another way to think about it is this.

1638
01:29:20,910 --> 01:29:25,810
If you keep your pet in the same spot for too long, you'll just get a big dark puddle of ink.

1639
01:29:26,770 --> 01:29:29,090
So just move your hand and go with the flow.

1640
01:29:30,070 --> 01:29:31,330
That's my advice for today.

1641
01:29:32,070 --> 01:29:32,990
Thanks for listening.

1642
01:29:38,070 --> 01:29:39,770
I guess that's everyone.

1643
01:29:40,350 --> 01:29:41,810
My glance around the room.

1644
01:29:42,790 --> 01:29:45,110
That was a little more stressful than I anticipated.

1645
01:29:46,530 --> 01:29:49,690
It's as if everyone is judging me for my mediocre writing abilities.

1646
01:29:50,750 --> 01:29:54,530
Even if they're just being nice, there's no way my poems can stand up to theirs.

1647
01:29:55,590 --> 01:29:57,210
Fuck, I wish I could write my poem.

1648
01:30:00,110 --> 01:30:01,850
This is a literature club after all.

1649
01:30:04,430 --> 01:30:06,990
I guess that's what I ended up getting myself into.

1650
01:30:08,650 --> 01:30:10,290
Biggest mistake of my life.

1651
01:30:10,870 --> 01:30:11,890
Going to this club.

1652
01:30:12,710 --> 01:30:16,070
Across the room, Sayori and Monika are happily chatting.

1653
01:30:16,670 --> 01:30:18,870
My eyes land on Yuri and Natsuki.

1654
01:30:19,530 --> 01:30:24,010
They gingerly exchange sheets of paper, sharing their respective poems.

1655
01:30:25,110 --> 01:30:28,750
As they read in tandem, I watch each of their expressions change.

1656
01:30:29,730 --> 01:30:32,010
Natsuki's eyebrows furrow in frustration.

1657
01:30:32,830 --> 01:30:35,190
Meanwhile, Yuri smiles sadly.

1658
01:30:45,130 --> 01:30:48,610
Natsuki dismissively returns the poem to the desk with one hand.

1659
01:30:49,350 --> 01:30:51,990
I guess you could say it's... fancy.

1660
01:30:53,250 --> 01:30:54,190
Ah, thanks.

1661
01:30:54,950 --> 01:30:56,650
Yours is... cute.

1662
01:30:57,630 --> 01:30:58,290
Cute?

1663
01:30:59,190 --> 01:31:01,710
Did you completely miss the symbolism or something?

1664
01:31:02,450 --> 01:31:04,430
It's clearly about the feeling of giving up!

1665
01:31:05,390 --> 01:31:06,430
How can that be cute?

1666
01:31:07,550 --> 01:31:09,070
I... I know that.

1667
01:31:09,650 --> 01:31:10,810
I just meant...

1668
01:31:10,810 --> 01:31:12,510
The language, I guess...

1669
01:31:12,990 --> 01:31:14,530
I was trying to say something nice.

1670
01:31:15,870 --> 01:31:16,310
Eh?

1671
01:31:17,570 --> 01:31:20,070
You mean you have to try that hard to come up with something nice to say?

1672
01:31:21,030 --> 01:31:21,510
Thanks.

1673
01:31:21,770 --> 01:31:23,650
But it really didn't come out nice at all!

1674
01:31:25,770 --> 01:31:28,530
Well, I do have a couple of suggestions.

1675
01:31:29,530 --> 01:31:29,870
Hmm.

1676
01:31:31,130 --> 01:31:34,550
If I was looking for suggestions, I wouldn't have asked someone who actually liked it.

1677
01:31:35,330 --> 01:31:37,030
Which people did, by the way.

1678
01:31:38,090 --> 01:31:39,170
Sayori liked it.

1679
01:31:39,710 --> 01:31:41,050
And Oliver did too!

1680
01:31:41,750 --> 01:31:44,690
So based on that, I'll gladly give you some suggestions of my own.

1681
01:31:45,570 --> 01:31:46,870
First of all...

1682
01:31:47,690 --> 01:31:48,490
Excuse me.

1683
01:31:48,990 --> 01:31:50,890
I appreciate the offer, but...

1684
01:31:50,890 --> 01:31:53,330
I've spent a long time establishing my writing style.

1685
01:31:54,230 --> 01:31:59,090
I don't expect it to change anytime soon, unless of course I come across something particularly inspiring.

1686
01:32:00,170 --> 01:32:01,170
Which I haven't yet.

1687
01:32:02,190 --> 01:32:02,910
Th-th-th...

1688
01:32:03,650 --> 01:32:05,370
And Oliver liked my poem too, you know?

1689
01:32:05,970 --> 01:32:07,910
He even told me he was impressed by it!

1690
01:32:08,690 --> 01:32:09,330
Did I?

1691
01:32:10,190 --> 01:32:11,670
Matsuki suddenly stands up.

1692
01:32:12,730 --> 01:32:13,210
Oh?

1693
01:32:14,570 --> 01:32:18,310
I didn't realize you were so invested in trying to impress our new member, Yuri!

1694
01:32:19,390 --> 01:32:19,830
Eh?

1695
01:32:21,730 --> 01:32:22,870
That's not what I...

1696
01:32:24,210 --> 01:32:24,690
Uh...

1697
01:32:24,690 --> 01:32:26,170
You-you're just...

1698
01:32:26,170 --> 01:32:27,490
Yuri stands up as well.

1699
01:32:28,410 --> 01:32:31,590
Maybe you're just jealous that Oliver appreciates my advice more than he appreciated yours?

1700
01:32:32,350 --> 01:32:32,830
Huh?

1701
01:32:33,230 --> 01:32:35,050
How you know he didn't appreciate my advice more?

1702
01:32:35,590 --> 01:32:36,870
Are you that full of yourself?

1703
01:32:37,890 --> 01:32:38,370
I...

1704
01:32:38,370 --> 01:32:39,150
No.

1705
01:32:40,690 --> 01:32:42,430
If I was full of myself...

1706
01:32:43,030 --> 01:32:46,130
I would deliberately go out of my way to make everything I do overly cutesy.

1707
01:32:47,910 --> 01:32:48,470
Ooh...

1708
01:32:49,330 --> 01:32:49,890
Um...

1709
01:32:49,890 --> 01:32:51,130
Is everyone okay?

1710
01:32:52,430 --> 01:32:53,670
Well, you know what?

1711
01:32:54,010 --> 01:32:56,230
I wasn't the one whose boobs magically grew...

1712
01:32:58,050 --> 01:32:58,650
What?!

1713
01:32:58,650 --> 01:32:59,230
Natsuki!

1714
01:33:01,730 --> 01:33:03,450
I'm Natsuki that...

1715
01:33:03,450 --> 01:33:05,230
That's a little...

1716
01:33:06,750 --> 01:33:08,050
This doesn't involve you!

1717
01:33:08,810 --> 01:33:10,390
I don't like fighting, guys!

1718
01:33:11,650 --> 01:33:15,870
Suddenly, both girls turned towards me, as if they just noticed I was standing there.

1719
01:33:16,930 --> 01:33:17,290
Oliver!

1720
01:33:18,490 --> 01:33:18,850
She...

1721
01:33:18,850 --> 01:33:20,450
She's just trying to make me look bad!

1722
01:33:21,430 --> 01:33:22,310
That's not true!

1723
01:33:23,370 --> 01:33:24,410
She started it!

1724
01:33:25,190 --> 01:33:29,230
If she could get over herself and learn to appreciate that simple writing is more effective...

1725
01:33:29,690 --> 01:33:31,450
Then this wouldn't have happened in the first place!

1726
01:33:32,230 --> 01:33:34,790
What's the point in making your poems all convoluted for no reason?

1727
01:33:35,530 --> 01:33:38,390
The meaning should jump out at the reader, not force them to have to figure it out!

1728
01:33:39,050 --> 01:33:40,610
Help me explain that to her, Oliver!

1729
01:33:41,770 --> 01:33:42,130
But...

1730
01:33:42,130 --> 01:33:42,470
Wait!

1731
01:33:43,310 --> 01:33:45,770
There's a reason we have so many deep and expressive...

1732
01:33:45,770 --> 01:33:46,870
words in our language!

1733
01:33:47,470 --> 01:33:50,190
It's the only way to convey complex feelings...

1734
01:33:50,190 --> 01:33:51,810
And meaning them most effectively!

1735
01:33:52,910 --> 01:33:56,010
Avoiding them is not only unnecessarily limiting yourself...

1736
01:33:56,010 --> 01:33:57,630
It's also a waste!

1737
01:33:58,350 --> 01:33:59,810
You understand that, right, Oliver?

1738
01:34:02,430 --> 01:34:02,910
Um...

1739
01:34:03,810 --> 01:34:04,290
Well...

1740
01:34:06,810 --> 01:34:09,050
How did I get dragged into this in the first place?

1741
01:34:09,770 --> 01:34:12,050
It's not like I know anything about writing...

1742
01:34:12,570 --> 01:34:15,350
But whomever I agree with, they'll probably think more highly of me!

1743
01:34:17,110 --> 01:34:18,650
That's what's going through my mind!

1744
01:34:19,570 --> 01:34:21,510
So of course that's gonna be...

1745
01:34:26,100 --> 01:34:26,580
Natsuki!

1746
01:34:28,120 --> 01:34:31,580
Natsuki glares at me, drying up any words I had in my mouth.

1747
01:34:32,080 --> 01:34:33,560
So instead I turn to Yuri.

1748
01:34:34,880 --> 01:34:35,360
Yuri!

1749
01:34:37,780 --> 01:34:41,800
But Yuri's expression is so defenseless that I can't bring myself to say anything to her.

1750
01:34:45,370 --> 01:34:45,850
Sayori?

1751
01:34:48,030 --> 01:34:48,590
Eh?

1752
01:34:49,810 --> 01:34:50,370
Yeah?

1753
01:34:52,270 --> 01:34:54,910
Everyone's fighting is making Sayori uncomfortable!

1754
01:34:56,630 --> 01:35:00,850
How can the two of you keep fighting when you know you're making your friend feel like this?

1755
01:35:02,230 --> 01:35:02,790
Oliver!

1756
01:35:04,450 --> 01:35:05,790
Well, that's her problem!

1757
01:35:05,910 --> 01:35:06,650
This isn't about her!

1758
01:35:07,550 --> 01:35:07,930
I...

1759
01:35:07,930 --> 01:35:08,770
I agree.

1760
01:35:09,370 --> 01:35:12,850
It's unfair for others to interject their own feelings into our conflict.

1761
01:35:13,750 --> 01:35:14,270
Yeah!

1762
01:35:14,530 --> 01:35:17,270
Unless Sayori wants to tell Yuri what a stuck-up jerk she's being!

1763
01:35:18,630 --> 01:35:19,690
She would never!

1764
01:35:20,270 --> 01:35:23,050
It's your immaturity that's made her upset in the first place!

1765
01:35:24,110 --> 01:35:25,150
Excuse me?!

1766
01:35:25,150 --> 01:35:27,050
Are you listening to yourself?

1767
01:35:27,630 --> 01:35:29,290
This is exactly why...

1768
01:35:29,290 --> 01:35:31,190
Exactly why nobody likes...

1769
01:35:31,190 --> 01:35:31,810
Stop!

1770
01:35:36,050 --> 01:35:36,970
Natsuki...

1771
01:35:36,970 --> 01:35:37,590
Yuri...

1772
01:35:38,490 --> 01:35:40,030
You guys...

1773
01:35:40,030 --> 01:35:40,990
Are my friends.

1774
01:35:41,870 --> 01:35:42,530
I...

1775
01:35:42,530 --> 01:35:45,770
I just want everyone to get along and be happy!

1776
01:35:46,950 --> 01:35:49,050
My friends are wonderful people!

1777
01:35:49,470 --> 01:35:51,650
And I love them because of their differences!

1778
01:35:53,310 --> 01:35:54,510
Natsuki's poems...

1779
01:35:54,510 --> 01:35:55,630
They're amazing!

1780
01:35:55,930 --> 01:35:58,590
Because they gave you so many feelings with just a few words!

1781
01:35:59,290 --> 01:36:01,010
And Yuri's poems are amazing!

1782
01:36:01,150 --> 01:36:03,130
Because they paint beautiful pictures in your head!

1783
01:36:04,390 --> 01:36:05,750
Everyone's so talented!

1784
01:36:06,430 --> 01:36:07,770
So why are we fighting?

1785
01:36:09,270 --> 01:36:10,470
B-because...

1786
01:36:11,510 --> 01:36:12,110
Well...

1787
01:36:12,110 --> 01:36:13,130
Also...

1788
01:36:13,810 --> 01:36:15,650
Natsuki's cute and there's nothing wrong!

1789
01:36:15,770 --> 01:36:16,410
Look at that!

1790
01:36:17,310 --> 01:36:20,170
And Yuri's boobs are the same as they always were!

1791
01:36:21,570 --> 01:36:22,870
Big and beautiful!

1792
01:36:24,790 --> 01:36:26,910
What the fuck?!

1793
01:36:28,410 --> 01:36:29,310
Say, Yuri!

1794
01:36:30,270 --> 01:36:31,930
She stands up triumphantly.

1795
01:36:32,810 --> 01:36:35,370
Monica stands behind her with a bewildered expression.

1796
01:36:36,530 --> 01:36:37,670
I'll make some tea.

1797
01:36:38,450 --> 01:36:39,650
She rushes off.

1798
01:36:40,710 --> 01:36:44,010
Natsuki sits down with a blank expression on her face, staring at nothing.

1799
01:36:44,970 --> 01:36:47,430
So this is why Sayori is vice president.

1800
01:36:48,670 --> 01:36:50,050
I whisper to Monica.

1801
01:36:50,810 --> 01:36:51,950
She nods in return.

1802
01:36:53,130 --> 01:36:59,050
To be honest, I might come off as a good leader, but I can organize things.

1803
01:37:00,070 --> 01:37:01,830
But I'm not very good with people.

1804
01:37:02,390 --> 01:37:04,310
I couldn't even bring myself to interject.

1805
01:37:05,130 --> 01:37:08,330
As president, that's kind of embarrassing for me.

1806
01:37:09,210 --> 01:37:10,050
Ha-ha-ha.

1807
01:37:10,910 --> 01:37:11,210
Nah.

1808
01:37:12,010 --> 01:37:13,490
It's not like I can blame you.

1809
01:37:14,050 --> 01:37:15,730
I wasn't able to say anything either.

1810
01:37:17,110 --> 01:37:17,570
Well...

1811
01:37:18,270 --> 01:37:21,010
I guess that just means Sayori is amazing in her own ways.

1812
01:37:21,010 --> 01:37:22,250
I guess that she...

1813
01:37:22,750 --> 01:37:23,910
You could say that.

1814
01:37:25,050 --> 01:37:29,670
She might be an airhead, but sometimes it's weirdly suspicious that she knows exactly what she's doing.

1815
01:37:30,550 --> 01:37:31,110
I see.

1816
01:37:31,510 --> 01:37:32,930
I hate this pose.

1817
01:37:34,030 --> 01:37:35,290
Take good care of her, okay?

1818
01:37:36,330 --> 01:37:37,970
I would hate to see her get herself hurt.

1819
01:37:38,750 --> 01:37:40,030
That makes two of us.

1820
01:37:40,590 --> 01:37:42,050
You can count on me!

1821
01:37:43,770 --> 01:37:47,310
Monica smiles sweetly at me, causing my stomach to knot.

1822
01:37:48,270 --> 01:37:52,870
Such a genuine person really does make a good president, regardless of what she says.

1823
01:37:53,730 --> 01:37:56,170
If only I could get the chance to talk to her a little more.

1824
01:37:58,830 --> 01:37:59,970
Are you for real?

1825
01:38:00,790 --> 01:38:03,770
Have we not been fucking talking constantly?

1826
01:38:05,470 --> 01:38:06,430
Okay, everyone!

1827
01:38:07,950 --> 01:38:09,770
It's just about time for us to leave.

1828
01:38:10,850 --> 01:38:12,550
How did you all feel about sharing poems?

1829
01:38:13,970 --> 01:38:15,110
It was a lot of fun.

1830
01:38:16,030 --> 01:38:17,650
Well, I'd say it was worth it.

1831
01:38:18,450 --> 01:38:19,210
It was alright.

1832
01:38:19,510 --> 01:38:20,710
Well, mostly.

1833
01:38:21,810 --> 01:38:22,250
Oliver?

1834
01:38:22,630 --> 01:38:23,410
How about you?

1835
01:38:24,610 --> 01:38:25,890
Yeah, I'd say the same.

1836
01:38:26,550 --> 01:38:28,950
It was a neat thing to talk about with everyone.

1837
01:38:30,210 --> 01:38:30,650
Awesome!

1838
01:38:31,230 --> 01:38:33,110
In that case, we'll do the same thing tomorrow.

1839
01:38:33,910 --> 01:38:35,950
And maybe you'll learn something from your friends, too!

1840
01:38:36,730 --> 01:38:38,910
So your poems will turn out even better!

1841
01:38:40,570 --> 01:38:41,330
God help me.

1842
01:38:41,850 --> 01:38:43,030
I think to myself.

1843
01:38:43,450 --> 01:38:43,850
Exactly!

1844
01:38:44,470 --> 01:38:47,450
I did learn a little more about the kinds of poems everyone likes.

1845
01:38:48,390 --> 01:38:52,310
With any luck, that means I can at least do a better job impressing those I want to impress.

1846
01:38:53,810 --> 01:38:56,050
I nod to myself with newfound determination.

1847
01:38:56,390 --> 01:38:56,590
Oh!

1848
01:38:57,150 --> 01:38:58,030
Is that a reference?

1849
01:38:59,310 --> 01:38:59,710
Oliver!

1850
01:39:01,430 --> 01:39:02,330
Ready to walk home?

1851
01:39:03,410 --> 01:39:03,890
Sure!

1852
01:39:04,530 --> 01:39:04,990
Sure!

1853
01:39:04,990 --> 01:39:05,390
Let's go!

1854
01:39:08,050 --> 01:39:09,490
Sayori beams at me!

1855
01:39:10,210 --> 01:39:13,870
It truly has been a while since Sayori and I have spent this much time together.

1856
01:39:14,130 --> 01:39:17,450
Why does it feel like these four girls want to get into my pants?!

1857
01:39:20,270 --> 01:39:22,350
I can't really say I'm not enjoying it either.

1858
01:39:22,990 --> 01:39:24,070
Haha, that was perfect!

1859
01:39:26,910 --> 01:39:27,470
Sayori!

1860
01:39:28,390 --> 01:39:29,790
How about what happened earlier?

1861
01:39:30,870 --> 01:39:31,170
Eh?

1862
01:39:31,670 --> 01:39:32,510
What do you mean?

1863
01:39:33,110 --> 01:39:35,670
You know, between Yuri and Natsuki.

1864
01:39:36,450 --> 01:39:38,150
Does that kind of thing happen often?

1865
01:39:39,110 --> 01:39:39,870
No, no, no.

1866
01:39:40,650 --> 01:39:43,130
That's really the first time I've seen them fight like that.

1867
01:39:43,830 --> 01:39:45,950
I promise they're both wonderful people.

1868
01:39:46,530 --> 01:39:47,230
You don't...

1869
01:39:47,230 --> 01:39:48,430
You don't hate them, do you?

1870
01:39:49,910 --> 01:39:51,150
No, I don't hate them.

1871
01:39:52,170 --> 01:39:53,710
I just wanted your opinion, that's all.

1872
01:39:54,790 --> 01:39:56,570
I can see why they'd make good friends with you.

1873
01:39:58,370 --> 01:40:02,450
You know, Oliver, it's nice that I got to spend time with you in the club.

1874
01:40:03,850 --> 01:40:06,630
But I think seeing you get along with everyone is what makes me the happiest.

1875
01:40:07,450 --> 01:40:09,190
And I think everyone really likes you too.

1876
01:40:11,570 --> 01:40:11,930
That's...

1877
01:40:13,170 --> 01:40:15,010
Every day is going to be so much fun!

1878
01:40:16,190 --> 01:40:17,810
Oh, what did I do?

1879
01:40:18,830 --> 01:40:22,070
It looks like Sayori still hasn't caught on to the kind of situation I'm in.

1880
01:40:23,010 --> 01:40:25,510
Sure, being friends with everyone is nice, but...

1881
01:40:26,070 --> 01:40:27,750
Does it really need to stop there?

1882
01:40:28,530 --> 01:40:28,970
What?

1883
01:40:30,490 --> 01:40:32,510
We'll just have to see what the future holds, Sayori.

1884
01:40:33,930 --> 01:40:35,810
I pat Sayori on the shoulders.

1885
01:40:37,390 --> 01:40:38,390
What, is she a dog?

1886
01:40:39,250 --> 01:40:44,030
I said that more to myself than to her, but it's easy to use Sayori as an internal monologue sometimes.

1887
01:40:44,970 --> 01:40:45,570
Okay.

1888
01:40:46,170 --> 01:40:46,610
Yeah.

1889
01:40:47,310 --> 01:40:48,230
Let's do this.

1890
01:40:52,250 --> 01:40:53,330
Oh, not...

1891
01:40:53,330 --> 01:40:54,010
Okay, hold on.

1892
01:40:54,690 --> 01:40:56,050
What is actually happening?

1893
01:40:56,810 --> 01:40:58,670
I need to understand this system here.

1894
01:40:59,170 --> 01:40:59,570
Hold on.

1895
01:41:06,920 --> 01:41:08,200
Why are there only three?

1896
01:41:09,200 --> 01:41:10,320
Shouldn't there be four?

1897
01:41:10,320 --> 01:41:11,360
This is Yuri?

1898
01:41:11,760 --> 01:41:17,400
This is Sayori and Natsuki?

1899
01:41:19,220 --> 01:41:20,360
Who's missing?

1900
01:41:21,280 --> 01:41:21,980
Wait.

1901
01:41:22,480 --> 01:41:23,440
What did I say?

1902
01:41:24,320 --> 01:41:24,760
Yuri.

1903
01:41:25,640 --> 01:41:26,520
Sayori.

1904
01:41:26,680 --> 01:41:27,140
Natsuki.

1905
01:41:27,300 --> 01:41:27,860
There's someone else.

1906
01:41:28,360 --> 01:41:28,900
Uh, Monica.

1907
01:41:29,360 --> 01:41:30,580
Why is she not there?

1908
01:41:33,080 --> 01:41:34,640
Fucking graveyard, bro.

1909
01:41:35,880 --> 01:41:36,480
Sadness.

1910
01:41:39,020 --> 01:41:39,620
Wait.

1911
01:41:41,800 --> 01:41:43,600
What does Doki Doki even mean?

1912
01:41:44,740 --> 01:41:45,340
Empty.

1913
01:41:47,280 --> 01:41:48,840
Wait, they jump when I click.

1914
01:41:50,200 --> 01:41:50,800
Depression.

1915
01:41:51,980 --> 01:41:54,880
Wait, wait, is she into, like, sad shit?

1916
01:41:57,900 --> 01:41:59,040
Is that what's happening?

1917
01:42:01,540 --> 01:42:02,020
Disarray.

1918
01:42:03,860 --> 01:42:05,300
Yuri jumped at that one.

1919
01:42:06,820 --> 01:42:07,300
Disaster.

1920
01:42:11,070 --> 01:42:11,550
Wait.

1921
01:42:13,670 --> 01:42:14,150
Massacre.

1922
01:42:15,330 --> 01:42:16,150
Hey, look!

1923
01:42:16,470 --> 01:42:17,510
We have her number.

1924
01:42:19,490 --> 01:42:21,530
I have a direct line to her mind.

1925
01:42:22,090 --> 01:42:23,490
I guess it would be Whirlwind.

1926
01:42:27,090 --> 01:42:29,190
Let me try and pick as many of the same as I can.

1927
01:42:31,170 --> 01:42:33,510
What would the chaotic thing be here?

1928
01:42:33,750 --> 01:42:34,130
Fickle.

1929
01:42:34,490 --> 01:42:34,950
Yeah!

1930
01:42:42,830 --> 01:42:43,320
Twirl?

1931
01:42:43,720 --> 01:42:44,480
Fuck!

1932
01:42:49,330 --> 01:42:50,150
Infinite.

1933
01:42:50,690 --> 01:42:51,310
Yes!

1934
01:42:52,110 --> 01:42:52,550
Entropy.

1935
01:42:56,410 --> 01:42:57,220
I don't know.

1936
01:43:00,860 --> 01:43:01,420
Grief?

1937
01:43:01,540 --> 01:43:01,700
No.

1938
01:43:03,720 --> 01:43:04,560
Determination?

1939
01:43:05,300 --> 01:43:05,940
Hey!

1940
01:43:06,140 --> 01:43:06,440
There we go.

1941
01:43:10,710 --> 01:43:11,690
Uh...

1942
01:43:11,690 --> 01:43:12,650
Infallible?

1943
01:43:12,870 --> 01:43:13,430
No variance.

1944
01:43:14,130 --> 01:43:14,450
Yeah!

1945
01:43:15,810 --> 01:43:16,830
Wait a minute.

1946
01:43:17,190 --> 01:43:19,110
Maybe they have multiple words they like each.

1947
01:43:20,210 --> 01:43:21,070
Yeah, probably.

1948
01:43:21,650 --> 01:43:22,800
I would say...

1949
01:43:24,930 --> 01:43:25,710
Aura.

1950
01:43:26,190 --> 01:43:27,610
Oh my god, I'm good.

1951
01:43:30,440 --> 01:43:31,220
Spinning.

1952
01:43:31,640 --> 01:43:32,260
Fuck!

1953
01:43:33,340 --> 01:43:34,680
I'm bad at this.

1954
01:43:36,040 --> 01:43:36,660
Analysis.

1955
01:43:38,040 --> 01:43:38,820
Starscape.

1956
01:43:40,400 --> 01:43:40,960
Incongruent.

1957
01:43:41,640 --> 01:43:42,200
Uh...

1958
01:43:42,200 --> 01:43:43,100
Incongruent.

1959
01:43:46,590 --> 01:43:47,430
Last one.

1960
01:43:51,040 --> 01:43:51,600
Uh...

1961
01:43:53,980 --> 01:43:54,960
Uh...

1962
01:43:55,700 --> 01:43:56,260
No!

1963
01:44:01,460 --> 01:44:02,740
Okay, let's see...

1964
01:44:02,740 --> 01:44:03,480
Okay, so hold on a minute.

1965
01:44:05,460 --> 01:44:07,040
I think I understand what's happening.

1966
01:44:08,100 --> 01:44:08,920
I think I...

1967
01:44:08,920 --> 01:44:09,960
I understand this game.

1968
01:44:10,140 --> 01:44:11,120
And it's super dumb.

1969
01:44:12,040 --> 01:44:13,180
But here's what it is, okay?

1970
01:44:15,840 --> 01:44:16,240
Basically...

1971
01:44:17,420 --> 01:44:19,860
I am trying to get into one of these girls' pants.

1972
01:44:20,500 --> 01:44:21,700
That's what this game is.

1973
01:44:22,120 --> 01:44:24,560
It sounds dumb as fuck, but that's what it is.

1974
01:44:25,270 --> 01:44:29,800
And the way to do that is to write poems that impress the girl in question.

1975
01:44:31,720 --> 01:44:32,740
That's all it is!

1976
01:44:33,320 --> 01:44:36,380
You show up the next day like, Look at what I made!

1977
01:44:37,280 --> 01:44:39,760
And she'll be like, You know?

1978
01:44:41,500 --> 01:44:44,060
Another day passes, and it's time for the club meeting already.

1979
01:44:44,640 --> 01:44:50,920
Let's see if anything has changed now that we have picked a lot of the same words that made Yuri jump.

1980
01:44:52,020 --> 01:44:55,100
Also, I can't not think of Yuri from Red Alert when I read her name.

1981
01:44:55,420 --> 01:44:59,380
I'm thinking of the bald Russian guy with the mind control every time I see her.

1982
01:44:59,920 --> 01:45:02,740
I've gotten a little more comfortable here over the past couple days.

1983
01:45:04,060 --> 01:45:06,360
Entering the club room, the usual scene greets me.

1984
01:45:07,760 --> 01:45:08,200
Christ.

1985
01:45:09,520 --> 01:45:10,220
Hi, Oliver.

1986
01:45:11,500 --> 01:45:12,380
Yo, Sayori!

1987
01:45:13,160 --> 01:45:15,000
Looks like you're in a good mood today!

1988
01:45:17,260 --> 01:45:19,640
I'm just still not used to you being in the club, that's all.

1989
01:45:20,480 --> 01:45:21,100
I see.

1990
01:45:22,060 --> 01:45:24,400
That's a pretty simple thing to get you in a good mood.

1991
01:45:25,240 --> 01:45:27,680
But I guess it's always the simple things with you anyway.

1992
01:45:27,680 --> 01:45:29,220
Did I just call her simple?

1993
01:45:30,680 --> 01:45:33,220
Speaking of which, I'm kinda hungry.

1994
01:45:33,960 --> 01:45:35,660
Will you come with me to buy a snack?

1995
01:45:36,780 --> 01:45:37,660
No thanks.

1996
01:45:37,900 --> 01:45:39,160
What do you mean, I would love a snack?

1997
01:45:42,290 --> 01:45:42,770
Eh?

1998
01:45:43,830 --> 01:45:45,330
That's not like you at all!

1999
01:45:46,490 --> 01:45:47,990
I have my reasons.

2000
01:45:49,510 --> 01:45:51,670
Why don't we take a look at your purse, Sayori?

2001
01:45:52,070 --> 01:45:52,410
Huh?

2002
01:45:53,930 --> 01:45:54,410
Eh?

2003
01:45:55,590 --> 01:45:57,170
Why that all of a sudden?

2004
01:45:57,970 --> 01:45:59,130
No reason, really.

2005
01:45:59,930 --> 01:46:01,450
I just wanted to look at it.

2006
01:46:02,070 --> 01:46:03,450
Bro, what the fuck?

2007
01:46:04,230 --> 01:46:05,310
Am I saying?

2008
01:46:07,270 --> 01:46:07,870
Uh...

2009
01:46:07,870 --> 01:46:10,330
Sayori nervously retrieves her coin purse.

2010
01:46:10,970 --> 01:46:13,190
She fumbles with the latch and gets it open.

2011
01:46:13,910 --> 01:46:17,450
Then she turns it upside down and lets its contents spill onto the desk.

2012
01:46:18,890 --> 01:46:20,670
Only two small coins fall out.

2013
01:46:22,870 --> 01:46:23,990
I knew it.

2014
01:46:24,810 --> 01:46:26,910
I can see right through you, Sayori.

2015
01:46:27,050 --> 01:46:28,830
Oh, she wanted me to buy her lunch!

2016
01:46:29,930 --> 01:46:30,650
Fucking hell.

2017
01:46:32,030 --> 01:46:32,910
That's not fair.

2018
01:46:33,850 --> 01:46:34,730
How did you know?

2019
01:46:35,570 --> 01:46:36,330
It's simple.

2020
01:46:37,010 --> 01:46:41,610
If you had enough money in the first place, you would have brought a snack before coming to the club room.

2021
01:46:42,070 --> 01:46:42,410
Right.

2022
01:46:43,230 --> 01:46:52,550
So either you're not hungry and wanted an excuse to take a walk, or you plan to conveniently forget that you spent all your money, so that I would lend you some.

2023
01:46:53,350 --> 01:46:54,590
But there's one more thing.

2024
01:46:55,530 --> 01:46:56,790
YOU'RE ALWAYS HUNGRY!

2025
01:46:57,470 --> 01:46:59,250
And so that only leaves one option.

2026
01:47:01,650 --> 01:47:02,490
I give up.

2027
01:47:03,270 --> 01:47:04,510
Don't make me feel guilty!

2028
01:47:05,750 --> 01:47:08,330
If you feel guilty, that means you deserve to feel guilty.

2029
01:47:11,390 --> 01:47:12,850
Yuri suddenly giggles.

2030
01:47:14,050 --> 01:47:14,310
Eh?

2031
01:47:15,370 --> 01:47:17,330
I didn't notice that she was listening in.

2032
01:47:18,010 --> 01:47:19,930
Her face is in her book, as always.

2033
01:47:21,010 --> 01:47:21,930
Ah, ah.

2034
01:47:22,590 --> 01:47:24,230
I wasn't listening or anything.

2035
01:47:24,970 --> 01:47:27,050
It was just something in my book.

2036
01:47:28,210 --> 01:47:28,710
Yuri!

2037
01:47:29,870 --> 01:47:31,670
Tell Oliver to let me borrow some money!

2038
01:47:33,270 --> 01:47:33,810
That's...

2039
01:47:33,810 --> 01:47:35,650
Don't get me involved like that, Sayori.

2040
01:47:37,230 --> 01:47:40,330
Besides, you should only buy what you can responsibly afford.

2041
01:47:40,930 --> 01:47:46,050
And frankly, after pulling a mischievous little stunt like that, your suffering is fair enough retribution.

2042
01:47:46,970 --> 01:47:48,210
Damn right.

2043
01:47:49,050 --> 01:47:49,630
Ah!

2044
01:47:50,310 --> 01:47:51,270
Did I just...

2045
01:47:51,650 --> 01:47:52,330
I...

2046
01:47:52,330 --> 01:47:53,570
I didn't mean that...

2047
01:47:53,570 --> 01:47:55,730
I got too absorbed into my book.

2048
01:47:58,850 --> 01:48:00,730
I really like when you speak your mind, Yuri.

2049
01:48:01,370 --> 01:48:03,430
It doesn't happen much, but it's a fun side of you.

2050
01:48:04,810 --> 01:48:05,170
That's...

2051
01:48:05,170 --> 01:48:06,690
There's no way you could think that.

2052
01:48:07,610 --> 01:48:08,330
You were right, though.

2053
01:48:08,900 --> 01:48:11,470
I did something bad, and now I have to accept the revolution.

2054
01:48:12,310 --> 01:48:12,890
What?

2055
01:48:14,650 --> 01:48:15,230
Retribution.

2056
01:48:16,350 --> 01:48:16,790
That!

2057
01:48:17,690 --> 01:48:19,450
Still, coming from you, Sayori.

2058
01:48:20,550 --> 01:48:22,930
I guess there's a little devil inside all of us, isn't there?

2059
01:48:25,490 --> 01:48:26,750
Don't let her fool you.

2060
01:48:27,850 --> 01:48:29,590
Sayori knows exactly what she's doing.

2061
01:48:30,710 --> 01:48:34,110
After all, she told you guys she was bringing me to the club before she even told me.

2062
01:48:35,690 --> 01:48:36,090
But...

2063
01:48:36,510 --> 01:48:38,410
You wouldn't have come if it weren't for the cupcakes!

2064
01:48:39,370 --> 01:48:41,270
So I had to trick Natsuki into making them.

2065
01:48:42,310 --> 01:48:42,950
Come on.

2066
01:48:43,370 --> 01:48:44,930
Give me more credit than that, Sayori.

2067
01:48:45,730 --> 01:48:46,370
Sayori.

2068
01:48:46,750 --> 01:48:51,030
Out of nowhere, something smacks Sayori in the face and tumbles onto the desk.

2069
01:48:53,090 --> 01:48:54,530
Fucking hell, what was that?

2070
01:48:56,370 --> 01:48:58,070
What the fuck is happening?

2071
01:49:08,120 --> 01:49:11,080
Sure enough, it's a giant cookie wrapped in plastic.

2072
01:49:11,860 --> 01:49:13,360
Sayori glances around.

2073
01:49:14,460 --> 01:49:16,480
Is this a miracle?

2074
01:49:17,880 --> 01:49:19,620
It's because I paid my restitution!

2075
01:49:21,400 --> 01:49:22,040
Retribution.

2076
01:49:23,200 --> 01:49:24,700
Actually, that one almost worked.

2077
01:49:27,060 --> 01:49:29,040
I was just gonna give it to you.

2078
01:49:29,340 --> 01:49:31,420
But then I heard you blab about the cupcakes.

2079
01:49:32,020 --> 01:49:34,180
I was totally worth seeing your reaction though.

2080
01:49:34,500 --> 01:49:34,600
Haha!

2081
01:49:35,820 --> 01:49:36,380
Natsuki!

2082
01:49:37,320 --> 01:49:38,480
That's so nice of you.

2083
01:49:39,260 --> 01:49:40,400
I'm so happy!

2084
01:49:41,480 --> 01:49:42,740
Sayori hugs the cookie.

2085
01:49:44,540 --> 01:49:46,280
She hugs the cookie?!

2086
01:49:47,160 --> 01:49:48,560
Fuck on out of here!

2087
01:49:49,360 --> 01:49:50,340
That's stupid.

2088
01:49:51,680 --> 01:49:54,980
Sayori rapidly tears open the wrapper and takes a big bite.

2089
01:49:56,980 --> 01:49:59,640
God, this is testing me.

2090
01:50:00,980 --> 01:50:02,440
So good!

2091
01:50:04,840 --> 01:50:07,800
Sayori suddenly clasps her hands over her mouth.

2092
01:50:08,680 --> 01:50:10,180
I bit my tongue!

2093
01:50:12,400 --> 01:50:14,300
You're going through a lot over just one cookie!

2094
01:50:15,560 --> 01:50:18,280
Like, I- Can we stop talking about this cookie?!

2095
01:50:19,960 --> 01:50:20,480
Natsuki!

2096
01:50:20,480 --> 01:50:22,080
Natsuki takes a bite of her own cookie.

2097
01:50:23,540 --> 01:50:24,060
Ah!

2098
01:50:25,140 --> 01:50:27,040
Yours looked really good too, Natsuki.

2099
01:50:28,020 --> 01:50:34,240
Am I the only one who's seeing this weird sexual, you know, overtone with these words here?

2100
01:50:35,640 --> 01:50:36,740
Can I try it?

2101
01:50:37,980 --> 01:50:38,260
Can I try it?

2102
01:50:38,260 --> 01:50:38,400
Jeez!

2103
01:50:39,920 --> 01:50:41,140
Beggars can't be choosers!

2104
01:50:42,200 --> 01:50:43,540
But yours is chocolate!

2105
01:50:44,800 --> 01:50:46,740
Yeah, why do you think I gave you that one?

2106
01:50:47,720 --> 01:50:48,200
Fine.

2107
01:50:49,400 --> 01:50:51,560
Still, I'm really happy that you shared this one with me.

2108
01:50:52,200 --> 01:50:52,880
Heh heh heh!

2109
01:50:53,560 --> 01:50:57,440
Sayori gets out of her seat and goes behind Natsuki, then wraps her arms around her.

2110
01:50:58,520 --> 01:50:59,040
Ah!

2111
01:50:59,580 --> 01:51:00,100
Jeez!

2112
01:51:00,640 --> 01:51:02,080
I get it, I get it!

2113
01:51:03,140 --> 01:51:06,580
Cookie's still in hand, Natsuki reaches up to nudge Sayori off of her.

2114
01:51:07,760 --> 01:51:07,920
Oh!

2115
01:51:09,240 --> 01:51:14,260
Sayori suddenly leans down and takes a bite out of- We're done.

2116
01:51:15,540 --> 01:51:16,980
I am so close, dude.

2117
01:51:19,280 --> 01:51:20,300
Oh my god.

2118
01:51:26,220 --> 01:51:28,020
Oh my god, this is so rough.

2119
01:51:31,200 --> 01:51:34,660
This has to be some of the worst writing I've ever seen in a video game.

2120
01:51:37,240 --> 01:51:42,140
Maybe eclipsed by some of the writing in a certain village in a certain game of the year.

2121
01:51:42,740 --> 01:51:47,400
Other than that, Sayori suddenly leans down and takes a bite out of Natsuki's cookie.

2122
01:51:48,260 --> 01:51:48,760
Hey!

2123
01:51:49,860 --> 01:51:51,280
Did you seriously just do that?

2124
01:51:53,820 --> 01:51:56,560
Mouth full, Sayori trots away to safety.

2125
01:51:57,880 --> 01:51:59,240
Yuri and I laugh as well.

2126
01:52:00,020 --> 01:52:00,500
Jeez!

2127
01:52:00,900 --> 01:52:02,320
You're such a kid sometimes!

2128
01:52:03,220 --> 01:52:03,800
Monika!

2129
01:52:04,060 --> 01:52:05,000
Can you tell Sayori?

2130
01:52:05,640 --> 01:52:06,040
Eh?

2131
01:52:07,040 --> 01:52:08,540
Natsuki glances around.

2132
01:52:09,140 --> 01:52:10,920
Monika isn't in the club room.

2133
01:52:11,800 --> 01:52:13,240
Where the hell is Monika?

2134
01:52:16,500 --> 01:52:20,550
Like, I'm waiting for the fucking shoe to drop here, like...

2135
01:52:27,510 --> 01:52:32,230
I was expecting something because the game even had like a content warning at the beginning.

2136
01:52:32,950 --> 01:52:33,910
And I was like, whatever.

2137
01:52:34,710 --> 01:52:35,450
What do you mean?

2138
01:52:35,550 --> 01:52:36,370
Is it because of sex?

2139
01:52:36,570 --> 01:52:37,430
Like sexual...

2140
01:52:37,430 --> 01:52:38,550
They said boobs or what?

2141
01:52:38,630 --> 01:52:39,090
Is that it?

2142
01:52:39,330 --> 01:52:41,010
Is that what people are going...

2143
01:52:41,010 --> 01:52:42,470
They said boobs in the video game.

2144
01:52:42,550 --> 01:52:42,950
Is that it?

2145
01:52:44,090 --> 01:52:45,050
I fucking hope not.

2146
01:52:45,930 --> 01:52:46,870
Where's Monika anyway?

2147
01:52:47,690 --> 01:52:48,690
Good question.

2148
01:52:49,590 --> 01:52:51,890
Have any of you heard anything about her being late today?

2149
01:52:52,710 --> 01:52:53,490
Not me!

2150
01:52:54,230 --> 01:52:55,310
Yeah, I haven't either.

2151
01:52:56,350 --> 01:52:56,450
Hmm.

2152
01:52:57,410 --> 01:52:58,270
That's a bit unusual.

2153
01:52:59,110 --> 01:53:00,190
I hope she's okay.

2154
01:53:01,250 --> 01:53:02,450
Of course she's okay.

2155
01:53:03,210 --> 01:53:04,870
She probably just had something to do today.

2156
01:53:05,550 --> 01:53:07,170
She's pretty popular after all.

2157
01:53:07,970 --> 01:53:08,270
Eh?

2158
01:53:08,850 --> 01:53:09,870
You don't think she...

2159
01:53:10,750 --> 01:53:11,710
She has a...

2160
01:53:12,770 --> 01:53:14,210
I wouldn't be surprised.

2161
01:53:15,010 --> 01:53:17,290
She's probably more desirable than all of us combined.

2162
01:53:18,610 --> 01:53:19,550
That's true.

2163
01:53:20,550 --> 01:53:21,290
Excuse me?

2164
01:53:23,090 --> 01:53:24,770
Suddenly the door swings open.

2165
01:53:25,970 --> 01:53:26,390
Sorry!

2166
01:53:26,730 --> 01:53:27,790
I'm super sorry!

2167
01:53:28,890 --> 01:53:28,990
Ah!

2168
01:53:29,490 --> 01:53:30,290
There you are.

2169
01:53:30,750 --> 01:53:32,210
I didn't mean to be late.

2170
01:53:32,890 --> 01:53:34,670
I hope you guys weren't worried or anything.

2171
01:53:35,990 --> 01:53:36,590
Ugh.

2172
01:53:37,570 --> 01:53:38,150
Eh?

2173
01:53:39,750 --> 01:53:42,150
Monica chose the club over her boyfriend after all.

2174
01:53:43,130 --> 01:53:43,210
Yeah.

2175
01:53:46,220 --> 01:53:47,500
You're so strong-willed.

2176
01:53:48,080 --> 01:53:48,720
I'm a...

2177
01:53:48,720 --> 01:53:49,300
I need to.

2178
01:53:49,420 --> 01:53:50,460
I need to take a moment here.

2179
01:54:00,940 --> 01:54:03,260
I feel like I should have had something else to drink in this.

2180
01:54:07,810 --> 01:54:08,510
Okay, here we go.

2181
01:54:08,950 --> 01:54:09,710
Fucking lock in.

2182
01:54:10,010 --> 01:54:10,870
We're doing this.

2183
01:54:14,670 --> 01:54:16,550
What on earth are you talking about?

2184
01:54:17,890 --> 01:54:19,630
Monica quizzically glances at me.

2185
01:54:21,050 --> 01:54:22,310
Ah, never mind that.

2186
01:54:24,150 --> 01:54:24,970
What held you up anyway?

2187
01:54:27,290 --> 01:54:27,770
Uh...

2188
01:54:28,310 --> 01:54:30,190
Well, my last period today was study hall.

2189
01:54:31,230 --> 01:54:33,810
To be honest, I kinda just lost track of time.

2190
01:54:35,950 --> 01:54:37,390
That makes no sense though.

2191
01:54:38,070 --> 01:54:40,410
You would have heard the bell ring at least.

2192
01:54:41,290 --> 01:54:44,530
I must have not heard it since I was practicing piano.

2193
01:54:46,450 --> 01:54:46,930
Piano?

2194
01:54:48,150 --> 01:54:50,510
I wasn't aware you played music as well, Monica.

2195
01:54:51,710 --> 01:54:53,090
I don't really.

2196
01:54:53,650 --> 01:54:55,950
I kinda just started recently.

2197
01:54:56,930 --> 01:54:58,610
I've always wanted to learn piano.

2198
01:55:00,170 --> 01:55:02,170
That's so cool.

2199
01:55:03,010 --> 01:55:04,750
You should play something for us, Monica.

2200
01:55:06,750 --> 01:55:07,270
That's...

2201
01:55:07,270 --> 01:55:08,410
Monica looks at me.

2202
01:55:09,630 --> 01:55:11,470
Maybe once I get a little bit better I will.

2203
01:55:17,890 --> 01:55:20,970
I can't wait to hear you play piano.

2204
01:55:21,210 --> 01:55:24,310
I can't wait to see you read books and make food and...

2205
01:55:25,150 --> 01:55:28,170
Do all kinds of nonsensical everyday things that all people do.

2206
01:55:29,530 --> 01:55:30,790
Is that so?

2207
01:55:31,430 --> 01:55:32,490
In that case...

2208
01:55:33,530 --> 01:55:35,190
I won't let you down, Oliver!

2209
01:55:36,490 --> 01:55:38,370
And Monica smiles sweetly.

2210
01:55:39,210 --> 01:55:39,630
Ah!

2211
01:55:40,150 --> 01:55:41,750
I didn't mean any pressure or anything like that.

2212
01:55:45,220 --> 01:55:47,640
I've been practicing a whole lot recently.

2213
01:55:48,260 --> 01:55:51,100
And I really love the chance to share once I'm ready.

2214
01:55:51,860 --> 01:55:52,860
I see.

2215
01:55:53,480 --> 01:55:54,600
In that case...

2216
01:55:55,200 --> 01:55:56,120
Best of luck!

2217
01:55:56,880 --> 01:55:57,560
Thanks!

2218
01:55:58,380 --> 01:56:00,300
So, I didn't miss anything, did I?

2219
01:56:00,920 --> 01:56:01,480
No.

2220
01:56:01,820 --> 01:56:02,700
Not really.

2221
01:56:03,860 --> 01:56:06,760
I choose to leave out Sayori's mischievous escapade.

2222
01:56:07,580 --> 01:56:10,340
I'm sure Natsuki will end up complaining to her anyway.

2223
01:56:11,140 --> 01:56:13,380
It looks like everyone has already settled down.

2224
01:56:13,520 --> 01:56:15,800
God, this music is fucking driving me nuts!

2225
01:56:17,480 --> 01:56:19,940
Sayori somehow already finished her entire cookie.

2226
01:56:21,140 --> 01:56:24,140
Yuri is back to her book and Natsuki disappeared into the closet.

2227
01:56:27,330 --> 01:56:28,230
Hey, Yuri!

2228
01:56:29,590 --> 01:56:30,130
Eh?

2229
01:56:31,450 --> 01:56:31,990
Ah!

2230
01:56:33,590 --> 01:56:37,910
I suddenly noticed that Yuri is reading a different book from the one we've been reading together.

2231
01:56:39,250 --> 01:56:39,790
Sorry!

2232
01:56:40,250 --> 01:56:41,770
I didn't mean to interrupt.

2233
01:56:42,670 --> 01:56:43,610
Ah, no.

2234
01:56:44,330 --> 01:56:46,430
I was kind of just waiting for you.

2235
01:56:47,390 --> 01:56:48,750
Ah, if that's the case...

2236
01:56:49,710 --> 01:56:51,330
Why don't we go ahead and get started?

2237
01:56:52,150 --> 01:56:53,310
Yes, let's!

2238
01:56:54,710 --> 01:56:56,450
Actually, I have a request.

2239
01:56:57,510 --> 01:56:59,070
You mind if I make some tea first?

2240
01:57:00,110 --> 01:57:01,070
Not at all.

2241
01:57:02,030 --> 01:57:03,230
Thanks very much.

2242
01:57:04,490 --> 01:57:09,210
If there's one thing that can make my reading time here any better, it's a nice cup of tea.

2243
01:57:10,210 --> 01:57:12,230
Not to mention for yourself as well.

2244
01:57:13,530 --> 01:57:15,530
Yuri stands up and makes her way to the closet.

2245
01:57:16,310 --> 01:57:21,230
I follow, and watch, as she retrieves a small water pitcher from the shelf.

2246
01:57:21,860 --> 01:57:23,230
The kind with a filter inside.

2247
01:57:24,270 --> 01:57:25,650
Can you hold this for a second?

2248
01:57:26,590 --> 01:57:27,090
Sure.

2249
01:57:28,250 --> 01:57:31,130
Yuri hands me the water pitcher and also fetches an electric kettle.

2250
01:57:32,350 --> 01:57:35,950
I'm gonna pluck this in at the teacher's desk, and then we'll go get some water.

2251
01:57:36,710 --> 01:57:39,750
She walks past me and sets the kettle down on the teacher's desk.

2252
01:57:40,510 --> 01:57:42,250
I simply watch her movements.

2253
01:57:43,150 --> 01:57:46,950
To my surprise, the way she moves really contrasts her speaking mannerisms.

2254
01:57:47,930 --> 01:57:49,030
What does that even mean?

2255
01:57:49,850 --> 01:57:53,410
Especially because of her long legs, Yuri appears elegant and methodical.

2256
01:57:55,150 --> 01:57:58,090
I sound like a freaking creep weirdo.

2257
01:58:01,270 --> 01:58:01,830
Jesus.

2258
01:58:03,090 --> 01:58:04,590
Okay, may I have the water pitcher?

2259
01:58:05,710 --> 01:58:07,330
Thanks, I'll be right back.

2260
01:58:08,310 --> 01:58:10,710
Ah, I might as well walk with you!

2261
01:58:11,930 --> 01:58:13,330
Yeah, why not?

2262
01:58:14,150 --> 01:58:15,110
Shall we go then?

2263
01:58:15,830 --> 01:58:16,390
Yeah.

2264
01:58:17,510 --> 01:58:17,850
Hmm?

2265
01:58:18,630 --> 01:58:19,670
What are you two off to?

2266
01:58:20,510 --> 01:58:21,030
Eh?

2267
01:58:22,130 --> 01:58:24,830
We're just... Yuri was gonna make some tea, so...

2268
01:58:25,410 --> 01:58:28,510
I suddenly realize how weird it sounds to explain this to Monica.

2269
01:58:28,730 --> 01:58:30,830
WHY IS IT WEIRD EXPLAINING THAT WE'RE MAKING TEA?

2270
01:58:31,590 --> 01:58:33,090
WE'RE LITERALLY GETTING WATER!

2271
01:58:33,350 --> 01:58:34,410
TO MAKE TEA!

2272
01:58:34,530 --> 01:58:35,510
IT'S NOT WEIRD!

2273
01:58:35,970 --> 01:58:36,970
IT'S NORMAL!

2274
01:58:38,330 --> 01:58:39,990
WE'RE JUST FILLING THE WATER PITCHER!

2275
01:58:41,550 --> 01:58:42,750
Nah, okay.

2276
01:58:43,750 --> 01:58:45,790
Sorry, I was just a bit curious.

2277
01:58:46,930 --> 01:58:48,850
That's kind of a one-person job, isn't it?

2278
01:58:51,130 --> 01:58:51,650
That's...

2279
01:58:51,650 --> 01:58:54,390
Monica, please mind your own business for once.

2280
01:58:55,070 --> 01:58:58,910
Or do you want to tell me there's something wrong with helping involve Oliver in club activities?

2281
01:59:00,150 --> 01:59:01,030
Eh...

2282
01:59:03,310 --> 01:59:04,910
My mouth gapes!

2283
01:59:06,310 --> 01:59:06,830
I...

2284
01:59:07,430 --> 01:59:08,930
I suppose there's nothing wrong with that?

2285
01:59:10,050 --> 01:59:10,330
Hmm.

2286
01:59:11,270 --> 01:59:12,190
Then let's go, Oliver.

2287
01:59:13,230 --> 01:59:13,490
Ah!

2288
01:59:14,290 --> 01:59:16,790
Yuri quickly exits the room, and I follow.

2289
01:59:20,470 --> 01:59:24,650
Once in the hallway, she suddenly puts her forehead against the wall.

2290
01:59:26,550 --> 01:59:28,090
I spoke without thinking.

2291
01:59:30,150 --> 01:59:31,970
How could I say something like that?

2292
01:59:34,430 --> 01:59:34,910
Yuri?

2293
01:59:36,890 --> 01:59:37,850
I just...

2294
01:59:38,330 --> 01:59:39,970
Something about the way she said that.

2295
01:59:41,110 --> 01:59:42,270
It made me feel so...

2296
01:59:43,510 --> 01:59:43,990
irritated.

2297
01:59:45,590 --> 01:59:46,630
What's wrong with me?

2298
01:59:47,850 --> 01:59:48,710
No, Yuri!

2299
01:59:49,510 --> 01:59:50,550
I think...

2300
01:59:50,550 --> 01:59:51,930
You did the right thing.

2301
01:59:52,610 --> 01:59:54,350
I wasn't expecting it, but...

2302
01:59:55,110 --> 01:59:57,790
It's also not right for Monica to judge people like that.

2303
01:59:59,410 --> 02:00:00,070
Oliver.

2304
02:00:01,710 --> 02:00:06,710
How come even when I do something bad, you're being nice to me?

2305
02:00:09,250 --> 02:00:10,310
What did you do?

2306
02:00:10,310 --> 02:00:11,170
That's bad!

2307
02:00:11,530 --> 02:00:12,050
I don't...

2308
02:00:14,340 --> 02:00:16,340
There's drama out of nothing!

2309
02:00:19,660 --> 02:00:20,120
Because...

2310
02:00:20,120 --> 02:00:23,400
Nothing that you do is as bad as you make it seem in your head.

2311
02:00:24,500 --> 02:00:25,560
Nobody's perfect.

2312
02:00:26,480 --> 02:00:27,900
We have emotions.

2313
02:00:28,380 --> 02:00:30,480
And we can't always hide them away.

2314
02:00:31,260 --> 02:00:33,720
But you always amplify things in your head.

2315
02:00:34,440 --> 02:00:37,820
Your mind turns a light rain shower into a hurricane.

2316
02:00:39,920 --> 02:00:40,560
Ah...

2317
02:00:40,560 --> 02:00:41,240
No!

2318
02:00:42,420 --> 02:00:45,140
Wouldn't you hate me for something as terrible as that?

2319
02:00:45,880 --> 02:00:46,700
You're right!

2320
02:00:46,820 --> 02:00:47,880
I fucking hate you!

2321
02:00:48,880 --> 02:00:49,740
Nah, kidding.

2322
02:00:49,980 --> 02:00:50,600
I mean...

2323
02:00:50,600 --> 02:00:52,060
Why would I hate you?

2324
02:00:52,660 --> 02:00:55,120
I can't hate someone for having emotions.

2325
02:00:56,180 --> 02:00:57,900
What kind of friend would do that?

2326
02:00:59,020 --> 02:00:59,580
Friend?

2327
02:01:00,720 --> 02:01:01,520
You're saying?

2328
02:01:05,030 --> 02:01:06,430
Yuri lifts her head.

2329
02:01:07,490 --> 02:01:08,050
Oliver.

2330
02:01:09,310 --> 02:01:10,410
I really like...

2331
02:01:11,710 --> 02:01:12,970
being friends with you.

2332
02:01:14,070 --> 02:01:14,510
Haha!

2333
02:01:15,810 --> 02:01:16,450
Thanks, Yuri.

2334
02:01:17,010 --> 02:01:18,790
I like being friends with you, too.

2335
02:01:19,570 --> 02:01:21,870
I feel kind of awkward saying something like that.

2336
02:01:21,970 --> 02:01:22,870
Yeah, nobody says that.

2337
02:01:24,010 --> 02:01:26,130
But I'm doing my best to help Yuri feel better.

2338
02:01:27,370 --> 02:01:27,990
Anyway.

2339
02:01:29,490 --> 02:01:30,110
Ah...

2340
02:01:30,110 --> 02:01:30,690
Yeah.

2341
02:01:31,550 --> 02:01:32,370
Shall we go?

2342
02:01:33,210 --> 02:01:33,730
Yeah.

2343
02:01:34,590 --> 02:01:37,110
Yuri and I walk to the nearest water fountain.

2344
02:01:37,630 --> 02:01:39,590
Bro, this is like a...

2345
02:01:40,400 --> 02:01:41,010
Oh my...

2346
02:01:41,010 --> 02:01:41,610
It's like...

2347
02:01:41,610 --> 02:01:44,290
Just walking to get water for the tea is like...

2348
02:01:44,810 --> 02:01:45,830
pulling out teeth!

2349
02:01:47,650 --> 02:01:50,890
Once we fill up the water pitcher, we return to the classroom.

2350
02:01:51,970 --> 02:01:52,390
Really?

2351
02:01:52,470 --> 02:01:54,290
We didn't have an existential crisis on the way back?

2352
02:01:56,430 --> 02:01:56,870
Oliver.

2353
02:01:57,570 --> 02:01:58,930
Do you like oolong tea?

2354
02:02:00,730 --> 02:02:01,310
Uh...

2355
02:02:01,310 --> 02:02:02,110
Yeah?

2356
02:02:03,610 --> 02:02:04,610
Anything is fine.

2357
02:02:05,690 --> 02:02:06,250
Very well.

2358
02:02:07,190 --> 02:02:09,790
Yuri sets the temperature on the kettle to 200 degrees.

2359
02:02:11,150 --> 02:02:12,410
Fahrenheit, I imagine.

2360
02:02:13,850 --> 02:02:15,570
Now it's time to get the teapot.

2361
02:02:16,670 --> 02:02:18,810
You really do this properly, don't you?

2362
02:02:20,670 --> 02:02:21,390
Of course.

2363
02:02:23,050 --> 02:02:25,470
I shouldn't do any less when I'm making tea for others.

2364
02:02:27,710 --> 02:02:30,250
I'm facepalming so hard, dude.

2365
02:02:31,050 --> 02:02:35,250
I think my forehead is as red as...

2366
02:02:35,250 --> 02:02:35,730
I don't even know.

2367
02:02:35,990 --> 02:02:36,890
Something that is very red.

2368
02:02:37,370 --> 02:02:38,130
A crayon.

2369
02:02:39,230 --> 02:02:41,230
Even if I'm not an expert on tea or anything...

2370
02:02:42,830 --> 02:02:45,990
In that case, you'll only be even more impressed.

2371
02:02:47,150 --> 02:02:48,510
Ah, perhaps I will.

2372
02:02:49,830 --> 02:02:52,830
Yuri fetches the teapot and begins measuring the tea leaves.

2373
02:02:53,990 --> 02:02:57,170
To my surprise, she even starts humming a little to herself.

2374
02:02:58,390 --> 02:02:59,270
So I'm just...

2375
02:02:59,270 --> 02:03:00,210
I'm standing there.

2376
02:03:00,390 --> 02:03:02,010
I look at her walk over.

2377
02:03:02,510 --> 02:03:04,170
She starts humming and I go, huh?

2378
02:03:04,950 --> 02:03:05,870
That's surprising.

2379
02:03:06,290 --> 02:03:07,970
I didn't expect her to hum.

2380
02:03:08,730 --> 02:03:10,150
What a weird...

2381
02:03:10,870 --> 02:03:12,410
set of coincidences here.

2382
02:03:12,550 --> 02:03:13,530
Like, what is happening?

2383
02:03:13,730 --> 02:03:14,750
That is so weird.

2384
02:03:14,750 --> 02:03:15,610
I can't believe it.

2385
02:03:16,050 --> 02:03:17,490
What a highlight of my day.

2386
02:03:18,650 --> 02:03:20,170
You must be in a good mood now.

2387
02:03:21,090 --> 02:03:22,050
Is that so?

2388
02:03:23,130 --> 02:03:24,350
I was letting it show.

2389
02:03:25,370 --> 02:03:26,330
And you noticed.

2390
02:03:29,090 --> 02:03:30,810
I was doing a bit of thinking.

2391
02:03:31,750 --> 02:03:34,970
And I decided that I would try expressing myself a little bit more.

2392
02:03:35,890 --> 02:03:38,110
It turns out it's not very hard for me to do.

2393
02:03:39,070 --> 02:03:40,670
When it's you who's around anyway.

2394
02:03:42,770 --> 02:03:43,210
Ah...

2395
02:03:43,210 --> 02:03:44,570
That's great, Yuri.

2396
02:03:45,510 --> 02:03:47,430
Just don't push yourself too much.

2397
02:03:47,950 --> 02:03:50,490
Yeah, I wouldn't want you to dying of a stroke or something.

2398
02:03:50,830 --> 02:03:52,970
From all that whistling and humming.

2399
02:03:53,330 --> 02:03:57,590
And I know it's a big burden on your mind and your physicality.

2400
02:03:57,670 --> 02:03:58,830
I know it's tough, you know.

2401
02:03:58,950 --> 02:04:00,250
But we all gotta go through it.

2402
02:04:00,470 --> 02:04:01,870
Life is hard, man.

2403
02:04:02,850 --> 02:04:04,810
You're always worrying about me, Oliver.

2404
02:04:05,490 --> 02:04:07,050
It's very endearing.

2405
02:04:08,990 --> 02:04:09,470
That's...

2406
02:04:09,470 --> 02:04:10,610
Yuri wasn't kidding.

2407
02:04:11,230 --> 02:04:13,150
I don't even know if I can keep up with this.

2408
02:04:13,790 --> 02:04:16,490
I watched Yuri pour a cup of tea for each of us.

2409
02:04:18,190 --> 02:04:20,110
Oliver, I have another request.

2410
02:04:21,430 --> 02:04:23,330
Do you mind if we sit on the floor today?

2411
02:04:24,670 --> 02:04:25,190
Eh?

2412
02:04:25,850 --> 02:04:26,750
Why is that?

2413
02:04:27,930 --> 02:04:29,670
It's a little bit easier on my back.

2414
02:04:29,790 --> 02:04:30,610
Said nobody ever.

2415
02:04:31,850 --> 02:04:35,890
I can read with my back against the wall rather than bending over at my desk.

2416
02:04:36,850 --> 02:04:38,070
Yuri, listen to me.

2417
02:04:38,330 --> 02:04:40,290
Do you understand how a chair works?

2418
02:04:41,850 --> 02:04:46,690
There's a back rest on the chair designed for your specific problem.

2419
02:04:48,350 --> 02:04:49,430
Ah, sorry.

2420
02:04:49,590 --> 02:04:50,510
I didn't realize.

2421
02:04:51,450 --> 02:04:52,170
No worries.

2422
02:04:52,830 --> 02:04:57,410
I just have back pain fairly regularly, so I do my best to manage it.

2423
02:04:58,010 --> 02:04:59,090
Is that so?

2424
02:04:59,770 --> 02:05:01,450
I wonder why that is.

2425
02:05:02,230 --> 02:05:06,050
It's most likely because my, um, my...

2426
02:05:07,190 --> 02:05:08,810
Your posture, right?

2427
02:05:10,290 --> 02:05:12,230
Always hunched over like that while reading?

2428
02:05:13,050 --> 02:05:13,550
Yes!

2429
02:05:14,190 --> 02:05:15,730
I have terrible reading posture.

2430
02:05:16,530 --> 02:05:18,130
So that's why we should sit on the floor.

2431
02:05:19,470 --> 02:05:20,290
Fair enough.

2432
02:05:21,650 --> 02:05:23,050
I'll go ahead and get the book.

2433
02:05:24,130 --> 02:05:26,030
I retrieve the book from my bag.

2434
02:05:27,010 --> 02:05:27,450
Ah!

2435
02:05:27,870 --> 02:05:29,390
I have some chocolate as well.

2436
02:05:30,390 --> 02:05:34,310
It's a bag of small chocolate candies that I kept hidden from Sayori's candy radar.

2437
02:05:35,610 --> 02:05:38,290
I take it, since it'll go well with the tea.

2438
02:05:39,210 --> 02:05:42,690
Yuri and I then sit against the wall, teacups at our sides.

2439
02:05:43,490 --> 02:05:49,730
As if in sync, we assume the same reading position as last time, each holding one half of the book.

2440
02:05:49,730 --> 02:05:56,450
Except this time, our bodies are even closer to each other.

2441
02:05:58,300 --> 02:05:59,650
I can't see too well.

2442
02:06:01,830 --> 02:06:04,870
Yuri slides closer until our shoulders are touching.

2443
02:06:06,170 --> 02:06:08,690
How am I supposed to focus on reading like this?

2444
02:06:10,050 --> 02:06:12,130
Yuri was always kind of cute, but...

2445
02:06:12,890 --> 02:06:15,010
When she's being less apprehensive...

2446
02:06:15,010 --> 02:06:16,610
It's almost more than I can handle!

2447
02:06:17,990 --> 02:06:19,030
Your teacup?

2448
02:06:19,790 --> 02:06:21,330
Yuri hands me my teacup.

2449
02:06:22,110 --> 02:06:27,630
Holding it with my hand that's not holding the book, I end up in a position that makes it even harder to focus.

2450
02:06:28,990 --> 02:06:31,530
So weirdly overly descriptive, you know?

2451
02:06:33,010 --> 02:06:36,470
Because now I need to worry about making sure I don't accidentally touch her chest!

2452
02:06:37,650 --> 02:06:39,830
Meanwhile, Yuri hasn't noticed a single thing.

2453
02:06:40,890 --> 02:06:46,270
She wears her intense reading expression, and I can only presume the world around her has faded away.

2454
02:06:47,330 --> 02:06:49,750
I use all of my willpower to focus on reading.

2455
02:06:51,670 --> 02:06:54,610
After a few minutes, I finally manage to relax a little.

2456
02:06:55,550 --> 02:06:59,630
I put the teacup between my legs, and fumble with the chocolate wrapper.

2457
02:07:00,710 --> 02:07:01,150
Ah!

2458
02:07:01,730 --> 02:07:02,170
Sorry.

2459
02:07:02,910 --> 02:07:05,470
I briefly let go of the book to finish opening the wrapper.

2460
02:07:06,350 --> 02:07:07,910
You can have as much as you want.

2461
02:07:09,270 --> 02:07:10,150
Ah, that's...

2462
02:07:10,690 --> 02:07:12,230
That's okay, I won't take any.

2463
02:07:13,170 --> 02:07:13,650
Huh?

2464
02:07:13,830 --> 02:07:14,310
Are you sure?

2465
02:07:15,870 --> 02:07:16,350
Well...

2466
02:07:17,130 --> 02:07:19,750
If I touch it, then I might get smudges on the pages.

2467
02:07:22,530 --> 02:07:24,030
How do you manage to do anything?

2468
02:07:24,890 --> 02:07:25,990
Ah, you're right!

2469
02:07:27,190 --> 02:07:29,650
I didn't even think about that stupid me!

2470
02:07:30,350 --> 02:07:31,050
My bad.

2471
02:07:32,170 --> 02:07:33,190
No need to apologize.

2472
02:07:34,250 --> 02:07:35,550
I'll hold the book, okay?

2473
02:07:36,550 --> 02:07:37,650
Are you sure?

2474
02:07:38,610 --> 02:07:39,330
Of course.

2475
02:07:41,110 --> 02:07:42,170
Wow, a new picture!

2476
02:07:43,470 --> 02:07:44,590
There's stars in the background!

2477
02:07:44,770 --> 02:07:46,090
No, that's just, uh...

2478
02:07:46,090 --> 02:07:46,750
Dust, I guess.

2479
02:07:47,810 --> 02:07:49,450
Yuri opens the book with both hands.

2480
02:07:50,690 --> 02:07:53,450
She holds it that I don't have any harder time reading from it.

2481
02:07:54,290 --> 02:07:58,230
But as a result, her left arm is practically resting on top of my LEG!

2482
02:07:59,310 --> 02:08:04,610
Well, in that case, Yuri is already totally focused on reading again.

2483
02:08:05,410 --> 02:08:08,470
I take a chocolate candy and pop it in my mouth.

2484
02:08:09,130 --> 02:08:14,130
Then, I take another chocolate, and I hold it up to Yuri.

2485
02:08:15,390 --> 02:08:17,770
She doesn't even look away from the book.

2486
02:08:18,770 --> 02:08:23,670
She simply parts her lips, as if the situation was completely natural.

2487
02:08:24,410 --> 02:08:26,450
But that means I can't stop here.

2488
02:08:27,310 --> 02:08:30,390
I then proceed to put the other chocolate up my BUTT!

2489
02:08:31,450 --> 02:08:34,970
Now, I apprehensively place the chocolate in her mouth.

2490
02:08:36,070 --> 02:08:39,750
Look at the fucking chocolate in her mouth!

2491
02:08:43,830 --> 02:08:49,310
It's like someone just took a little chocolate image and just dragged it on top of her mouth!

2492
02:08:50,690 --> 02:08:54,190
Just like that, Yuri closes her lips over it.

2493
02:08:54,870 --> 02:08:55,770
The hell?

2494
02:08:56,650 --> 02:08:58,590
Yuri's expression suddenly breaks.

2495
02:09:00,250 --> 02:09:02,050
Did... did I just...

2496
02:09:02,710 --> 02:09:05,290
Yuri looks at me like she needs to confirm what just happened.

2497
02:09:07,590 --> 02:09:08,350
Um...

2498
02:09:09,870 --> 02:09:10,250
Oliver...

2499
02:09:11,410 --> 02:09:11,790
Sorry...

2500
02:09:12,170 --> 02:09:14,110
I guess I shouldn't have done that.

2501
02:09:15,570 --> 02:09:16,630
Ah, that's...

2502
02:09:17,790 --> 02:09:18,550
Well...

2503
02:09:18,550 --> 02:09:20,150
You were just helping...

2504
02:09:21,210 --> 02:09:22,250
That's something that...

2505
02:09:22,710 --> 02:09:23,430
Friends do...

2506
02:09:24,110 --> 02:09:24,630
Right?

2507
02:09:25,530 --> 02:09:26,570
I mean...

2508
02:09:27,190 --> 02:09:29,170
Not really in this kind of context, but...

2509
02:09:29,750 --> 02:09:30,270
Yeah.

2510
02:09:31,370 --> 02:09:32,410
That's all it was.

2511
02:09:33,450 --> 02:09:33,970
Yeah.

2512
02:09:36,070 --> 02:09:36,590
Then...

2513
02:09:36,590 --> 02:09:38,350
You don't need to stop her or anything.

2514
02:09:39,690 --> 02:09:40,410
I see.

2515
02:09:41,610 --> 02:09:43,130
So wait, she wants more chocolate?

2516
02:09:44,050 --> 02:09:45,830
I just keep feeding her chocolate, or what?

2517
02:09:46,130 --> 02:09:48,310
The situation has gotten really tense.

2518
02:09:49,010 --> 02:09:51,190
Yuri tries to return to the book.

2519
02:09:51,890 --> 02:09:55,930
But I can just tell by her expression that even she can't focus now.

2520
02:09:56,650 --> 02:09:58,070
My heart is pounding.

2521
02:09:58,670 --> 02:10:01,530
I nervously take another chocolate between my feet.

2522
02:10:01,850 --> 02:10:03,850
Don't feed her another chocolate, you asshole.

2523
02:10:04,650 --> 02:10:06,990
But this time Yuri's eyes meet mine.

2524
02:10:08,810 --> 02:10:10,890
How did it even come to this?

2525
02:10:11,850 --> 02:10:13,390
Yuri doesn't avert her gaze.

2526
02:10:14,150 --> 02:10:18,150
I notice her chest rising and falling to the rhythm of her breaths.

2527
02:10:19,010 --> 02:10:19,350
Yeah.

2528
02:10:20,110 --> 02:10:20,170
Yeah.

2529
02:10:20,170 --> 02:10:21,390
That's called HAVING A LUNG!

2530
02:10:22,590 --> 02:10:24,050
I raise my arm.

2531
02:10:27,030 --> 02:10:29,310
Like before, Yuri parts her lips.

2532
02:10:30,130 --> 02:10:32,110
But it's different this time.

2533
02:10:33,210 --> 02:10:35,490
I take the chocolate and place it in her mouth.

2534
02:10:36,730 --> 02:10:37,330
Stop!

2535
02:10:38,110 --> 02:10:40,210
Stop putting chocolate in her mouth!

2536
02:10:40,410 --> 02:10:41,450
It's weird, dude!

2537
02:10:42,650 --> 02:10:45,530
I feel her hot breath on my fingers.

2538
02:10:46,770 --> 02:10:47,730
Okay, everyone!

2539
02:10:49,050 --> 02:10:49,590
Huh?

2540
02:10:52,780 --> 02:10:54,060
Yuri jolts back.

2541
02:10:54,840 --> 02:10:56,380
It's time to share poems.

2542
02:10:56,580 --> 02:10:58,740
Oh no, not poems.

2543
02:11:00,580 --> 02:11:03,460
Oliver, can you help Yuri put away the tea stuff, right?

2544
02:11:04,560 --> 02:11:06,720
Yeah, of course.

2545
02:11:07,740 --> 02:11:08,700
Okay, thanks.

2546
02:11:09,840 --> 02:11:11,360
The spell is abruptly broken.

2547
02:11:13,160 --> 02:11:13,680
Ow.

2548
02:11:14,340 --> 02:11:15,560
I'll take care of the cups.

2549
02:11:16,540 --> 02:11:17,060
Yeah.

2550
02:11:18,660 --> 02:11:20,160
Fucking cock blocking!

2551
02:11:21,440 --> 02:11:24,300
Thank you, Sayori, or whatever the fuck your name is.

2552
02:11:24,300 --> 02:11:25,560
Sorry for swearing so much.

2553
02:11:26,300 --> 02:11:28,120
Yuri picks up the teacups from the floor.

2554
02:11:29,100 --> 02:11:30,880
I pick up the bag of chocolates.

2555
02:11:31,680 --> 02:11:35,240
In the end, we hastily clean up without so much as a word between us.

2556
02:11:36,080 --> 02:11:40,560
I get the feeling this is something neither of us will have the courage to bring up.

2557
02:11:43,300 --> 02:11:45,140
Who should I show my poem to first?

2558
02:11:47,340 --> 02:11:48,380
What do you mean first?

2559
02:11:50,040 --> 02:11:50,480
Yuri!

2560
02:11:51,520 --> 02:11:52,980
We had our chocolate thing going.

2561
02:11:54,160 --> 02:11:56,240
Let's see what you've written for me today.

2562
02:12:00,090 --> 02:12:03,610
Yuri stares at the poem with a surprised expression on her face.

2563
02:12:04,730 --> 02:12:07,090
Do you... like it?

2564
02:12:08,990 --> 02:12:09,750
Oliver...

2565
02:12:10,990 --> 02:12:14,490
This one might even be better than yesterday's.

2566
02:12:15,410 --> 02:12:17,490
How did you even pick up on this so quickly?

2567
02:12:18,450 --> 02:12:21,890
Just yesterday I was telling you the kind of techniques worth practicing.

2568
02:12:23,050 --> 02:12:24,110
Maybe that's why.

2569
02:12:24,790 --> 02:12:26,190
You did a good job explaining.

2570
02:12:27,170 --> 02:12:29,490
I really wanted to try giving it more imagery.

2571
02:12:30,790 --> 02:12:32,290
Yuri visibly swallows.

2572
02:12:33,150 --> 02:12:35,010
Even her hands appear sweaty.

2573
02:12:36,530 --> 02:12:38,710
I'm not... used to this.

2574
02:12:39,670 --> 02:12:40,530
Used to what?

2575
02:12:40,850 --> 02:12:41,910
Being fed chocolate?

2576
02:12:42,830 --> 02:12:43,770
I don't know.

2577
02:12:44,810 --> 02:12:45,430
It's fine.

2578
02:12:45,870 --> 02:12:46,650
Take your time.

2579
02:12:47,870 --> 02:12:49,590
Yuri breathes and collects her thoughts.

2580
02:12:50,450 --> 02:12:52,970
I know that Yuri likes to think before she speaks.

2581
02:12:53,310 --> 02:12:55,050
So I offer her that patience.

2582
02:12:55,910 --> 02:13:04,250
Yeah, just being appreciated like this, I guess, probably sounds really stupid.

2583
02:13:04,470 --> 02:13:07,110
Oh my god, stop putting yourself down!

2584
02:13:07,890 --> 02:13:09,950
It is so unattractive!

2585
02:13:11,110 --> 02:13:11,570
Yuri.

2586
02:13:13,010 --> 02:13:19,050
But seeing someone motivated by my writing, it just makes me really happy.

2587
02:13:20,230 --> 02:13:22,790
Are you saying you've never shared your writing before?

2588
02:13:23,770 --> 02:13:24,990
She nods.

2589
02:13:25,790 --> 02:13:26,310
Really?

2590
02:13:26,730 --> 02:13:27,970
I don't believe it.

2591
02:13:28,970 --> 02:13:30,890
I really only write for myself.

2592
02:13:31,330 --> 02:13:33,010
Are you not part of a literature club?

2593
02:13:34,510 --> 02:13:36,390
What are you even talking about?

2594
02:13:38,350 --> 02:13:41,810
And besides, people would just laugh at me.

2595
02:13:42,710 --> 02:13:44,190
Do you really think that?

2596
02:13:44,950 --> 02:13:46,430
Again, Yuri nods.

2597
02:13:47,630 --> 02:13:47,970
Ha!

2598
02:13:49,110 --> 02:13:50,590
Even your close friends?

2599
02:13:52,930 --> 02:13:54,610
Yuri doesn't respond to that.

2600
02:13:55,350 --> 02:13:56,550
I wonder why.

2601
02:13:58,010 --> 02:13:58,570
Anyway!

2602
02:13:59,310 --> 02:14:01,110
You want to share the poem you wrote today?

2603
02:14:01,970 --> 02:14:02,570
Yeah.

2604
02:14:03,270 --> 02:14:04,090
I do.

2605
02:14:04,850 --> 02:14:06,370
If it's with you.

2606
02:14:07,450 --> 02:14:08,370
Okay, here we go.

2607
02:14:09,730 --> 02:14:10,930
The Raccoon!

2608
02:14:11,450 --> 02:14:15,590
It happened in the dead of night while I was slicing bread for a guilty snack.

2609
02:14:16,310 --> 02:14:20,470
My attention was caught by the scuttering of a raccoon outside my window.

2610
02:14:21,070 --> 02:14:27,090
That was, I believe, the first time I noticed my strange tendencies as an unordinary human.

2611
02:14:27,650 --> 02:14:32,870
I gave the raccoon a piece of bread, my subconscious well aware of the consequences.

2612
02:14:33,610 --> 02:14:36,810
Well aware that a raccoon that is fed will always come back for more.

2613
02:14:37,290 --> 02:14:41,010
The enticing beauty of my cutting knife was the symptom.

2614
02:14:41,550 --> 02:14:43,410
The bread, my hungry curiosity.

2615
02:14:43,790 --> 02:14:45,570
The raccoon, an urge.

2616
02:14:46,190 --> 02:14:51,370
The moon increments its phase and reflects that much more light off of my cutting knife.

2617
02:14:51,690 --> 02:14:54,870
The very same light that glistens in the eyes of my raccoon friend.

2618
02:14:55,510 --> 02:14:57,370
I slice the bread, fresh and soft.

2619
02:14:57,570 --> 02:14:59,350
The raccoon becomes excited.

2620
02:14:59,710 --> 02:15:04,730
Or perhaps I'm merely projecting my emotions onto the newly satisfied animal.

2621
02:15:05,110 --> 02:15:06,970
The raccoon has taken to following me.

2622
02:15:07,290 --> 02:15:10,650
You could say that we've gotten quite used to each other.

2623
02:15:11,030 --> 02:15:13,430
The raccoon becomes hungry more and more frequently.

2624
02:15:13,690 --> 02:15:15,290
So my bread is always handy.

2625
02:15:16,090 --> 02:15:19,690
Every time I brandish my cutting knife, the raccoon shows me its excitement.

2626
02:15:20,130 --> 02:15:21,050
A rush of blood.

2627
02:15:21,730 --> 02:15:23,970
Classic Pavlovian conditioning.

2628
02:15:24,470 --> 02:15:25,390
I slice the bread.

2629
02:15:25,630 --> 02:15:27,010
And I feed myself again.

2630
02:15:27,150 --> 02:15:27,490
That, dude.

2631
02:15:27,870 --> 02:15:28,930
This is not a poem.

2632
02:15:29,030 --> 02:15:29,990
This is a shitty story.

2633
02:15:33,040 --> 02:15:33,540
Um.

2634
02:15:34,520 --> 02:15:37,280
I was a little more daring with this one than yesterday's.

2635
02:15:38,360 --> 02:15:39,540
I can see that.

2636
02:15:41,400 --> 02:15:43,100
It's a lot more metaphorical.

2637
02:15:45,420 --> 02:15:45,920
Wait.

2638
02:15:46,800 --> 02:15:47,300
Metaphorical?

2639
02:15:48,640 --> 02:15:49,460
Wait a second.

2640
02:15:51,820 --> 02:15:52,560
Is she?

2641
02:15:54,100 --> 02:15:55,200
Wait a minute.

2642
02:15:56,860 --> 02:16:01,340
I don't know if it's my fault, but I can't begin to imagine what this poem is about.

2643
02:16:03,780 --> 02:16:04,600
That's right.

2644
02:16:06,060 --> 02:16:08,620
It's a bit closer to my preferred writing style.

2645
02:16:10,420 --> 02:16:15,380
Using the poem as a canvas to express vivid imagery and conveying emotions through them.

2646
02:16:17,880 --> 02:16:21,760
Yeah, if I take it at face value, then I can't even figure out what it's supposed to mean.

2647
02:16:24,420 --> 02:16:26,120
She sees me as a raccoon!

2648
02:16:27,780 --> 02:16:30,420
She wants to feed me, to have me come back.

2649
02:16:31,780 --> 02:16:32,500
Pavlovian conditioning.

2650
02:16:33,540 --> 02:16:34,360
That's what it means.

2651
02:16:34,480 --> 02:16:34,920
I'm kidding.

2652
02:16:35,820 --> 02:16:37,840
Yeah, if I take it at face value, then I can't...

2653
02:16:37,840 --> 02:16:38,580
Yeah, I read that already.

2654
02:16:39,080 --> 02:16:43,100
Well, I think it's something that different people can relate to in their own way.

2655
02:16:44,980 --> 02:16:48,640
I wanted to express the way it feels for me to indulge in my more unusual hobbies.

2656
02:16:50,240 --> 02:16:52,800
It's those sorts of things I'm usually forced to keep to myself.

2657
02:16:54,020 --> 02:16:56,820
So, I sometimes enjoy writing about them.

2658
02:16:57,760 --> 02:16:59,440
Why do you keep them to yourself?

2659
02:17:01,340 --> 02:17:03,780
Because they're embarrassing.

2660
02:17:05,300 --> 02:17:07,240
And people would make fun of me.

2661
02:17:08,440 --> 02:17:10,320
Don't you have anything like that, Oliver?

2662
02:17:12,580 --> 02:17:14,600
Well, yeah, I guess I do.

2663
02:17:16,740 --> 02:17:17,920
I feel like he...

2664
02:17:17,920 --> 02:17:20,100
I feel like everyone has a little something like that.

2665
02:17:21,260 --> 02:17:25,000
The best we can do is respect each other, and our individualities.

2666
02:17:26,160 --> 02:17:29,760
Even if it is difficult sometimes, and some things make us uncomfortable.

2667
02:17:30,900 --> 02:17:35,320
After all, if I hadn't learned to embrace my own weirdness, I would probably hate myself.

2668
02:17:36,580 --> 02:17:39,260
I... I... I might be ranting a little bit too now.

2669
02:17:40,020 --> 02:17:41,540
But I'm glad you're a good listener.

2670
02:17:42,520 --> 02:17:43,960
You're good at a lot of things.

2671
02:17:46,100 --> 02:17:46,980
Writing, listening...

2672
02:17:47,540 --> 02:17:49,540
There really aren't many people like you, Oliver.

2673
02:17:49,540 --> 02:17:50,280
Yeah, I'm great.

2674
02:17:51,480 --> 02:17:53,060
That's exaggerating a little bit.

2675
02:17:54,040 --> 02:17:55,980
It's just... how I feel.

2676
02:17:57,300 --> 02:18:00,160
I never thought I would feel so comfortable sharing my right...

2677
02:18:00,160 --> 02:18:00,300
Oh.

2678
02:18:04,440 --> 02:18:06,520
Have we not had this conversation before?

2679
02:18:08,160 --> 02:18:10,620
But now I almost feel like I look forward to it.

2680
02:18:11,200 --> 02:18:13,700
It's just... a really nice feeling.

2681
02:18:14,820 --> 02:18:16,160
And you're to thank for that!

2682
02:18:16,560 --> 02:18:17,880
Yeah, you said that.

2683
02:18:19,320 --> 02:18:19,800
It's...

2684
02:18:20,280 --> 02:18:21,360
It's nothing, really.

2685
02:18:22,500 --> 02:18:24,120
Yuri smiles sincerely at me.

2686
02:18:24,820 --> 02:18:27,480
For just a moment, her timidness seems to disappear.

2687
02:18:28,860 --> 02:18:29,560
Oh yeah?

2688
02:18:30,400 --> 02:18:32,080
Who should I show my poem to next?

2689
02:18:32,320 --> 02:18:32,980
Oh my god.

2690
02:18:35,460 --> 02:18:35,960
Sayori!

2691
02:18:40,200 --> 02:18:40,700
Oh!

2692
02:18:41,740 --> 02:18:42,940
I like this one, Oliver!

2693
02:18:43,680 --> 02:18:45,240
It has some nice feelings in it.

2694
02:18:46,080 --> 02:18:47,320
Ah, I'm glad.

2695
02:18:48,320 --> 02:18:49,920
Does that mean it's better than yesterday's?

2696
02:18:51,480 --> 02:18:52,300
Let me think.

2697
02:18:53,220 --> 02:18:53,940
I don't know.

2698
02:18:54,700 --> 02:18:55,900
I guess I like them both.

2699
02:18:58,200 --> 02:19:00,080
That's not very helpful, you know?

2700
02:19:01,200 --> 02:19:04,660
Well, I'm not very good at figuring out if poems are good or bad.

2701
02:19:05,600 --> 02:19:07,280
But that's why I just go by my heart.

2702
02:19:08,180 --> 02:19:11,120
If it makes me feel things, then it must be a good poem.

2703
02:19:12,040 --> 02:19:14,020
I'm not sure that's exactly how it works.

2704
02:19:14,860 --> 02:19:18,500
Then again, I guess conveying feelings is a pretty important part of this whole thing.

2705
02:19:18,500 --> 02:19:19,800
And who am I to judge?

2706
02:19:19,940 --> 02:19:20,780
I'm not the expert here.

2707
02:19:20,900 --> 02:19:21,840
These are the literature people.

2708
02:19:23,120 --> 02:19:23,820
Yeah, maybe.

2709
02:19:24,820 --> 02:19:27,460
Honestly, I don't even know what kind of writing you like in the first place.

2710
02:19:28,400 --> 02:19:28,800
Yeah.

2711
02:19:29,920 --> 02:19:30,560
Me neither.

2712
02:19:31,960 --> 02:19:32,360
Ah.

2713
02:19:33,560 --> 02:19:35,340
Why don't you at least try giving it some thought?

2714
02:19:36,960 --> 02:19:38,680
Oh, you want to write something for me?

2715
02:19:39,440 --> 02:19:40,480
That's so sweet.

2716
02:19:41,480 --> 02:19:42,280
Yeah, right.

2717
02:19:43,040 --> 02:19:44,800
But you're always thinking about other people.

2718
02:19:45,600 --> 02:19:49,000
You need to think for yourself or about yourself once in a while.

2719
02:19:50,020 --> 02:19:52,680
If you don't, you might end up getting hurt at some point.

2720
02:19:53,940 --> 02:19:54,400
Huh?

2721
02:19:56,940 --> 02:20:00,280
Well, I don't really know what you mean, but I'll try to keep it in mind.

2722
02:20:01,680 --> 02:20:02,600
Well, whatever.

2723
02:20:03,520 --> 02:20:04,500
Anyway, let's see.

2724
02:20:05,380 --> 02:20:05,740
Hmm.

2725
02:20:06,520 --> 02:20:09,540
I guess I like happy poems.

2726
02:20:10,160 --> 02:20:10,800
Wait.

2727
02:20:11,440 --> 02:20:12,960
Sometimes I like sad poems too.

2728
02:20:14,560 --> 02:20:16,420
Sometimes a little bit of both.

2729
02:20:17,320 --> 02:20:19,120
There's a word for that, right?

2730
02:20:20,320 --> 02:20:22,100
What's the word I'm looking for?

2731
02:20:23,600 --> 02:20:24,160
Bittersweet.

2732
02:20:24,720 --> 02:20:25,200
Yeah.

2733
02:20:26,200 --> 02:20:28,300
I like things that are happy and things that are sad.

2734
02:20:29,440 --> 02:20:30,500
Happy and sad?

2735
02:20:31,560 --> 02:20:33,960
I can't see you liking something sad, Sayori.

2736
02:20:35,580 --> 02:20:37,480
Well, I like happy the most.

2737
02:20:38,280 --> 02:20:45,120
But sometimes when you have a little rain cloud in your head, a sad poem can help give the rain cloud a little hug.

2738
02:20:46,140 --> 02:20:48,780
And make a nice, happy rainbow.

2739
02:20:50,240 --> 02:20:52,340
Sayori, that's unexpectedly poetic.

2740
02:20:53,380 --> 02:20:53,640
Huh?

2741
02:20:54,280 --> 02:20:55,100
It is?

2742
02:20:56,160 --> 02:20:58,780
Maybe I'm getting better at expressing my feelings after all.

2743
02:20:59,680 --> 02:21:00,600
Thanks, Oliver.

2744
02:21:01,360 --> 02:21:02,780
I should go write that down then.

2745
02:21:03,340 --> 02:21:04,960
You can read my poem now, okay?

2746
02:21:04,960 --> 02:21:07,140
All right, let's do it.

2747
02:21:07,220 --> 02:21:07,860
Bottles!

2748
02:21:08,960 --> 02:21:13,280
I pop off my scalp like the lid of a cookie jar.

2749
02:21:13,620 --> 02:21:17,500
It's the secret place where I keep all my dreams.

2750
02:21:18,120 --> 02:21:21,520
Little balls of sunshine all rubbing together.

2751
02:21:22,020 --> 02:21:30,560
Like a bundle of kittens, I reach inside with my thumb and forefinger.

2752
02:21:30,560 --> 02:21:35,000
And pluck one out, it's warm and tingly.

2753
02:21:35,740 --> 02:21:38,120
But there's no time to waste.

2754
02:21:38,460 --> 02:21:41,900
I put it in a bottle to keep it safe.

2755
02:21:42,380 --> 02:21:47,100
And I put the bottle on the shelf with all the other bottles.

2756
02:21:47,520 --> 02:21:52,840
Happy thoughts, happy thoughts, happy thoughts in bottles all in a row.

2757
02:21:52,840 --> 02:21:57,220
My collection makes me lots of friends.

2758
02:21:57,540 --> 02:22:01,620
Each bottle, a starlight to make amends.

2759
02:22:02,060 --> 02:22:05,320
Sometimes my friend feels a certain way.

2760
02:22:05,880 --> 02:22:09,420
Down comes a bottle to save the day.

2761
02:22:10,240 --> 02:22:12,380
Night after night, more dreams.

2762
02:22:12,560 --> 02:22:14,260
Friend after friend, more bottles.

2763
02:22:14,820 --> 02:22:19,660
Deeper and deeper, my fingers go like exploring a dark cave.

2764
02:22:20,420 --> 02:22:24,120
Discovering the secrets, hiding in their nooks and crannies.

2765
02:22:24,620 --> 02:22:28,360
Digging and digging, scraping and scraping.

2766
02:22:28,820 --> 02:22:30,960
I blow dust off my bottle caps.

2767
02:22:31,700 --> 02:22:34,440
It doesn't feel like time elapsed.

2768
02:22:34,640 --> 02:22:37,020
My empty shelf could use some more.

2769
02:22:37,320 --> 02:22:40,220
My friends look through my locked front door.

2770
02:22:40,900 --> 02:22:45,280
Finally all done, I open up and income my friends.

2771
02:22:45,620 --> 02:22:49,540
If they come in such a hurry, do they want my bottles that much?

2772
02:22:49,740 --> 02:22:53,480
I frantically pull them from the shelf, one after the other.

2773
02:22:53,940 --> 02:22:57,000
Holding them out to each and every one.

2774
02:22:58,360 --> 02:22:59,840
Each and every bottle.

2775
02:23:00,260 --> 02:23:06,400
But every time I let one go, it shatters against the tile between my feet.

2776
02:23:06,400 --> 02:23:12,440
Happy thoughts, happy thoughts, happy thoughts and shards all over the floor.

2777
02:23:12,800 --> 02:23:14,980
They were supposed to be for my friends.

2778
02:23:15,320 --> 02:23:17,460
My friends who weren't smiling.

2779
02:23:17,740 --> 02:23:20,700
They're all shouting, pleading, something.

2780
02:23:21,240 --> 02:23:25,160
But all I hear is echo, echo, echo, echo, echo.

2781
02:23:25,420 --> 02:23:27,180
Inside my head.

2782
02:23:27,880 --> 02:23:29,100
Dude, this is fucking...

2783
02:23:30,640 --> 02:23:32,280
This is fucking dark, man.

2784
02:23:32,780 --> 02:23:33,760
What the hell?

2785
02:23:35,520 --> 02:23:36,480
Holy crap.

2786
02:23:38,620 --> 02:23:39,820
Sayori, did you...

2787
02:23:40,320 --> 02:23:41,540
Did you really write this?

2788
02:23:43,660 --> 02:23:45,000
Of course I did.

2789
02:23:46,200 --> 02:23:48,920
Didn't I tell you yesterday I was going to write the best poem ever?

2790
02:23:51,080 --> 02:23:52,040
Yeah, but...

2791
02:23:52,560 --> 02:23:56,680
I mean, I didn't expect something like this coming from you.

2792
02:23:58,680 --> 02:24:00,060
Monica taught me a whole lot.

2793
02:24:00,660 --> 02:24:02,940
And I've been really in touch with my feelings recently.

2794
02:24:03,820 --> 02:24:04,960
Yeah, I see that.

2795
02:24:05,720 --> 02:24:07,220
It's almost kind of creepy.

2796
02:24:08,820 --> 02:24:09,300
Creepy?

2797
02:24:11,120 --> 02:24:12,560
Well, not exactly.

2798
02:24:13,340 --> 02:24:15,540
Maybe because I'm so used to you being...

2799
02:24:15,540 --> 02:24:15,920
Cheerful?

2800
02:24:16,940 --> 02:24:18,160
Well, never mind.

2801
02:24:18,860 --> 02:24:20,300
I'm thinking too hard about it.

2802
02:24:20,840 --> 02:24:22,240
The point is, it came out good.

2803
02:24:22,400 --> 02:24:23,680
So you should be proud of it.

2804
02:24:24,400 --> 02:24:25,420
Aww, thanks.

2805
02:24:26,260 --> 02:24:27,440
I feel like...

2806
02:24:28,000 --> 02:24:30,380
I feel like I was meant to express myself this way.

2807
02:24:31,020 --> 02:24:33,800
It even helps me understand my own feelings a little bit better.

2808
02:24:34,820 --> 02:24:36,260
Writing is like magic.

2809
02:24:37,360 --> 02:24:39,340
You've gotten pretty passionate about this, huh?

2810
02:24:40,380 --> 02:24:41,540
I hope you keep it up.

2811
02:24:42,360 --> 02:24:42,880
Yeah.

2812
02:24:44,020 --> 02:24:45,060
Writing's the best.

2813
02:24:45,700 --> 02:24:47,600
I'm gonna keep writing until I die.

2814
02:24:49,260 --> 02:24:50,480
Don't get ahead of yourself.

2815
02:24:51,540 --> 02:24:56,320
Sayori's always had a habit of getting obsessed with something, before dropping it no more than a week later.

2816
02:24:56,940 --> 02:24:58,680
I wonder if this is one of those times.

2817
02:25:00,120 --> 02:25:04,160
But seeing the passion in her eyes makes it hard for me to be pessimistic.

2818
02:25:07,970 --> 02:25:10,150
Okay, uh, let's do...

2819
02:25:10,150 --> 02:25:10,570
Monica.

2820
02:25:13,130 --> 02:25:14,170
Hi again, Oliver!

2821
02:25:14,890 --> 02:25:16,370
How's the writing going?

2822
02:25:17,510 --> 02:25:18,770
All right, I guess.

2823
02:25:19,770 --> 02:25:20,870
I'll take that.

2824
02:25:21,490 --> 02:25:23,190
As long as it's not going bad.

2825
02:25:23,890 --> 02:25:25,990
I'm happy that you're applying yourself.

2826
02:25:26,830 --> 02:25:29,010
Maybe soon you'll come up with a masterpiece.

2827
02:25:30,110 --> 02:25:30,670
Ha!

2828
02:25:30,890 --> 02:25:32,130
I wouldn't count on that.

2829
02:25:32,850 --> 02:25:33,850
You never know.

2830
02:25:34,870 --> 02:25:36,470
Wanna share what you wrote for today?

2831
02:25:37,630 --> 02:25:38,190
Sure.

2832
02:25:38,710 --> 02:25:39,310
Here you go.

2833
02:25:40,070 --> 02:25:41,790
I give my poem to Monica.

2834
02:25:44,770 --> 02:25:45,510
All right.

2835
02:25:46,370 --> 02:25:47,590
This one's good.

2836
02:25:48,870 --> 02:25:56,010
It feels like you're not only getting more comfortable with your style, but the imagery is better than the last one I read.

2837
02:25:56,750 --> 02:26:00,890
Just wondering, have you been finding inspiration in Yuri's writing style?

2838
02:26:02,050 --> 02:26:02,810
Hmm.

2839
02:26:03,750 --> 02:26:04,670
Maybe I have?

2840
02:26:05,110 --> 02:26:06,370
I guess so.

2841
02:26:07,350 --> 02:26:08,990
You can't deny that she's talented.

2842
02:26:10,030 --> 02:26:11,190
Yeah, totally.

2843
02:26:11,690 --> 02:26:13,570
I think her poems are the most...

2844
02:26:14,410 --> 02:26:15,130
...romantic.

2845
02:26:16,110 --> 02:26:17,730
That's the best way to describe it.

2846
02:26:19,190 --> 02:26:21,910
She's like a totally different person when she picks up a pen.

2847
02:26:23,090 --> 02:26:24,330
Yeah, I noticed that too.

2848
02:26:25,490 --> 02:26:28,750
But when she's talking about literature, it's like a light turns on inside her.

2849
02:26:29,310 --> 02:26:29,950
Mm-hmm.

2850
02:26:31,250 --> 02:26:34,010
Sadly, it's hard to get much personal conversation of her.

2851
02:26:35,250 --> 02:26:36,950
Trust me, I've tried.

2852
02:26:37,790 --> 02:26:39,770
Who knows what goes on in that head of hers?

2853
02:26:40,830 --> 02:26:42,530
I hope you don't mean that in a bad way.

2854
02:26:43,390 --> 02:26:43,870
No!

2855
02:26:44,430 --> 02:26:45,130
Of course not.

2856
02:26:45,770 --> 02:26:48,370
I just meant that I wish she didn't keep so much to herself.

2857
02:26:49,710 --> 02:26:52,450
But still, defending her like that...

2858
02:26:53,070 --> 02:26:54,790
You must be pretty into her.

2859
02:26:55,610 --> 02:26:56,130
Eh?

2860
02:26:57,270 --> 02:26:59,190
You completely misunderstood.

2861
02:27:00,350 --> 02:27:00,710
Ha!

2862
02:27:01,190 --> 02:27:02,150
Calm down!

2863
02:27:02,890 --> 02:27:03,450
I'm kidding!

2864
02:27:04,550 --> 02:27:06,870
Besides, I'm pretty sure she's already got a boyfriend.

2865
02:27:07,830 --> 02:27:08,610
Wait, really?

2866
02:27:09,890 --> 02:27:11,530
Yeah, a fictional one anyway.

2867
02:27:12,730 --> 02:27:14,830
Monica kind of whispers that last part to me.

2868
02:27:15,430 --> 02:27:16,490
Wait, is she insane?

2869
02:27:17,250 --> 02:27:18,870
It's just a hunch, but...

2870
02:27:21,310 --> 02:27:21,790
Well...

2871
02:27:21,790 --> 02:27:23,890
It's not really anything wrong with that.

2872
02:27:24,290 --> 02:27:25,990
Yes, maybe there is?

2873
02:27:26,250 --> 02:27:28,090
Could be a sign of some kind of disorder?

2874
02:27:29,510 --> 02:27:31,850
Maybe you need to, I don't know, talk to someone?

2875
02:27:33,170 --> 02:27:34,930
Oh, well, I know.

2876
02:27:35,710 --> 02:27:36,710
I was just saying.

2877
02:27:37,470 --> 02:27:38,170
But anyway.

2878
02:27:39,390 --> 02:27:40,810
You want to read my poem now?

2879
02:27:41,810 --> 02:27:44,630
I like the way this one turned out, so I hope you do too!

2880
02:27:46,030 --> 02:27:47,870
All right, let's take a look.

2881
02:27:50,640 --> 02:27:51,700
Save me!

2882
02:27:51,940 --> 02:27:54,320
The colors, they won't stop.

2883
02:27:54,780 --> 02:27:56,940
Bright, beautiful colors.

2884
02:27:57,460 --> 02:27:59,540
Flashing, expanding, piercing.

2885
02:28:00,300 --> 02:28:01,220
Red, green, blue.

2886
02:28:01,820 --> 02:28:03,680
An endless, cacophony.

2887
02:28:04,040 --> 02:28:05,680
Of meaningless, noise.

2888
02:28:06,680 --> 02:28:09,460
The noise, it won't stop.

2889
02:28:10,200 --> 02:28:12,140
Violent grating waveforms.

2890
02:28:12,680 --> 02:28:14,660
Squeaking, screeching, piercing.

2891
02:28:15,460 --> 02:28:17,240
Sine, cosine, tangent.

2892
02:28:17,940 --> 02:28:20,620
Like playing a chalkboard on a turntable.

2893
02:28:21,220 --> 02:28:23,620
Like playing a vinyl on a pizza crust.

2894
02:28:24,100 --> 02:28:26,860
An endless poem of meaningless.

2895
02:28:27,880 --> 02:28:28,780
Load me.

2896
02:28:31,040 --> 02:28:31,820
Fucking hell.

2897
02:28:34,660 --> 02:28:36,780
These girls are depressed as heck, man.

2898
02:28:43,310 --> 02:28:45,470
It's even more abstract than your last one, huh?

2899
02:28:47,870 --> 02:28:49,870
I guess it's just the way I write.

2900
02:28:51,010 --> 02:28:52,330
I'm sorry if you don't like it.

2901
02:28:54,050 --> 02:28:55,270
No, I never said that.

2902
02:28:56,090 --> 02:28:59,070
It's just the kind of thing I've never really seen before, I guess.

2903
02:29:00,890 --> 02:29:03,350
I kind of like playing with my space on the paper.

2904
02:29:04,510 --> 02:29:09,390
Choosing where and how to space your words can totally change the mood of the poem.

2905
02:29:10,450 --> 02:29:11,730
It's almost like magic.

2906
02:29:12,730 --> 02:29:17,670
The way I wrote the lines really short makes it feel like you're trying to speak over the noise.

2907
02:29:18,570 --> 02:29:20,590
Yeah, regarding that noise that you hear in your head.

2908
02:29:21,110 --> 02:29:22,710
Can we talk about that for a second?

2909
02:29:22,830 --> 02:29:23,290
What do you mean?

2910
02:29:24,150 --> 02:29:25,630
And what does load me mean?

2911
02:29:27,870 --> 02:29:28,570
I see.

2912
02:29:30,050 --> 02:29:32,210
It's still hard for me to tell what it's about though.

2913
02:29:34,470 --> 02:29:37,710
Sometimes asking what a poem is about isn't the right question.

2914
02:29:38,870 --> 02:29:42,470
A poem can be as abstract as a physical expression of a feeling.

2915
02:29:43,410 --> 02:29:45,350
Or a conversation with the reader.

2916
02:29:46,690 --> 02:29:49,390
So putting it that way, not every poem is about something.

2917
02:29:50,110 --> 02:29:53,510
Anyway, here's Monica's writing tip of the day.

2918
02:29:54,110 --> 02:29:54,910
Oh god.

2919
02:29:55,910 --> 02:29:59,050
Sometimes you'll find yourself facing a difficult decision.

2920
02:29:59,050 --> 02:30:02,810
And when that happens, don't forget to save your game.

2921
02:30:04,350 --> 02:30:05,030
Oh!

2922
02:30:05,410 --> 02:30:06,750
Maybe I should save my game.

2923
02:30:09,830 --> 02:30:10,690
Why not?

2924
02:30:13,690 --> 02:30:14,370
Thanks!

2925
02:30:15,390 --> 02:30:18,030
You never know when you might change your mind.

2926
02:30:19,150 --> 02:30:21,890
Or when something unexpected may happen.

2927
02:30:23,210 --> 02:30:23,690
Wait.

2928
02:30:24,530 --> 02:30:26,490
Is this tip even about writing?

2929
02:30:26,850 --> 02:30:27,170
Nice.

2930
02:30:28,390 --> 02:30:30,570
What am I even talking about?

2931
02:30:31,890 --> 02:30:32,370
Ahaha!

2932
02:30:33,130 --> 02:30:34,410
That's my advice for today!

2933
02:30:35,550 --> 02:30:36,510
Thanks for listening!

2934
02:30:37,930 --> 02:30:38,310
Yeah.

2935
02:30:38,990 --> 02:30:39,770
Alright then.

2936
02:30:40,730 --> 02:30:40,910
Alright.

2937
02:30:42,270 --> 02:30:42,750
This...

2938
02:30:42,750 --> 02:30:44,870
These girls are fucked up, man!

2939
02:30:45,430 --> 02:30:46,490
What's going on here?

2940
02:30:48,210 --> 02:30:48,690
Hmm.

2941
02:30:49,890 --> 02:30:51,190
I like your last one better.

2942
02:30:52,330 --> 02:30:52,670
Huh?

2943
02:30:53,510 --> 02:30:53,990
Really?

2944
02:30:54,790 --> 02:30:55,510
Well, yeah.

2945
02:30:55,730 --> 02:30:57,570
I can tell you were a little more daring with this one.

2946
02:30:58,150 --> 02:30:59,830
But you're really not good enough for that yet.

2947
02:30:59,830 --> 02:31:00,910
It fell flat.

2948
02:31:02,090 --> 02:31:04,970
That may be true, but I just wanted to try something different.

2949
02:31:05,830 --> 02:31:07,430
I'm still figuring this all out!

2950
02:31:08,430 --> 02:31:11,030
I mean, I always like poems that aren't trying too hard.

2951
02:31:11,990 --> 02:31:17,470
I hate when people trying to sound fancy or have more meaning, just by using annoying and complicated language.

2952
02:31:18,430 --> 02:31:20,330
Just make it simple, cute, and to the point!

2953
02:31:22,630 --> 02:31:25,010
Yuri's head over heels for all this cryptic nonsense.

2954
02:31:25,590 --> 02:31:27,330
But I see right through that bullshit.

2955
02:31:28,890 --> 02:31:32,630
Making Yorita look so heartfelt, this deep meaning is just an excuse to have no meaning at all!

2956
02:31:33,790 --> 02:31:35,770
Well, I guess that's one way to look at it.

2957
02:31:36,750 --> 02:31:38,290
Well, everyone has their own opinion.

2958
02:31:38,990 --> 02:31:40,630
But my opinion is the best opinion.

2959
02:31:40,970 --> 02:31:42,450
I'm sure you figured that out already.

2960
02:31:44,950 --> 02:31:46,170
Anyway, here's my poem.

2961
02:31:46,930 --> 02:31:47,870
Maybe you'll learn something.

2962
02:31:50,490 --> 02:31:52,350
Amy likes spiders.

2963
02:31:52,830 --> 02:31:54,170
You know what I heard about Amy?

2964
02:31:54,850 --> 02:31:56,250
Amy likes spiders.

2965
02:31:56,990 --> 02:31:59,390
Icky, prickly, hairy, ugly spiders.

2966
02:31:59,950 --> 02:32:01,230
That's why I'm not friends with her.

2967
02:32:02,090 --> 02:32:03,670
Amy has a cute singing voice.

2968
02:32:04,070 --> 02:32:05,930
I heard her singing my favorite love song.

2969
02:32:06,390 --> 02:32:10,790
Every time she sang the chorus, my heart would pound to the rhythm of the words.

2970
02:32:11,190 --> 02:32:12,330
But she likes spiders.

2971
02:32:12,770 --> 02:32:14,050
That's why I'm not friends with her.

2972
02:32:16,390 --> 02:32:18,070
One time, I hurt my leg really bad.

2973
02:32:18,610 --> 02:32:20,090
Amy helped me up and took me to the nurse.

2974
02:32:20,510 --> 02:32:22,050
I tried not to let her touch me.

2975
02:32:22,370 --> 02:32:25,170
She likes spiders, so her hands are probably gross.

2976
02:32:25,570 --> 02:32:26,750
That's why I'm not friends with her.

2977
02:32:28,130 --> 02:32:29,290
Amy has a lot of friends.

2978
02:32:29,730 --> 02:32:31,370
I always see her talking to people.

2979
02:32:31,690 --> 02:32:33,190
She probably talks about spiders.

2980
02:32:33,910 --> 02:32:35,750
What if her friends start to like spiders, too?

2981
02:32:36,850 --> 02:32:38,030
That's why I'm not friends with her.

2982
02:32:39,030 --> 02:32:40,610
It doesn't matter if she has other hobbies.

2983
02:32:40,990 --> 02:32:42,450
It doesn't matter if she keeps it private.

2984
02:32:42,950 --> 02:32:44,590
It doesn't matter if she doesn't hurt anyone.

2985
02:32:45,030 --> 02:32:45,610
It's gross.

2986
02:32:46,050 --> 02:32:46,670
She's gross.

2987
02:32:47,190 --> 02:32:49,050
The world is better off without spider lovers.

2988
02:32:49,710 --> 02:32:50,890
And I'm gonna tell everyone.

2989
02:32:52,470 --> 02:32:53,230
Fuck, Amy.

2990
02:32:53,370 --> 02:32:54,130
Honestly, I agree.

2991
02:32:55,170 --> 02:32:56,190
Spiders are horrible.

2992
02:32:57,290 --> 02:32:58,150
Not bad, right?

2993
02:32:58,610 --> 02:32:59,870
I gotta say...

2994
02:33:00,490 --> 02:33:02,650
It's quite a bit longer than yesterday's, and...

2995
02:33:05,130 --> 02:33:06,110
I like it!

2996
02:33:07,010 --> 02:33:07,890
Very nice!

2997
02:33:09,290 --> 02:33:10,590
Yesterday's was way too short.

2998
02:33:11,450 --> 02:33:12,650
It was just warming up.

2999
02:33:13,730 --> 02:33:15,650
I hope you didn't think that was the best I could do.

3000
02:33:16,590 --> 02:33:17,830
No, of course not.

3001
02:33:18,730 --> 02:33:20,950
Anyway, the message is pretty straightforward in this poem.

3002
02:33:21,770 --> 02:33:23,410
I doubt I have to explain it.

3003
02:33:24,430 --> 02:33:27,850
Sometimes you can explain complicated issues with much simpler analogies.

3004
02:33:29,370 --> 02:33:31,350
And it helps people realize how stupid they're being.

3005
02:33:32,130 --> 02:33:32,690
Wait a minute.

3006
02:33:34,290 --> 02:33:36,230
Unless the spider is...

3007
02:33:36,230 --> 02:33:37,430
A metaphor?

3008
02:33:41,190 --> 02:33:42,150
What could that mean?

3009
02:33:45,660 --> 02:33:48,380
Maybe the spider is a metaphor for an ant.

3010
02:33:49,840 --> 02:33:50,780
Think about it.

3011
02:33:52,600 --> 02:33:56,160
Like, anyone would agree that the subject of this poem is an ignorant jerk.

3012
02:33:57,160 --> 02:33:57,680
Exactly.

3013
02:33:58,100 --> 02:33:58,800
That's what I meant.

3014
02:33:59,900 --> 02:34:01,020
Do you know people like that?

3015
02:34:02,180 --> 02:34:02,940
Of course!

3016
02:34:03,240 --> 02:34:05,520
It's about how everyone thinks my...

3017
02:34:06,240 --> 02:34:07,060
That doesn't matter.

3018
02:34:07,320 --> 02:34:08,580
It can't be about anything.

3019
02:34:09,780 --> 02:34:11,520
I wrote it to be easy to relate to.

3020
02:34:12,600 --> 02:34:15,060
Everyone has some kind of weird hobby or a guilty pleasure.

3021
02:34:16,140 --> 02:34:19,260
Something that you're afraid if people find out they'd make fun of you or think less of you.

3022
02:34:20,240 --> 02:34:21,600
But that just makes people stupid.

3023
02:34:22,440 --> 02:34:25,620
Who cares what someone likes as long as they're not hurting anyone and it makes them happy?

3024
02:34:26,540 --> 02:34:27,740
Fucking damn right, dude!

3025
02:34:28,580 --> 02:34:29,420
Couldn't agree more!

3026
02:34:30,600 --> 02:34:33,580
I think people really need to learn to respect others for liking weird things.

3027
02:34:34,120 --> 02:34:34,800
Fucking A!

3028
02:34:36,460 --> 02:34:36,820
Huh.

3029
02:34:37,180 --> 02:34:37,820
That's funny.

3030
02:34:39,120 --> 02:34:41,040
Yuri wrote about something similar today.

3031
02:34:42,420 --> 02:34:42,900
Huh?

3032
02:34:43,820 --> 02:34:44,840
Did you say Yuri?

3033
02:34:45,960 --> 02:34:46,440
Yeah.

3034
02:34:47,940 --> 02:34:50,500
She said her poem was about an unusual hobby of hers.

3035
02:34:50,860 --> 02:34:51,980
Oh, that's what I said.

3036
02:34:52,720 --> 02:34:55,340
I didn't really get it, but she said something similar to you.

3037
02:34:56,260 --> 02:34:59,000
That people shouldn't make each other feel insecure about those things.

3038
02:35:00,120 --> 02:35:00,600
Really?

3039
02:35:02,040 --> 02:35:02,520
Well...

3040
02:35:03,220 --> 02:35:07,340
I mean, Yuri's pretty weird, so I wouldn't doubt that she's got some weird hobbies.

3041
02:35:08,660 --> 02:35:10,020
Not that there's anything wrong with that.

3042
02:35:11,760 --> 02:35:14,720
Uh, it's not like I would judge her or anything.

3043
02:35:16,060 --> 02:35:17,720
Matsuki has trouble finding words.

3044
02:35:19,140 --> 02:35:21,260
I guess I should try not to be so mean to her.

3045
02:35:22,240 --> 02:35:24,740
If she feels insecure about her weird behaviors and stuff.

3046
02:35:25,520 --> 02:35:28,540
I mean, I always hate people who make me feel insecure.

3047
02:35:32,320 --> 02:35:34,980
But the way you put it, it sounds like she's learned her lesson.

3048
02:35:35,760 --> 02:35:36,940
Well, I would say so.

3049
02:35:37,860 --> 02:35:41,740
Even if her writing style is really different, I'm sure she'll appreciate the message in your poem.

3050
02:35:42,880 --> 02:35:44,360
It's what I do best after all.

3051
02:35:45,200 --> 02:35:47,820
I don't like writing unless there's a good message to take away from it.

3052
02:35:49,140 --> 02:35:50,920
Like, conveying emotions is important.

3053
02:35:51,620 --> 02:35:53,720
But I want to make people think, not just feel.

3054
02:35:54,760 --> 02:35:55,380
Remember that.

3055
02:35:56,260 --> 02:35:58,880
I'm gonna write a good one for tomorrow too, so look forward to it.

3056
02:36:02,610 --> 02:36:03,330
Okay, everyone!

3057
02:36:04,650 --> 02:36:06,350
We're all done reading each other's poems, right?

3058
02:36:07,510 --> 02:36:09,670
I have something extra planned today!

3059
02:36:10,290 --> 02:36:12,510
So if everyone could come sit at the front of the room...

3060
02:36:13,810 --> 02:36:15,030
Is this about the festival?

3061
02:36:16,270 --> 02:36:17,410
Well, sort of.

3062
02:36:19,130 --> 02:36:21,010
Did we really have to do something for the festival?

3063
02:36:21,930 --> 02:36:24,470
It's not like we can put together anything good in just a few days.

3064
02:36:25,310 --> 02:36:28,110
We'll just end up embarrassing ourselves instead of getting any new members.

3065
02:36:29,290 --> 02:36:30,850
That's a concern of mine as well.

3066
02:36:31,910 --> 02:36:34,290
I don't really do well with last-minute preparations.

3067
02:36:35,590 --> 02:36:36,930
Don't worry so much!

3068
02:36:37,730 --> 02:36:39,270
We're gonna keep it simple, okay?

3069
02:36:40,530 --> 02:36:42,670
We won't need much more than a few decorations!

3070
02:36:44,310 --> 02:36:45,830
Sayori has been working on posters.

3071
02:36:46,230 --> 02:36:49,230
And now I have to sign some pamphlets that we can give out during the event!

3072
02:36:50,270 --> 02:36:51,450
Okay, that's great and all.

3073
02:36:52,230 --> 02:36:54,810
Well, that doesn't tell us what we're actually gonna be doing for the event.

3074
02:36:55,870 --> 02:36:56,930
Ah, sorry.

3075
02:36:57,650 --> 02:36:59,130
I thought you heard about it already.

3076
02:37:00,070 --> 02:37:01,390
We're going to be performing!

3077
02:37:03,150 --> 02:37:03,550
Performing?

3078
02:37:04,670 --> 02:37:05,850
P...

3079
02:37:05,850 --> 02:37:07,070
Um, Monica?

3080
02:37:08,350 --> 02:37:08,770
Yeah!

3081
02:37:09,370 --> 02:37:11,610
We're gonna be having a poetry performance!

3082
02:37:12,970 --> 02:37:15,890
Each of us are gonna choose a poem to recite during the event!

3083
02:37:16,730 --> 02:37:20,410
But the cool part is, we're also gonna let everyone else come up and recite poems too!

3084
02:37:21,890 --> 02:37:25,350
Sayori's putting it all on the posters in case anyone wants to prepare ahead of time.

3085
02:37:28,110 --> 02:37:31,690
Sayori, who's been coloring a poster, holds it up for us to see!

3086
02:37:33,210 --> 02:37:34,030
Are you kidding me, Monica?

3087
02:37:34,810 --> 02:37:35,670
You didn't...

3088
02:37:35,670 --> 02:37:38,110
You didn't already start putting those posters up, did you?

3089
02:37:39,270 --> 02:37:40,990
Uh, well, I did.

3090
02:37:42,190 --> 02:37:43,990
Do you really think it's a bad of an idea?

3091
02:37:45,530 --> 02:37:46,090
Well, no.

3092
02:37:47,190 --> 02:37:48,170
It's not a bad idea.

3093
02:37:49,090 --> 02:37:50,430
But I didn't sign up for this, you know?

3094
02:37:51,290 --> 02:37:54,570
There is no way I'm gonna be performing in front of a group of people like that!

3095
02:37:55,510 --> 02:37:56,070
I...

3096
02:37:56,070 --> 02:37:57,550
I agree with Natsuki.

3097
02:37:58,230 --> 02:38:02,150
I could never, in my life, do something like that.

3098
02:38:03,390 --> 02:38:05,610
Imagining it, Yuri shakes her head in fear.

3099
02:38:06,690 --> 02:38:07,250
Guys!

3100
02:38:08,270 --> 02:38:09,610
No, Sayori!

3101
02:38:10,150 --> 02:38:11,990
I understand where they're coming from.

3102
02:38:12,770 --> 02:38:18,110
Remember that Natsuki and Yuri have never shared their poems with anyone, until just a couple days ago.

3103
02:38:18,970 --> 02:38:24,270
It's a lot to ask for them to recite their poems out loud, to a whole room full of people!

3104
02:38:25,110 --> 02:38:26,730
I guess I kinda overlooked that.

3105
02:38:27,610 --> 02:38:28,990
So, I'm sorry.

3106
02:38:32,500 --> 02:38:34,200
I still think we should give it our best!

3107
02:38:34,940 --> 02:38:38,140
We're the only ones responsible for the fate of this club!

3108
02:38:39,020 --> 02:38:45,320
If we start the event and put on a good performance, and it will inspire others to do the same.

3109
02:38:46,260 --> 02:38:51,020
And the more people who perform, the better we'll be able to show everyone what literature is all about!

3110
02:38:52,180 --> 02:38:52,660
Yeah!

3111
02:38:53,680 --> 02:38:55,420
It's about expressing your feelings.

3112
02:38:56,580 --> 02:38:57,820
Being intimate with yourself.

3113
02:38:58,600 --> 02:38:59,900
Finding new horizons.

3114
02:39:00,660 --> 02:39:01,760
And having fun!

3115
02:39:02,960 --> 02:39:03,640
That's right!

3116
02:39:04,320 --> 02:39:07,120
And it's those reasons that we're all in this club today!

3117
02:39:07,880 --> 02:39:09,420
Don't you want to share that with others?

3118
02:39:10,260 --> 02:39:13,600
To inspire them to find the same feelings that brought you here in the first place?

3119
02:39:14,500 --> 02:39:15,440
I know you do.

3120
02:39:16,180 --> 02:39:17,300
I know we all do.

3121
02:39:18,800 --> 02:39:22,640
And if all it takes is standing in front of the room for two minutes and reciting a poem...

3122
02:39:23,760 --> 02:39:25,640
And I know you can do it!

3123
02:39:29,410 --> 02:39:31,510
Natsuki and Yuri remain silent.

3124
02:39:32,550 --> 02:39:33,810
Sayori looks worried.

3125
02:39:34,650 --> 02:39:36,410
I guess that leaves me no choice.

3126
02:39:37,990 --> 02:39:38,650
I agree.

3127
02:39:39,770 --> 02:39:41,190
I don't think it's too much to ask.

3128
02:39:42,410 --> 02:39:46,070
I think that Sayori and Monika have been trying really hard to get new members.

3129
02:39:47,710 --> 02:39:49,990
Where the hell did I get these opinions from all of a sudden?

3130
02:39:50,710 --> 02:39:52,610
The least we can do is help them out a little bit.

3131
02:39:54,110 --> 02:39:56,080
Well, maybe, but...

3132
02:39:57,530 --> 02:40:00,170
It looks like Natsuki doesn't have any arguments left.

3133
02:40:02,170 --> 02:40:03,330
Okay, fine!

3134
02:40:03,790 --> 02:40:05,590
I guess I'll just have to get it over with.

3135
02:40:06,590 --> 02:40:07,250
All right!

3136
02:40:08,930 --> 02:40:10,050
Thanks, Natsuki!

3137
02:40:10,730 --> 02:40:11,890
What about you, Yuri?

3138
02:40:13,870 --> 02:40:17,610
Yuri dejectedly glances around at everyone else's expected faces.

3139
02:40:20,890 --> 02:40:23,110
I guess I don't really have a choice.

3140
02:40:23,950 --> 02:40:24,490
Haha!

3141
02:40:24,850 --> 02:40:25,750
That's everyone!

3142
02:40:26,490 --> 02:40:27,670
You're the best, Yuri!

3143
02:40:28,730 --> 02:40:31,110
This club is seriously gonna be the death of me.

3144
02:40:31,930 --> 02:40:32,810
Oh gosh!

3145
02:40:33,810 --> 02:40:35,270
You'll be fine, Yuri!

3146
02:40:36,350 --> 02:40:39,690
But anyway, let's move on to the main event!

3147
02:40:40,650 --> 02:40:43,850
I want each of you to choose a poem of yours!

3148
02:40:44,810 --> 02:40:47,250
We're gonna practice reciting them in front of each other!

3149
02:40:48,430 --> 02:40:49,270
No way!

3150
02:40:51,490 --> 02:40:51,910
Monica...

3151
02:40:51,910 --> 02:40:53,470
This is too sudden!

3152
02:40:55,110 --> 02:40:59,150
Well, if you can't recite your poem in front of the club, how do you expect to do it in front of strangers?

3153
02:41:01,230 --> 02:41:02,070
Oh no...

3154
02:41:02,070 --> 02:41:02,590
Don't worry!

3155
02:41:03,410 --> 02:41:05,710
I'll start off to help everyone feel a little more comfortable!

3156
02:41:07,390 --> 02:41:08,250
Can I go next?

3157
02:41:09,530 --> 02:41:10,330
Of course!

3158
02:41:11,290 --> 02:41:12,240
Now, let's see...

3159
02:41:13,150 --> 02:41:16,710
Monica flips through her notebook to the specific poem she has in mind for herself.

3160
02:41:17,610 --> 02:41:19,070
She then stands behind the podium.

3161
02:41:20,230 --> 02:41:21,910
The title of this poem is...

3162
02:41:22,650 --> 02:41:24,110
The way they fly!

3163
02:41:25,130 --> 02:41:25,630
Ahem!

3164
02:41:26,590 --> 02:41:28,250
Monica begins reciting her poem.

3165
02:41:28,970 --> 02:41:31,670
Her clear, confident voice fills the room.

3166
02:41:32,230 --> 02:41:35,110
More than that, her inflection is pristine.

3167
02:41:35,750 --> 02:41:41,310
She knows exactly how to apply emotion behind each line she recites, bringing the words to life.

3168
02:41:42,130 --> 02:41:43,790
Is this something she's done before?

3169
02:41:44,210 --> 02:41:45,730
Or is she simply a natural?

3170
02:41:46,410 --> 02:41:47,790
I glance around me.

3171
02:41:48,410 --> 02:41:50,050
Everyone has their eyes on Monica.

3172
02:41:51,070 --> 02:41:52,250
Sayori looks amazed.

3173
02:41:53,150 --> 02:41:55,970
Yuri has an intense expression on her face that I don't understand.

3174
02:41:56,910 --> 02:42:00,250
Finally, Monica finishes the recitation.

3175
02:42:00,850 --> 02:42:01,950
The four of us applaud.

3176
02:42:02,690 --> 02:42:04,350
Monica takes a breath and smiles.

3177
02:42:05,690 --> 02:42:06,210
That...

3178
02:42:06,210 --> 02:42:07,570
That was so good, Monica!

3179
02:42:08,450 --> 02:42:08,710
Ha!

3180
02:42:09,030 --> 02:42:09,930
Thank you very much!

3181
02:42:10,410 --> 02:42:12,170
I was just hoping to set a good example.

3182
02:42:12,990 --> 02:42:14,350
Are you ready to go next, Sayori?

3183
02:42:15,510 --> 02:42:15,950
I...

3184
02:42:16,490 --> 02:42:17,310
I'll go next.

3185
02:42:19,030 --> 02:42:19,470
What?!

3186
02:42:19,470 --> 02:42:21,050
Yuri's fired up all of a sudden!

3187
02:42:22,190 --> 02:42:24,770
Yuri clutches a sheet of paper between her hands and stands up.

3188
02:42:25,770 --> 02:42:28,290
Keeping her head down, she walks quickly over to the podium.

3189
02:42:29,350 --> 02:42:30,890
This poem is called...

3190
02:42:32,250 --> 02:42:34,250
Yuri anxiously glances at each of us.

3191
02:42:35,350 --> 02:42:36,410
You can do it, Yuri.

3192
02:42:37,550 --> 02:42:37,990
It...

3193
02:42:37,990 --> 02:42:38,830
It's called...

3194
02:42:40,370 --> 02:42:41,490
After image...

3195
02:42:41,490 --> 02:42:42,590
Of a crimson eye.

3196
02:42:44,150 --> 02:42:46,670
Yuri's voice shakes as she starts reading the poem.

3197
02:42:47,750 --> 02:42:50,670
Just a moment ago, she practically refused to do this.

3198
02:42:51,630 --> 02:42:53,910
Why is she suddenly putting in so much effort?

3199
02:42:55,070 --> 02:42:58,090
As Yuri gets past the first couple of lines, her voice changes.

3200
02:42:58,870 --> 02:43:02,030
It's almost like what happens when Yuri gets absorbed into her books.

3201
02:43:02,770 --> 02:43:07,890
Her quivering words transform into the sharp syllables of a fierce and confident woman.

3202
02:43:08,630 --> 02:43:13,530
The poem is full of twists and turns and a structure that she enunciates with perfect timing.

3203
02:43:14,470 --> 02:43:18,970
This must be a rare glimpse into the whirling fire Yuri keeps concealed inside her head.

3204
02:43:19,950 --> 02:43:21,270
Suddenly, she's finished.

3205
02:43:21,910 --> 02:43:23,030
Everyone is stunned.

3206
02:43:23,950 --> 02:43:26,010
Yuri snaps back into reality...

3207
02:43:26,010 --> 02:43:27,950
And glances around her.

3208
02:43:28,250 --> 02:43:30,210
As if she bewildered even herself.

3209
02:43:31,730 --> 02:43:32,250
I...

3210
02:43:32,890 --> 02:43:34,450
It's up to me to save this situation.

3211
02:43:35,350 --> 02:43:37,010
I'm the first to start applauding.

3212
02:43:37,910 --> 02:43:41,250
Everyone joins me afterward, and we give Yuri the recognition she deserves.

3213
02:43:51,890 --> 02:43:59,410
It's not that we didn't want to applaud for her, but we were cut off so guard that we must have forgotten.

3214
02:44:00,510 --> 02:44:04,530
As we applaud, Yuri holds the poem to her chest and rushes back into her seat.

3215
02:44:06,290 --> 02:44:06,810
Yuri!

3216
02:44:07,210 --> 02:44:08,450
That was really good!

3217
02:44:09,050 --> 02:44:10,190
Thank you for sharing!

3218
02:44:11,830 --> 02:44:13,630
Looks like Yuri is down for the count.

3219
02:44:14,690 --> 02:44:15,210
Okay!

3220
02:44:15,970 --> 02:44:17,330
I guess I'm next, then!

3221
02:44:18,570 --> 02:44:21,290
Sayori hops out of her chair and cheerfully walks to the podium.

3222
02:44:22,330 --> 02:44:23,290
This one's called...

3223
02:44:24,110 --> 02:44:25,010
My Meadow.

3224
02:44:28,150 --> 02:44:29,330
Sorry, I giggled.

3225
02:44:31,930 --> 02:44:32,410
Sayori!

3226
02:44:33,610 --> 02:44:34,970
It's a lot harder than I thought!

3227
02:44:35,910 --> 02:44:37,570
How did you guys do it so easily?

3228
02:44:39,150 --> 02:44:39,470
Ah!

3229
02:44:40,170 --> 02:44:42,390
Try not to think of it like you're reciting to other people.

3230
02:44:43,450 --> 02:44:45,630
Imagine you're reciting it to yourself.

3231
02:44:46,130 --> 02:44:47,710
Like, in front of a mirror.

3232
02:44:47,930 --> 02:44:49,070
Or in your own head.

3233
02:44:49,770 --> 02:44:52,590
It's your poem, so it'll come out best that way.

3234
02:44:53,650 --> 02:44:55,050
I see, I see.

3235
02:44:55,830 --> 02:44:56,630
Okay, then.

3236
02:44:57,550 --> 02:44:58,810
Sayori begins her poem.

3237
02:44:59,530 --> 02:45:02,930
Somehow it feels like her soft voice was made as a perfect match.

3238
02:45:03,530 --> 02:45:06,390
The poem isn't aimlessly cheery like Sayori is.

3239
02:45:06,910 --> 02:45:08,630
It's serene and bittersweet.

3240
02:45:09,270 --> 02:45:12,730
If I were to read this on paper, I probably wouldn't think much of it.

3241
02:45:13,390 --> 02:45:17,430
But hearing it come from Sayori's voice almost gives it a whole new meaning.

3242
02:45:17,830 --> 02:45:19,470
I mean, that's what poems should do, right?

3243
02:45:20,510 --> 02:45:22,230
That's like what makes poetry poetry.

3244
02:45:23,610 --> 02:45:25,250
But of course, I mean, also the writing.

3245
02:45:29,170 --> 02:45:32,690
But like, saying it in the right way definitely adds to it, right?

3246
02:45:35,430 --> 02:45:38,130
Maybe this is what Sayori meant when she said she likes my poems.

3247
02:45:39,350 --> 02:45:42,770
It's like I get to reach more deeply into someone I thought I knew through and through.

3248
02:45:43,750 --> 02:45:45,850
Sayori finishes, and we applaud.

3249
02:45:46,810 --> 02:45:47,950
I did it!

3250
02:45:48,450 --> 02:45:49,990
Good job, Sayori!

3251
02:45:50,890 --> 02:45:53,330
Hehe, even Oliver liked it.

3252
02:45:53,630 --> 02:45:54,610
What do you mean, even me?

3253
02:45:55,270 --> 02:45:56,610
I guess that's a good sign.

3254
02:45:57,530 --> 02:45:58,950
What does that even mean?

3255
02:46:00,290 --> 02:46:02,550
It came out nicely, Sayori.

3256
02:46:03,570 --> 02:46:06,010
The atmosphere of the poem fits you really nicely.

3257
02:46:06,890 --> 02:46:11,250
But it might be that other poems wouldn't work quite as well with that kind of delivery.

3258
02:46:12,490 --> 02:46:12,970
Eh?

3259
02:46:13,670 --> 02:46:14,870
I don't really understand.

3260
02:46:16,350 --> 02:46:21,230
In other words, I've seen poems of yours where that sort of gentle delivery wouldn't work as well.

3261
02:46:22,070 --> 02:46:25,650
They might need a little more force behind them, depending on what you're reading.

3262
02:46:26,830 --> 02:46:27,950
Oh, I know what you mean.

3263
02:46:28,930 --> 02:46:31,750
That's, well, I've been practicing that kind of thing.

3264
02:46:32,670 --> 02:46:34,650
It's just embarrassing to do in front of everyone.

3265
02:46:37,210 --> 02:46:40,530
The next time, I'm gonna make you pick a poem that challenges you a little more.

3266
02:46:41,390 --> 02:46:43,470
We don't have much time before the festival, you know?

3267
02:46:44,370 --> 02:46:44,850
Okay.

3268
02:46:46,290 --> 02:46:47,510
Now, who's next?

3269
02:46:48,830 --> 02:46:49,310
Natsuki?

3270
02:46:52,390 --> 02:46:53,890
Don't make me go before Oliver.

3271
02:46:54,610 --> 02:46:56,810
It's not like I can compare it to you guys anyway.

3272
02:46:58,370 --> 02:47:01,930
Might as well let Oliver lower everyone's standards a little before I have to do it.

3273
02:47:03,130 --> 02:47:03,610
Wow!

3274
02:47:05,650 --> 02:47:06,690
She didn't go there.

3275
02:47:07,990 --> 02:47:08,570
Natsuki!

3276
02:47:09,650 --> 02:47:10,350
It's fine!

3277
02:47:10,990 --> 02:47:11,630
It's fine.

3278
02:47:12,770 --> 02:47:14,290
I might as well get it over with.

3279
02:47:14,890 --> 02:47:17,290
But it's not like I have much of a selection of what to read.

3280
02:47:18,350 --> 02:47:20,050
I'll just have to go with what I wrote for today.

3281
02:47:21,090 --> 02:47:23,070
I stand up and step in front of the podium.

3282
02:47:24,650 --> 02:47:28,010
Everyone has their eyes on me, making me feel terribly awkward.

3283
02:47:28,930 --> 02:47:30,250
I recite my poem.

3284
02:47:31,350 --> 02:47:35,630
Since I'm not exactly confident in my own writing, it's hard to put energy into it.

3285
02:47:36,610 --> 02:47:39,130
Despite that, once I finish, I receive applause anyway!

3286
02:47:40,730 --> 02:47:42,990
Sorry, I'm not really good as everyone else.

3287
02:47:44,030 --> 02:47:45,690
Don't worry about it so much!

3288
02:47:46,470 --> 02:47:50,310
I think it's less about your abilities and more about your lack of confidence in your writing.

3289
02:47:51,290 --> 02:47:53,230
That's something that'll improve over time, though.

3290
02:47:55,170 --> 02:47:56,090
Yeah, maybe.

3291
02:47:57,050 --> 02:47:57,790
All right, then.

3292
02:47:58,470 --> 02:47:59,850
I'll just leave you, Natsuki.

3293
02:48:00,730 --> 02:48:01,630
Yeah, yeah.

3294
02:48:02,290 --> 02:48:03,170
I'm going.

3295
02:48:04,550 --> 02:48:07,810
Natsuki begrudgingly gets out of her seat and makes her way to the podium.

3296
02:48:09,750 --> 02:48:11,430
The poem is called...

3297
02:48:11,430 --> 02:48:12,350
It's called...

3298
02:48:13,450 --> 02:48:14,790
Why are you all looking at me?

3299
02:48:15,730 --> 02:48:16,950
Because you're presenting.

3300
02:48:19,390 --> 02:48:21,050
Anyway, the poem is called Jump!

3301
02:48:22,410 --> 02:48:23,530
Natsuki takes a breath.

3302
02:48:24,930 --> 02:48:28,970
Once she starts reciting the poem, her sour attitude disappears a little.

3303
02:48:29,830 --> 02:48:33,450
While she's still a little unenthused, her poem has a rhythm and rhyme to it.

3304
02:48:34,330 --> 02:48:39,250
It's Natsuki's trademark style, and it works surprisingly well when spoken aloud.

3305
02:48:40,310 --> 02:48:43,890
The words feel like they bounce up and down as if giving life to the poem.

3306
02:48:45,010 --> 02:48:47,010
Natsuki finishes and everyone applauds.

3307
02:48:47,550 --> 02:48:51,670
Is that referencing the bouncing up and down that happens when I pick words?

3308
02:48:52,330 --> 02:48:53,470
Kind of feels like that.

3309
02:48:54,690 --> 02:48:55,750
She huffs back to her seat.

3310
02:48:56,850 --> 02:48:58,430
That wasn't so bad, was it?

3311
02:48:59,310 --> 02:49:00,350
Easy for you to say.

3312
02:49:01,230 --> 02:49:02,690
You'd better not make me do that again.

3313
02:49:04,050 --> 02:49:06,530
Uh, aren't you supposed to do it tomorrow?

3314
02:49:08,930 --> 02:49:12,010
You at least feel prepared enough to recite a poem in front of other people?

3315
02:49:13,270 --> 02:49:16,390
I mean, doing in front of other people will be way easier.

3316
02:49:17,110 --> 02:49:19,430
I can put on whatever face I want for other people.

3317
02:49:20,410 --> 02:49:24,150
But when it's just my friends, it's just embarrassing.

3318
02:49:25,370 --> 02:49:26,770
That's a surprise, Natsuki.

3319
02:49:28,170 --> 02:49:30,010
I think it would be the other way around for me.

3320
02:49:31,230 --> 02:49:32,490
Well, that's just how it is, so.

3321
02:49:33,410 --> 02:49:37,610
Well, I guess in that case, you won't have much to worry about for the festival.

3322
02:49:38,810 --> 02:49:41,510
That said, I want to thank everyone for coming through.

3323
02:49:42,510 --> 02:49:46,030
It might be hard, but I hope you all have an idea of what it's like now.

3324
02:49:46,870 --> 02:49:50,290
Make sure you pick a poem and get enough practice before the festival, okay?

3325
02:49:51,430 --> 02:49:52,790
I'll be making pamphlets.

3326
02:49:53,350 --> 02:49:55,470
So let me know ahead of time what you'll be reciting.

3327
02:49:56,770 --> 02:49:57,210
Jeez.

3328
02:49:58,410 --> 02:50:00,950
I should probably find some other poem to recite instead.

3329
02:50:02,130 --> 02:50:02,950
That's fine too.

3330
02:50:03,570 --> 02:50:04,890
It doesn't have to be your own.

3331
02:50:06,190 --> 02:50:09,410
I'm already pleasantly surprised that you're putting in all this effort for the club.

3332
02:50:10,730 --> 02:50:11,710
So am I.

3333
02:50:12,630 --> 02:50:13,950
It makes me really happy.

3334
02:50:15,010 --> 02:50:16,190
Stop that post.

3335
02:50:16,370 --> 02:50:17,450
I swear to god, I will...

3336
02:50:17,450 --> 02:50:19,550
I don't know what I will do, but I will do something.

3337
02:50:20,750 --> 02:50:21,230
Ah, yeah.

3338
02:50:21,830 --> 02:50:22,690
No problem.

3339
02:50:25,230 --> 02:50:26,250
Okay, everyone.

3340
02:50:27,310 --> 02:50:29,270
I think that's about it for today.

3341
02:50:30,690 --> 02:50:35,270
I know the festival is coming up, but let's try to write poems for tomorrow as well.

3342
02:50:36,170 --> 02:50:38,510
It's been working out really nicely so far.

3343
02:50:38,950 --> 02:50:40,410
So I'd like to continue that.

3344
02:50:41,350 --> 02:50:44,210
As for the festival, we'll finish planning tomorrow.

3345
02:50:44,570 --> 02:50:46,310
And then we'll have the weekend to prepare.

3346
02:50:47,270 --> 02:50:48,350
I'm getting the feeling...

3347
02:50:50,010 --> 02:50:51,390
that there's not gonna be any gameplay.

3348
02:50:51,870 --> 02:50:53,170
I think it's dawned on me.

3349
02:50:53,390 --> 02:50:54,170
This is what it is.

3350
02:50:57,480 --> 02:50:58,600
Monday is the big day!

3351
02:50:59,680 --> 02:51:01,180
I can't wait.

3352
02:51:02,160 --> 02:51:03,100
I can't wait.

3353
02:51:03,100 --> 02:51:03,400
I can do this.

3354
02:51:04,000 --> 02:51:05,040
I can do this.

3355
02:51:06,100 --> 02:51:06,860
All right.

3356
02:51:08,360 --> 02:51:09,880
I stand up.

3357
02:51:10,980 --> 02:51:14,820
There's no way I'll be able to find the same enthusiasm as Sayori and Monica.

3358
02:51:15,620 --> 02:51:17,020
But I'll do my best to get through it.

3359
02:51:18,020 --> 02:51:19,540
If it's for the sake of the club.

3360
02:51:20,600 --> 02:51:20,920
Right.

3361
02:51:22,160 --> 02:51:23,140
And impressing Monica.

3362
02:51:23,340 --> 02:51:24,080
What do you mean Monica?

3363
02:51:25,200 --> 02:51:27,400
I thought we were doing Yuri.

3364
02:51:29,200 --> 02:51:30,560
Then I'll have to do my best.

3365
02:51:32,020 --> 02:51:33,120
Ready to go, Sayori?

3366
02:51:34,180 --> 02:51:34,620
Yep.

3367
02:51:35,840 --> 02:51:37,960
Look at you two always going home together like that.

3368
02:51:38,800 --> 02:51:40,360
It's kind of adorable, isn't it?

3369
02:51:42,260 --> 02:51:43,600
Jeez, guys.

3370
02:51:44,660 --> 02:51:46,800
Don't make such a big deal out of it.

3371
02:51:48,120 --> 02:51:49,600
It must be a little nice though.

3372
02:51:50,780 --> 02:51:51,220
Well.

3373
02:51:54,180 --> 02:51:55,860
How am I supposed to respond to that?

3374
02:51:57,300 --> 02:51:58,200
It's okay, Oliver.

3375
02:51:58,420 --> 02:51:59,280
You don't have to say it.

3376
02:52:00,740 --> 02:52:01,180
Whatever.

3377
02:52:01,520 --> 02:52:02,300
Let's go already.

3378
02:52:05,320 --> 02:52:06,480
I thought it though.

3379
02:52:07,360 --> 02:52:09,700
I walk home with Sayori once more.

3380
02:52:10,360 --> 02:52:11,740
These are some nice houses.

3381
02:52:13,360 --> 02:52:16,720
Even though it's been only a few days, a lot of things have already changed.

3382
02:52:17,560 --> 02:52:21,180
But today, Sayori is being a little quieter than usual on the way home.

3383
02:52:21,580 --> 02:52:22,000
Oh yeah?

3384
02:52:26,190 --> 02:52:26,970
Why's that?

3385
02:52:28,650 --> 02:52:29,630
Hey, Sayori!

3386
02:52:32,070 --> 02:52:33,950
Sorry, I was spacing out.

3387
02:52:35,170 --> 02:52:35,710
Ah.

3388
02:52:36,090 --> 02:52:37,050
No wonder.

3389
02:52:39,650 --> 02:52:42,530
I was thinking about something from earlier.

3390
02:52:43,610 --> 02:52:44,950
I like how we get to...

3391
02:52:46,050 --> 02:52:46,530
I...

3392
02:52:46,530 --> 02:52:47,390
I mean...

3393
02:52:48,490 --> 02:52:50,130
Sayori fumbles with her words.

3394
02:52:51,430 --> 02:52:54,130
So let's just say that one day, Yuri asks to walk home with you?

3395
02:52:55,130 --> 02:52:55,610
Huh?

3396
02:52:56,970 --> 02:52:57,870
What would you do?

3397
02:52:58,490 --> 02:52:59,690
I would do her.

3398
02:53:01,510 --> 02:53:03,090
What kind of question is that?

3399
02:53:04,010 --> 02:53:05,670
I wish I could say that to her though.

3400
02:53:06,970 --> 02:53:08,430
You're kind of putting me on the spot here.

3401
02:53:13,560 --> 02:53:15,300
I would walk home with Yuri.

3402
02:53:17,960 --> 02:53:19,460
Walking home with Yuri, huh?

3403
02:53:21,060 --> 02:53:23,200
Why does the thought of that make my heart pound?

3404
02:53:24,820 --> 02:53:25,660
I mean...

3405
02:53:26,680 --> 02:53:28,720
Even how hard it is for her to socialize...

3406
02:53:29,440 --> 02:53:32,680
I would feel awful turning her down, so...

3407
02:53:32,680 --> 02:53:34,600
Isn't she so beautiful and smart?

3408
02:53:35,740 --> 02:53:38,040
That has nothing to do with what I just said.

3409
02:53:38,140 --> 02:53:38,640
Except it does.

3410
02:53:39,740 --> 02:53:40,260
Haha!

3411
02:53:40,700 --> 02:53:41,420
You admitted it!

3412
02:53:42,220 --> 02:53:42,740
Jeez.

3413
02:53:43,720 --> 02:53:47,140
There's not even any point in speculating something that's never gonna happen.

3414
02:53:48,460 --> 02:53:48,980
Well...

3415
02:53:48,980 --> 02:53:49,460
Maybe.

3416
02:53:50,760 --> 02:53:52,380
But I just like to think about it.

3417
02:53:52,780 --> 02:53:54,580
What if we were to all walk home...

3418
02:53:55,540 --> 02:53:56,060
Together?

3419
02:53:56,840 --> 02:53:58,340
Now there's a bold idea.

3420
02:53:59,900 --> 02:54:02,520
It's not long before you won't need me anymore, you know?

3421
02:54:04,220 --> 02:54:04,940
Need you?

3422
02:54:06,840 --> 02:54:07,360
Sayori.

3423
02:54:08,060 --> 02:54:08,580
Sayori.

3424
02:54:09,200 --> 02:54:11,980
I can't figure out how you're seeing things in your head right now.

3425
02:54:13,600 --> 02:54:14,080
Sorry.

3426
02:54:15,440 --> 02:54:16,620
What is she even saying?

3427
02:54:17,240 --> 02:54:18,220
Everyone is different!

3428
02:54:19,240 --> 02:54:21,340
Nobody in the club is a replacement for you!

3429
02:54:22,300 --> 02:54:22,720
Hmm.

3430
02:54:23,540 --> 02:54:24,560
If you say so.

3431
02:54:25,600 --> 02:54:28,560
The conversation trails off, and I'm left feeling awkward.

3432
02:54:29,560 --> 02:54:32,820
But it was kind of her fault for trapping me with such a weird question.

3433
02:54:33,880 --> 02:54:35,340
I can't just lie to her!

3434
02:54:36,260 --> 02:54:39,520
But if there's something that makes her happy, I would hate to take that away from her.

3435
02:54:40,460 --> 02:54:42,300
That's why I said there's no point in speculating.

3436
02:54:43,000 --> 02:54:45,760
Then again, the festival is only a few days away.

3437
02:54:45,920 --> 02:54:46,700
Oh, I thought it was tomorrow.

3438
02:54:47,960 --> 02:54:49,420
Who knows what will happen in that time?

3439
02:54:52,940 --> 02:54:58,380
I hope there's a new frickin' backdrop for the festival.

3440
02:54:58,740 --> 02:54:59,360
That's all I want.

3441
02:54:59,520 --> 02:55:05,020
I'm pining for just something different.

3442
02:55:06,240 --> 02:55:09,100
Okay, let's go for existence.

3443
02:55:10,480 --> 02:55:13,720
Let's see if I can do 20 out of 20 on Yuri.

3444
02:55:14,200 --> 02:55:14,760
Here we go.

3445
02:55:14,960 --> 02:55:15,300
Whirlwind.

3446
02:55:16,600 --> 02:55:21,900
Uh, it's gotta be incongruent.

3447
02:55:22,180 --> 02:55:22,800
Yeah!

3448
02:55:26,460 --> 02:55:27,540
Uh...

3449
02:55:27,540 --> 02:55:28,700
Could be contamination.

3450
02:55:29,260 --> 02:55:29,940
Yes!

3451
02:55:30,480 --> 02:55:31,860
I think empty.

3452
02:55:32,440 --> 02:55:33,240
Well, let me see.

3453
02:55:34,600 --> 02:55:35,440
Electricity.

3454
02:55:36,840 --> 02:55:39,640
I don't know what effulgent means, but it's probably that.

3455
02:55:40,880 --> 02:55:41,520
Yeah!

3456
02:55:42,100 --> 02:55:42,760
Universe.

3457
02:55:45,340 --> 02:55:46,320
Uh...

3458
02:55:48,430 --> 02:55:49,140
What now?

3459
02:55:50,780 --> 02:55:51,760
Unrestrained?

3460
02:55:52,080 --> 02:55:52,680
Yeah!

3461
02:55:53,760 --> 02:55:54,400
Disarray.

3462
02:55:54,400 --> 02:55:54,780
I think.

3463
02:55:54,920 --> 02:55:54,980
I think.

3464
02:55:54,980 --> 02:55:55,340
No, variance.

3465
02:55:56,840 --> 02:55:57,320
Determination.

3466
02:55:59,620 --> 02:56:00,100
And...

3467
02:56:01,420 --> 02:56:02,680
Oh, this is a tough one.

3468
02:56:03,440 --> 02:56:03,920
Uncanny?

3469
02:56:04,040 --> 02:56:04,280
Maybe?

3470
02:56:05,500 --> 02:56:05,980
Yeah!

3471
02:56:06,440 --> 02:56:06,860
Sick!

3472
02:56:08,540 --> 02:56:09,680
Let's see, uh...

3473
02:56:11,320 --> 02:56:12,120
Uh, let me see.

3474
02:56:12,320 --> 02:56:12,800
Heartbeat.

3475
02:56:14,000 --> 02:56:14,480
Giggle.

3476
02:56:15,140 --> 02:56:16,000
Vertigo, I think.

3477
02:56:16,660 --> 02:56:17,520
But let me see.

3478
02:56:18,360 --> 02:56:18,800
Hopeless.

3479
02:56:18,960 --> 02:56:19,540
Could also be hopeless.

3480
02:56:22,490 --> 02:56:22,970
Yeah!

3481
02:56:23,570 --> 02:56:24,050
Nice.

3482
02:56:25,110 --> 02:56:26,050
Then we have...

3483
02:56:28,550 --> 02:56:30,070
Oh god, this is a tough one.

3484
02:56:32,130 --> 02:56:33,090
Could be...

3485
02:56:33,090 --> 02:56:33,230
Time.

3486
02:56:33,630 --> 02:56:34,110
Yes!

3487
02:56:35,150 --> 02:56:35,810
Good shit.

3488
02:56:41,020 --> 02:56:41,500
Uh...

3489
02:56:41,500 --> 02:56:41,900
I don't know.

3490
02:56:46,330 --> 02:56:46,810
Captive?

3491
02:56:49,790 --> 02:56:50,270
Dude!

3492
02:56:50,530 --> 02:56:51,170
15 out of 20!

3493
02:56:51,290 --> 02:56:51,630
Holy shit!

3494
02:56:55,010 --> 02:56:55,490
Uh...

3495
02:56:56,190 --> 02:56:57,790
I'm feeling destiny, but let's see.

3496
02:56:59,490 --> 02:56:59,970
Shame.

3497
02:57:00,190 --> 02:57:00,630
Precious.

3498
02:57:00,910 --> 02:57:01,210
Marshmallow.

3499
02:57:01,210 --> 02:57:01,570
I think.

3500
02:57:02,050 --> 02:57:02,450
Yes!

3501
02:57:02,830 --> 02:57:03,230
Fuck, I'm good.

3502
02:57:03,350 --> 02:57:03,610
Fuck, I'm good.

3503
02:57:03,710 --> 02:57:04,030
Fuck, I'm good.

3504
02:57:05,090 --> 02:57:06,570
Now it's gotta be, uh...

3505
02:57:06,570 --> 02:57:07,170
Dark, I think.

3506
02:57:08,950 --> 02:57:09,350
Or...

3507
02:57:09,770 --> 02:57:10,170
Uh...

3508
02:57:10,170 --> 02:57:10,890
It could be snow.

3509
02:57:11,050 --> 02:57:11,530
It's not spinning.

3510
02:57:13,230 --> 02:57:13,630
Dark.

3511
02:57:14,190 --> 02:57:14,550
Fuck!

3512
02:57:21,350 --> 02:57:21,750
Uh...

3513
02:57:21,750 --> 02:57:22,210
What do we do?

3514
02:57:23,390 --> 02:57:23,790
Unrequited?

3515
02:57:24,810 --> 02:57:25,210
Nooo!

3516
02:57:25,450 --> 02:57:26,310
I'm on a losing streak!

3517
02:57:29,550 --> 02:57:31,290
Oh my god, I don't know.

3518
02:57:31,410 --> 02:57:31,790
Parfait?

3519
02:57:32,450 --> 02:57:32,990
Nooo!

3520
02:57:35,820 --> 02:57:36,460
I'm incapable!

3521
02:57:37,580 --> 02:57:37,880
Never mind.

3522
02:57:39,080 --> 02:57:39,640
Entropy!

3523
02:57:40,460 --> 02:57:41,220
Ah...

3524
02:57:41,220 --> 02:57:43,360
Oh my god!

3525
02:57:44,620 --> 02:57:49,000
I really wanted to see what would happen if I got everything right.

3526
02:57:50,360 --> 02:57:51,600
Aw, man!

3527
02:57:52,500 --> 02:57:54,180
I'm the last one here again!

3528
02:57:55,440 --> 02:57:58,060
Don't worry, I just walked in too.

3529
02:58:00,260 --> 02:58:01,820
Were you practicing piano again?

3530
02:58:02,960 --> 02:58:03,340
Yeah.

3531
02:58:05,600 --> 02:58:07,700
These girls laugh a lot, don't they?

3532
02:58:07,980 --> 02:58:09,400
And they sweat a lot too.

3533
02:58:10,400 --> 02:58:11,480
Must be hot in here.

3534
02:58:12,180 --> 02:58:14,000
You must have a lot of determination.

3535
02:58:14,920 --> 02:58:18,160
Starting this club and now picking up piano?

3536
02:58:19,300 --> 02:58:20,720
Well, maybe not determination.

3537
02:58:21,780 --> 02:58:22,900
But I guess passion.

3538
02:58:24,120 --> 02:58:26,700
Remember that the club wouldn't be here if it wasn't all for you!

3539
02:58:28,320 --> 02:58:31,760
And I'm super happy that you're all willing to help out for the festival too!

3540
02:58:33,100 --> 02:58:34,620
I can't wait for the festival!

3541
02:58:35,240 --> 02:58:36,320
It's gonna be great!

3542
02:58:37,020 --> 02:58:37,500
Eh?

3543
02:58:38,620 --> 02:58:40,960
Weren't you complaining about it just yesterday, Natsuki?

3544
02:58:41,840 --> 02:58:42,760
Well, yeah.

3545
02:58:43,660 --> 02:58:45,940
I'm not talking about OUR part of the festival.

3546
02:58:46,680 --> 02:58:50,020
But it's a whole day of school where we get to play and eat all kinds of delicious food!

3547
02:58:51,200 --> 02:58:53,320
You sound a bit like Sayori all of a sudden.

3548
02:58:54,860 --> 02:58:55,260
Monica!

3549
02:58:56,100 --> 02:58:57,560
Do they usually have fried squid?

3550
02:58:59,360 --> 02:58:59,800
Squid?

3551
02:59:01,140 --> 02:59:03,560
That's a pretty specific thing to look forward to!

3552
02:59:04,420 --> 02:59:05,360
Oh, come on!

3553
02:59:05,920 --> 02:59:07,120
Are you saying you don't like squid?

3554
02:59:08,260 --> 02:59:09,300
You of all people?

3555
02:59:10,620 --> 02:59:12,080
Eh, I didn't say I didn't like it!

3556
02:59:12,940 --> 02:59:15,220
Besides, what do you mean by you of all people?

3557
02:59:16,320 --> 02:59:16,800
Because!

3558
02:59:17,720 --> 02:59:19,020
That's right in your name!

3559
02:59:20,520 --> 02:59:21,000
Monica!

3560
02:59:22,460 --> 02:59:22,940
Huh?

3561
02:59:26,270 --> 02:59:27,850
That makes no sense, Natsuki.

3562
02:59:28,870 --> 02:59:29,090
Eh?

3563
02:59:30,470 --> 02:59:32,170
That's not how you say my name at all!

3564
02:59:33,130 --> 02:59:35,210
Also, that joke makes no sense in translation!

3565
02:59:36,510 --> 02:59:39,210
Oh wait, did it make sense in Japanese or something?

3566
02:59:40,610 --> 02:59:41,870
That's probably what it is!

3567
02:59:43,130 --> 02:59:43,570
Ah!

3568
02:59:43,730 --> 02:59:44,170
Nevermind!

3569
02:59:45,470 --> 02:59:47,350
Let's just focus on our event for now!

3570
02:59:47,610 --> 02:59:47,830
Okay!

3571
02:59:48,970 --> 02:59:49,270
Hehe!

3572
02:59:50,250 --> 02:59:50,830
Fine, fine!

3573
02:59:51,830 --> 02:59:54,210
Your reactions aren't as fun as yours and Sayori's anyway!

3574
02:59:55,830 --> 02:59:56,870
Excuse me...

3575
02:59:56,870 --> 02:59:58,410
Where is Sayori anyway?

3576
02:59:59,550 --> 03:00:00,550
Oh, there you are!

3577
03:00:02,090 --> 03:00:04,310
Sayori is sitting at a desk in the corner of the room.

3578
03:00:04,610 --> 03:00:05,810
Looking down at nothing.

3579
03:00:06,430 --> 03:00:07,830
I walk over to her.

3580
03:00:08,830 --> 03:00:09,710
Hey Sayori!

3581
03:00:10,910 --> 03:00:12,830
I wave my hand in front of her face.

3582
03:00:13,710 --> 03:00:14,230
Eh?

3583
03:00:15,310 --> 03:00:17,030
You're spacing out again!

3584
03:00:19,350 --> 03:00:19,870
Uh...

3585
03:00:19,870 --> 03:00:20,210
Hehe!

3586
03:00:20,650 --> 03:00:21,010
Sorry!

3587
03:00:21,790 --> 03:00:22,730
Don't mind me!

3588
03:00:23,830 --> 03:00:25,470
You can go talk to everyone else!

3589
03:00:26,530 --> 03:00:27,050
Huh?

3590
03:00:27,990 --> 03:00:28,990
Is everything alright?

3591
03:00:28,990 --> 03:00:30,190
Are you jealous?

3592
03:00:31,590 --> 03:00:32,470
Of course!

3593
03:00:33,430 --> 03:00:34,330
Why wouldn't it be?

3594
03:00:35,030 --> 03:00:36,910
It just feels like you're a little...

3595
03:00:36,910 --> 03:00:37,090
Off!

3596
03:00:37,390 --> 03:00:38,150
Is all I'm saying.

3597
03:00:39,390 --> 03:00:40,650
Sorry for assuming things.

3598
03:00:40,810 --> 03:00:40,890
No!

3599
03:00:41,210 --> 03:00:43,150
Don't start apologizing as well!

3600
03:00:45,670 --> 03:00:46,110
Geez!

3601
03:00:46,370 --> 03:00:47,470
You worry too much about me!

3602
03:00:47,610 --> 03:00:48,010
I do!

3603
03:00:48,950 --> 03:00:49,730
I'm fine!

3604
03:00:50,090 --> 03:00:50,210
See?

3605
03:00:51,370 --> 03:00:52,950
Sayori shows me a big smile.

3606
03:00:53,930 --> 03:00:55,930
Don't let me distract you from having fun with everyone!

3607
03:00:57,010 --> 03:00:57,610
Well...

3608
03:00:57,610 --> 03:00:58,070
Alright.

3609
03:00:58,930 --> 03:01:00,150
If you say so.

3610
03:01:01,490 --> 03:01:05,070
I worriedly glanced at Sayori before turning back toward everyone else.

3611
03:01:05,950 --> 03:01:10,110
But the conversation is already dispersed, with everyone back at their usual activities.

3612
03:01:11,630 --> 03:01:15,190
Maybe I should ask Monica if she's noticed anything about Sayori recently.

3613
03:01:16,190 --> 03:01:16,790
Why?

3614
03:01:17,150 --> 03:01:18,270
What is it about her?

3615
03:01:20,270 --> 03:01:21,350
I feel like...

3616
03:01:22,010 --> 03:01:25,570
Dude, I truly feel like an alien came to Earth and wrote this.

3617
03:01:26,470 --> 03:01:27,070
Like...

3618
03:01:27,070 --> 03:01:32,090
Like some alien has some vague idea of what a conversation between two humans is like.

3619
03:01:32,990 --> 03:01:34,470
And then this is it, you know?

3620
03:01:34,970 --> 03:01:36,090
I don't understand...

3621
03:01:36,730 --> 03:01:37,650
This writing!

3622
03:01:39,130 --> 03:01:39,690
Why...

3623
03:01:39,690 --> 03:01:40,090
Wha...

3624
03:01:40,090 --> 03:01:40,970
I don't get it.

3625
03:01:41,450 --> 03:01:45,830
Since they've been preparing for the festival, they must be spending a lot of time together.

3626
03:01:46,610 --> 03:01:51,030
I timidly approach Monica, who is shuffling through some papers at her desk.

3627
03:01:52,070 --> 03:01:52,350
Oliver!

3628
03:01:52,950 --> 03:01:53,470
What's up?

3629
03:01:54,490 --> 03:01:55,550
Hey, uh...

3630
03:01:55,550 --> 03:01:58,010
This might sound a little strange, but...

3631
03:01:58,010 --> 03:02:00,410
Have you noticed anything up with Sayori recently?

3632
03:02:02,050 --> 03:02:02,530
Anything...

3633
03:02:02,530 --> 03:02:03,370
Up with her?

3634
03:02:04,690 --> 03:02:05,910
In what way do you mean?

3635
03:02:07,010 --> 03:02:09,350
Maybe I'm reading into it a little too much, but...

3636
03:02:09,350 --> 03:02:11,110
She seems a bit downcast today?

3637
03:02:12,250 --> 03:02:12,730
Oh?

3638
03:02:12,970 --> 03:02:13,550
You think so?

3639
03:02:14,410 --> 03:02:16,390
I can't say I've noticed anything about her.

3640
03:02:16,830 --> 03:02:17,890
Yeah, people have...

3641
03:02:17,890 --> 03:02:19,110
Good and bad days, dude!

3642
03:02:19,950 --> 03:02:20,930
It's not weird!

3643
03:02:21,170 --> 03:02:21,690
It's normal!

3644
03:02:22,830 --> 03:02:28,270
Monica peers across the room at Sayori, who is idly dragging a rubber eraser up and down her desk.

3645
03:02:29,230 --> 03:02:30,270
She's bored!

3646
03:02:32,430 --> 03:02:33,930
Maybe there's something on her mind?

3647
03:02:34,810 --> 03:02:36,370
But I'm surprised I'm not...

3648
03:02:36,370 --> 03:02:38,030
The one asking you, Oliver.

3649
03:02:38,990 --> 03:02:41,270
You certainly know her a lot better than I do.

3650
03:02:42,570 --> 03:02:44,950
Yeah, but she's never really liked this.

3651
03:02:45,810 --> 03:02:48,210
She's always talked to me about things that bothered her.

3652
03:02:48,950 --> 03:02:51,650
But this time when I asked her, she was really dismissive.

3653
03:02:52,850 --> 03:02:54,450
Sorry, I know it's not your problem.

3654
03:02:55,170 --> 03:02:57,770
I just wanted to ask you if you knew anything, so I'll drop it now.

3655
03:02:58,710 --> 03:02:59,090
No, no.

3656
03:02:59,730 --> 03:03:00,890
It's important for me too.

3657
03:03:01,750 --> 03:03:03,930
I mean, I'm also friends with her.

3658
03:03:04,790 --> 03:03:07,690
And I also care about the well-being of my club members, you know?

3659
03:03:08,590 --> 03:03:10,190
Maybe I'll try talking to her myself.

3660
03:03:11,350 --> 03:03:12,650
Uh, you sure about that?

3661
03:03:13,910 --> 03:03:16,170
She seemed like she wanted to be left alone.

3662
03:03:17,070 --> 03:03:17,890
Are you sure?

3663
03:03:18,950 --> 03:03:21,870
Maybe she just has a hard time bringing it up with the person of interest.

3664
03:03:23,930 --> 03:03:24,410
Person...

3665
03:03:25,230 --> 03:03:26,110
Of interest?

3666
03:03:27,850 --> 03:03:29,190
What do you mean by that?

3667
03:03:30,330 --> 03:03:33,910
I'm saying that maybe the thing on her mind is you, Oliver!

3668
03:03:35,550 --> 03:03:36,070
Me?

3669
03:03:37,630 --> 03:03:40,510
How on earth would you come to that conclusion?

3670
03:03:42,250 --> 03:03:42,770
Well...

3671
03:03:43,390 --> 03:03:45,910
I probably shouldn't say too much, but...

3672
03:03:46,730 --> 03:03:49,650
Sayori talks about you more than anything else, you know?

3673
03:03:50,530 --> 03:03:51,750
Yeah, okay, she's jealous.

3674
03:03:52,690 --> 03:03:52,990
Eh?

3675
03:03:54,090 --> 03:03:56,910
She's been so much happier ever since you've joined the club.

3676
03:03:57,650 --> 03:03:59,970
It's like an extra light was turned on inside of her.

3677
03:04:00,870 --> 03:04:01,390
What?

3678
03:04:02,230 --> 03:04:03,070
No way.

3679
03:04:04,250 --> 03:04:06,270
Sayori is always like that.

3680
03:04:06,350 --> 03:04:07,370
Except she's not!

3681
03:04:07,890 --> 03:04:09,670
I just said the other thing.

3682
03:04:09,930 --> 03:04:12,030
I'm contradicting myself here.

3683
03:04:14,050 --> 03:04:15,690
She's always been full of sunshine.

3684
03:04:16,950 --> 03:04:19,730
It's not any different now than it always has been.

3685
03:04:20,330 --> 03:04:20,990
What do you mean?

3686
03:04:21,030 --> 03:04:22,110
You just said it was different!

3687
03:04:23,130 --> 03:04:23,390
Hehe!

3688
03:04:24,510 --> 03:04:25,650
You're so funny, Oliver.

3689
03:04:26,650 --> 03:04:29,190
Have you thought that maybe you've seen her as so cheerful?

3690
03:04:30,150 --> 03:04:32,230
Because that's just how she is when she's around you?

3691
03:04:33,550 --> 03:04:34,870
Except she wasn't!

3692
03:04:35,110 --> 03:04:35,210
She wasn't!

3693
03:04:35,210 --> 03:04:35,610
Just now!

3694
03:04:37,690 --> 03:04:38,050
Ah...

3695
03:04:38,050 --> 03:04:39,030
I said too much.

3696
03:04:40,070 --> 03:04:41,450
I'm sorry, what do I know anyway?

3697
03:04:42,550 --> 03:04:44,250
I didn't mean to jump to conclusions.

3698
03:04:45,090 --> 03:04:46,770
So you should just forget about what I said.

3699
03:04:47,690 --> 03:04:50,450
I'll try to talk to her, so just try not to think about it for now.

3700
03:04:52,410 --> 03:04:52,850
Uh...

3701
03:04:53,330 --> 03:04:53,550
Alright!

3702
03:04:54,670 --> 03:04:56,250
Monica smiles meaningfully.

3703
03:04:57,310 --> 03:04:59,230
I know she said to forget about it.

3704
03:04:59,970 --> 03:05:02,950
But I already know that I won't be able to get her words out of my head.

3705
03:05:04,170 --> 03:05:07,910
Monica stands up from her desk and walks across the room to where Sayori is sitting.

3706
03:05:09,170 --> 03:05:12,490
I watch her kneel down next to Sayori and gently talk to her.

3707
03:05:13,530 --> 03:05:16,730
But she's keeping her voice so quiet that I can't hear her from here.

3708
03:05:17,230 --> 03:05:18,490
We'll get closer then!

3709
03:05:18,910 --> 03:05:20,630
Let's drop some eeeves!

3710
03:05:22,130 --> 03:05:24,010
I sigh and sit myself down.

3711
03:05:25,430 --> 03:05:27,810
I know Sayori told me not to worry about her.

3712
03:05:28,050 --> 03:05:29,730
And to have fun with everyone else.

3713
03:05:30,650 --> 03:05:33,410
But that's impossible to do when she's behaving like this.

3714
03:05:34,510 --> 03:05:38,610
Exactly how much do I care about her that I'm letting this weigh me down so much?

3715
03:05:39,650 --> 03:05:42,190
Now it feels like I'm the one behaving out of the ordinary.

3716
03:05:42,590 --> 03:05:45,330
Oh my god, you're becoming just like the girls!

3717
03:05:46,370 --> 03:05:46,850
Stop!

3718
03:05:46,850 --> 03:05:49,330
We need to leave this club immediately.

3719
03:05:49,690 --> 03:05:50,670
It's infecting us.

3720
03:05:51,650 --> 03:05:54,050
But there's nothing I can do besides wait for Monica.

3721
03:05:55,090 --> 03:05:56,910
Why does it feel like I'm being watched?

3722
03:05:57,910 --> 03:05:59,490
I glance around the room.

3723
03:06:00,710 --> 03:06:03,810
Suddenly, I notice Yuri peering at me from over her book.

3724
03:06:04,750 --> 03:06:07,910
But she looks away just as quickly with a flustered look on her face.

3725
03:06:08,910 --> 03:06:11,170
I realize that she won't get anywhere like this.

3726
03:06:12,010 --> 03:06:16,070
I've never really seen Yuri approach anyone or start a conversation on her own accord.

3727
03:06:17,270 --> 03:06:19,630
So, I have no choice but to approach her myself.

3728
03:06:20,170 --> 03:06:21,370
And feed her some chocolate.

3729
03:06:21,510 --> 03:06:22,130
I want more of that!

3730
03:06:22,190 --> 03:06:22,650
That was fun!

3731
03:06:23,070 --> 03:06:23,770
Can we do that again?

3732
03:06:24,770 --> 03:06:26,730
By now, it's a little easier for me to do that.

3733
03:06:27,690 --> 03:06:29,990
I stand up from my desk and sit in...

3734
03:06:30,410 --> 03:06:31,590
One next to her own.

3735
03:06:31,670 --> 03:06:32,010
Oh, right.

3736
03:06:32,170 --> 03:06:32,330
Okay.

3737
03:06:35,710 --> 03:06:36,050
I...

3738
03:06:36,430 --> 03:06:38,030
Didn't mean to bother you or anything.

3739
03:06:38,750 --> 03:06:40,490
Dude, can we feed her something else today?

3740
03:06:41,710 --> 03:06:43,230
Can we feed her...

3741
03:06:43,230 --> 03:06:43,910
Jelly beans.

3742
03:06:44,750 --> 03:06:46,750
I want to see a little jelly bean in her mouth.

3743
03:06:47,270 --> 03:06:48,490
And then I want to see...

3744
03:06:48,850 --> 03:06:49,630
Broccoli in her mouth.

3745
03:06:50,190 --> 03:06:52,110
I want to see different types of things in her mouth.

3746
03:06:54,650 --> 03:06:55,010
Relax!

3747
03:06:55,290 --> 03:06:56,350
You didn't even do anything.

3748
03:06:57,770 --> 03:06:58,130
But...

3749
03:06:58,510 --> 03:07:00,810
I could tell that you wanted to be alone with your thoughts.

3750
03:07:02,910 --> 03:07:04,190
Alone with my thoughts.

3751
03:07:05,590 --> 03:07:08,290
How were you even able to tell what I was thinking like that?

3752
03:07:09,690 --> 03:07:10,170
Well...

3753
03:07:10,890 --> 03:07:12,430
It's something that I do a lot.

3754
03:07:12,970 --> 03:07:13,710
You know...

3755
03:07:13,710 --> 03:07:14,330
Read minds.

3756
03:07:15,670 --> 03:07:19,330
So it wasn't that hard for me to spot based on your posture and expression.

3757
03:07:20,610 --> 03:07:22,430
Not that I was staring or anything.

3758
03:07:23,910 --> 03:07:25,670
I didn't do anything creepy like that.

3759
03:07:25,670 --> 03:07:26,570
But...

3760
03:07:26,570 --> 03:07:28,410
In any case, I guess you were right.

3761
03:07:29,170 --> 03:07:30,950
I'm sorry if I caused you any concern.

3762
03:07:32,230 --> 03:07:33,110
Don't apologize.

3763
03:07:34,130 --> 03:07:37,530
Your troubles are only the concern of those who willingly share in that concern.

3764
03:07:38,830 --> 03:07:42,710
Of course, there are certainly those who find the most comfort in keeping to themselves.

3765
03:07:43,590 --> 03:07:44,250
That's me!

3766
03:07:45,490 --> 03:07:48,890
But if you would prefer to share what's on your mind, then I would be glad to listen.

3767
03:07:50,770 --> 03:07:51,130
Ah...

3768
03:07:51,130 --> 03:07:53,690
It's really not that big of a deal, but...

3769
03:07:54,130 --> 03:07:56,570
Remember the other day when I put chocolate in your mouth?

3770
03:07:57,370 --> 03:07:59,290
What do you say we do that again but with jelly beans?

3771
03:08:00,710 --> 03:08:02,810
I was just feeling a bit uneasy about Sayori.

3772
03:08:05,130 --> 03:08:05,490
Sayori?

3773
03:08:06,590 --> 03:08:06,950
Yeah!

3774
03:08:07,350 --> 03:08:08,550
She seems a little off today.

3775
03:08:08,990 --> 03:08:11,290
But when I asked her about it, she didn't want to admit it to me.

3776
03:08:12,070 --> 03:08:14,350
So I can't help but wonder if something happened to her.

3777
03:08:15,470 --> 03:08:16,390
Dude, I...

3778
03:08:16,390 --> 03:08:17,030
This is like...

3779
03:08:17,030 --> 03:08:18,710
Overthinking the game.

3780
03:08:19,510 --> 03:08:20,470
These characters.

3781
03:08:21,550 --> 03:08:22,890
That's quite romantic.

3782
03:08:23,450 --> 03:08:23,970
What?!

3783
03:08:25,370 --> 03:08:26,630
How is that romantic?

3784
03:08:27,390 --> 03:08:27,910
Eh?

3785
03:08:29,110 --> 03:08:29,630
Sorry.

3786
03:08:30,650 --> 03:08:32,230
I didn't mean to say something stupid.

3787
03:08:32,370 --> 03:08:32,930
Well, you did!

3788
03:08:34,110 --> 03:08:35,070
It's not that.

3789
03:08:35,270 --> 03:08:37,530
I just didn't want you to misunderstand, is all.

3790
03:08:38,750 --> 03:08:39,910
Sayori and I...

3791
03:08:39,910 --> 03:08:42,030
Have been friends for a long time.

3792
03:08:42,470 --> 03:08:42,930
That's all.

3793
03:08:44,210 --> 03:08:44,530
Ah.

3794
03:08:44,790 --> 03:08:45,490
I see.

3795
03:08:46,690 --> 03:08:50,390
And perhaps it is unusual for her to be dismissive to you about her feelings.

3796
03:08:51,530 --> 03:08:53,810
Or maybe I'm just reading into it a little too much.

3797
03:08:53,950 --> 03:08:54,930
YOU THINK?!

3798
03:08:55,770 --> 03:08:56,450
Oliver.

3799
03:08:57,490 --> 03:08:59,410
The world is full of meaning.

3800
03:08:59,950 --> 03:09:02,510
Often hidden deep beneath plain sight.

3801
03:09:03,490 --> 03:09:06,590
And there are many untold mysteries behind every person.

3802
03:09:06,930 --> 03:09:08,830
No matter how well you may know them.

3803
03:09:10,290 --> 03:09:10,690
Ah.

3804
03:09:12,410 --> 03:09:15,590
So you think that there might be something behind it after all?

3805
03:09:17,330 --> 03:09:17,850
Hmm.

3806
03:09:18,830 --> 03:09:21,390
I think that Sayori is a very complex person.

3807
03:09:22,430 --> 03:09:27,090
Her mannerisms on the outside don't always match what they may be going on inside her head.

3808
03:09:27,850 --> 03:09:29,930
And she may not always know what she wants.

3809
03:09:30,930 --> 03:09:33,150
I noticed her strange behavior today too.

3810
03:09:34,090 --> 03:09:36,210
And I also feel some concern for her.

3811
03:09:37,030 --> 03:09:38,210
But in your case?

3812
03:09:38,630 --> 03:09:41,910
It looked like she was fully occupying your thoughts, wasn't she?

3813
03:09:43,650 --> 03:09:44,210
Well...

3814
03:09:44,910 --> 03:09:46,350
I guess that was the case.

3815
03:09:48,390 --> 03:09:48,950
Sayori...

3816
03:09:48,950 --> 03:09:51,690
She really means a lot to you, doesn't she?

3817
03:09:53,630 --> 03:09:54,190
Uh...

3818
03:09:54,670 --> 03:09:55,650
I guess...

3819
03:09:55,650 --> 03:09:56,010
Yeah.

3820
03:09:56,570 --> 03:09:57,110
Yeah.

3821
03:09:58,210 --> 03:09:59,810
But you don't need to put it that way.

3822
03:10:00,630 --> 03:10:02,150
We're just good friends, that's all.

3823
03:10:04,170 --> 03:10:06,590
Yuri suddenly looks deeply into my eyes.

3824
03:10:07,270 --> 03:10:09,530
Her expression is gentle and curious.

3825
03:10:09,530 --> 03:10:13,290
As if she was searching for something like a jelly bean...

3826
03:10:13,290 --> 03:10:14,230
In her mouth.

3827
03:10:15,270 --> 03:10:17,110
Embarrassed, I avert my gaze.

3828
03:10:18,630 --> 03:10:19,390
Sometimes...

3829
03:10:20,310 --> 03:10:23,390
A person's mysteries are untold, even to themselves.

3830
03:10:24,190 --> 03:10:25,490
And you...

3831
03:10:25,490 --> 03:10:27,530
As someone honest and caring...

3832
03:10:28,370 --> 03:10:31,570
May uncover feelings you weren't aware were in you.

3833
03:10:32,570 --> 03:10:34,030
That is...

3834
03:10:34,030 --> 03:10:35,830
I think that...

3835
03:10:35,830 --> 03:10:37,950
She would be a very fortunate person...

3836
03:10:38,690 --> 03:10:40,270
To have you...

3837
03:10:40,270 --> 03:10:41,390
Feel that way about her.

3838
03:10:43,870 --> 03:10:44,350
Yuri...

3839
03:10:44,350 --> 03:10:45,750
You're giving me too much credit.

3840
03:10:46,630 --> 03:10:48,010
I'm a pretty simple guy.

3841
03:10:48,370 --> 03:10:49,750
I see jelly bean...

3842
03:10:49,750 --> 03:10:50,770
I put in your mouth.

3843
03:10:51,450 --> 03:10:53,870
So I think I'm pretty good at understanding my own feelings.

3844
03:10:54,870 --> 03:10:56,790
I'm not nearly as sophisticated as you.

3845
03:10:57,590 --> 03:10:59,010
I'm obsessed with this jelly bean.

3846
03:11:00,870 --> 03:11:01,270
Ah...

3847
03:11:01,270 --> 03:11:02,290
That's not...

3848
03:11:02,290 --> 03:11:03,630
A compliment, is it?

3849
03:11:04,830 --> 03:11:05,830
It is what it is.

3850
03:11:06,770 --> 03:11:07,170
Anyway...

3851
03:11:07,170 --> 03:11:08,370
As long as we're here...

3852
03:11:08,370 --> 03:11:09,170
Why don't we do some reading?

3853
03:11:10,830 --> 03:11:11,230
Well...

3854
03:11:11,650 --> 03:11:13,010
As long as you're okay with it...

3855
03:11:14,790 --> 03:11:15,190
Yeah...

3856
03:11:15,830 --> 03:11:18,090
I should be taking my mind off this whole thing anyway.

3857
03:11:19,950 --> 03:11:21,390
Oh my god...

3858
03:11:21,390 --> 03:11:22,530
We're doing the reading thing?

3859
03:11:23,230 --> 03:11:24,090
Okay, everyone!

3860
03:11:24,950 --> 03:11:27,930
After some time passes, Monica calls out to the club room.

3861
03:11:28,770 --> 03:11:30,290
Why don't we share our poems now?

3862
03:11:31,470 --> 03:11:33,650
Before I know it, everything is back to normal.

3863
03:11:34,650 --> 03:11:37,330
Everyone goes to retrieve their poems, and I do the same.

3864
03:11:38,010 --> 03:11:41,790
I make eye contact with Monica, and she smiles at me.

3865
03:11:42,390 --> 03:11:44,690
I wonder what she was talking about with Sayori.

3866
03:11:45,830 --> 03:11:46,810
Yeah, I wonder.

3867
03:11:47,910 --> 03:11:49,790
Who should I show my poem to first?

3868
03:11:50,070 --> 03:11:52,350
Let's show it to Sayori, since she's feeling down.

3869
03:11:57,900 --> 03:11:58,900
It's nice, I guess.

3870
03:12:00,180 --> 03:12:00,980
Come on!

3871
03:12:01,640 --> 03:12:03,480
I can already tell you don't like it.

3872
03:12:05,280 --> 03:12:05,740
Well...

3873
03:12:06,240 --> 03:12:08,080
You don't need to worry about what I think.

3874
03:12:08,940 --> 03:12:11,180
After all, you wrote this for someone else, didn't you?

3875
03:12:12,600 --> 03:12:13,380
Probably Yuri.

3876
03:12:14,360 --> 03:12:14,820
Huh?

3877
03:12:16,440 --> 03:12:18,280
I didn't write this for anyone specifically?

3878
03:12:18,960 --> 03:12:19,440
Maybe?

3879
03:12:21,180 --> 03:12:21,660
Maybe?

3880
03:12:23,260 --> 03:12:24,760
That's not really what I meant, though.

3881
03:12:25,680 --> 03:12:26,380
It's okay.

3882
03:12:27,540 --> 03:12:29,620
You're making new friends, just like I was hoping.

3883
03:12:30,340 --> 03:12:32,120
That makes me really happy.

3884
03:12:32,980 --> 03:12:34,340
And you're happy too, right?

3885
03:12:35,020 --> 03:12:35,880
In this club?

3886
03:12:37,600 --> 03:12:38,080
Well...

3887
03:12:41,190 --> 03:12:42,150
You know...

3888
03:12:42,970 --> 03:12:44,270
Of course I am!

3889
03:12:45,010 --> 03:12:45,630
Good.

3890
03:12:46,310 --> 03:12:47,750
That's all that matters to me.

3891
03:12:48,490 --> 03:12:49,530
Thank you, Oliver.

3892
03:12:50,910 --> 03:12:51,550
Sayori?

3893
03:12:52,990 --> 03:12:54,230
Is there something wrong?

3894
03:12:55,090 --> 03:12:55,670
Huh?

3895
03:12:56,430 --> 03:12:57,910
No, nothing.

3896
03:12:58,490 --> 03:13:00,210
I'm just a little tired today.

3897
03:13:00,450 --> 03:13:01,850
Fucking tell me what's wrong!

3898
03:13:04,490 --> 03:13:05,210
Eh-heh!

3899
03:13:05,690 --> 03:13:06,050
Alright.

3900
03:13:07,550 --> 03:13:08,770
Just tell me if you need anything.

3901
03:13:09,810 --> 03:13:10,490
I will.

3902
03:13:11,290 --> 03:13:12,490
Don't worry about me, okay?

3903
03:13:13,070 --> 03:13:13,870
Okay, you said it!

3904
03:13:13,930 --> 03:13:14,810
I will never worry about you again!

3905
03:13:14,910 --> 03:13:15,050
Done!

3906
03:13:15,870 --> 03:13:17,310
I'm gonna go play with everyone else now.

3907
03:13:18,510 --> 03:13:19,530
If you insist!

3908
03:13:20,110 --> 03:13:20,470
Ahem!

3909
03:13:20,630 --> 03:13:21,350
Don't mind if I do!

3910
03:13:22,390 --> 03:13:22,750
Yay!

3911
03:13:23,710 --> 03:13:25,370
I'm gonna go home a little bit early today.

3912
03:13:26,830 --> 03:13:27,190
Sayori?

3913
03:13:28,350 --> 03:13:30,130
Tell Monica I wasn't feeling well, okay?

3914
03:13:31,510 --> 03:13:32,750
Should we walk home together though?

3915
03:13:34,170 --> 03:13:35,150
I'll see you tomorrow.

3916
03:13:36,390 --> 03:13:39,390
Before I can say anything else, Sayori cheerfully walks out of the classroom.

3917
03:13:40,070 --> 03:13:41,010
Humming to herself.

3918
03:13:43,710 --> 03:13:45,370
Who should I show my poem to next?

3919
03:13:45,530 --> 03:13:45,730
Yuri!

3920
03:13:51,120 --> 03:13:51,560
Oliver...

3921
03:13:51,560 --> 03:13:54,980
Your writing has only improved in these last few days.

3922
03:13:55,460 --> 03:13:55,980
I know!

3923
03:13:56,600 --> 03:13:58,820
Would you say my rating is 17 out of 20?

3924
03:13:59,060 --> 03:13:59,180
Maybe?

3925
03:14:00,940 --> 03:14:05,080
Every poem you've shown me has been nothing short of spectacular.

3926
03:14:06,280 --> 03:14:08,500
I can really feel the emotions.

3927
03:14:09,680 --> 03:14:11,120
I'm a little envious even.

3928
03:14:12,260 --> 03:14:15,240
I don't think it ever came to me this naturally.

3929
03:14:16,260 --> 03:14:18,260
Yuri, that's the wrong way to put it.

3930
03:14:18,680 --> 03:14:20,100
It's supposed to go in your mouth.

3931
03:14:20,200 --> 03:14:21,360
The jelly bean in your mouth.

3932
03:14:22,600 --> 03:14:24,740
This never did come naturally to me, you know?

3933
03:14:25,240 --> 03:14:27,660
But I've been able to improve so much thanks to you.

3934
03:14:28,680 --> 03:14:30,820
You're really the example I was chasing after.

3935
03:14:31,780 --> 03:14:32,720
Is that so?

3936
03:14:34,160 --> 03:14:35,900
Yuri gently smiles to herself.

3937
03:14:38,180 --> 03:14:39,140
This feeling...

3938
03:14:39,680 --> 03:14:42,000
I'm so glad I got the chance to share my...

3939
03:14:43,440 --> 03:14:46,480
The next time you say that, I will chop off your limbs!

3940
03:14:48,960 --> 03:14:50,860
I never thought I would feel like this.

3941
03:14:51,760 --> 03:14:53,940
I remember you mentioning that yesterday.

3942
03:14:55,460 --> 03:14:59,560
I can't believe that you're so good at something and you've never even shared it with anyone.

3943
03:15:00,680 --> 03:15:01,940
It's kind of a shame.

3944
03:15:03,580 --> 03:15:04,620
Maybe, but...

3945
03:15:04,620 --> 03:15:07,500
It's not like I really had a choice.

3946
03:15:08,020 --> 03:15:08,940
What do you mean?

3947
03:15:09,380 --> 03:15:11,120
Are you here against your will?

3948
03:15:11,620 --> 03:15:15,180
Did someone put a gun to your head and make you join the literary club?

3949
03:15:16,340 --> 03:15:17,520
What do you mean?

3950
03:15:19,600 --> 03:15:20,160
Well...

3951
03:15:20,160 --> 03:15:21,520
Yuri smiles sadly.

3952
03:15:23,620 --> 03:15:24,180
Oliver...

3953
03:15:24,180 --> 03:15:26,460
During lunch time I eat by myself.

3954
03:15:27,380 --> 03:15:28,420
Did you know that?

3955
03:15:30,240 --> 03:15:33,160
It's a great time to find a quiet spot and do some reading.

3956
03:15:35,120 --> 03:15:36,240
In fact...

3957
03:15:36,840 --> 03:15:38,480
I always have some books with me.

3958
03:15:38,540 --> 03:15:39,040
Wait, are you...

3959
03:15:39,040 --> 03:15:40,280
Are you saying you're eating the books?

3960
03:15:42,480 --> 03:15:43,920
I get you're lonely, but...

3961
03:15:43,920 --> 03:15:44,400
What do you mean?

3962
03:15:44,640 --> 03:15:46,560
You eat by yourself and then you said books?

3963
03:15:46,700 --> 03:15:47,000
I'm...

3964
03:15:47,000 --> 03:15:47,620
I'm confused.

3965
03:15:48,700 --> 03:15:50,180
You could say I really enjoy reading.

3966
03:15:52,060 --> 03:15:52,860
Well, that's...

3967
03:15:52,860 --> 03:15:53,940
One way to put it anyway.

3968
03:15:55,600 --> 03:15:56,000
But...

3969
03:15:56,000 --> 03:15:58,800
Books are so full of amazing and inspiring people.

3970
03:15:59,700 --> 03:16:01,280
People you want to fall in love with.

3971
03:16:02,260 --> 03:16:04,840
Or people you just know would make a really good friend.

3972
03:16:04,840 --> 03:16:06,940
No, listen to me Yuri.

3973
03:16:07,340 --> 03:16:08,320
That's bullshit.

3974
03:16:08,900 --> 03:16:10,200
That's in your mind.

3975
03:16:10,700 --> 03:16:13,480
You are imagining the best parts in your mind.

3976
03:16:14,040 --> 03:16:15,480
That's not reality.

3977
03:16:16,140 --> 03:16:16,680
Okay?

3978
03:16:17,100 --> 03:16:18,740
That's a one way relationship.

3979
03:16:19,120 --> 03:16:21,100
It's bad for you, it's not healthy.

3980
03:16:21,620 --> 03:16:22,060
Don't.

3981
03:16:22,300 --> 03:16:22,820
Do it.

3982
03:16:24,440 --> 03:16:25,780
Cheerful people...

3983
03:16:25,780 --> 03:16:27,800
Who always put a smile on your face.

3984
03:16:28,580 --> 03:16:30,940
Or deep thinkers and problem solvers.

3985
03:16:31,300 --> 03:16:32,980
Who discover the mysteries of life.

3986
03:16:34,660 --> 03:16:36,160
It's when you look at it that way.

3987
03:16:37,520 --> 03:16:39,460
I'm surrounded by friends everyday.

3988
03:16:40,540 --> 03:16:41,220
You know?

3989
03:16:42,120 --> 03:16:43,920
And those friends don't laugh at me.

3990
03:16:44,860 --> 03:16:47,620
They don't tease me for spacing out all the time.

3991
03:16:48,500 --> 03:16:50,420
They don't make fun of my body type.

3992
03:16:51,680 --> 03:16:52,360
What do you mean?

3993
03:16:52,400 --> 03:16:53,100
They don't go...

3994
03:16:53,800 --> 03:16:55,500
Look at how hot she is!

3995
03:16:56,680 --> 03:16:57,160
And...

3996
03:16:57,760 --> 03:17:00,000
And they don't hate me for acting like a know-it-all.

3997
03:17:02,360 --> 03:17:02,760
People...

3998
03:17:02,760 --> 03:17:04,200
Say that about you?

3999
03:17:05,640 --> 03:17:07,040
I'm not a know-it-all, Oliver.

4000
03:17:07,720 --> 03:17:08,560
It's the opposite.

4001
03:17:10,060 --> 03:17:11,040
I don't know anything.

4002
03:17:12,740 --> 03:17:14,420
I don't know how to talk to people.

4003
03:17:15,740 --> 03:17:18,300
I don't know how to make people see me as normal.

4004
03:17:19,800 --> 03:17:21,960
I don't even know how to make myself happy.

4005
03:17:23,140 --> 03:17:24,760
I have all these feelings.

4006
03:17:25,680 --> 03:17:27,660
And all I can do with them is read.

4007
03:17:27,940 --> 03:17:28,540
And write.

4008
03:17:29,480 --> 03:17:30,960
But it wasn't until now...

4009
03:17:31,760 --> 03:17:33,700
That I started sharing it with you.

4010
03:17:34,400 --> 03:17:37,300
That I really understood what was missing all this time.

4011
03:17:39,640 --> 03:17:41,360
But I haven't really done anything.

4012
03:17:42,260 --> 03:17:42,980
No.

4013
03:17:44,000 --> 03:17:45,000
That's wrong.

4014
03:17:45,920 --> 03:17:48,100
Just being patient and respectful.

4015
03:17:49,240 --> 03:17:50,560
That's really...

4016
03:17:50,560 --> 03:17:51,700
Important to me.

4017
03:17:52,320 --> 03:17:54,400
I know I'm a difficult person, Oliver.

4018
03:17:55,000 --> 03:17:56,660
I speak too slowly.

4019
03:17:57,220 --> 03:17:58,600
At least you admit it.

4020
03:17:59,100 --> 03:18:01,240
I second-guess myself all the time.

4021
03:18:02,020 --> 03:18:04,100
I read too deeply into things.

4022
03:18:04,660 --> 03:18:06,320
Thank God you're self-aware.

4023
03:18:07,140 --> 03:18:08,240
But every time...

4024
03:18:08,960 --> 03:18:11,320
You've always treated me just like anyone else.

4025
03:18:12,300 --> 03:18:15,480
It's so rare that I feel comfortable with myself when I talk to others.

4026
03:18:17,140 --> 03:18:19,300
But that's why every time I talk to you...

4027
03:18:20,440 --> 03:18:21,940
I just feel really happy.

4028
03:18:23,320 --> 03:18:24,000
I see.

4029
03:18:25,240 --> 03:18:27,860
Well, I treat you how you deserve to be treated, Yuri.

4030
03:18:29,100 --> 03:18:31,880
And if other people don't see it that way, then screw them.

4031
03:18:32,940 --> 03:18:35,620
I mean, I joined this club hoping I would make friends.

4032
03:18:36,600 --> 03:18:38,760
And I would say I've had at least one success.

4033
03:18:39,660 --> 03:18:40,280
Wouldn't you?

4034
03:18:42,020 --> 03:18:42,460
Um...

4035
03:18:42,460 --> 03:18:43,740
If you put it that way...

4036
03:18:45,000 --> 03:18:45,440
Yeah.

4037
03:18:46,740 --> 03:18:48,540
We really are friends now, aren't we?

4038
03:18:48,980 --> 03:18:50,840
Oh, I was talking about Sayori.

4039
03:18:51,660 --> 03:18:52,260
Just kidding.

4040
03:18:52,720 --> 03:18:54,820
Yuri puts her head in her hands.

4041
03:18:55,620 --> 03:18:57,700
But this time she's smiling as she does it.

4042
03:18:59,440 --> 03:19:00,820
You want to show me your poem?

4043
03:19:01,540 --> 03:19:02,200
Yeah.

4044
03:19:02,500 --> 03:19:03,360
I do.

4045
03:19:03,840 --> 03:19:05,120
Let me get it for you.

4046
03:19:06,080 --> 03:19:07,100
Okay, let's see.

4047
03:19:07,620 --> 03:19:09,020
Oh, it's the second part!

4048
03:19:09,180 --> 03:19:09,980
Ghost under the light!

4049
03:19:11,040 --> 03:19:14,840
The tentrils of my hair illuminate beneath the amber glow.

4050
03:19:15,640 --> 03:19:16,300
Battling.

4051
03:19:16,740 --> 03:19:19,500
In the distance, a blue-green light flickers.

4052
03:19:20,100 --> 03:19:22,320
A lone figure crosses its path.

4053
03:19:22,640 --> 03:19:25,100
A silhouette obstructing the eerie glow.

4054
03:19:25,560 --> 03:19:26,540
My heart pounds.

4055
03:19:27,380 --> 03:19:28,720
The silhouette grows.

4056
03:19:28,960 --> 03:19:29,560
Closer.

4057
03:19:30,020 --> 03:19:30,640
Closer.

4058
03:19:31,120 --> 03:19:32,600
I open my umbrella.

4059
03:19:33,140 --> 03:19:35,420
Casting a shadow to shield me from visibility.

4060
03:19:36,220 --> 03:19:37,520
But I'm too late.

4061
03:19:38,360 --> 03:19:39,760
He steps into the streetlight.

4062
03:19:40,080 --> 03:19:42,180
I gasp and drop my umbrella.

4063
03:19:42,660 --> 03:19:43,900
The light flickers.

4064
03:19:44,420 --> 03:19:45,600
My heart pounds.

4065
03:19:45,980 --> 03:19:47,000
He raises his arm.

4066
03:19:47,740 --> 03:19:48,600
Time stops.

4067
03:19:49,300 --> 03:19:54,420
The only indication of movement is the amber light flickering against his outstretched arm.

4068
03:19:55,150 --> 03:19:58,140
The flickering light is in rhythm with the pounding of my heart.

4069
03:19:59,130 --> 03:20:01,460
Teasing me for succumbing to this forbidden emotion.

4070
03:20:02,400 --> 03:20:05,240
Have you ever heard of a ghost-feeling warmth before?

4071
03:20:05,960 --> 03:20:07,380
Giving up on understanding?

4072
03:20:07,600 --> 03:20:08,280
I laugh.

4073
03:20:09,000 --> 03:20:10,280
Understanding is overrated.

4074
03:20:10,740 --> 03:20:11,980
I touch his hand.

4075
03:20:12,420 --> 03:20:13,620
The flickering stops.

4076
03:20:14,260 --> 03:20:15,400
Ghosts are blue-green.

4077
03:20:16,140 --> 03:20:17,440
My heart is amber.

4078
03:20:18,860 --> 03:20:20,860
Why do you consider this feeling forbidden?

4079
03:20:22,020 --> 03:20:23,620
That right there is toxic.

4080
03:20:23,620 --> 03:20:24,560
That's bad for you.

4081
03:20:26,680 --> 03:20:28,720
Also, she's totally thinking about me, I think.

4082
03:20:31,460 --> 03:20:33,800
Finishing the poem, I start to hand it back to Yuri.

4083
03:20:34,780 --> 03:20:37,100
But instead of taking it from me, she looks away.

4084
03:20:39,940 --> 03:20:40,820
Do you...

4085
03:20:40,820 --> 03:20:41,800
Dislike it?

4086
03:20:42,780 --> 03:20:43,680
Uh, no.

4087
03:20:43,900 --> 03:20:44,640
Of course not.

4088
03:20:45,700 --> 03:20:46,580
I just...

4089
03:20:46,580 --> 03:20:48,360
Don't really know how I should respond.

4090
03:20:49,900 --> 03:20:55,040
Despite Yuri's poems usually being cryptic, it wasn't hard to figure out what this one was about.

4091
03:20:55,720 --> 03:20:56,600
Yeah, it was about me!

4092
03:20:57,820 --> 03:20:58,260
Uh...

4093
03:20:58,260 --> 03:21:00,340
I don't know if I'll be able to explain this one.

4094
03:21:00,500 --> 03:21:00,920
Don't need.

4095
03:21:01,120 --> 03:21:01,720
I get it.

4096
03:21:02,480 --> 03:21:03,280
That's fine.

4097
03:21:03,980 --> 03:21:05,260
I understand this one.

4098
03:21:07,680 --> 03:21:10,880
Yuri is having an even harder time speaking than usual.

4099
03:21:12,760 --> 03:21:14,920
Does this one mean a lot to you?

4100
03:21:16,220 --> 03:21:17,200
She nods.

4101
03:21:18,220 --> 03:21:20,720
I'm not really good with words, but...

4102
03:21:20,720 --> 03:21:22,540
I'm happy that you shared it with me.

4103
03:21:23,200 --> 03:21:24,180
So thank you.

4104
03:21:25,020 --> 03:21:26,860
And I hope we keep spending time together.

4105
03:21:28,240 --> 03:21:32,720
Despite my inability to make eye contact, I see a faint smile emerge on Yuri's lips.

4106
03:21:32,920 --> 03:21:33,900
Yeah, cause she's looking away!

4107
03:21:35,720 --> 03:21:38,200
I once again try to hand the poem back to her.

4108
03:21:38,780 --> 03:21:41,700
But instead, Yuri gently takes my hands...

4109
03:21:41,700 --> 03:21:43,940
And pushes them back toward me!

4110
03:21:45,260 --> 03:21:47,820
I hesitate in response to her warm touch.

4111
03:21:49,540 --> 03:21:50,660
You can...

4112
03:21:51,280 --> 03:21:51,840
Um...

4113
03:21:51,840 --> 03:21:54,100
The poem is...

4114
03:21:54,960 --> 03:21:57,740
Once again, Yuri fails to form a complete sentence.

4115
03:21:59,320 --> 03:22:00,780
You mean I can keep it?

4116
03:22:01,660 --> 03:22:02,580
She nods.

4117
03:22:03,420 --> 03:22:04,320
I'd love to.

4118
03:22:05,580 --> 03:22:07,140
Again, Yuri faintly smiles.

4119
03:22:07,580 --> 03:22:09,260
As if she doesn't want me to notice.

4120
03:22:11,080 --> 03:22:11,560
Dude...

4121
03:22:11,980 --> 03:22:13,860
They're acting like kindergarteners here!

4122
03:22:14,000 --> 03:22:14,920
What the fuck?

4123
03:22:15,080 --> 03:22:16,240
Maybe I'm too Danish, but...

4124
03:22:17,780 --> 03:22:18,260
Just...

4125
03:22:18,260 --> 03:22:18,980
Be like...

4126
03:22:18,980 --> 03:22:19,560
Hey, what's up?

4127
03:22:19,780 --> 03:22:20,600
You want a date?

4128
03:22:20,940 --> 03:22:21,980
You know, like...

4129
03:22:21,980 --> 03:22:23,100
What's all this?

4130
03:22:23,420 --> 03:22:24,460
It's too much, man!

4131
03:22:27,280 --> 03:22:28,120
You always...

4132
03:22:28,120 --> 03:22:30,040
You always make me feel nice.

4133
03:22:31,620 --> 03:22:34,060
I know I'm not good with people, but...

4134
03:22:34,060 --> 03:22:35,500
I hope that...

4135
03:22:35,500 --> 03:22:37,020
I can return the favor sometimes.

4136
03:22:38,180 --> 03:22:38,720
Yep!

4137
03:22:40,120 --> 03:22:40,880
Don't worry.

4138
03:22:41,580 --> 03:22:43,400
I think you do a great job.

4139
03:22:44,360 --> 03:22:46,440
Yuri finally turns back toward me.

4140
03:22:47,580 --> 03:22:50,280
I guess we should move on before Monica says something.

4141
03:22:51,180 --> 03:22:52,820
But I'm sure we can talk again later.

4142
03:22:54,080 --> 03:22:54,520
Yeah.

4143
03:22:55,320 --> 03:22:56,360
I'm sure we will.

4144
03:22:57,180 --> 03:23:01,300
With that, Yuri timidly smiles at me, and I'll return to my seat so I can put her poem away.

4145
03:23:03,120 --> 03:23:03,560
Anyway...

4146
03:23:04,100 --> 03:23:06,560
I'm just trying to understand someone...

4147
03:23:07,120 --> 03:23:09,040
Sitting down, and thinking...

4148
03:23:09,040 --> 03:23:09,600
You know what?

4149
03:23:10,220 --> 03:23:11,480
I'm gonna make a video game.

4150
03:23:12,580 --> 03:23:14,240
It's gonna be about POEMS!

4151
03:23:15,340 --> 03:23:16,700
But it's gonna...

4152
03:23:17,200 --> 03:23:19,780
Be a poem club, and you're gonna talk to these girls.

4153
03:23:20,200 --> 03:23:22,300
And they clearly wanna have sex with you, and...

4154
03:23:24,000 --> 03:23:24,440
Something...

4155
03:23:24,440 --> 03:23:25,360
Let's do it!

4156
03:23:25,460 --> 03:23:25,980
Let's make a game!

4157
03:23:25,980 --> 03:23:27,840
I don't understand!

4158
03:23:28,580 --> 03:23:29,460
So weird!

4159
03:23:33,580 --> 03:23:33,920
Meh.

4160
03:23:34,700 --> 03:23:36,960
I guess you really haven't learned anything after all.

4161
03:23:37,760 --> 03:23:40,720
Honestly, I don't know why I got my hopes up in the first place.

4162
03:23:42,140 --> 03:23:42,620
What?!

4163
03:23:43,280 --> 03:23:44,880
I didn't think this one was that bad.

4164
03:23:45,660 --> 03:23:46,760
What did I do wrong?

4165
03:23:48,340 --> 03:23:51,140
Poems don't need to be all deep-sounding to express something.

4166
03:23:52,260 --> 03:23:55,420
It's gonna just sound like you're forcing it unless you really don't suck at it.

4167
03:23:56,680 --> 03:23:57,200
Honestly?

4168
03:23:57,500 --> 03:24:00,100
Don't bother trying to write poems like this until you're on Yuri's level!

4169
03:24:01,760 --> 03:24:03,480
Natsuki stops short all of a sudden.

4170
03:24:04,620 --> 03:24:05,140
The...

4171
03:24:05,140 --> 03:24:05,780
Don't...

4172
03:24:05,780 --> 03:24:06,700
Don't tell me!

4173
03:24:07,420 --> 03:24:07,940
Huh?

4174
03:24:09,200 --> 03:24:10,020
You're not...

4175
03:24:10,020 --> 03:24:12,140
You're not just trying to impress Yuri, are you?

4176
03:24:13,140 --> 03:24:14,800
What are you talking about?

4177
03:24:15,380 --> 03:24:16,460
Keep your voice down!

4178
03:24:17,620 --> 03:24:19,420
You know, Yuri would love this kind of...

4179
03:24:19,420 --> 03:24:20,420
This angsty...

4180
03:24:21,780 --> 03:24:24,680
Just because she's a talented writer doesn't mean I...

4181
03:24:25,980 --> 03:24:26,780
I mean...

4182
03:24:28,140 --> 03:24:29,280
Looks like I'm in trouble.

4183
03:24:30,000 --> 03:24:31,940
I somehow struck a nerve, though...

4184
03:24:31,940 --> 03:24:33,000
What I did is beyond me.

4185
03:24:33,920 --> 03:24:35,120
Man, you are oblivious!

4186
03:24:35,720 --> 03:24:37,600
She's clearly into you and she's...

4187
03:24:37,600 --> 03:24:38,140
Jealous!

4188
03:24:38,240 --> 03:24:39,440
How do you not see this?!

4189
03:24:40,180 --> 03:24:42,780
And how do you not see that these girls are depressed as fuck?!

4190
03:24:44,160 --> 03:24:45,620
I am so done with you!

4191
03:24:47,060 --> 03:24:49,640
Natsuki shoves the poem I handed her back over to me.

4192
03:24:51,020 --> 03:24:52,240
Take your stupid poem!

4193
03:24:52,660 --> 03:24:54,680
If you wrote it for someone else, just don't show it to me!

4194
03:24:56,480 --> 03:24:56,840
Ouch!

4195
03:24:57,680 --> 03:25:00,460
This is what I get for letting a younger girl step into my business.

4196
03:25:01,900 --> 03:25:05,160
Unless I was a mind reader, I was destined to be in a world of pain from the start.

4197
03:25:06,280 --> 03:25:09,680
At least Natsuki wasn't really the girl I was trying to impress in the first place.

4198
03:25:10,600 --> 03:25:16,760
I hate dancing around this whole thing between two people.

4199
03:25:16,920 --> 03:25:19,240
It's just... it's driving me nuts, man.

4200
03:25:23,320 --> 03:25:25,080
Who should I show my poem to next?

4201
03:25:26,380 --> 03:25:27,380
Uh, let me think.

4202
03:25:27,500 --> 03:25:28,860
Who should I... who should I...

4203
03:25:28,860 --> 03:25:30,040
Oh man, I can't decide.

4204
03:25:31,040 --> 03:25:32,660
Ah, it's gotta be... let's do Monica.

4205
03:25:36,890 --> 03:25:39,570
Have you thought about what you want to submit to perform at the festival?

4206
03:25:42,910 --> 03:25:43,790
Well, uh...

4207
03:25:43,790 --> 03:25:45,550
Being in this club is one thing.

4208
03:25:45,910 --> 03:25:47,830
But performing in front of a bunch of people?

4209
03:25:49,630 --> 03:25:50,930
I'll have to give it some more thought.

4210
03:25:52,070 --> 03:25:53,170
Okay, no pressure.

4211
03:25:53,930 --> 03:25:56,850
But whatever you do, I'm sure it'll turn out great!

4212
03:25:57,930 --> 03:26:00,970
It would also make me happy to see...

4213
03:26:00,970 --> 03:26:01,410
Ah!

4214
03:26:01,910 --> 03:26:03,550
Anyway, let's take a look at today's poem.

4215
03:26:04,550 --> 03:26:05,030
Sure.

4216
03:26:05,930 --> 03:26:08,650
I let Monica take the poem I'm holding in my hands.

4217
03:26:09,570 --> 03:26:10,430
Let me guess.

4218
03:26:11,050 --> 03:26:14,990
She's gonna be like, Oh, the... you... you... you wrote this for...

4219
03:26:15,430 --> 03:26:15,670
Yuri?

4220
03:26:17,390 --> 03:26:19,610
Your style's gotten so refined, Oliver.

4221
03:26:20,370 --> 03:26:22,470
Yuri's been teaching you a lot of things, hasn't she?

4222
03:26:23,890 --> 03:26:24,370
Well...

4223
03:26:24,910 --> 03:26:26,450
I guess you could say that.

4224
03:26:27,730 --> 03:26:28,210
Yeah.

4225
03:26:28,550 --> 03:26:30,710
I've been noticing how much time you spent with her.

4226
03:26:31,690 --> 03:26:35,510
I think I've heard her say more words these past couple days than she's talked in the whole year.

4227
03:26:36,530 --> 03:26:38,590
Not sure how you did it, but that's pretty impressive.

4228
03:26:39,790 --> 03:26:43,270
Well, she just needs some patience and a way to talk about all the things in her head, I guess.

4229
03:26:44,470 --> 03:26:45,890
I'm still getting the hang of it myself.

4230
03:26:47,870 --> 03:26:48,190
Hmm...

4231
03:26:48,190 --> 03:26:50,050
You're certainly putting in a lot of effort.

4232
03:26:51,530 --> 03:26:52,990
You must really like her.

4233
03:26:53,970 --> 03:26:54,430
Ah?

4234
03:26:55,290 --> 03:26:55,750
That's...

4235
03:26:55,750 --> 03:26:56,350
Ah!

4236
03:26:56,990 --> 03:26:58,470
It's awfully suspicious, you know?

4237
03:26:59,210 --> 03:27:01,110
Spending time with her in the club room every day?

4238
03:27:01,110 --> 03:27:03,350
Aren't we all in the club room every day?

4239
03:27:03,510 --> 03:27:05,750
What do you mean I'm spending time equally with every one of you?

4240
03:27:08,530 --> 03:27:10,130
Reading that edgy novel with her?

4241
03:27:10,330 --> 03:27:11,430
Okay, I guess we did do that.

4242
03:27:12,030 --> 03:27:14,050
And I guess I did put some chocolate in her mouth.

4243
03:27:16,090 --> 03:27:16,450
Well...

4244
03:27:17,290 --> 03:27:18,010
I just...

4245
03:27:18,010 --> 03:27:20,830
feel bad that she has a hard time socializing, you know?

4246
03:27:20,990 --> 03:27:21,630
That's what it is.

4247
03:27:21,630 --> 03:27:24,610
It makes me want to make sure she doesn't spend all her time alone!

4248
03:27:26,310 --> 03:27:26,810
Besides...

4249
03:27:26,810 --> 03:27:28,850
The novel isn't too bad either, you know?

4250
03:27:30,050 --> 03:27:31,050
Alright, alright.

4251
03:27:31,830 --> 03:27:32,970
I get you.

4252
03:27:34,330 --> 03:27:34,830
Just...

4253
03:27:34,830 --> 03:27:36,130
Be careful, alright?

4254
03:27:37,190 --> 03:27:40,250
I know that Yuri isn't used to opening herself up.

4255
03:27:42,050 --> 03:27:45,110
So if something bad happens while she's vulnerable...

4256
03:27:45,630 --> 03:27:47,130
Then it could be really hard for her.

4257
03:27:47,950 --> 03:27:50,230
Her books aren't a total escape from reality.

4258
03:27:51,130 --> 03:27:52,390
They're just a bandage.

4259
03:27:53,270 --> 03:27:55,270
You say that like I'm gonna hurt her.

4260
03:27:56,310 --> 03:27:58,050
Sorry, I didn't really mean that.

4261
03:27:59,570 --> 03:28:01,970
If anything, she might accidentally hurt herself.

4262
03:28:03,070 --> 03:28:03,590
What?

4263
03:28:05,750 --> 03:28:06,270
Anyway...

4264
03:28:06,270 --> 03:28:08,050
I'll share my poem with you now, alright?

4265
03:28:08,450 --> 03:28:09,730
The fuck are you talking about?

4266
03:28:11,030 --> 03:28:11,550
Uh...

4267
03:28:11,550 --> 03:28:12,230
Alright.

4268
03:28:13,850 --> 03:28:14,730
The lady?

4269
03:28:15,290 --> 03:28:16,550
Who knows everything?

4270
03:28:17,410 --> 03:28:19,970
An old tale tells of a lady who wanders earth.

4271
03:28:20,470 --> 03:28:22,550
The lady who knows everything.

4272
03:28:23,290 --> 03:28:25,450
A beautiful lady who has found every answer.

4273
03:28:25,850 --> 03:28:26,670
All meaning.

4274
03:28:27,070 --> 03:28:27,930
All purpose.

4275
03:28:28,310 --> 03:28:30,010
And all that was ever sought.

4276
03:28:30,410 --> 03:28:31,410
And here I am.

4277
03:28:31,750 --> 03:28:32,430
A feather.

4278
03:28:33,110 --> 03:28:34,970
Lost, adrift the sky.

4279
03:28:35,390 --> 03:28:37,010
Victim of the currents of the wind.

4280
03:28:37,750 --> 03:28:39,130
Day after day I search.

4281
03:28:39,130 --> 03:28:42,810
I search with a little hope, knowing legends don't exist.

4282
03:28:43,490 --> 03:28:48,990
But when all else has failed me, when all others have turned away, the legend is all that remains.

4283
03:28:49,430 --> 03:28:52,470
The last dim star glimmering in the twilight sky.

4284
03:28:53,170 --> 03:28:55,550
Until one day, the wind ceases to blow.

4285
03:28:55,550 --> 03:28:56,830
I fall.

4286
03:28:57,110 --> 03:28:57,670
I fall.

4287
03:28:57,670 --> 03:28:58,170
And I fall.

4288
03:28:58,370 --> 03:28:58,990
And fall.

4289
03:28:59,210 --> 03:29:00,450
And fall even more.

4290
03:29:01,010 --> 03:29:02,170
Gentle as a feather.

4291
03:29:02,610 --> 03:29:03,830
A dry quill.

4292
03:29:04,150 --> 03:29:04,710
Expressionless.

4293
03:29:05,210 --> 03:29:08,150
But a hand catches me between the thumb and forefinger.

4294
03:29:08,530 --> 03:29:10,090
The hand of a beautiful lady.

4295
03:29:10,510 --> 03:29:13,090
I look at her eyes and find no end to her gaze.

4296
03:29:13,510 --> 03:29:16,030
The lady who knows everything knows what I'm thinking.

4297
03:29:16,790 --> 03:29:20,190
Before I can speak, she responds in a hollow voice.

4298
03:29:20,850 --> 03:29:22,330
I found every answer.

4299
03:29:22,690 --> 03:29:24,110
All of which amount to nothing.

4300
03:29:24,570 --> 03:29:25,670
There is no meaning.

4301
03:29:26,230 --> 03:29:27,330
There is no purpose.

4302
03:29:27,910 --> 03:29:29,630
And we seek only the impossible.

4303
03:29:30,230 --> 03:29:31,630
I am not your legend.

4304
03:29:32,170 --> 03:29:33,630
Your legend does not exist.

4305
03:29:34,290 --> 03:29:37,570
And with a breath, she blows me back afloat.

4306
03:29:37,890 --> 03:29:39,470
And I pick up a gust of wind.

4307
03:29:40,990 --> 03:29:41,470
Man.

4308
03:29:42,770 --> 03:29:43,850
I like this one!

4309
03:29:45,490 --> 03:29:48,190
This may be one of the first ones that I actually kinda really like.

4310
03:29:51,510 --> 03:29:52,930
It's still kinda depressing though.

4311
03:29:54,990 --> 03:29:55,790
You know...

4312
03:29:56,410 --> 03:29:59,690
I feel like learning and looking for answers are the sorts of things that give life meaning.

4313
03:30:00,890 --> 03:30:03,070
Not to get too philosophical or anything.

4314
03:30:04,210 --> 03:30:06,790
But it was kinda on my mind, so that's what I wrote about.

4315
03:30:07,790 --> 03:30:08,570
I see.

4316
03:30:09,770 --> 03:30:11,670
I never really put much thought into it.

4317
03:30:12,750 --> 03:30:14,630
In a way, it's almost paradoxical.

4318
03:30:15,670 --> 03:30:18,730
Because if we had all the answers, wouldn't the world start to lose its meaning?

4319
03:30:19,450 --> 03:30:20,130
Fuck yeah.

4320
03:30:21,170 --> 03:30:22,830
You know, there's one thing I noticed.

4321
03:30:23,870 --> 03:30:28,130
It seems like everyone in the club prefers writing about things that are more sad than happy.

4322
03:30:28,990 --> 03:30:30,830
That's an understatement of the year.

4323
03:30:32,970 --> 03:30:34,010
Are you surprised?

4324
03:30:34,590 --> 03:30:35,050
Yeah.

4325
03:30:36,070 --> 03:30:37,730
I mean, if everything was okay...

4326
03:30:38,890 --> 03:30:41,190
We wouldn't really have anything to write about, would we?

4327
03:30:41,190 --> 03:30:42,810
That's what I hate about the world.

4328
03:30:43,870 --> 03:30:45,950
Yes, we wanna write about things that are okay.

4329
03:30:46,610 --> 03:30:48,450
Don't just post negative things.

4330
03:30:49,210 --> 03:30:51,290
I hate that everything has become negative lately.

4331
03:30:52,190 --> 03:30:53,390
Good things are good, man.

4332
03:30:53,530 --> 03:30:54,150
Talk about that.

4333
03:30:56,490 --> 03:30:58,270
Humans aren't two-dimensional creatures.

4334
03:30:58,990 --> 03:31:00,810
I think you'd know that better than anyone.

4335
03:31:02,390 --> 03:31:03,530
You mean one-dimensional?

4336
03:31:04,750 --> 03:31:05,510
Aha, yeah.

4337
03:31:05,970 --> 03:31:06,250
That.

4338
03:31:07,150 --> 03:31:07,590
Anyway.

4339
03:31:08,430 --> 03:31:10,070
Here's Monica's writing tip of the day!

4340
03:31:10,930 --> 03:31:14,630
Are you ever too shy to share your writing because you're afraid it's not that good?

4341
03:31:15,470 --> 03:31:19,070
It can be really disheartening to get a lukewarm response to something you put so much into.

4342
03:31:20,210 --> 03:31:24,010
But if you find other people who enjoy writing, and sharing becomes a lot easier!

4343
03:31:24,870 --> 03:31:30,210
Because instead of just telling you that your writing is good or okay or bad, they'll wanna focus more on everything that went into it!

4344
03:31:30,330 --> 03:31:31,470
And the things you can work on.

4345
03:31:32,630 --> 03:31:35,530
It's much more encouraging that way, and it will make you wanna continue improving.

4346
03:31:36,670 --> 03:31:39,310
It's almost like having your own little literature club, don't you think?

4347
03:31:39,890 --> 03:31:42,570
Bro, your advice are way too long, make it like one line.

4348
03:31:44,030 --> 03:31:44,990
That's my advice for today.

4349
03:31:45,650 --> 03:31:46,450
Thanks for listening!

4350
03:31:46,450 --> 03:31:49,430
I hate this pose, it's making me angry.

4351
03:31:52,010 --> 03:31:52,970
It's like...

4352
03:31:54,070 --> 03:31:54,810
I don't know.

4353
03:31:59,030 --> 03:32:00,050
Oh my god.

4354
03:32:00,830 --> 03:32:01,810
Okay, you three!

4355
03:32:02,610 --> 03:32:04,210
We're all done sharing poems, right?

4356
03:32:05,490 --> 03:32:07,570
Why don't we start figuring out...

4357
03:32:07,570 --> 03:32:08,590
Hold on a second!

4358
03:32:09,210 --> 03:32:11,690
Is it just me, or did you say something strange just now?

4359
03:32:12,710 --> 03:32:13,110
Eh?

4360
03:32:14,230 --> 03:32:15,810
Something did sound a bit unusual.

4361
03:32:17,310 --> 03:32:18,110
That's right.

4362
03:32:19,010 --> 03:32:22,690
You deviated from your usual catchphrase when addressing the club!

4363
03:32:23,630 --> 03:32:24,750
C-catchphrase?

4364
03:32:25,530 --> 03:32:27,210
I don't have a catchphrase!

4365
03:32:28,070 --> 03:32:28,630
Geez!

4366
03:32:29,590 --> 03:32:31,390
Why is the mood so weird today?

4367
03:32:32,250 --> 03:32:34,050
Look, even Yuri isn't immune to it.

4368
03:32:36,550 --> 03:32:37,110
Uh...

4369
03:32:37,110 --> 03:32:41,090
Stagnating air is common foreshadowing that something terrible is about to happen.

4370
03:32:42,510 --> 03:32:43,990
In your books, maybe.

4371
03:32:45,110 --> 03:32:47,850
Look, the only thing different is that Sayori isn't here.

4372
03:32:49,630 --> 03:32:50,150
Ah...

4373
03:32:50,150 --> 03:32:51,310
Seems you're right.

4374
03:32:53,330 --> 03:32:53,850
Ah...

4375
03:32:53,850 --> 03:32:56,630
Sayori always helps lighten the mood a little bit, doesn't she?

4376
03:32:57,770 --> 03:33:00,950
It's almost like everyone's balance is thrown off a little when she's not around.

4377
03:33:02,310 --> 03:33:03,990
Where the heck did she run off to anyway?

4378
03:33:04,750 --> 03:33:06,110
I thought she just went to pee.

4379
03:33:07,510 --> 03:33:07,970
That's okay.

4380
03:33:08,370 --> 03:33:09,790
Please, show some decency.

4381
03:33:10,870 --> 03:33:11,650
Ah, come on!

4382
03:33:12,970 --> 03:33:15,870
Ah, she actually wasn't feeling too well and went home early.

4383
03:33:16,630 --> 03:33:17,530
Is that so?

4384
03:33:18,250 --> 03:33:19,370
I hope she's alright.

4385
03:33:19,770 --> 03:33:20,430
She's not!

4386
03:33:20,590 --> 03:33:21,290
She's not feeling well.

4387
03:33:22,390 --> 03:33:22,870
Seriously?

4388
03:33:23,630 --> 03:33:28,190
Of all the times to not go home with her, you pick the time she's not feeling well?

4389
03:33:29,090 --> 03:33:31,230
So much for you two being all lovey-dovey.

4390
03:33:32,290 --> 03:33:32,670
Ah!

4391
03:33:33,150 --> 03:33:33,630
No!

4392
03:33:34,530 --> 03:33:37,750
First of all, stop misunderstanding my friendship with Sayori.

4393
03:33:39,250 --> 03:33:40,210
And second...

4394
03:33:40,210 --> 03:33:42,930
She's kind of been avoiding me today, so I didn't want to force it.

4395
03:33:44,010 --> 03:33:44,390
Oh?

4396
03:33:45,450 --> 03:33:48,910
That curious expression coming from Yuri, of all people.

4397
03:33:50,530 --> 03:33:51,730
Calm down, guys.

4398
03:33:52,250 --> 03:33:54,770
I talked to her earlier, and everything is fine.

4399
03:33:56,410 --> 03:33:57,450
What did she say?

4400
03:33:58,950 --> 03:34:03,210
Anyway, we need to figure out the rest of the festival preparations, so...

4401
03:34:03,210 --> 03:34:06,150
Let's decide what everyone will be doing this weekend.

4402
03:34:07,090 --> 03:34:08,690
I already know what I'm doing.

4403
03:34:09,670 --> 03:34:10,430
That's right.

4404
03:34:11,750 --> 03:34:13,190
Natsuki will be making cupcakes.

4405
03:34:14,390 --> 03:34:17,350
But, we might need a lot of them, and different flavors.

4406
03:34:18,450 --> 03:34:20,630
Can you handle that all by yourself, Natsuki?

4407
03:34:21,930 --> 03:34:22,730
Challenge accepted.

4408
03:34:23,810 --> 03:34:24,810
And as for myself?

4409
03:34:25,430 --> 03:34:28,210
I'm gonna be printing and assembling all the poetry pamphlets.

4410
03:34:29,050 --> 03:34:30,350
Also called the PBs.

4411
03:34:30,990 --> 03:34:32,470
Sayori will be helping me design them.

4412
03:34:33,290 --> 03:34:34,390
And as for Yuri...

4413
03:34:35,610 --> 03:34:36,540
Yuri, you can...

4414
03:34:37,960 --> 03:34:38,560
Uh...

4415
03:34:40,340 --> 03:34:40,940
Um...

4416
03:34:41,540 --> 03:34:42,080
Guys!

4417
03:34:42,720 --> 03:34:44,340
Can you help me come up with something for Yuri?

4418
03:34:45,900 --> 03:34:46,500
I...

4419
03:34:47,300 --> 03:34:48,000
I'm useless.

4420
03:34:48,920 --> 03:34:49,360
No!

4421
03:34:49,920 --> 03:34:51,060
That's not it at all!

4422
03:34:51,440 --> 03:34:53,260
You're the most talented person here.

4423
03:34:53,420 --> 03:34:53,780
You know.

4424
03:34:56,260 --> 03:34:57,660
Now Natsuki's pouting too?

4425
03:34:59,000 --> 03:34:59,400
Geez!

4426
03:34:59,780 --> 03:35:00,780
Even I can tell now!

4427
03:35:01,760 --> 03:35:03,720
I guess I never gave Sayori enough credit.

4428
03:35:04,160 --> 03:35:06,880
But I can tell things are even harder on you when she's not around.

4429
03:35:08,420 --> 03:35:08,820
Ah...

4430
03:35:08,820 --> 03:35:10,180
That may be the case.

4431
03:35:10,880 --> 03:35:13,940
But if I can't also be leader on my own, then I won't grow as a person.

4432
03:35:14,900 --> 03:35:16,360
So, Yuri...

4433
03:35:16,940 --> 03:35:17,440
You...

4434
03:35:17,440 --> 03:35:19,220
Have beautiful handwriting, you know?

4435
03:35:20,060 --> 03:35:24,480
So, you should make some banners and decorations to help set the atmosphere.

4436
03:35:25,540 --> 03:35:26,220
Atmosphere?

4437
03:35:27,480 --> 03:35:28,160
Um...

4438
03:35:28,160 --> 03:35:29,580
About that...

4439
03:35:29,580 --> 03:35:30,740
I...

4440
03:35:30,740 --> 03:35:32,180
I love atmosphere!

4441
03:35:32,920 --> 03:35:38,320
Yuri's expression suddenly changes as she stares at her desk in focus and starts nodding to herself.

4442
03:35:39,440 --> 03:35:41,660
Your mind is already racing, I see.

4443
03:35:42,600 --> 03:35:43,480
That's great!

4444
03:35:44,140 --> 03:35:45,680
You'll be wonderful, help Yuri.

4445
03:35:46,800 --> 03:35:47,800
But anyway...

4446
03:35:47,800 --> 03:35:49,280
That just leaves you, Oliver.

4447
03:35:50,360 --> 03:35:52,320
The one who is truly useless.

4448
03:35:53,380 --> 03:35:53,740
Haha!

4449
03:35:54,080 --> 03:35:54,900
Don't say that.

4450
03:35:55,840 --> 03:35:56,840
In fact...

4451
03:35:56,840 --> 03:36:00,080
Both Natsuki and Yuri have some pretty heavy tasks to handle.

4452
03:36:00,960 --> 03:36:03,560
It would probably go a long way to give one of them a hand.

4453
03:36:04,280 --> 03:36:06,060
You could always help me out as well.

4454
03:36:06,680 --> 03:36:08,820
I would be really appreciative of that.

4455
03:36:11,540 --> 03:36:12,100
Uhh...

4456
03:36:13,480 --> 03:36:14,040
That's...

4457
03:36:14,040 --> 03:36:17,840
Is Monica suggesting I spend the weekend with one of my club members?

4458
03:36:19,080 --> 03:36:19,640
No!

4459
03:36:20,480 --> 03:36:24,000
She's suggesting you help them with whatever fucking task they have!

4460
03:36:24,840 --> 03:36:25,820
How are you...

4461
03:36:25,820 --> 03:36:26,780
I don't understand.

4462
03:36:27,020 --> 03:36:27,300
This guy.

4463
03:36:27,760 --> 03:36:30,880
How on earth are they going to respond to a suggestion like that?

4464
03:36:33,060 --> 03:36:33,380
Ah...

4465
03:36:34,020 --> 03:36:34,340
I...

4466
03:36:34,740 --> 03:36:36,000
Suppose I wouldn't mind a bit of help.

4467
03:36:37,500 --> 03:36:40,840
Well, even if you don't know how to bake, there's always some dirty work I could give to you.

4468
03:36:41,720 --> 03:36:44,980
It's not like Monica's going to give me a choice and you shouldn't be sitting on your butt anyway.

4469
03:36:45,900 --> 03:36:48,580
Natsuki tries to mumble a bunch of excuses like that.

4470
03:36:49,860 --> 03:36:50,340
Umm...

4471
03:36:50,340 --> 03:36:51,980
If I recall Natsuki...

4472
03:36:53,060 --> 03:36:56,160
You mentioned that you would like to handle the baking on your own?

4473
03:36:57,620 --> 03:37:02,360
Oliver may not like to be around if you only make him out to be a nuisance.

4474
03:37:04,100 --> 03:37:05,060
So therefore...

4475
03:37:05,060 --> 03:37:08,480
He may be more suited to assisting with the decorations.

4476
03:37:09,380 --> 03:37:10,120
Hold on!

4477
03:37:10,280 --> 03:37:11,000
I never said that!

4478
03:37:11,640 --> 03:37:13,680
How hard could it be to make a few decorations anyway?

4479
03:37:14,460 --> 03:37:17,260
Sounds more like you're just making excuses for Oliver to...

4480
03:37:17,260 --> 03:37:17,720
W...

4481
03:37:17,720 --> 03:37:18,640
What are you saying?

4482
03:37:19,980 --> 03:37:22,040
It will be extremely meticulous work.

4483
03:37:23,100 --> 03:37:24,160
And baking isn't?

4484
03:37:25,080 --> 03:37:26,020
Just what do you think?

4485
03:37:26,700 --> 03:37:27,060
Guys!

4486
03:37:27,600 --> 03:37:27,960
Guys!

4487
03:37:29,080 --> 03:37:30,300
Let's settle down one moment.

4488
03:37:31,040 --> 03:37:34,100
In the end, I think it's up to Oliver to decide how he'd like to contribute.

4489
03:37:35,600 --> 03:37:35,960
Besides...

4490
03:37:35,960 --> 03:37:38,900
He hasn't really gotten the chance to spend any time with me yet, you know?

4491
03:37:39,780 --> 03:37:41,080
I'm sure he's interested in...

4492
03:37:41,720 --> 03:37:43,100
You literally just said...

4493
03:37:43,820 --> 03:37:44,260
I...

4494
03:37:44,620 --> 03:37:45,740
I'm surprised as well.

4495
03:37:46,720 --> 03:37:47,080
Sorry.

4496
03:37:47,340 --> 03:37:47,640
Sorry.

4497
03:37:48,180 --> 03:37:49,940
I was just saying though...

4498
03:37:50,900 --> 03:37:51,440
Jeez.

4499
03:37:52,360 --> 03:37:53,520
Haven't you settled this already?

4500
03:37:54,200 --> 03:37:54,860
Yeah.

4501
03:37:55,320 --> 03:37:57,180
Oliver, you're okay with this, right?

4502
03:37:57,700 --> 03:37:59,680
In the end, it's up to you.

4503
03:38:01,220 --> 03:38:01,940
Ah!

4504
03:38:03,800 --> 03:38:04,520
Wire!

4505
03:38:05,720 --> 03:38:06,460
Of course!

4506
03:38:07,400 --> 03:38:07,780
Hm.

4507
03:38:08,480 --> 03:38:09,500
Very well.

4508
03:38:10,840 --> 03:38:12,900
In that case...

4509
03:38:13,180 --> 03:38:14,540
Everyone looks straight at me.

4510
03:38:15,320 --> 03:38:17,880
But of course, I'm gonna go with...

4511
03:38:18,180 --> 03:38:18,940
You knew it!

4512
03:38:19,320 --> 03:38:19,820
Yuri.

4513
03:38:21,220 --> 03:38:25,140
Well, I'll probably be most useful helping out, Yuri.

4514
03:38:28,000 --> 03:38:29,340
Are you serious?

4515
03:38:29,820 --> 03:38:31,520
Why would you...

4516
03:38:31,520 --> 03:38:32,160
Natsuki?

4517
03:38:32,740 --> 03:38:34,860
I can already tell you're about to say something mean.

4518
03:38:35,920 --> 03:38:36,380
No.

4519
03:38:36,980 --> 03:38:38,980
I was just saying...

4520
03:38:40,300 --> 03:38:42,340
So, you'll be helping Yuri then, Oliver?

4521
03:38:43,340 --> 03:38:43,820
Yeah.

4522
03:38:44,920 --> 03:38:46,120
That's what I'm gonna do.

4523
03:38:47,020 --> 03:38:47,660
I'm glad.

4524
03:38:48,200 --> 03:38:51,020
I have a bad habit of overthinking these sorts of things.

4525
03:38:51,620 --> 03:38:52,340
I think...

4526
03:38:52,340 --> 03:38:52,980
We all do.

4527
03:38:54,500 --> 03:38:56,100
So I think your assistance will be very...

4528
03:38:56,760 --> 03:38:57,160
Useful.

4529
03:38:58,220 --> 03:38:59,380
That's great to hear.

4530
03:39:00,380 --> 03:39:02,220
Natsuki, will you be able to handle the bacon yourself?

4531
03:39:03,180 --> 03:39:04,120
I mean, yeah.

4532
03:39:04,900 --> 03:39:06,200
I already said it would be fine.

4533
03:39:07,260 --> 03:39:08,060
Okay, okay.

4534
03:39:08,860 --> 03:39:11,220
Everyone can tell that Natsuki is feeling a little sour.

4535
03:39:12,680 --> 03:39:14,620
So, is that everything we needed to go over?

4536
03:39:16,060 --> 03:39:17,420
Yeah, that should be about it.

4537
03:39:18,240 --> 03:39:19,260
Are you guys excited?

4538
03:39:21,320 --> 03:39:21,760
Well...

4539
03:39:22,240 --> 03:39:23,460
Excited may not be the right word.

4540
03:39:24,620 --> 03:39:26,540
But I suppose I'm looking forward to it...

4541
03:39:26,540 --> 03:39:27,220
A little bit.

4542
03:39:28,460 --> 03:39:29,680
You feel the same way, Oliver?

4543
03:39:30,440 --> 03:39:30,880
Me?

4544
03:39:32,580 --> 03:39:33,020
Ah!

4545
03:39:33,480 --> 03:39:35,920
I guess you could say I'm interested to see how it'll turn out.

4546
03:39:37,120 --> 03:39:38,200
That's good enough for me!

4547
03:39:38,880 --> 03:39:40,000
What about you, Natsuki?

4548
03:39:42,640 --> 03:39:43,080
Natsuki?

4549
03:39:44,280 --> 03:39:44,720
What?!

4550
03:39:45,360 --> 03:39:46,760
Why is everyone yelling at me?!

4551
03:39:47,420 --> 03:39:48,540
I didn't even do anything!

4552
03:39:49,280 --> 03:39:50,020
N-No!

4553
03:39:50,580 --> 03:39:51,080
That...

4554
03:39:51,080 --> 03:39:52,200
That's not what I meant at all.

4555
03:39:54,300 --> 03:39:54,800
Ah...

4556
03:39:54,800 --> 03:39:57,420
Yuri anxiously glances between everyone in the room.

4557
03:39:59,060 --> 03:40:00,560
I-I'm sorry for this.

4558
03:40:01,440 --> 03:40:03,460
I don't really know why Oliver picked me.

4559
03:40:04,680 --> 03:40:05,680
And also...

4560
03:40:05,680 --> 03:40:08,280
Your cupcakes are the best cupcakes I've ever had.

4561
03:40:09,080 --> 03:40:10,820
They go really well with my tea.

4562
03:40:11,600 --> 03:40:14,420
And nothing that I do for the event will compare to that, so...

4563
03:40:16,280 --> 03:40:16,780
So...

4564
03:40:16,780 --> 03:40:18,320
I get it, I get it.

4565
03:40:18,780 --> 03:40:20,000
I'm kinda surprised though.

4566
03:40:20,960 --> 03:40:21,360
Why?

4567
03:40:22,460 --> 03:40:22,860
Um...

4568
03:40:22,860 --> 03:40:24,660
Well, I'm the one acting immature!

4569
03:40:25,460 --> 03:40:26,600
I already know that.

4570
03:40:27,280 --> 03:40:29,100
But you're trying to cheer me up all of a sudden.

4571
03:40:30,020 --> 03:40:31,700
I... I know I'm not very good at it.

4572
03:40:32,420 --> 03:40:33,940
I'm sorry if I said something bad.

4573
03:40:34,940 --> 03:40:36,540
Natsuki isn't the only one surprised.

4574
03:40:37,380 --> 03:40:39,800
Monica and I are also taken aback by Yuri's words.

4575
03:40:40,600 --> 03:40:41,400
Am I?!

4576
03:40:41,980 --> 03:40:46,520
When she already has trouble with words, trying to cheer someone up must be far out of her own comfort zone.

4577
03:40:46,980 --> 03:40:48,680
Just like I'm out of my comfort zone!

4578
03:40:49,600 --> 03:40:50,820
But I begin to understand.

4579
03:40:51,820 --> 03:40:53,280
Yuri was trying to sound like Sayori.

4580
03:40:54,120 --> 03:40:58,600
Even if it didn't work perfectly, I can tell that she tried to say something Sayori would say at a time like this.

4581
03:40:59,180 --> 03:41:02,520
Because Sayori always helps everyone smile and feel good about themselves.

4582
03:41:03,740 --> 03:41:04,120
No!

4583
03:41:05,200 --> 03:41:06,900
I kinda appreciated it.

4584
03:41:07,440 --> 03:41:09,960
I'm sorry for making a big deal out of nothing.

4585
03:41:10,720 --> 03:41:12,760
But I'm gonna say this...

4586
03:41:13,980 --> 03:41:17,300
You better bet that my cupcakes are gonna be the best part of the whole event!

4587
03:41:18,600 --> 03:41:19,160
Ah...

4588
03:41:19,160 --> 03:41:20,300
I believe you.

4589
03:41:21,020 --> 03:41:21,440
Yeah!

4590
03:41:21,920 --> 03:41:23,640
I hope to see everyone do their best!

4591
03:41:24,200 --> 03:41:25,500
But with that...

4592
03:41:25,500 --> 03:41:27,000
There's nothing more for today!

4593
03:41:27,540 --> 03:41:29,160
So I guess it's time for us to head out!

4594
03:41:30,100 --> 03:41:31,260
Alright, let's get out of here then!

4595
03:41:32,840 --> 03:41:34,260
Everyone packs up their things.

4596
03:41:34,900 --> 03:41:38,760
I start to follow Monika and Natsuki out the door as they chat between each other.

4597
03:41:41,000 --> 03:41:41,440
Uh...

4598
03:41:42,760 --> 03:41:43,200
Um...

4599
03:41:43,200 --> 03:41:44,040
Eh?!

4600
03:41:45,240 --> 03:41:46,380
I turn around.

4601
03:41:48,160 --> 03:41:48,600
Sorry...

4602
03:41:49,120 --> 03:41:50,460
I realize that I...

4603
03:41:50,460 --> 03:41:53,140
Don't have any way of contacting you this weekend.

4604
03:41:54,320 --> 03:41:55,100
Oh, you're right!

4605
03:41:55,160 --> 03:41:55,480
That's fine!

4606
03:41:55,560 --> 03:41:57,240
You can catch me at, uh...

4607
03:41:57,240 --> 03:41:58,660
I don't know...

4608
03:41:58,660 --> 03:41:59,820
You know smoke signaling?

4609
03:42:00,820 --> 03:42:02,580
I can't believe that slipped my mind.

4610
03:42:03,340 --> 03:42:04,580
Should I give you my phone number?

4611
03:42:04,700 --> 03:42:05,760
Is what I meant to say before.

4612
03:42:05,880 --> 03:42:06,080
Sorry.

4613
03:42:07,040 --> 03:42:07,900
I think...

4614
03:42:07,900 --> 03:42:09,180
That would be best...

4615
03:42:09,180 --> 03:42:09,460
Yes.

4616
03:42:10,120 --> 03:42:10,920
Alright then!

4617
03:42:11,760 --> 03:42:13,440
Yuri and I exchange phone numbers.

4618
03:42:14,240 --> 03:42:14,720
Okay.

4619
03:42:15,380 --> 03:42:17,380
Then I'll be stopping by your house on Sunday.

4620
03:42:18,780 --> 03:42:19,240
Eh?!

4621
03:42:20,680 --> 03:42:21,600
My house?!

4622
03:42:21,600 --> 03:42:23,020
Is that a problem?

4623
03:42:23,740 --> 03:42:24,060
No!

4624
03:42:24,300 --> 03:42:25,500
Not at all!

4625
03:42:26,360 --> 03:42:28,240
I just thought that it would be...

4626
03:42:28,240 --> 03:42:29,100
You know...

4627
03:42:29,100 --> 03:42:30,100
Going to your house!

4628
03:42:30,120 --> 03:42:31,300
Since I'm the one helping you!

4629
03:42:32,580 --> 03:42:34,300
Ah, I suppose that makes sense.

4630
03:42:35,200 --> 03:42:36,720
But if you don't mind...

4631
03:42:37,240 --> 03:42:39,080
I think I would prefer going to your house.

4632
03:42:40,000 --> 03:42:40,460
Alright!

4633
03:42:41,540 --> 03:42:43,200
In that case, it won't be a problem.

4634
03:42:43,960 --> 03:42:46,140
I decide not to press Yuri for a reason.

4635
03:42:47,720 --> 03:42:48,120
Hahahaha!

4636
03:42:49,080 --> 03:42:50,000
Just for a reason.

4637
03:42:50,800 --> 03:42:52,260
It's not like it should matter much either way.

4638
03:42:52,500 --> 03:42:54,300
So I'll just need to make sure my room is clean.

4639
03:42:55,520 --> 03:42:57,860
I hope I can manage to make myself useful in some way.

4640
03:42:58,840 --> 03:43:00,620
I'm not nearly as creative as you are.

4641
03:43:01,920 --> 03:43:03,700
Don't underestimate yourself, Oliver.

4642
03:43:04,080 --> 03:43:06,040
I think that will make a very productive team.

4643
03:43:06,920 --> 03:43:09,440
Even if you only choose me because you felt bad or something.

4644
03:43:10,740 --> 03:43:11,140
Wait!

4645
03:43:12,420 --> 03:43:14,100
You don't actually think that, do you?

4646
03:43:16,600 --> 03:43:16,960
I...

4647
03:43:16,960 --> 03:43:17,580
Don't know.

4648
03:43:18,480 --> 03:43:21,040
It's difficult to come up with any other reason you may have chosen me.

4649
03:43:22,620 --> 03:43:25,060
You're forgetting the one reason with the most common sense!

4650
03:43:26,020 --> 03:43:30,620
I choose to help you because that's what I want to do, and there's no other way to get into your pants!

4651
03:43:31,420 --> 03:43:32,280
But...

4652
03:43:32,280 --> 03:43:35,820
That's what I probably would have said if it wasn't this weirdly COMPLICATED!

4653
03:43:37,000 --> 03:43:38,260
Just get to it, man!

4654
03:43:39,380 --> 03:43:41,860
Yuri thinks to herself with an extremely tense expression.

4655
03:43:43,780 --> 03:43:44,300
Yuri...

4656
03:43:44,300 --> 03:43:45,700
You're overthinking this...

4657
03:43:47,040 --> 03:43:47,560
Pants...

4658
03:43:47,560 --> 03:43:48,320
Me...

4659
03:43:48,320 --> 03:43:48,840
In them.

4660
03:43:49,460 --> 03:43:49,980
Bean...

4661
03:43:49,980 --> 03:43:50,720
In your mouth.

4662
03:43:51,360 --> 03:43:52,080
You see?

4663
03:43:52,580 --> 03:43:54,660
You wanted me to point out when you were overthinking, right?

4664
03:43:55,460 --> 03:43:56,100
Huh?

4665
03:43:57,220 --> 03:43:57,860
I...

4666
03:43:57,860 --> 03:43:59,740
Didn't realize...

4667
03:43:59,740 --> 03:44:00,900
I'm telling you!

4668
03:44:01,120 --> 03:44:02,240
I want to!

4669
03:44:03,020 --> 03:44:04,620
That's all there is to it!

4670
03:44:05,260 --> 03:44:06,480
Do you believe me?

4671
03:44:08,180 --> 03:44:08,820
I...

4672
03:44:08,820 --> 03:44:10,540
Yuri thinks really hard again.

4673
03:44:10,680 --> 03:44:10,800
Dude!

4674
03:44:11,920 --> 03:44:16,220
You know how they say men are, you know, oblivious to signs?

4675
03:44:17,200 --> 03:44:19,760
This girl is really oblivious to signs!

4676
03:44:21,900 --> 03:44:24,200
She looks straight into my eyes for a long while.

4677
03:44:25,200 --> 03:44:26,220
I believe you!

4678
03:44:27,880 --> 03:44:32,380
As if it took her tremendous effort, Yuri finally says that and relaxes her expression.

4679
03:44:34,900 --> 03:44:36,640
And I'm really looking forward to Sunday.

4680
03:44:38,820 --> 03:44:39,300
Yeah.

4681
03:44:40,160 --> 03:44:41,060
I am too.

4682
03:44:43,300 --> 03:44:46,280
After that exchange, I make my way out the door and Yuri follows.

4683
03:44:48,420 --> 03:44:49,580
Wait, what day is it today?

4684
03:44:50,500 --> 03:44:51,620
I can't believe this.

4685
03:44:52,540 --> 03:44:54,420
Yuri is gonna be coming to my house on Sunday?

4686
03:44:55,140 --> 03:44:56,980
My anxiety shoots through the roof!

4687
03:44:57,720 --> 03:45:00,180
Even though I've gotten pretty used to handling her at this point.

4688
03:45:00,820 --> 03:45:03,960
There's no telling what might end up happening when we're outside of school.

4689
03:45:05,200 --> 03:45:07,660
More than that, she told me she was looking forward to it.

4690
03:45:08,480 --> 03:45:10,240
Is this the chance I have?

4691
03:45:10,420 --> 03:45:12,560
To make something happen between us?

4692
03:45:13,740 --> 03:45:15,080
Or is it too early for that?

4693
03:45:15,080 --> 03:45:17,620
Bro, now my character is oblivious!

4694
03:45:19,120 --> 03:45:21,300
She totally is into me!

4695
03:45:21,520 --> 03:45:21,840
Come on!

4696
03:45:22,460 --> 03:45:23,260
Don't be an idiot!

4697
03:45:24,120 --> 03:45:25,140
Only time will tell.

4698
03:45:25,980 --> 03:45:28,260
But until then, I won't be able to take my mind off it.

4699
03:45:28,680 --> 03:45:30,200
I seriously can't wait.

4700
03:45:32,880 --> 03:45:34,100
It'll be interesting.

4701
03:45:34,300 --> 03:45:35,220
Hopefully there's a new backdrop.

4702
03:45:36,020 --> 03:45:37,200
It's already Sunday!

4703
03:45:37,200 --> 03:45:37,920
No shit!

4704
03:45:38,560 --> 03:45:39,240
Here we go!

4705
03:45:40,480 --> 03:45:43,480
I've been getting increasingly anxious about Yuri's upcoming visit.

4706
03:45:44,200 --> 03:45:46,460
I keep telling myself there's no reason to be nervous.

4707
03:45:46,860 --> 03:45:48,020
But it doesn't help much.

4708
03:45:48,780 --> 03:45:51,980
Yuri is clearly an introvert and also an intimate person in general.

4709
03:45:52,620 --> 03:45:55,480
There's no doubt that she'll open up a little bit when it's just the two of us.

4710
03:45:56,680 --> 03:45:57,560
Meanwhile, we've...

4711
03:45:57,560 --> 03:45:59,260
Even been texting occasionally.

4712
03:45:59,740 --> 03:46:01,660
She was extremely apprehensive at first.

4713
03:46:01,960 --> 03:46:04,640
But it wasn't long before I was already learning more about her.

4714
03:46:05,220 --> 03:46:06,920
But putting Yuri aside...

4715
03:46:06,920 --> 03:46:10,420
I haven't heard a thing from Sayori since she left club early the other day.

4716
03:46:10,980 --> 03:46:13,380
It's not like we text each other all the time or anything.

4717
03:46:14,060 --> 03:46:16,100
But I've been worried about her in the back of my mind.

4718
03:46:16,940 --> 03:46:19,600
Between what Sayori said and what Monica said...

4719
03:46:19,600 --> 03:46:23,500
Is it really okay for me to put Sayori's feelings aside when she might need me?

4720
03:46:25,700 --> 03:46:26,680
I think it's okay.

4721
03:46:27,620 --> 03:46:30,220
I decide to visit Sayori before Yuri comes over.

4722
03:46:31,760 --> 03:46:36,000
Rather than asking, I simply tell her, I'm coming over, much like we've done in the past.

4723
03:46:37,160 --> 03:46:41,060
Once I reach Sayori's house, I knock on the door before entering it myself.

4724
03:46:42,380 --> 03:46:47,980
Again, we used to play so often that we've made it a habit of simply entering each other's houses, like we were family.

4725
03:46:49,820 --> 03:46:50,960
The house is quiet.

4726
03:46:52,960 --> 03:46:54,900
Sayori isn't anywhere on the first floor.

4727
03:46:55,400 --> 03:46:57,060
So I assume she's up in her room.

4728
03:46:58,540 --> 03:47:00,900
It's already strange of her not to run down and greet me.

4729
03:47:02,380 --> 03:47:04,960
I head up to her bedroom when I finally find her.

4730
03:47:08,350 --> 03:47:09,910
Oh my god, we actually get a backdrop.

4731
03:47:10,690 --> 03:47:11,110
Sayori?

4732
03:47:12,770 --> 03:47:13,530
Hi Oliver!

4733
03:47:14,790 --> 03:47:16,070
I sit down in the room.

4734
03:47:16,970 --> 03:47:19,790
Sayori forces a smile, but it's easy to tell that she's different.

4735
03:47:21,310 --> 03:47:22,890
Yeah, she's wearing different clothes.

4736
03:47:24,270 --> 03:47:25,970
There's a minute of silence between us.

4737
03:47:26,870 --> 03:47:28,050
Wait, is that even Sayori?

4738
03:47:28,390 --> 03:47:30,010
Wait a minute, did they do a swap on me?

4739
03:47:31,630 --> 03:47:32,570
That's not Sayori.

4740
03:47:34,470 --> 03:47:34,970
Is it?

4741
03:47:35,630 --> 03:47:36,250
Maybe it is.

4742
03:47:37,150 --> 03:47:39,230
You haven't come over like this in a long time, have you?

4743
03:47:40,450 --> 03:47:41,630
Ah, I guess you're right.

4744
03:47:42,790 --> 03:47:44,050
It has been a long time.

4745
03:47:44,870 --> 03:47:46,570
Not much has really changed, has it?

4746
03:47:47,750 --> 03:47:50,090
Sayori's room is as messy as it's always been.

4747
03:47:51,070 --> 03:47:52,370
This is not a messy room.

4748
03:47:53,650 --> 03:47:58,030
I also recognize the same stuffed animals and wall decorations that she's had for years now.

4749
03:47:59,150 --> 03:47:59,630
Cute.

4750
03:48:01,790 --> 03:48:04,330
If you came over more often, it wouldn't be such a mess.

4751
03:48:05,150 --> 03:48:07,070
That's because I ended up cleaning it for you!

4752
03:48:08,030 --> 03:48:09,870
How come you suddenly wanted to come over today?

4753
03:48:10,870 --> 03:48:12,470
Aren't you supposed to see Yuri today?

4754
03:48:14,750 --> 03:48:15,630
Yeah, but...

4755
03:48:16,630 --> 03:48:17,990
Wait, how did you know that?

4756
03:48:19,350 --> 03:48:22,590
Sayori had already left by the time we decided that last meeting.

4757
03:48:24,390 --> 03:48:25,290
Monica told me.

4758
03:48:26,170 --> 03:48:29,790
It's only natural for her to keep me informed about the festival preparations, right?

4759
03:48:31,010 --> 03:48:32,190
Ah, that's true.

4760
03:48:33,350 --> 03:48:34,110
But what about you?

4761
03:48:34,910 --> 03:48:36,450
Aren't you gonna be helping Monica today?

4762
03:48:37,630 --> 03:48:38,390
Of course!

4763
03:48:39,190 --> 03:48:40,730
But I'm just helping her online.

4764
03:48:41,430 --> 03:48:43,050
We didn't plan to meet up or anything.

4765
03:48:44,070 --> 03:48:46,030
Ah, so it's just me and Yuri then.

4766
03:48:46,950 --> 03:48:47,250
Yep.

4767
03:48:48,270 --> 03:48:49,990
There's some silence between us.

4768
03:48:50,950 --> 03:48:52,170
Maybe I should save the game.

4769
03:48:52,590 --> 03:48:53,470
Let me save here.

4770
03:48:53,830 --> 03:48:54,310
Or here.

4771
03:48:55,010 --> 03:48:55,510
Yeah.

4772
03:48:57,610 --> 03:48:59,490
Sayori stares in a random direction.

4773
03:49:00,330 --> 03:49:03,050
Everything about her behavior is really uncharacteristic.

4774
03:49:03,870 --> 03:49:05,470
She's been replaced by a robot!

4775
03:49:07,170 --> 03:49:08,950
I'm making up scenarios in my mind.

4776
03:49:09,410 --> 03:49:10,490
Something's gotta happen soon.

4777
03:49:11,550 --> 03:49:13,110
I finally get to the point.

4778
03:49:13,330 --> 03:49:13,750
Please!

4779
03:49:14,970 --> 03:49:16,130
I just...

4780
03:49:16,130 --> 03:49:17,650
wanted to see how you were doing.

4781
03:49:19,340 --> 03:49:20,390
After you left on Friday.

4782
03:49:21,210 --> 03:49:23,390
When something's wrong, you can't hide it from me.

4783
03:49:24,530 --> 03:49:25,750
I know you too well.

4784
03:49:27,470 --> 03:49:27,930
So...

4785
03:49:27,930 --> 03:49:29,770
Sayori smiles, shaking her head.

4786
03:49:30,950 --> 03:49:32,030
That's no good, Oliver.

4787
03:49:32,990 --> 03:49:33,290
Eh?

4788
03:49:34,490 --> 03:49:36,470
Why can't it just be like it's always been?

4789
03:49:37,470 --> 03:49:38,650
This is all my fault.

4790
03:49:39,650 --> 03:49:42,390
If I didn't get so weak and accidentally express my feelings...

4791
03:49:43,570 --> 03:49:45,650
If I didn't make that stupid mistake...

4792
03:49:46,270 --> 03:49:48,170
Then you wouldn't have been worried about me at all.

4793
03:49:49,130 --> 03:49:50,210
You wouldn't have come here.

4794
03:49:51,110 --> 03:49:53,090
You wouldn't even be thinking about me right now.

4795
03:49:54,430 --> 03:49:55,230
But this...

4796
03:49:55,230 --> 03:49:56,850
It's just my punishment, isn't it?

4797
03:49:57,510 --> 03:49:58,510
Bro, you need to...

4798
03:50:01,190 --> 03:50:01,830
Calm down.

4799
03:50:02,210 --> 03:50:02,930
It's gonna be alright.

4800
03:50:04,050 --> 03:50:06,130
I'm getting punished for being so selfish.

4801
03:50:06,810 --> 03:50:09,250
I think that's why the world decided to have you come over today.

4802
03:50:10,370 --> 03:50:11,970
It just wants to torture me.

4803
03:50:14,690 --> 03:50:15,210
Sayori?

4804
03:50:16,170 --> 03:50:18,290
I grabbed Sayori by the shoulders.

4805
03:50:19,290 --> 03:50:21,130
What on earth are you saying?

4806
03:50:21,910 --> 03:50:23,810
Are you listening to yourself right now?

4807
03:50:24,550 --> 03:50:26,250
I know something happened to you.

4808
03:50:26,890 --> 03:50:29,150
There's no other explanation for you to be like this.

4809
03:50:29,150 --> 03:50:31,410
Dude, my character is so oblivious.

4810
03:50:31,970 --> 03:50:33,450
She is clearly...

4811
03:50:33,910 --> 03:50:34,390
Into me.

4812
03:50:34,550 --> 03:50:35,250
She's envious.

4813
03:50:35,550 --> 03:50:36,550
Because of the other girls.

4814
03:50:37,270 --> 03:50:39,470
She's super duper depressed about it.

4815
03:50:40,150 --> 03:50:41,450
How is this not obvious?

4816
03:50:42,150 --> 03:50:43,310
So tell me already.

4817
03:50:44,110 --> 03:50:46,530
Until I know, I won't be able to stop thinking about it.

4818
03:50:47,690 --> 03:50:47,930
Ah.

4819
03:50:48,670 --> 03:50:49,090
Haha.

4820
03:50:50,170 --> 03:50:51,950
Sayori gives me an empty smile.

4821
03:50:52,470 --> 03:50:54,710
What's with this sound effect in the background?

4822
03:50:56,370 --> 03:50:58,090
You really put me in a trap, Oliver.

4823
03:50:59,770 --> 03:51:00,210
But...

4824
03:51:00,210 --> 03:51:01,130
You're wrong.

4825
03:51:02,250 --> 03:51:03,370
Nothing happened to me.

4826
03:51:04,030 --> 03:51:05,350
I've always been like this.

4827
03:51:06,030 --> 03:51:07,690
You're just seeing it for the first time.

4828
03:51:08,930 --> 03:51:09,630
Seeing what?

4829
03:51:10,890 --> 03:51:12,430
What are you talking about, Sayori?

4830
03:51:13,530 --> 03:51:13,970
Ahem.

4831
03:51:14,350 --> 03:51:16,250
You're really just gonna make me say it, aren't you, Oliver?

4832
03:51:16,390 --> 03:51:17,170
Yeah, please do.

4833
03:51:18,090 --> 03:51:19,690
I guess I have no choice this time.

4834
03:51:20,730 --> 03:51:21,910
The thing is...

4835
03:51:22,870 --> 03:51:25,010
I've had really bad depression my whole life.

4836
03:51:25,150 --> 03:51:25,830
Yeah, I figured.

4837
03:51:26,770 --> 03:51:27,650
Did you know that?

4838
03:51:28,770 --> 03:51:30,630
Why do you think I'm late to school every day?

4839
03:51:31,450 --> 03:51:34,090
Because most days I can't even find a reason to get out of bed.

4840
03:51:35,670 --> 03:51:38,810
What reason is there to do anything when I fully know how worthless I am?

4841
03:51:38,990 --> 03:51:39,770
Oh my god.

4842
03:51:39,930 --> 03:51:40,850
Please don't think like that.

4843
03:51:41,650 --> 03:51:42,670
Why go to school?

4844
03:51:43,370 --> 03:51:44,190
Why eat?

4845
03:51:44,790 --> 03:51:45,770
Why make friends?

4846
03:51:46,990 --> 03:51:51,170
Why make other people put their energy and caring to waste by having them spend it on me?

4847
03:51:51,830 --> 03:51:53,730
That is such a wrong way to think about it.

4848
03:51:53,790 --> 03:51:54,870
You can't be thinking like that.

4849
03:51:58,630 --> 03:51:59,730
That's bad for you.

4850
03:52:00,790 --> 03:52:01,970
That's what it feels like.

4851
03:52:02,490 --> 03:52:04,550
And that's why I just want to make everyone happy.

4852
03:52:05,550 --> 03:52:07,190
Without anyone worrying about me.

4853
03:52:10,730 --> 03:52:11,730
I'm in shock.

4854
03:52:12,790 --> 03:52:14,910
I can't even figure out how to respond.

4855
03:52:16,430 --> 03:52:20,310
How is it possible that Sayori kept this from me the entire time that I've known her?

4856
03:52:21,130 --> 03:52:28,370
Because it's pretty easy to display a front of happiness and hide what you're really feeling.

4857
03:52:29,830 --> 03:52:32,450
Did she really want so badly for me to just not think about her?

4858
03:52:34,130 --> 03:52:34,850
Why Sayori?

4859
03:52:36,630 --> 03:52:36,990
Eh?

4860
03:52:38,010 --> 03:52:40,110
Why is it that you've never told me about this?

4861
03:52:41,270 --> 03:52:44,030
It almost feels like I've been betrayed as your close friend.

4862
03:52:44,190 --> 03:52:44,410
No!

4863
03:52:44,690 --> 03:52:45,230
Don't say that.

4864
03:52:46,610 --> 03:52:49,290
Because if I knew I would have done everything I could to support you.

4865
03:52:49,490 --> 03:52:50,210
Okay, do say that.

4866
03:52:51,150 --> 03:52:53,330
Even if there is only so much that I could do.

4867
03:52:54,610 --> 03:52:57,550
I would have tried a little bit harder to make every day a little bit better for you.

4868
03:52:58,370 --> 03:52:59,390
That's why I'm your friend.

4869
03:53:00,750 --> 03:53:02,130
All you had to do was tell me.

4870
03:53:03,910 --> 03:53:05,590
You don't understand at all, Oliver.

4871
03:53:06,490 --> 03:53:08,010
Why do you think I didn't tell you?

4872
03:53:09,570 --> 03:53:13,970
Because if I told you, then you would have to waste effort caring about me instead of doing important things.

4873
03:53:15,450 --> 03:53:16,810
I don't want to be cared about.

4874
03:53:18,230 --> 03:53:20,830
It's bittersweet when people try to care about me.

4875
03:53:21,310 --> 03:53:23,170
Yeah, it's a paradox when you feel like that.

4876
03:53:23,410 --> 03:53:24,330
It really is.

4877
03:53:25,330 --> 03:53:26,630
It feels nice sometimes.

4878
03:53:27,370 --> 03:53:30,090
But it also feels like a bat being swung against my head.

4879
03:53:31,110 --> 03:53:31,490
Ahem.

4880
03:53:32,270 --> 03:53:34,850
That's why I wanted so badly for you to make friends with everyone else.

4881
03:53:35,810 --> 03:53:38,490
Helping everyone be happy together is the best thing for me.

4882
03:53:39,590 --> 03:53:41,370
But then I discovered something else too.

4883
03:53:42,690 --> 03:53:45,250
Seeing you make friends and get close up with everyone in the club?

4884
03:53:45,790 --> 03:53:47,870
It feels like a spear going through my heart.

4885
03:53:48,710 --> 03:53:49,670
So that's why.

4886
03:53:50,630 --> 03:53:52,950
That's why I decided the world just wants to torture me.

4887
03:53:54,310 --> 03:53:56,210
Every path leads to nothing but hurt.

4888
03:53:59,670 --> 03:54:01,170
You're right that I don't understand.

4889
03:54:02,170 --> 03:54:04,670
I don't understand your feelings at all, Sayori.

4890
03:54:05,490 --> 03:54:07,010
But I don't need to understand.

4891
03:54:07,750 --> 03:54:10,210
Whatever it takes for me to help you stop hurting...

4892
03:54:10,210 --> 03:54:11,690
It's not as simple as that.

4893
03:54:11,690 --> 03:54:14,490
You can't just help someone and then they're okay.

4894
03:54:16,530 --> 03:54:17,490
That's what I'll do.

4895
03:54:18,370 --> 03:54:19,130
No, Oliver.

4896
03:54:19,730 --> 03:54:20,530
There's nothing.

4897
03:54:20,970 --> 03:54:21,810
Nothing at all.

4898
03:54:22,650 --> 03:54:25,970
The only thing that could have helped is if everything could be like it always was.

4899
03:54:27,270 --> 03:54:28,350
But I was selfish.

4900
03:54:29,630 --> 03:54:32,070
I finally showed you what a horrible person I am.

4901
03:54:32,070 --> 03:54:33,330
Oh my god!

4902
03:54:34,550 --> 03:54:37,850
It is super depressing to hear someone talk like this though.

4903
03:54:39,110 --> 03:54:40,970
Tears streak down Sayori's face.

4904
03:54:42,790 --> 03:54:45,470
I made you join the literature club because I was selfish.

4905
03:54:46,350 --> 03:54:48,030
And I was punished by my heart.

4906
03:54:48,290 --> 03:54:49,950
Hurting in a way that I couldn't understand.

4907
03:54:50,890 --> 03:54:53,130
And now you came here and I made you hurt too.

4908
03:54:54,190 --> 03:54:55,590
I'm just weak and selfish.

4909
03:54:56,410 --> 03:54:57,410
That's all I am.

4910
03:54:57,890 --> 03:54:59,990
And that's why I'm gonna accept these punishments.

4911
03:55:00,950 --> 03:55:02,870
Because I deserve every last one.

4912
03:55:03,550 --> 03:55:04,470
Christ, man.

4913
03:55:05,250 --> 03:55:08,090
Without thinking, I once again grab Sayori's shoulders.

4914
03:55:08,770 --> 03:55:11,010
This time I pull her into a tight embrace.

4915
03:55:12,150 --> 03:55:12,930
That's it.

4916
03:55:15,490 --> 03:55:16,090
Ah...

4917
03:55:16,090 --> 03:55:16,930
Oliver.

4918
03:55:18,210 --> 03:55:18,810
Sayori.

4919
03:55:19,910 --> 03:55:21,690
I don't care if you feel selfish.

4920
03:55:22,250 --> 03:55:24,830
I'm really happy that you convinced me to join the club.

4921
03:55:25,570 --> 03:55:27,790
Seeing you every day makes it worthwhile enough.

4922
03:55:29,110 --> 03:55:32,070
If I make friends with everyone else, then that's just a bonus.

4923
03:55:32,810 --> 03:55:35,430
But please never underestimate how much I care about you.

4924
03:55:36,110 --> 03:55:37,790
I wouldn't have it any other way.

4925
03:55:38,990 --> 03:55:39,530
Oliver.

4926
03:55:40,570 --> 03:55:42,230
Sayori isn't hugging me back.

4927
03:55:42,910 --> 03:55:47,350
Despite my arms being wrapped around her, Sayori's arms remain at her sides.

4928
03:55:48,290 --> 03:55:50,250
She starts sobbing next to my ear.

4929
03:55:52,030 --> 03:55:52,570
No.

4930
03:55:53,670 --> 03:55:55,190
Don't do this to me.

4931
03:55:55,990 --> 03:55:57,230
Please don't do this.

4932
03:55:58,550 --> 03:55:59,050
Oliver.

4933
03:56:00,830 --> 03:56:01,330
I...

4934
03:56:01,330 --> 03:56:04,050
Sayori barely manages to speak between her sobs.

4935
03:56:05,110 --> 03:56:06,930
I don't know if I'm doing the right thing.

4936
03:56:07,610 --> 03:56:09,790
But all I want is for her to know that I care.

4937
03:56:11,870 --> 03:56:16,190
If you have it in you to call yourself selfish, Then you have to let me be selfish too.

4938
03:56:17,070 --> 03:56:19,530
No matter what it takes, I'll figure out what needs to change.

4939
03:56:19,970 --> 03:56:22,770
Dude, my character has the whole wrong idea behind this.

4940
03:56:23,510 --> 03:56:26,990
He's seeing this as if it's something that I can change or fix, you know?

4941
03:56:27,050 --> 03:56:27,970
It doesn't work like that.

4942
03:56:28,690 --> 03:56:33,910
All you gotta do is be there, Send memes her way sometimes, Acknowledge her, you know?

4943
03:56:34,450 --> 03:56:36,650
Accept that she is feeling that way, and that's it.

4944
03:56:36,950 --> 03:56:38,270
There's nothing you can really do.

4945
03:56:39,170 --> 03:56:40,190
It is what it is.

4946
03:56:41,670 --> 03:56:44,690
And me trying to, you know, try to fix it is...

4947
03:56:44,690 --> 03:56:47,250
It's not good, and it's not good for her to hear that.

4948
03:56:48,850 --> 03:56:50,290
Don't say this shit, man.

4949
03:56:51,230 --> 03:56:52,950
I'll make these feelings go away.

4950
03:56:54,730 --> 03:56:57,210
And if there's anything that you need me to do...

4951
03:56:57,210 --> 03:56:58,730
Then you'd better tell me.

4952
03:56:59,490 --> 03:57:00,870
I'll get mad if you don't.

4953
03:57:04,900 --> 03:57:05,890
I... I don't know.

4954
03:57:07,710 --> 03:57:08,590
I don't know.

4955
03:57:09,370 --> 03:57:10,490
I don't know.

4956
03:57:11,490 --> 03:57:14,410
Gently, Sayori finally puts her arms around me in return.

4957
03:57:15,230 --> 03:57:16,390
I don't know anything.

4958
03:57:17,070 --> 03:57:18,330
It's all really scary.

4959
03:57:19,170 --> 03:57:21,510
I don't understand any of my feelings, Oliver.

4960
03:57:22,110 --> 03:57:22,890
That's okay.

4961
03:57:23,670 --> 03:57:24,530
Nobody does.

4962
03:57:25,150 --> 03:57:27,610
The only time I'm not feeling nothing is when I'm feeling pain.

4963
03:57:29,070 --> 03:57:29,470
But...

4964
03:57:29,470 --> 03:57:30,910
Your hugs are so warm.

4965
03:57:31,650 --> 03:57:33,050
And that's really scary, too.

4966
03:57:35,530 --> 03:57:36,510
Sayori lets me go.

4967
03:57:37,110 --> 03:57:39,010
As she does so, I let her go as well.

4968
03:57:40,470 --> 03:57:41,690
The festival is tomorrow.

4969
03:57:42,730 --> 03:57:43,130
Yeah.

4970
03:57:44,150 --> 03:57:45,250
It's gonna be fun, right?

4971
03:57:46,270 --> 03:57:46,670
Yeah.

4972
03:57:47,870 --> 03:57:49,590
How would you like for me to spend it all with you?

4973
03:57:52,270 --> 03:57:52,670
Um...

4974
03:57:53,490 --> 03:57:53,890
Uh...

4975
03:57:53,890 --> 03:57:55,310
It's what I want.

4976
03:57:55,630 --> 03:57:56,150
It's what I want.

4977
03:57:56,870 --> 03:57:57,790
I promise.

4978
03:57:58,570 --> 03:57:59,210
I have...

4979
03:57:59,210 --> 03:57:59,710
Man...

4980
03:58:02,170 --> 03:58:03,130
You shouldn't...

4981
03:58:03,710 --> 03:58:05,090
This is just my opinion, of course.

4982
03:58:05,850 --> 03:58:11,570
But you shouldn't go out of your way to treat people differently because they told you they have a depression, you know?

4983
03:58:12,330 --> 03:58:14,290
I feel like that only makes it worse, in a way.

4984
03:58:15,210 --> 03:58:16,690
Because then they start overthinking it.

4985
03:58:16,750 --> 03:58:17,010
I don't know.

4986
03:58:17,090 --> 03:58:18,630
Maybe, uh...

4987
03:58:19,330 --> 03:58:20,230
Don't do this, man.

4988
03:58:20,350 --> 03:58:22,110
Unless it's like 100% genuine.

4989
03:58:23,190 --> 03:58:23,510
I...

4990
03:58:24,530 --> 03:58:25,770
I think that would be nice, then.

4991
03:58:26,730 --> 03:58:27,050
Yeah.

4992
03:58:28,910 --> 03:58:30,090
Sayori wipes her eyes.

4993
03:58:31,210 --> 03:58:33,010
If I could spend the whole day here, I would.

4994
03:58:34,210 --> 03:58:36,590
Of all days, this has to be the one where I have other plans.

4995
03:58:37,570 --> 03:58:38,490
Maybe I should cancel.

4996
03:58:38,670 --> 03:58:38,990
No, no, no.

4997
03:58:39,110 --> 03:58:39,370
This is...

4998
03:58:39,370 --> 03:58:39,770
Oh, man.

4999
03:58:40,770 --> 03:58:41,790
The thing is, though...

5000
03:58:41,790 --> 03:58:44,050
The game is going to guilt trip me into...

5001
03:58:46,330 --> 03:58:46,690
Um...

5002
03:58:47,090 --> 03:58:48,190
Deciding her, I guess.

5003
03:58:48,970 --> 03:58:50,890
Instead of the others, because she has a depression.

5004
03:58:51,590 --> 03:58:52,650
And I'm not gonna do that.

5005
03:58:53,170 --> 03:58:53,730
I'm not.

5006
03:58:54,850 --> 03:58:55,730
Life is life.

5007
03:58:56,030 --> 03:58:56,350
You know?

5008
03:58:57,690 --> 03:58:58,490
No, don't.

5009
03:58:58,590 --> 03:58:58,870
Exactly.

5010
03:58:59,430 --> 03:59:00,050
Please don't.

5011
03:59:01,350 --> 03:59:03,370
If you did that, then I really wouldn't forgive you.

5012
03:59:04,550 --> 03:59:04,970
But...

5013
03:59:05,790 --> 03:59:08,010
It's almost time for Yuri to meet me at my house.

5014
03:59:09,310 --> 03:59:11,670
At the very least, do you want me to come along and help out?

5015
03:59:12,610 --> 03:59:13,410
Could be fun.

5016
03:59:14,390 --> 03:59:16,450
To my surprise, Sayori shakes her head.

5017
03:59:17,730 --> 03:59:18,430
I'm sorry.

5018
03:59:19,030 --> 03:59:20,810
I don't know if that would be very good for me today.

5019
03:59:21,590 --> 03:59:22,550
You understand, right?

5020
03:59:24,690 --> 03:59:25,090
Ah...

5021
03:59:25,090 --> 03:59:27,110
It's kinda hard for me to fully understand.

5022
03:59:27,870 --> 03:59:29,030
But I'm trying my hardest.

5023
03:59:30,090 --> 03:59:30,670
It's okay.

5024
03:59:31,410 --> 03:59:32,690
Don't worry too much about it.

5025
03:59:33,730 --> 03:59:34,490
You know what?

5026
03:59:34,630 --> 03:59:35,790
In my... in my opinion...

5027
03:59:37,110 --> 03:59:37,910
You know...

5028
03:59:38,370 --> 03:59:39,650
People who are depressed...

5029
03:59:39,650 --> 03:59:41,210
And I'm not saying that I've had this, because...

5030
03:59:41,210 --> 03:59:42,410
When do you really know, right?

5031
03:59:42,410 --> 03:59:44,710
I've never been diagnosed, but obviously I've had my moments.

5032
03:59:46,210 --> 03:59:48,170
But, when you're feeling really down...

5033
03:59:48,710 --> 03:59:50,230
And let's just have an example, right?

5034
03:59:50,230 --> 03:59:51,650
Someone calls you and is like...

5035
03:59:51,650 --> 03:59:52,850
Hey, you wanna come to this thing today?

5036
03:59:53,530 --> 03:59:54,870
And you really don't wanna go, right?

5037
03:59:54,950 --> 03:59:55,550
Because of...

5038
03:59:55,550 --> 03:59:56,670
You know, you're feeling down or whatever.

5039
03:59:56,730 --> 03:59:57,330
And you say no.

5040
03:59:58,190 --> 03:59:59,170
And then you feel like...

5041
03:59:59,170 --> 04:00:00,230
Oh, they're gonna...

5042
04:00:00,830 --> 04:00:03,070
They're gonna think badly of me because I say no, right?

5043
04:00:04,010 --> 04:00:04,330
Because...

5044
04:00:04,330 --> 04:00:06,050
You also wanna be a good friend, I guess.

5045
04:00:07,430 --> 04:00:10,150
But the best thing that other person can do and just say...

5046
04:00:10,150 --> 04:00:10,810
That's cool.

5047
04:00:11,050 --> 04:00:11,890
Some other time then.

5048
04:00:12,050 --> 04:00:12,650
And then hang up.

5049
04:00:13,150 --> 04:00:13,590
That's it.

5050
04:00:14,950 --> 04:00:15,510
That's it.

5051
04:00:15,690 --> 04:00:16,130
No more.

5052
04:00:16,350 --> 04:00:17,230
Just leave it at that.

5053
04:00:17,510 --> 04:00:17,950
You know?

5054
04:00:19,270 --> 04:00:20,530
I'll see you tomorrow, okay?

5055
04:00:21,450 --> 04:00:21,890
Alright.

5056
04:00:23,390 --> 04:00:24,530
I look forward to it.

5057
04:00:27,300 --> 04:00:29,660
I say goodbye to Sayori and exit her house.

5058
04:00:32,340 --> 04:00:34,820
On the way home, I find myself still feeling uneasy.

5059
04:00:35,620 --> 04:00:38,780
But it's hard for me to keep thinking about it when Yuri is about to come over too.

5060
04:00:39,620 --> 04:00:41,040
I think Sayori is right.

5061
04:00:41,460 --> 04:00:43,060
I shouldn't be worrying too much.

5062
04:00:43,060 --> 04:00:45,640
And we're definitely gonna have a great time tomorrow.

5063
04:00:46,520 --> 04:00:48,360
I should just focus on what's ahead of me.

5064
04:00:51,750 --> 04:00:55,070
As I approach my house, I see something that makes me feel a moment of panic.

5065
04:00:56,970 --> 04:00:57,450
Yuri?

5066
04:00:59,630 --> 04:01:00,110
Uh...

5067
04:01:00,110 --> 04:01:00,930
Thank goodness.

5068
04:01:03,390 --> 04:01:04,850
You're a little early.

5069
04:01:05,810 --> 04:01:07,210
I'm sorry I wasn't home yet.

5070
04:01:08,250 --> 04:01:09,830
Were you waiting a long time?

5071
04:01:10,950 --> 04:01:12,470
No, I just got here.

5072
04:01:13,210 --> 04:01:15,970
But I started to get really nervous when nobody answered the doorbell.

5073
04:01:17,250 --> 04:01:18,850
You always could have texted me.

5074
04:01:19,550 --> 04:01:22,570
If I had known, I would have reassured you and hurried more on my way home.

5075
04:01:23,770 --> 04:01:25,210
Ah, I suppose that's true.

5076
04:01:25,790 --> 04:01:27,930
Well, didn't we agree on a time or something?

5077
04:01:30,770 --> 04:01:32,230
I didn't think for that...

5078
04:01:32,570 --> 04:01:34,170
Or think of that for some reason.

5079
04:01:35,170 --> 04:01:37,750
It should be common sense to do that, but I decided to ignore it.

5080
04:01:38,870 --> 04:01:39,730
Anyway, let's go inside.

5081
04:01:41,770 --> 04:01:43,210
I see you brought a lot of stuff with you.

5082
04:01:44,430 --> 04:01:44,930
That's right.

5083
04:01:46,630 --> 04:01:48,910
And did you manage to find everything I asked you to buy as well?

5084
04:01:49,830 --> 04:01:50,670
Yeah, pretty much.

5085
04:01:51,810 --> 04:01:53,250
At least I hope I got everything right.

5086
04:01:54,650 --> 04:01:55,670
I'm sure it will be fine.

5087
04:01:57,710 --> 04:01:59,190
I take Yuri to my room.

5088
04:02:00,670 --> 04:02:04,070
The first thing she does is glance around curiously, which makes me feel anxious.

5089
04:02:05,430 --> 04:02:06,810
That's a very...

5090
04:02:06,810 --> 04:02:08,070
barren room, man.

5091
04:02:08,690 --> 04:02:09,570
Need something on the walls.

5092
04:02:11,130 --> 04:02:12,110
It's so clean.

5093
04:02:13,130 --> 04:02:13,490
Ha!

5094
04:02:14,210 --> 04:02:16,670
I cleaned it before you came over, so...

5095
04:02:16,670 --> 04:02:18,410
That's very considerate of you to do.

5096
04:02:20,070 --> 04:02:20,910
Ah, no.

5097
04:02:21,930 --> 04:02:24,810
I would be really embarrassed for my room to be a mess while you were here.

5098
04:02:25,610 --> 04:02:25,990
Hmm.

5099
04:02:27,310 --> 04:02:28,510
Well, I do enjoy cleaning.

5100
04:02:29,870 --> 04:02:31,450
I would have gladly helped you clean.

5101
04:02:31,630 --> 04:02:31,890
No!

5102
04:02:32,890 --> 04:02:33,650
Absolutely not.

5103
04:02:35,050 --> 04:02:36,950
That would be even more embarrassing, exactly.

5104
04:02:38,050 --> 04:02:39,070
Wait, don't look in there!

5105
04:02:39,930 --> 04:02:43,570
I snatched Yuri's wrist, which was in the process of opening a desk drawer of mine.

5106
04:02:45,230 --> 04:02:45,670
Uh...

5107
04:02:45,670 --> 04:02:46,670
I'm sorry.

5108
04:02:47,450 --> 04:02:48,830
I wasn't thinking for some reason.

5109
04:02:49,510 --> 04:02:50,710
I was just spacing out.

5110
04:02:51,670 --> 04:02:52,750
It's fine, it's fine.

5111
04:02:53,690 --> 04:02:55,170
I let go of Yuri's wrist.

5112
04:02:56,050 --> 04:03:00,790
She puts both of her hands firmly in her lap, as if making sure she's keeping track of them.

5113
04:03:01,770 --> 04:03:02,610
No, she's not.

5114
04:03:02,710 --> 04:03:03,710
She has them behind her back.

5115
04:03:05,370 --> 04:03:06,250
So, um...

5116
04:03:06,650 --> 04:03:07,910
Should we get started?

5117
04:03:10,190 --> 04:03:10,630
Ah...

5118
04:03:10,630 --> 04:03:11,350
Yes.

5119
04:03:12,670 --> 04:03:15,210
Um, I have a few things planned that you can help with.

5120
04:03:15,210 --> 04:03:18,590
Decorations and other atmospheric enhancements?

5121
04:03:19,850 --> 04:03:22,550
Atmospheric enhancements?!

5122
04:03:24,090 --> 04:03:25,490
You know...

5123
04:03:26,070 --> 04:03:27,210
Mood lighting...

5124
04:03:27,210 --> 04:03:28,630
Aromatherapy...

5125
04:03:28,630 --> 04:03:29,110
Candles?

5126
04:03:30,430 --> 04:03:30,950
Oh!

5127
04:03:31,230 --> 04:03:31,710
Wow!

5128
04:03:32,610 --> 04:03:34,830
I didn't know you planned on taking it that far.

5129
04:03:35,490 --> 04:03:36,310
Of course!

5130
04:03:37,010 --> 04:03:39,850
I want to help take our guests to a far away place.

5131
04:03:40,610 --> 04:03:43,010
Although many will stop by just out of curiosity.

5132
04:03:44,130 --> 04:03:45,170
And for...

5133
04:03:45,170 --> 04:03:46,290
Cupcakes, I guess.

5134
04:03:47,130 --> 04:03:50,330
I'm determined to provide an experience that will leave them wanting more.

5135
04:03:51,130 --> 04:03:51,950
That's great!

5136
04:03:52,610 --> 04:03:55,010
It's easy to forget that you're a pretty intense person.

5137
04:03:56,930 --> 04:03:57,370
Uh...

5138
04:03:57,870 --> 04:03:58,310
Intense?

5139
04:03:59,230 --> 04:04:01,070
I guess that's the best way to put it.

5140
04:04:01,250 --> 04:04:01,930
Oh, I said that.

5141
04:04:02,630 --> 04:04:03,870
Is that a bad thing?

5142
04:04:04,430 --> 04:04:05,450
No, not at all.

5143
04:04:06,010 --> 04:04:07,850
It's something that I like about you, actually.

5144
04:04:08,770 --> 04:04:10,350
Is that so...

5145
04:04:11,070 --> 04:04:12,390
That makes me feel relieved.

5146
04:04:13,250 --> 04:04:14,330
And kinda happy.

5147
04:04:14,670 --> 04:04:15,110
Man...

5148
04:04:15,110 --> 04:04:17,690
This game REALLY likes its ellipses!

5149
04:04:19,710 --> 04:04:21,150
Yeah, no need to be so anxious.

5150
04:04:21,710 --> 04:04:22,870
You can relax a little.

5151
04:04:23,910 --> 04:04:24,310
Relax?

5152
04:04:25,570 --> 04:04:27,630
I brought some things for relaxation.

5153
04:04:29,230 --> 04:04:30,870
I was gonna use them during the...

5154
04:04:30,870 --> 04:04:31,690
Poetry event.

5155
04:04:32,430 --> 04:04:33,010
Oh yeah?

5156
04:04:33,490 --> 04:04:34,450
Haha, like what?

5157
04:04:36,190 --> 04:04:36,990
Let's see...

5158
04:04:36,990 --> 04:04:38,490
Yuri rummages through her bag.

5159
04:04:39,510 --> 04:04:42,370
She pulls out a few candles on a wooden cylinder-shaped object.

5160
04:04:42,470 --> 04:04:43,070
It's a dildo!

5161
04:04:44,490 --> 04:04:47,310
I did some shopping on the way here, so I happen to have these in my bag.

5162
04:04:47,370 --> 04:04:48,890
I'm sorry, I have the child of a ten-year-old.

5163
04:04:50,730 --> 04:04:52,930
Pfft, I meant mind of a ten-year-old.

5164
04:04:53,190 --> 04:04:54,090
I have the...

5165
04:04:54,450 --> 04:04:55,170
What did I say?

5166
04:04:55,210 --> 04:04:56,750
I have the child of a ten-year-old?

5167
04:04:56,850 --> 04:04:57,630
That doesn't even make sense.

5168
04:04:58,290 --> 04:05:01,250
I plan to cover the windows in black paper and use the candles to light the room.

5169
04:05:02,410 --> 04:05:03,890
I think that would be amazing, don't you?

5170
04:05:05,230 --> 04:05:06,690
Yeah, that would be really neat.

5171
04:05:07,930 --> 04:05:09,050
What's that wooden thing though?

5172
04:05:10,390 --> 04:05:11,110
Oh, this?

5173
04:05:12,550 --> 04:05:13,070
It's...

5174
04:05:13,070 --> 04:05:14,730
It's a diffuser for essential oils.

5175
04:05:15,810 --> 04:05:19,490
Dude, what the hell does essential oils even mean?

5176
04:05:21,010 --> 04:05:24,050
What makes them essential and what kind of oils are they?

5177
04:05:24,090 --> 04:05:24,530
Are they...

5178
04:05:24,530 --> 04:05:25,590
Are we talking olive oil?

5179
04:05:27,270 --> 04:05:28,730
It's always been so...

5180
04:05:28,730 --> 04:05:29,170
You know what?

5181
04:05:29,430 --> 04:05:31,010
Hold on, I'm gonna Google it.

5182
04:05:32,290 --> 04:05:33,910
I'm gonna find out what this means.

5183
04:05:36,530 --> 04:05:37,570
Essential oils.

5184
04:05:39,090 --> 04:05:40,930
What makes them so damn essential?

5185
04:05:42,450 --> 04:05:43,890
Hold on, what are they?

5186
04:05:45,510 --> 04:05:46,850
What are they?

5187
04:05:48,770 --> 04:05:49,730
Okay, Google.

5188
04:05:50,130 --> 04:05:54,770
Essential oils are concentrated plant extracts that capture the natural...

5189
04:05:54,770 --> 04:05:57,050
Oh my god, my phone is now recording me.

5190
04:05:57,150 --> 04:05:57,390
Stop!

5191
04:06:00,290 --> 04:06:05,750
Capture the natural smell, flavor, and chemical qualities of their source.

5192
04:06:06,560 --> 04:06:08,780
People make them using methods like steam distillation...

5193
04:06:09,390 --> 04:06:10,750
Or cold pressing.

5194
04:06:11,110 --> 04:06:12,630
To pull oils from...

5195
04:06:12,630 --> 04:06:12,810
Oh!

5196
04:06:13,170 --> 04:06:14,970
So it's like, if I wanted my...

5197
04:06:14,970 --> 04:06:16,810
Room to smell of...

5198
04:06:16,810 --> 04:06:17,790
I don't know.

5199
04:06:18,010 --> 04:06:18,910
Construction site.

5200
04:06:19,350 --> 04:06:22,310
I would, you know, distill a construction site into a bottle?

5201
04:06:22,810 --> 04:06:24,010
Something like that, I guess?

5202
04:06:25,570 --> 04:06:27,810
How familiar are you with aromatherapy?

5203
04:06:28,590 --> 04:06:30,150
Not familiar at all.

5204
04:06:31,330 --> 04:06:31,910
Ah.

5205
04:06:32,150 --> 04:06:33,070
Is that so?

5206
04:06:33,890 --> 04:06:36,790
It's one of my favorite contributors to a positive atmosphere.

5207
04:06:37,790 --> 04:06:41,350
Depending on the oils or herbs you choose, you can change the mood of the air itself.

5208
04:06:42,510 --> 04:06:42,790
Mmm.

5209
04:06:43,990 --> 04:06:44,930
Don't know about that.

5210
04:06:45,730 --> 04:06:48,030
You can even feel it permeate through your body.

5211
04:06:49,670 --> 04:06:50,150
Relaxation.

5212
04:06:50,370 --> 04:06:51,230
Positive energy.

5213
04:06:52,090 --> 04:06:52,570
Romance.

5214
04:06:52,710 --> 04:06:53,150
Reflection.

5215
04:06:54,350 --> 04:06:55,510
It's almost like magic.

5216
04:06:56,910 --> 04:06:59,450
Yuri takes the cylinder and pushes a switch on the bottom.

5217
04:07:00,830 --> 04:07:04,970
In just a moment, a thin ray of vapor begins to spout through a small hole on the top.

5218
04:07:06,650 --> 04:07:07,090
Wow!

5219
04:07:07,370 --> 04:07:08,570
That smells wonderful!

5220
04:07:09,510 --> 04:07:10,930
What kind of mood is that one for?

5221
04:07:12,010 --> 04:07:13,730
This is a jasmine essential oil.

5222
04:07:14,490 --> 04:07:16,610
It smells a little sweet and flowery, right?

5223
04:07:18,150 --> 04:07:19,710
Yeah, that's a good way to describe it.

5224
04:07:21,050 --> 04:07:23,950
I chose jasmine for the event because it provides more than relaxation.

5225
04:07:26,150 --> 04:07:29,450
Jasmine enhances your emotions and helps you feel them flow through your body.

5226
04:07:30,190 --> 04:07:32,770
You feel warmer and your heart pounds more heavily.

5227
04:07:33,870 --> 04:07:36,150
Don't you think that would be perfect for sharing our poems?

5228
04:07:37,250 --> 04:07:38,510
It does sound suitable.

5229
04:07:39,330 --> 04:07:42,650
But you seem to know a lot about this, so I'll trust your opinion with anything.

5230
04:07:43,790 --> 04:07:46,490
Yuri smiles gently, clearly enjoying herself.

5231
04:07:47,590 --> 04:07:51,110
She again reaches into her bag and pulls out several spools of thin ribbon.

5232
04:07:52,470 --> 04:07:53,550
What are those for?

5233
04:07:54,890 --> 04:07:55,370
Well...

5234
04:07:56,230 --> 04:07:59,050
Did you purchase the origami paper I asked you to get?

5235
04:08:00,870 --> 04:08:02,130
Yeah, I have it over here.

5236
04:08:03,810 --> 04:08:05,950
We won't be using the paper for folding origami.

5237
04:08:06,170 --> 04:08:06,370
Oh!

5238
04:08:07,750 --> 04:08:10,210
What I'd like to do is write a different word in each paper.

5239
04:08:11,730 --> 04:08:13,170
We'll need about a hundred of them.

5240
04:08:13,630 --> 04:08:14,010
Damn!

5241
04:08:14,510 --> 04:08:14,970
Oh yeah?

5242
04:08:16,110 --> 04:08:16,910
A hundred?

5243
04:08:17,810 --> 04:08:19,010
What will those be used for?

5244
04:08:20,190 --> 04:08:23,910
Well, I'm gonna cut pieces of ribbon to hang from the doorway of the classroom.

5245
04:08:25,170 --> 04:08:28,310
Then we can fasten the paper onto the ribbons to create a doorway curtain.

5246
04:08:29,450 --> 04:08:30,250
Wouldn't that be beautiful?

5247
04:08:31,470 --> 04:08:34,050
It would also catch the eye of those passing by the room.

5248
04:08:34,870 --> 04:08:36,650
It may attract some to peek inside.

5249
04:08:38,430 --> 04:08:39,510
That's really creative!

5250
04:08:40,270 --> 04:08:42,210
I had no idea you'd be so good at this, Yuri.

5251
04:08:43,230 --> 04:08:44,670
Is that so...

5252
04:08:44,670 --> 04:08:47,690
Well, I suppose I do get a little intense, as you'd put it.

5253
04:08:48,490 --> 04:08:48,930
Ugh!

5254
04:08:49,890 --> 04:08:51,630
Yuri giggles with red cheeks.

5255
04:08:52,490 --> 04:08:53,670
No, they're yellow.

5256
04:08:54,910 --> 04:08:57,790
Is it just me, or is she more relaxed when it's just the two of us?

5257
04:08:59,350 --> 04:09:02,410
Or maybe it's the excitement she feels from sharing something that she enjoys.

5258
04:09:03,750 --> 04:09:04,830
Here's a marker, Oliver.

5259
04:09:05,930 --> 04:09:07,550
You can write any characters you want.

5260
04:09:08,430 --> 04:09:10,390
I'll help you once I finish cutting the ribbons.

5261
04:09:11,450 --> 04:09:12,010
Oh, alright.

5262
04:09:13,090 --> 04:09:15,410
Sitting on the floor together, the two of us get to work.

5263
04:09:16,270 --> 04:09:21,250
I carefully draw a different character on each paper, doing my best to manage my bad handwriting.

5264
04:09:22,470 --> 04:09:25,510
Yuri unravels a long strand of red ribbon to her desired length.

5265
04:09:25,510 --> 04:09:29,750
Then, she reaches into her bag once more and pulls out a pocket knife.

5266
04:09:31,010 --> 04:09:31,590
Uh...

5267
04:09:31,590 --> 04:09:33,470
The knife is strangely beautiful.

5268
04:09:34,090 --> 04:09:37,630
The silver handle has an intricate pattern of waves etched into it.

5269
04:09:38,170 --> 04:09:40,350
The blade itself is gently tinted blue.

5270
04:09:41,670 --> 04:09:43,590
That's no ordinary pocket knife.

5271
04:09:44,410 --> 04:09:45,910
It looks really fancy.

5272
04:09:47,230 --> 04:09:47,810
Uh...

5273
04:09:48,690 --> 04:09:49,270
Well...

5274
04:09:49,270 --> 04:09:50,970
Embarrassed, Yuri looks away.

5275
04:09:52,190 --> 04:09:53,070
What is it?

5276
04:09:54,130 --> 04:09:55,430
You're gonna think it's weird.

5277
04:09:56,530 --> 04:09:59,110
Yuri, whatever it is, I have no reason to judge.

5278
04:10:00,090 --> 04:10:01,470
To each their own, you know?

5279
04:10:02,990 --> 04:10:05,070
If you promise you won't be weirded out...

5280
04:10:06,210 --> 04:10:07,210
Yeah, I promise.

5281
04:10:08,090 --> 04:10:08,610
Alright.

5282
04:10:10,110 --> 04:10:11,390
The thing is...

5283
04:10:11,390 --> 04:10:12,950
I'm kind of into knives.

5284
04:10:13,930 --> 04:10:14,450
Really?

5285
04:10:15,610 --> 04:10:17,670
Well, let me tell you something!

5286
04:10:18,750 --> 04:10:20,290
Did you know I invented the knife?

5287
04:10:21,610 --> 04:10:22,330
They're just so...

5288
04:10:22,890 --> 04:10:23,290
Pretty.

5289
04:10:24,850 --> 04:10:25,250
I...

5290
04:10:25,250 --> 04:10:26,070
I can't help it.

5291
04:10:26,210 --> 04:10:27,430
Wait, it...

5292
04:10:28,390 --> 04:10:28,790
She's...

5293
04:10:28,790 --> 04:10:30,530
She's a knife murderer!

5294
04:10:30,830 --> 04:10:31,370
I knew it!

5295
04:10:31,650 --> 04:10:33,190
I knew it from the moment I saw her.

5296
04:10:33,810 --> 04:10:34,670
I don't know what it is.

5297
04:10:35,710 --> 04:10:38,530
A combination of craftsmanship and feeling of danger, maybe?

5298
04:10:40,330 --> 04:10:41,390
Ah, what am I saying?

5299
04:10:42,250 --> 04:10:43,630
Please don't think I'm weird for this.

5300
04:10:44,690 --> 04:10:47,250
Haha, you're laughing at me?

5301
04:10:47,930 --> 04:10:49,350
No, I'm laughing at you.

5302
04:10:49,910 --> 04:10:51,070
I mean, I'm not laughing...

5303
04:10:51,070 --> 04:10:51,630
Wait, no.

5304
04:10:51,810 --> 04:10:53,170
I'm not laughing at you.

5305
04:10:54,630 --> 04:10:56,610
It's just funny how nervous you got about sharing.

5306
04:10:57,690 --> 04:10:59,770
It's... well, it's an interesting thing to be into, I guess.

5307
04:11:00,530 --> 04:11:01,930
But I think it kind of suits you.

5308
04:11:03,110 --> 04:11:03,730
Suits me?

5309
04:11:03,970 --> 04:11:05,490
Yeah, you're kind of dark and mysterious.

5310
04:11:06,630 --> 04:11:07,750
Yeah, it's kind of intense.

5311
04:11:08,590 --> 04:11:09,250
Dude, I...

5312
04:11:09,250 --> 04:11:10,930
This is like something...

5313
04:11:11,510 --> 04:11:13,570
14-year-olds write in text messages, you know?

5314
04:11:13,670 --> 04:11:13,930
They're like...

5315
04:11:14,510 --> 04:11:15,550
After every message.

5316
04:11:17,210 --> 04:11:18,830
Besides, it's a really cool-looking knife.

5317
04:11:18,910 --> 04:11:19,650
I can't deny that.

5318
04:11:20,430 --> 04:11:21,250
It is, isn't it?

5319
04:11:22,730 --> 04:11:24,750
Yuri relaxes her expression once again.

5320
04:11:25,750 --> 04:11:26,950
Would you like to hold it?

5321
04:11:27,970 --> 04:11:28,430
Sure!

5322
04:11:29,230 --> 04:11:30,170
I'll check it out.

5323
04:11:31,130 --> 04:11:32,790
Yuri carefully hands me the knife.

5324
04:11:33,050 --> 04:11:34,410
With the handle facing me.

5325
04:11:35,390 --> 04:11:37,450
I take it and turn it around in my hands.

5326
04:11:38,050 --> 04:11:40,110
It feels heavy and extremely solid.

5327
04:11:40,970 --> 04:11:42,870
Where do you even get a knife like this?

5328
04:11:43,910 --> 04:11:47,130
Curious of its sharpness, I feel the point of the knife with my index finger.

5329
04:11:48,030 --> 04:11:48,390
Ow!

5330
04:11:49,490 --> 04:11:49,950
Oliver!

5331
04:11:50,870 --> 04:11:52,030
Why did you do that?

5332
04:11:53,170 --> 04:11:55,030
I didn't expect it to be that sharp!

5333
04:11:56,250 --> 04:11:57,730
I barely touched it at all!

5334
04:11:58,610 --> 04:11:59,550
It's my fault.

5335
04:11:59,930 --> 04:12:01,070
I should've warned you.

5336
04:12:01,790 --> 04:12:03,470
Knives are sharp and they can hurt you.

5337
04:12:05,110 --> 04:12:06,670
This knife is extremely sharp.

5338
04:12:07,450 --> 04:12:09,250
It can cut through skin like it's paper.

5339
04:12:09,990 --> 04:12:10,730
Oh no!

5340
04:12:11,650 --> 04:12:14,470
A small drop of blood trickles down the side of my finger.

5341
04:12:15,510 --> 04:12:18,090
That is totally and only on me!

5342
04:12:18,890 --> 04:12:20,370
What a dumbass I am.

5343
04:12:21,530 --> 04:12:24,090
Yuri takes my hand and gives the wound a closer look.

5344
04:12:25,570 --> 04:12:26,090
Ah...

5345
04:12:26,090 --> 04:12:28,490
She stares at it and noticeably fidgets.

5346
04:12:29,670 --> 04:12:31,950
If you're squeamish, I'll go wash it off now.

5347
04:12:33,370 --> 04:12:33,670
Ah!

5348
04:12:34,670 --> 04:12:37,990
Without warning, Yuri puts my finger in her mouth and licks the wound.

5349
04:12:38,430 --> 04:12:39,070
She's an empire!

5350
04:12:40,530 --> 04:12:42,510
I feel her tongue curl around my finger.

5351
04:12:43,870 --> 04:12:46,130
Startled, I instinctively pull my hand back.

5352
04:12:48,370 --> 04:12:48,970
Oh...

5353
04:12:48,970 --> 04:12:50,010
Please forgive me.

5354
04:12:50,810 --> 04:12:51,910
I wasn't thinking.

5355
04:12:53,330 --> 04:12:53,930
I...

5356
04:12:53,930 --> 04:12:56,350
Yuri lowers her head, her face burning up.

5357
04:12:57,450 --> 04:12:57,950
Yuri!

5358
04:12:58,930 --> 04:13:01,190
That's the most embarrassing thing I've ever done!

5359
04:13:02,030 --> 04:13:03,850
How could I do something like that?

5360
04:13:04,710 --> 04:13:06,370
I'm sorry, I'm sorry.

5361
04:13:07,730 --> 04:13:08,090
Ah!

5362
04:13:10,330 --> 04:13:12,750
Sure, it was a little weird, and it took me by surprise.

5363
04:13:13,530 --> 04:13:15,150
But I guess she was just trying to help, right?

5364
04:13:16,730 --> 04:13:18,930
Yuri, I think you're overreacting a little.

5365
04:13:21,550 --> 04:13:21,910
Oooh...

5366
04:13:21,910 --> 04:13:22,790
She doesn't lift her head.

5367
04:13:24,030 --> 04:13:26,950
What if she doesn't recover from this for the rest of the afternoon?

5368
04:13:29,290 --> 04:13:30,130
Fucking hell, man.

5369
04:13:31,090 --> 04:13:31,950
Alright, you know what?

5370
04:13:32,810 --> 04:13:34,810
This might be a stupid thing to do, but I do it anyway.

5371
04:13:35,410 --> 04:13:37,930
I take Yuri's hand and lick her index finger in return.

5372
04:13:38,070 --> 04:13:38,750
There we go!

5373
04:13:39,810 --> 04:13:40,150
Oliver!

5374
04:13:41,290 --> 04:13:42,570
Did you really just do that?

5375
04:13:43,850 --> 04:13:44,750
Now we're even.

5376
04:13:46,650 --> 04:13:48,870
Yuri just looks at me like I did something wrong.

5377
04:13:50,170 --> 04:13:50,830
Ha ha ha!

5378
04:13:51,650 --> 04:13:53,350
I knew that would be a bad idea!

5379
04:13:54,610 --> 04:13:58,850
If not for the sweet aroma of the jasmine oil, the air would be extremely heavy right now.

5380
04:14:00,210 --> 04:14:01,470
You're so weird, Oliver.

5381
04:14:02,770 --> 04:14:04,150
Yuri giggles shyly.

5382
04:14:04,810 --> 04:14:05,590
Eh?

5383
04:14:06,670 --> 04:14:08,370
Yuri calling me weird?

5384
04:14:08,650 --> 04:14:10,350
I have no response to that.

5385
04:14:11,690 --> 04:14:12,850
Where do you keep your bandages?

5386
04:14:14,650 --> 04:14:15,010
Ah!

5387
04:14:16,510 --> 04:14:18,730
I don't think I need one, actually.

5388
04:14:19,810 --> 04:14:20,990
That was a tiny cut.

5389
04:14:21,750 --> 04:14:23,190
Look, it already stopped bleeding.

5390
04:14:23,590 --> 04:14:24,470
I see.

5391
04:14:25,470 --> 04:14:26,270
That's relieving.

5392
04:14:27,230 --> 04:14:28,810
The tension is quickly lifted.

5393
04:14:29,330 --> 04:14:31,430
We each resume our respective activities.

5394
04:14:32,550 --> 04:14:35,630
I watch Yuri's knife cut through the ribbon like it's nothing but air.

5395
04:14:36,570 --> 04:14:38,710
Meanwhile, I continue to make progress on Yuri.

5396
04:14:41,390 --> 04:14:45,870
After we finish attaching the paper to the ribbons, we lay them all out side by side.

5397
04:14:46,990 --> 04:14:51,310
It looks better than I expected and will be very effective as a door curtain.

5398
04:14:52,450 --> 04:14:53,510
It looks great.

5399
04:14:54,450 --> 04:14:55,950
Good thinking coming up with this, Yuri.

5400
04:14:57,490 --> 04:14:58,230
Ah, thanks.

5401
04:14:59,510 --> 04:15:01,270
That's just something I saw online, really.

5402
04:15:02,290 --> 04:15:04,050
Are you ready to move on to the next task?

5403
04:15:04,990 --> 04:15:05,390
Yep!

5404
04:15:05,590 --> 04:15:06,030
Let's do it!

5405
04:15:06,490 --> 04:15:08,170
I mean, uh, what do you have in mind?

5406
04:15:09,250 --> 04:15:10,570
I'd like to create a banner.

5407
04:15:11,530 --> 04:15:13,390
That's why I asked you to buy the paint tablets.

5408
04:15:14,710 --> 04:15:15,590
Paint tablets?

5409
04:15:17,610 --> 04:15:18,050
Ah!

5410
04:15:18,050 --> 04:15:18,670
That's right!

5411
04:15:19,390 --> 04:15:23,070
One of the items Yuri had asked me to buy was a kit of watercolor paint tablets.

5412
04:15:24,290 --> 04:15:27,270
We'll need about six cups of water to put each of the tablets in.

5413
04:15:27,890 --> 04:15:29,370
You mind fetching those for us?

5414
04:15:30,390 --> 04:15:31,310
Of course not!

5415
04:15:32,370 --> 04:15:32,810
Wait.

5416
04:15:34,370 --> 04:15:35,170
Oh, right.

5417
04:15:35,350 --> 04:15:36,010
You would say that.

5418
04:15:36,350 --> 04:15:37,850
I thought he was like, no way I'm doing that.

5419
04:15:40,030 --> 04:15:42,090
Six cups of water...

5420
04:15:43,630 --> 04:15:44,730
I'll be right back in a minute.

5421
04:15:45,770 --> 04:15:46,630
Thank you very much.

5422
04:15:48,150 --> 04:15:49,750
Oh, and just a little bit of water is okay.

5423
04:15:50,510 --> 04:15:52,550
If you fill the cups too much, it'll be too diluted.

5424
04:15:53,530 --> 04:15:54,050
Got it.

5425
04:15:55,390 --> 04:15:59,630
Taking Yuri's advice, I decide to use small plastic bathroom cups rather than full-sized glasses.

5426
04:16:00,370 --> 04:16:04,430
I put them on a plate and catch any paint that drips, then bring it back into my room.

5427
04:16:05,830 --> 04:16:06,070
Yuri?

5428
04:16:07,230 --> 04:16:07,670
Yes?

5429
04:16:08,730 --> 04:16:13,750
I come in to see Yuri quickly unrolling her sleeve, pulling it back over her arm.

5430
04:16:15,870 --> 04:16:16,550
Oh no.

5431
04:16:18,770 --> 04:16:19,950
I see where this is going.

5432
04:16:21,570 --> 04:16:22,590
Ah, nothing.

5433
04:16:23,750 --> 04:16:24,950
Your face is a little red!

5434
04:16:25,670 --> 04:16:27,350
Is it too hot in here or anything?

5435
04:16:30,410 --> 04:16:31,610
No, not at all.

5436
04:16:32,870 --> 04:16:34,970
Wait, are all these girls gonna have depression?

5437
04:16:37,490 --> 04:16:38,770
That's gonna be so sad.

5438
04:16:39,370 --> 04:16:41,570
There's nothing wrong, so...

5439
04:16:41,570 --> 04:16:42,490
Let's mix the paint.

5440
04:16:43,770 --> 04:16:49,950
Yuri hurriedly dismisses me and takes it upon herself to unwrap the tablets, dropping them into the cups.

5441
04:16:51,950 --> 04:16:52,390
So...

5442
04:16:52,870 --> 04:16:55,250
I thought we should do something simple that would look very nice.

5443
04:16:55,950 --> 04:16:58,230
I'd like to paint a gradient across the banner.

5444
04:16:59,010 --> 04:17:02,250
Starting with the colors for sunrise, then daytime, then sunset and nighttime.

5445
04:17:03,210 --> 04:17:06,390
Once it dries, I'll write an inspirational quote across the banner.

5446
04:17:07,090 --> 04:17:09,850
We can hang it on the wall behind the podium at the front of the classroom.

5447
04:17:11,130 --> 04:17:11,570
Ah!

5448
04:17:11,570 --> 04:17:12,010
Neat!

5449
04:17:13,250 --> 04:17:14,210
What are you gonna write?

5450
04:17:16,190 --> 04:17:16,590
Well...

5451
04:17:17,050 --> 04:17:18,370
It'll be more fun to surprise you.

5452
04:17:19,670 --> 04:17:20,730
Yuri smiles at me.

5453
04:17:21,690 --> 04:17:22,710
If you say so.

5454
04:17:24,150 --> 04:17:28,570
After rolling out the banner, Yuri and I kneel on opposite sides so we don't get in the way of each other.

5455
04:17:29,670 --> 04:17:35,370
Yuri uses a brush and adds a few dots of our different colors across the banner to serve as a color guide when we paint.

5456
04:17:36,630 --> 04:17:38,710
This kind of reminds me of elementary school.

5457
04:17:39,990 --> 04:17:44,030
Painting on a banner with watercolors feels a lot like the art class projects we had back then.

5458
04:17:44,770 --> 04:17:45,570
It's relaxing.

5459
04:17:47,550 --> 04:17:49,270
I'm sorry if this feels too childish.

5460
04:17:50,230 --> 04:17:51,550
No, I didn't mean that at all.

5461
04:17:52,030 --> 04:17:53,110
It's kind of fun, you know?

5462
04:17:54,030 --> 04:17:54,410
Yeah.

5463
04:17:55,030 --> 04:17:55,770
It is fun.

5464
04:17:56,650 --> 04:17:57,950
I'm glad you feel that way too.

5465
04:17:59,090 --> 04:18:01,510
Yuri stops painting for a moment, thinking to herself.

5466
04:18:02,690 --> 04:18:03,410
For me...

5467
04:18:04,170 --> 04:18:06,350
I don't need to go out and do crazy things to have fun.

5468
04:18:07,150 --> 04:18:08,810
In fact, I usually don't even want to.

5469
04:18:09,290 --> 04:18:10,670
Dude, we'd be perfect together.

5470
04:18:12,070 --> 04:18:14,650
And just like when I can spend time with one other person.

5471
04:18:15,870 --> 04:18:19,230
Even if it's something simple like reading, it doesn't even matter if we don't talk much.

5472
04:18:20,270 --> 04:18:22,710
Just having a friend next to me makes things feel a little bit nicer.

5473
04:18:23,870 --> 04:18:25,570
I think that's all it takes for me to be happy.

5474
04:18:26,570 --> 04:18:27,490
Is that so?

5475
04:18:28,590 --> 04:18:31,830
Even if Yuri and I are quite different, I can understand where she's coming from.

5476
04:18:32,610 --> 04:18:35,190
I feel that way about things like anime and games.

5477
04:18:35,430 --> 04:18:38,170
Where simply sharing the experience with someone can make me happy.

5478
04:18:39,330 --> 04:18:40,610
I think I feel the same way.

5479
04:18:41,550 --> 04:18:42,810
Yuri smiles gently.

5480
04:18:43,450 --> 04:18:44,630
I knew you'd understand.

5481
04:18:45,850 --> 04:18:48,410
Yuri leans over the banner to grab an unused paintbrush.

5482
04:18:49,530 --> 04:18:52,510
But I move at the same time, causing my head to bump into hers.

5483
04:18:53,150 --> 04:18:53,630
Kya!

5484
04:18:54,590 --> 04:18:55,070
Sorry!

5485
04:18:56,190 --> 04:18:58,810
Yuri reels back and I quickly lift my hands in surprise.

5486
04:18:59,450 --> 04:18:59,990
Are you hurt?

5487
04:19:00,950 --> 04:19:02,010
No, I'm not hurt.

5488
04:19:02,770 --> 04:19:04,230
It just startled me, that's all.

5489
04:19:05,410 --> 04:19:07,550
Sorry, I should've asked you to get it for me.

5490
04:19:08,330 --> 04:19:09,370
It's not your fault.

5491
04:19:10,570 --> 04:19:12,090
Ah, your face.

5492
04:19:13,030 --> 04:19:15,650
There are droplets of paint on Yuri's face and neck.

5493
04:19:16,710 --> 04:19:18,090
Is there something on my face?

5494
04:19:19,170 --> 04:19:21,090
Yeah, I accidentally got paint on you.

5495
04:19:22,130 --> 04:19:23,430
Sorry, it's totally my fault.

5496
04:19:24,890 --> 04:19:26,210
I'll get a towel right away.

5497
04:19:26,830 --> 04:19:27,430
Oh no.

5498
04:19:28,470 --> 04:19:30,210
Can we grab the knife from her before we leave?

5499
04:19:31,030 --> 04:19:34,030
I rush out and fetch a small towel, then dampen it with hot water.

5500
04:19:34,550 --> 04:19:36,890
I return to my room and kneel back down in front of her.

5501
04:19:39,880 --> 04:19:40,320
Here.

5502
04:19:41,580 --> 04:19:44,020
I pat down Yuri's face and check with the towel.

5503
04:19:45,880 --> 04:19:46,320
Uh...

5504
04:19:46,320 --> 04:19:47,560
Is something wrong?

5505
04:19:48,480 --> 04:19:50,320
It's hot, I just didn't expect it.

5506
04:19:50,960 --> 04:19:51,560
Sorry.

5507
04:19:52,260 --> 04:19:53,920
I didn't want to use cold water.

5508
04:19:54,980 --> 04:19:56,960
Having finished, I start to retract my hand.

5509
04:19:57,540 --> 04:19:59,480
But Yuri suddenly holds my wrist.

5510
04:20:00,300 --> 04:20:00,840
Wait!

5511
04:20:01,700 --> 04:20:02,120
Huh?

5512
04:20:03,140 --> 04:20:04,680
Just... for a little longer.

5513
04:20:05,500 --> 04:20:06,860
It feels really nice.

5514
04:20:07,640 --> 04:20:08,040
Ah!

5515
04:20:08,660 --> 04:20:10,820
I keep my hand still against Yuri's neck.

5516
04:20:11,780 --> 04:20:13,580
She looks into my eyes.

5517
04:20:14,280 --> 04:20:18,540
It's an intense expression that I recognize from when she reads her books.

5518
04:20:19,520 --> 04:20:21,540
Almost as if she's lost in a daze.

5519
04:20:22,260 --> 04:20:23,380
Enveloped by her own thoughts.

5520
04:20:24,120 --> 04:20:25,460
She breathes gently.

5521
04:20:26,020 --> 04:20:27,780
Half through slightly parted lips.

5522
04:20:28,400 --> 04:20:30,660
That is such a tiny mouth, holy crap.

5523
04:20:31,520 --> 04:20:32,620
What is happening?

5524
04:20:34,080 --> 04:20:37,200
Is it the aroma of the jasmine oil giving me this dizzy feeling?

5525
04:20:37,840 --> 04:20:40,220
It's butterflies in your tummy, buddy!

5526
04:20:41,680 --> 04:20:45,980
Yuri's gentle fingers wrapped around my wrist send a tingling sensation through my arm.

5527
04:20:46,660 --> 04:20:50,400
And suddenly, her face seems to be much closer to mine than it was just a moment ago.

5528
04:20:52,140 --> 04:20:52,700
Ah...

5529
04:20:52,700 --> 04:20:54,080
Yuri slowly pulls away.

5530
04:20:54,640 --> 04:20:55,200
Sorry.

5531
04:20:55,200 --> 04:20:56,860
Why do you say sorry?

5532
04:20:57,480 --> 04:20:58,380
I fucking hate that.

5533
04:20:59,300 --> 04:21:00,900
I've been feeling a little light-headed today.

5534
04:21:01,920 --> 04:21:03,180
I didn't mean to space out.

5535
04:21:04,040 --> 04:21:04,860
It's... it's fine.

5536
04:21:06,900 --> 04:21:08,720
The moment is over as soon as it began.

5537
04:21:10,660 --> 04:21:12,860
I thought it said, Yuri picks up her brush again.

5538
04:21:13,500 --> 04:21:14,660
Yuri picks up her brush again.

5539
04:21:15,280 --> 04:21:16,800
Her movements seem clumsier.

5540
04:21:17,140 --> 04:21:18,540
Like she's unable to focus.

5541
04:21:19,580 --> 04:21:22,720
I remain silent, forced to ignore the event that just transpired.

5542
04:21:22,840 --> 04:21:24,080
Look at these dots in the back!

5543
04:21:24,380 --> 04:21:25,400
I just noticed them!

5544
04:21:25,820 --> 04:21:26,620
There's polka dots!

5545
04:21:27,620 --> 04:21:31,120
I hesitantly retrieve my own brush and continue following Yuri's example.

5546
04:21:34,380 --> 04:21:35,300
That should do it!

5547
04:21:36,060 --> 04:21:39,040
I finish filling the night sky with white dots that look like stars.

5548
04:21:39,520 --> 04:21:40,560
Speaking of dots!

5549
04:21:41,560 --> 04:21:44,440
Looking at the banner as a whole, it's very pretty and natural looking.

5550
04:21:46,040 --> 04:21:47,880
I think it came out better than I expected.

5551
04:21:48,880 --> 04:21:50,440
I'm really happy with the results.

5552
04:21:51,560 --> 04:21:52,520
Yeah, me too.

5553
04:21:53,980 --> 04:21:55,180
Are you gonna add the lettering now?

5554
04:21:56,260 --> 04:21:57,240
Uh, not yet.

5555
04:21:57,960 --> 04:21:59,020
I need to drive first.

5556
04:21:59,800 --> 04:22:01,660
That's true, but won't that take a while?

5557
04:22:03,420 --> 04:22:03,820
Well...

5558
04:22:04,220 --> 04:22:07,600
Perhaps it would be best to leave it here, than have you bring it in the morning.

5559
04:22:08,900 --> 04:22:11,580
I can do the lettering in the classroom before our event starts.

5560
04:22:12,520 --> 04:22:13,180
Is that okay?

5561
04:22:14,600 --> 04:22:15,320
Totally fine.

5562
04:22:16,420 --> 04:22:16,820
Wonderful.

5563
04:22:17,880 --> 04:22:20,640
In that case, I don't think there's anything more for us to do here.

5564
04:22:21,220 --> 04:22:21,620
Ahem!

5565
04:22:22,260 --> 04:22:22,760
Hold on!

5566
04:22:24,220 --> 04:22:24,620
Whew!

5567
04:22:26,640 --> 04:22:28,200
Say that like you're glad it's over.

5568
04:22:29,360 --> 04:22:32,060
Was I wrong to assume that you were at least enjoying yourself a little bit?

5569
04:22:32,960 --> 04:22:34,060
Ah, no, it's not that.

5570
04:22:34,840 --> 04:22:36,940
I'm just glad that we managed to get everything done.

5571
04:22:37,760 --> 04:22:38,380
I see.

5572
04:22:39,160 --> 04:22:39,920
I am too.

5573
04:22:40,820 --> 04:22:42,680
I was a little concerned about time.

5574
04:22:43,260 --> 04:22:44,960
I need to start making dinner soon!

5575
04:22:45,860 --> 04:22:46,280
Ah!

5576
04:22:46,540 --> 04:22:47,240
Me too!

5577
04:22:47,960 --> 04:22:49,100
I'm feeling peckish.

5578
04:22:51,160 --> 04:22:52,720
So... you don't have any time left?

5579
04:22:53,680 --> 04:22:57,060
I was secretly hoping we would have extra time after finishing the work!

5580
04:22:59,120 --> 04:22:59,720
Well...

5581
04:23:00,800 --> 04:23:02,200
Yuri thinks to herself.

5582
04:23:03,460 --> 04:23:04,060
I...

5583
04:23:04,060 --> 04:23:07,220
I think it would be too irresponsible for me to wait much longer.

5584
04:23:07,860 --> 04:23:08,700
I'm sorry.

5585
04:23:09,320 --> 04:23:11,260
I was hoping there would be more time as well.

5586
04:23:12,720 --> 04:23:14,580
It's probably my fault?

5587
04:23:15,860 --> 04:23:16,460
Huh?

5588
04:23:17,780 --> 04:23:19,800
Sorry for being such a slow worker.

5589
04:23:20,020 --> 04:23:20,500
Guy, you...

5590
04:23:22,200 --> 04:23:22,680
Stop!

5591
04:23:24,020 --> 04:23:25,500
No, it's not your fault at all!

5592
04:23:26,160 --> 04:23:28,640
And the important thing is that we get everything done, right?

5593
04:23:30,140 --> 04:23:30,620
Yeah...

5594
04:23:31,680 --> 04:23:32,160
So...

5595
04:23:32,160 --> 04:23:34,360
I shouldn't be disappointed or anything.

5596
04:23:35,460 --> 04:23:38,400
Gathering all her things, Yuri seems to look a little downcast.

5597
04:23:39,180 --> 04:23:40,400
I understand why.

5598
04:23:41,340 --> 04:23:45,800
It sounded like she rarely gets the opportunity to spend time with friends in a relaxed environment.

5599
04:23:48,260 --> 04:23:49,320
That sentence, man.

5600
04:23:51,040 --> 04:23:54,140
I never get to spend time with my friends in a relaxed environment.

5601
04:23:54,140 --> 04:23:57,700
I wish I had more time to spend time with friends in a relaxed environment.

5602
04:23:58,320 --> 04:23:59,020
So weird.

5603
04:23:59,640 --> 04:24:01,920
But that doesn't mean this is the last time it can happen.

5604
04:24:06,190 --> 04:24:08,770
Once Yuri packs up, I'll walk her out the front door.

5605
04:24:10,030 --> 04:24:11,690
Thank you very much for having me today.

5606
04:24:12,830 --> 04:24:13,530
No problem.

5607
04:24:13,710 --> 04:24:14,790
I'm glad I was able to help.

5608
04:24:15,910 --> 04:24:18,250
Just let me know if there's anything else you need me to bring tomorrow.

5609
04:24:19,010 --> 04:24:19,570
I will.

5610
04:24:21,670 --> 04:24:22,470
Well then...

5611
04:24:22,470 --> 04:24:23,510
Yuri Fidgets.

5612
04:24:24,750 --> 04:24:26,130
I guess I'll see you tomorrow?

5613
04:24:27,030 --> 04:24:27,430
Wait!

5614
04:24:28,550 --> 04:24:30,010
I kinda say that without thinking.

5615
04:24:32,770 --> 04:24:33,570
About... today...

5616
04:24:34,470 --> 04:24:36,630
It's fine that we didn't have as much time as we wanted.

5617
04:24:37,910 --> 04:24:38,930
Because we can do it again.

5618
04:24:40,090 --> 04:24:40,810
Whenever you want.

5619
04:24:40,890 --> 04:24:43,150
You can come over, we can go out somewhere, you know...

5620
04:24:43,790 --> 04:24:44,210
Jellybeans...

5621
04:24:44,210 --> 04:24:44,910
This, that...

5622
04:24:44,910 --> 04:24:45,530
I got it!

5623
04:24:46,530 --> 04:24:48,570
Oh, I forget you don't like going out much.

5624
04:24:48,690 --> 04:24:49,610
We can jellybean at home.

5625
04:24:50,550 --> 04:24:53,370
As I stumble over my words, Yuri simply smiles bashfully.

5626
04:24:54,510 --> 04:24:55,350
A-Anyway...

5627
04:24:55,350 --> 04:24:57,330
You know what I'm trying to say, so...

5628
04:24:58,210 --> 04:24:59,510
You're very thoughtful, Oliver.

5629
04:25:00,570 --> 04:25:03,590
Yuri takes a step closer to me, then briefly squeezes my hand.

5630
04:25:05,570 --> 04:25:07,030
I kinda like that about you.

5631
04:25:09,010 --> 04:25:09,430
Well...

5632
04:25:10,550 --> 04:25:12,210
How am I supposed to respond to that?

5633
04:25:14,170 --> 04:25:17,050
But I don't even got a chance to, as Yuri suddenly pulls back!

5634
04:25:19,070 --> 04:25:19,470
Ssss...

5635
04:25:19,470 --> 04:25:20,090
Sayori?

5636
04:25:21,790 --> 04:25:22,190
Eh?

5637
04:25:22,990 --> 04:25:26,530
Hi, Oliver!

5638
04:25:26,730 --> 04:25:28,110
Wait, was she stalking me?

5639
04:25:30,190 --> 04:25:30,590
Sayori?

5640
04:25:32,270 --> 04:25:34,250
Just now, we weren't, uh...

5641
04:25:34,250 --> 04:25:35,450
I mean, we were, but...

5642
04:25:35,450 --> 04:25:36,510
None of you...

5643
04:25:36,510 --> 04:25:36,910
Eh?

5644
04:25:37,970 --> 04:25:38,810
It's okay, Oliver.

5645
04:25:39,350 --> 04:25:40,770
I just stopped by to say hi.

5646
04:25:43,030 --> 04:25:43,590
Uh...

5647
04:25:44,230 --> 04:25:45,650
Well, it's nice to see you.

5648
04:25:46,190 --> 04:25:47,530
I'm sorry, but...

5649
04:25:47,530 --> 04:25:48,670
I'm already on my way to leave.

5650
04:25:49,790 --> 04:25:50,750
Oh, really?

5651
04:25:51,250 --> 04:25:52,290
That's too bad.

5652
04:25:53,270 --> 04:25:53,830
Sorry.

5653
04:25:54,970 --> 04:25:57,370
But we'll all be together at the festival tomorrow, so...

5654
04:25:57,930 --> 04:25:59,050
So that's fine, right?

5655
04:25:59,810 --> 04:26:00,690
Of course!

5656
04:26:01,610 --> 04:26:02,670
Sayori beams.

5657
04:26:03,410 --> 04:26:04,770
Except she's not beaming.

5658
04:26:05,350 --> 04:26:06,890
She's doing the opposite of that.

5659
04:26:09,190 --> 04:26:10,310
Yeah, so...

5660
04:26:10,310 --> 04:26:11,650
I'll see you tomorrow.

5661
04:26:12,950 --> 04:26:13,910
Ha-ha-ha!

5662
04:26:14,190 --> 04:26:14,510
Zoom!

5663
04:26:15,150 --> 04:26:16,350
Flew off the screen.

5664
04:26:16,910 --> 04:26:18,650
Clearly embarrassed, Yuri hurries off.

5665
04:26:19,730 --> 04:26:21,610
Sayori waves goodbye after her.

5666
04:26:23,530 --> 04:26:24,170
Sayori!

5667
04:26:25,390 --> 04:26:27,170
I thought you didn't want to come over today.

5668
04:26:28,350 --> 04:26:29,150
Ha-ha...

5669
04:26:29,730 --> 04:26:30,130
Well...

5670
04:26:30,630 --> 04:26:34,610
I tried staying in my room, but my imagination was being really mean to me.

5671
04:26:35,350 --> 04:26:37,190
So I had to come here and see it for myself.

5672
04:26:38,350 --> 04:26:39,130
See what?

5673
04:26:40,550 --> 04:26:41,650
What are you talking about?

5674
04:26:43,110 --> 04:26:43,910
You know...

5675
04:26:44,510 --> 04:26:46,070
How much fun you were having with Yuri.

5676
04:26:47,130 --> 04:26:48,490
And how close you got to her.

5677
04:26:49,250 --> 04:26:51,010
It makes me really happy.

5678
04:26:51,650 --> 04:26:54,070
She says with tears in her eyes.

5679
04:26:55,210 --> 04:26:56,970
That you've made such good friends.

5680
04:26:57,570 --> 04:26:58,930
That's all that matters to me.

5681
04:27:00,190 --> 04:27:02,470
Tears start to fall down Sayori's face.

5682
04:27:02,770 --> 04:27:03,790
This is bad!

5683
04:27:05,210 --> 04:27:06,550
That's all that matters to me.

5684
04:27:07,810 --> 04:27:09,450
Why am I feeling this way, Oliver?

5685
04:27:10,890 --> 04:27:12,610
I'm supposed to be happy for you.

5686
04:27:13,650 --> 04:27:14,610
You seem...

5687
04:27:14,610 --> 04:27:16,050
Overly obsessed with me.

5688
04:27:16,490 --> 04:27:18,030
I'm just gonna throw that out there.

5689
04:27:18,370 --> 04:27:18,650
That's...

5690
04:27:18,650 --> 04:27:19,210
It's not good.

5691
04:27:20,450 --> 04:27:21,810
You need to, you know...

5692
04:27:23,330 --> 04:27:24,710
Not do that, I guess.

5693
04:27:25,850 --> 04:27:28,010
Why does it feel like my heart is splitting in half?

5694
04:27:29,450 --> 04:27:30,730
It hurts so much.

5695
04:27:32,450 --> 04:27:33,890
Everything hurts so much.

5696
04:27:35,270 --> 04:27:37,750
This would be so much better if I could just disappear.

5697
04:27:40,030 --> 04:27:41,630
Sayori, don't say that.

5698
04:27:42,610 --> 04:27:43,430
It's true, Oliver.

5699
04:27:44,150 --> 04:27:47,070
If I wasn't here, then you wouldn't have to waste your sympathy on me.

5700
04:27:48,010 --> 04:27:50,090
You wouldn't have to put up with me being selfish.

5701
04:27:51,230 --> 04:27:52,150
Monica was right.

5702
04:27:52,790 --> 04:27:53,610
I should just...

5703
04:27:54,770 --> 04:27:55,170
Monica?

5704
04:27:56,450 --> 04:27:57,690
Monica was right about what?

5705
04:28:00,880 --> 04:28:01,280
Sayori.

5706
04:28:02,620 --> 04:28:04,000
What I said before is true.

5707
04:28:04,840 --> 04:28:06,280
I'm not gonna let this continue.

5708
04:28:07,360 --> 04:28:10,380
Caring about you like this isn't the burden your mind is making it out to be.

5709
04:28:11,320 --> 04:28:12,680
It's something that makes me happy.

5710
04:28:13,920 --> 04:28:15,840
It's something that I wouldn't trade for anything else.

5711
04:28:16,660 --> 04:28:21,800
So even if it takes an entire lifetime, I'm gonna be by your side until you don't feel any more pain.

5712
04:28:23,580 --> 04:28:24,020
But...

5713
04:28:24,880 --> 04:28:25,940
Sayori looks away.

5714
04:28:26,580 --> 04:28:28,840
I put a hand on her shoulder to reassure her.

5715
04:28:29,760 --> 04:28:30,880
I'm scared, Oliver.

5716
04:28:31,500 --> 04:28:32,520
I'm really scared.

5717
04:28:33,400 --> 04:28:34,820
What are you scared of, Sayori?

5718
04:28:35,660 --> 04:28:36,960
I'm scared that...

5719
04:28:36,960 --> 04:28:38,860
that I might like you more than you like me.

5720
04:28:39,400 --> 04:28:40,740
There's a high chance of that.

5721
04:28:42,280 --> 04:28:42,720
Sayori.

5722
04:28:43,440 --> 04:28:44,460
It's true, isn't it?

5723
04:28:45,100 --> 04:28:47,240
I was weak and started to like you too much.

5724
04:28:47,980 --> 04:28:49,260
I did this to myself.

5725
04:28:50,860 --> 04:28:51,300
Oliver.

5726
04:28:52,520 --> 04:28:54,520
I like you so much that I want to die.

5727
04:28:55,600 --> 04:28:56,680
That's how I feel.

5728
04:28:57,960 --> 04:28:59,080
And...

5729
04:28:59,720 --> 04:29:00,760
That's enough, Sayori.

5730
04:29:01,700 --> 04:29:03,120
I don't want you to hurt anymore.

5731
04:29:04,120 --> 04:29:07,500
I slide my hand down Sayori's arm and squeeze her hand on my own.

5732
04:29:08,500 --> 04:29:11,160
Do you remember how I said I always know what's best for you?

5733
04:29:12,240 --> 04:29:13,300
Did I say that?

5734
04:29:15,020 --> 04:29:16,480
What a weird thing to say.

5735
04:29:17,540 --> 04:29:18,760
Do you still believe me?

5736
04:29:20,880 --> 04:29:22,340
Wordlessly, Sayori nods.

5737
04:29:23,840 --> 04:29:25,600
Even if you don't understand all of your feelings...

5738
04:29:26,660 --> 04:29:28,580
I know what you need the most right now.

5739
04:29:29,520 --> 04:29:31,140
And that's what I'm gonna give to you.

5740
04:29:31,940 --> 04:29:32,380
Huh?

5741
04:29:34,340 --> 04:29:35,580
Yeah, I'm not saying that.

5742
04:29:37,500 --> 04:29:39,000
I don't know if she's my dearest friend.

5743
04:29:39,180 --> 04:29:40,740
But I guess she is...

5744
04:29:40,740 --> 04:29:42,420
From a lore perspective, right?

5745
04:29:42,460 --> 04:29:43,620
We are best friends, I guess?

5746
04:29:43,760 --> 04:29:43,940
Yeah.

5747
04:29:46,520 --> 04:29:48,120
You'll always be my dearest friend.

5748
04:29:49,500 --> 04:29:51,960
What you need most is for things to be like they've always been.

5749
04:29:53,120 --> 04:29:54,300
Monica told me the truth.

5750
04:29:55,140 --> 04:29:57,880
She told me how much happier you seemed after I joined the club.

5751
04:29:59,080 --> 04:30:02,980
I know you're struggling with some totally or really difficult feelings right now.

5752
04:30:04,420 --> 04:30:04,820
But...

5753
04:30:04,820 --> 04:30:06,620
Please trust me that I know what's best.

5754
04:30:07,580 --> 04:30:09,260
And what will make you happy in the end.

5755
04:30:10,100 --> 04:30:12,300
I promise I'll get things back to the way they were.

5756
04:30:13,980 --> 04:30:14,420
I...

5757
04:30:14,420 --> 04:30:15,040
See.

5758
04:30:17,240 --> 04:30:20,240
Sayori forces a smile through an incredibly pained expression.

5759
04:30:21,140 --> 04:30:21,940
Yeah, it's rough.

5760
04:30:22,360 --> 04:30:23,800
You never wanna be told this.

5761
04:30:25,260 --> 04:30:25,700
Ah...

5762
04:30:25,700 --> 04:30:27,940
Is this what it feels like to get stabbed in the chest?

5763
04:30:28,920 --> 04:30:30,560
I should write a poem about this.

5764
04:30:32,740 --> 04:30:33,180
Sayori...

5765
04:30:33,180 --> 04:30:34,360
It's okay.

5766
04:30:34,940 --> 04:30:35,460
It's okay.

5767
04:30:35,460 --> 04:30:36,680
This is just my punishment, remember?

5768
04:30:37,840 --> 04:30:39,280
For being so selfish.

5769
04:30:40,560 --> 04:30:41,600
So please...

5770
04:30:42,180 --> 04:30:43,840
Please don't worry about these stupid feelings.

5771
04:30:44,820 --> 04:30:45,920
I know you're right.

5772
04:30:45,920 --> 04:30:49,520
I knew this whole time that there's no happiness down that path.

5773
04:30:50,340 --> 04:30:51,460
That's why I came here.

5774
04:30:52,380 --> 04:30:54,340
Just so I could get the answer I needed to hear.

5775
04:30:54,980 --> 04:30:55,940
And the other thing.

5776
04:30:56,920 --> 04:30:59,720
You're also right that I just wanted to go back to the way it was.

5777
04:31:00,200 --> 04:31:01,400
I realize that now.

5778
04:31:02,100 --> 04:31:04,280
You really do know me better than anyone, Oliver.

5779
04:31:04,780 --> 04:31:06,020
I'll trust you with anything.

5780
04:31:06,820 --> 04:31:07,620
Anything at all.

5781
04:31:08,720 --> 04:31:09,240
So...

5782
04:31:09,240 --> 04:31:11,200
Sayori's smile finally breaks.

5783
04:31:11,620 --> 04:31:14,160
All of a sudden, she turns around and drops to her knees.

5784
04:31:15,720 --> 04:31:16,240
Aaaaaaah!

5785
04:31:18,540 --> 04:31:23,160
Clutching her head with both hands, she screams as loudly as she can- Damn.

5786
04:31:24,480 --> 04:31:27,160
I'm so shocked that I don't know how to react.

5787
04:31:30,560 --> 04:31:31,780
And then she flies off.

5788
04:31:32,340 --> 04:31:37,480
Sayori looks over her shoulder and flashes me one more weak smile, before turning around and running off.

5789
04:31:39,500 --> 04:31:39,940
Sayori!

5790
04:31:41,600 --> 04:31:43,920
I'm left helplessly standing in front of my house.

5791
04:31:45,040 --> 04:31:47,220
Why am I feeling so horrible about this?

5792
04:31:47,720 --> 04:31:50,660
Because it's never nice to tell someone that you don't love them back.

5793
04:31:52,600 --> 04:31:53,360
It sucks.

5794
04:31:54,700 --> 04:31:56,200
There's nothing more that I could have done.

5795
04:31:57,580 --> 04:32:02,040
The most I can do is support Sayori through her feelings and help her on the path that's right.

5796
04:32:02,860 --> 04:32:06,020
But I'm having as much trouble understanding Sayori's feelings as she is.

5797
04:32:06,920 --> 04:32:11,740
Even though I can comfort her, I keep wondering if I should be doing something more or something different.

5798
04:32:12,700 --> 04:32:16,480
I know these thoughts will continue to plague me until things are back to the way they were.

5799
04:32:17,340 --> 04:32:18,680
I'm gonna give it everything I've got.

5800
04:32:19,780 --> 04:32:21,660
Sayori will always be my dearest friend.

5801
04:32:22,280 --> 04:32:24,980
And I'll do whatever it takes to put a smile on her face every day.

5802
04:32:26,380 --> 04:32:30,200
And maybe also have some people check on her when I'm not able to.

5803
04:32:30,480 --> 04:32:31,940
Because I'm getting a bad vibe now.

5804
04:32:33,740 --> 04:32:35,860
Day of the fest... let's save the game and stop here, man.

5805
04:32:36,400 --> 04:32:37,320
It's been a long one.

5806
04:32:37,940 --> 04:32:38,760
Let's stop it there.

5807
04:32:42,670 --> 04:32:43,470
What's up, guys?

5808
04:32:48,630 --> 04:32:49,210
What's up?

5809
04:32:51,650 --> 04:32:52,590
Man, you...

5810
04:32:52,590 --> 04:32:53,250
Did you...

5811
04:32:53,250 --> 04:32:55,450
Haha, did I get you with the...

5812
04:32:56,200 --> 04:32:57,750
With the flip there at the start?

5813
04:32:58,530 --> 04:33:00,150
You didn't expect that, did you?

5814
04:33:05,390 --> 04:33:06,670
Yeah, that was a sad ending.

5815
04:33:09,910 --> 04:33:10,710
Pretty sad.

5816
04:33:18,470 --> 04:33:20,650
You can actually have, like, a physical...

5817
04:33:21,450 --> 04:33:25,470
Like, a really physical response to something emotional, you know?

5818
04:33:26,090 --> 04:33:28,650
Like, when I had to break up with one of my ex-girlfriends.

5819
04:33:29,330 --> 04:33:30,870
There was no good time to do it, right?

5820
04:33:30,890 --> 04:33:32,010
It was a long distance thing.

5821
04:33:33,570 --> 04:33:33,910
And...

5822
04:33:33,910 --> 04:33:38,070
Like, do you break up with her while you're visiting her in her country?

5823
04:33:38,650 --> 04:33:41,590
Or when they're visiting you and you're in my country?

5824
04:33:41,730 --> 04:33:42,710
Or do you do it over the...

5825
04:33:42,710 --> 04:33:43,950
There's no good way to do it.

5826
04:33:44,430 --> 04:33:44,750
Right?

5827
